window.__saMergeCourse({id:'al-physics-9702', board:'alevel', title:'Physics (9702)', urdu:'', class:'A Level', icon:'⚛️', desc:'Cambridge A Level Physics 9702 — full AS & A2 syllabus with lessons and quizzes.', chapters:[
  {id:'al-phy-t1', title:'Physical quantities and units (AS)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-phy-t1-l1', title:'Base and derived quantities', html:'<h3>Base and derived quantities</h3><p>Every quantity in physics is either a <strong>base quantity</strong> or a <strong>derived quantity</strong>. The SI system uses seven base quantities: mass (kg), length (m), time (s), current (A), temperature (K), amount of substance (mol) and luminous intensity (cd). Every other unit — the newton, joule, watt, volt — can be expressed as a product of base units.</p><p>To check a formula, express everything in base units. Force = mass × acceleration has base units kg m s<sup>-2</sup>, so 1 N = 1 kg m s<sup>-2</sup>. Work = force × distance gives kg m s<sup>-2</sup> × m = kg m<sup>2</sup> s<sup>-2</sup>, so 1 J = 1 kg m<sup>2</sup> s<sup>-2</sup>. Voltage = energy / charge gives (kg m<sup>2</sup> s<sup>-2</sup>) / (A s) = kg m<sup>2</sup> s<sup>-3</sup> A<sup>-1</sup>, so 1 V = 1 kg m<sup>2</sup> s<sup>-3</sup> A<sup>-1</sup>.</p>'},
    {id:'al-phy-t1-l2', title:'Homogeneity and dimensional analysis', html:'<h3>Homogeneity and dimensional analysis</h3><p>An equation is <strong>homogeneous</strong> if every term has the same base units. You can use this to verify a remembered formula — but note that a homogeneous equation can still be numerically wrong (e.g. missing a factor of 2π or a constant), so homogeneity is a check, not a proof.</p><p>Example: is v<sup>2</sup> = u<sup>2</sup> + 2as homogeneous? Left side: (m s<sup>-1</sup>)<sup>2</sup> = m<sup>2</sup> s<sup>-2</sup>. Right side: u<sup>2</sup> has m<sup>2</sup> s<sup>-2</sup>, and 2as has m s<sup>-2</sup> × m = m<sup>2</sup> s<sup>-2</sup>. All terms match, so the equation is homogeneous — consistent with a correct relation.</p><p>Worked exam-style reasoning: ρgh has units (kg m<sup>-3</sup>)(m s<sup>-2</sup>)(m) = kg m<sup>-1</sup> s<sup>-2</sup>, exactly the unit of pressure (force/area = kg m s<sup>-2</sup>/m<sup>2</sup> = kg m<sup>-1</sup> s<sup>-2</sup>). So p = ρgh is homogeneous and could be correct for the pressure at depth h in a fluid.</p>'},
    {id:'al-phy-t1-l3', title:'Estimates, precision and prefixes', html:'<h3>Estimates, precision and prefixes</h3><p>A-Level physics expects sensible <strong>order-of-magnitude estimates</strong>: know that a walking speed is ~1 m s<sup>-1</sup>, a car ~30 m s<sup>-1</sup>, atmospheric pressure ~10<sup>5</sup> Pa, room temperature ~300 K, the speed of sound ~340 m s<sup>-1</sup>, and the charge of an electron 1.60×10<sup>-19</sup> C. Estimate questions reward a clear line of reasoning more than the final number.</p><p>Know SI prefixes by heart: nano (10<sup>-9</sup>), micro (10<sup>-6</sup>), milli (10<sup>-3</sup>), kilo (10<sup>3</sup>), mega (10<sup>6</sup>), giga (10<sup>9</sup>), tera (10<sup>12</sup>). Conversions are a favourite exam trap: 1 nm = 10<sup>-9</sup> m, 1 cm<sup>3</sup> = 10<sup>-6</sup> m<sup>3</sup>, 1 mm<sup>2</sup> = 10<sup>-6</sup> m<sup>2</sup>.</p><p>Precision: quote answers to a number of significant figures consistent with the data (usually 2–4). Random errors scatter results and are reduced by averaging repeats; systematic errors shift every result the same way and cannot be removed by averaging.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'Which of the following is an SI base unit?', options:['newton','joule','kelvin','pascal'], answer:2, explain:'Kelvin is one of the seven SI base units (temperature). Newton, joule and pascal are all derived units.'},
    {q:'Expressed in base units, 1 newton is equal to', options:['kg m s<sup>-1</sup>','kg m s<sup>-2</sup>','kg m<sup>2</sup> s<sup>-2</sup>','kg m<sup>-1</sup> s<sup>-2</sup>'], answer:1, explain:'F = ma, so 1 N = 1 kg × 1 m s<sup>-2</sup> = 1 kg m s<sup>-2</sup>.'},
    {q:'1 volt in base units is', options:['kg m<sup>2</sup> s<sup>-3</sup> A<sup>-1</sup>','kg m<sup>2</sup> s<sup>-2</sup> A<sup>-1</sup>','kg m s<sup>-3</sup> A','A s kg m<sup>-2</sup>'], answer:0, explain:'V = J/C = (kg m<sup>2</sup> s<sup>-2</sup>)/(A s) = kg m<sup>2</sup> s<sup>-3</sup> A<sup>-1</sup>.'},
    {q:'Which equation is NOT homogeneous?', options:['v<sup>2</sup> = u<sup>2</sup> + 2as','W = Fd','P = F/v','T = 2π√(l/g)'], answer:2, explain:'Power should be force × velocity (Fv), not F/v. F/v has units kg s<sup>-1</sup>, while power needs kg m<sup>2</sup> s<sup>-3</sup>.'},
    {q:'A homogeneous equation must be', options:['numerically correct','dimensionally consistent','free of constants','a law of physics'], answer:1, explain:'Homogeneity means every term has the same base units. A homogeneous equation can still be numerically wrong (e.g. missing constants).'},
    {q:'1 MW is equal to', options:['10<sup>3</sup> W','10<sup>6</sup> W','10<sup>9</sup> W','10<sup>-6</sup> W'], answer:1, explain:'Mega = 10<sup>6</sup>, so 1 MW = 10<sup>6</sup> W.'},
    {q:'Which estimate is most reasonable for the mass of an apple?', options:['0.1 g','0.1 kg','10 kg','0.001 kg'], answer:1, explain:'A typical apple is about 100 g = 0.1 kg. The others are off by orders of magnitude.'},
    {q:'Averaging repeated measurements mainly reduces', options:['systematic errors','random errors','zero errors','human errors'], answer:1, explain:'Random errors scatter about the true value, so averaging cancels them. Systematic errors shift every reading and survive averaging.'},
    {q:'The SI unit of electrical charge is the', options:['volt','coulomb','ampere','ohm'], answer:1, explain:'Charge is measured in coulombs; 1 C = 1 A s. Current (ampere) is the base quantity.'},
    {q:'Which quantity is a vector?', options:['speed','distance','velocity','temperature'], answer:2, explain:'Velocity has magnitude and direction, so it is a vector. Speed, distance and temperature are scalars.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-phy-t2', title:'Kinematics (AS)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-phy-t2-l1', title:'Displacement, velocity and acceleration', html:'<h3>Displacement, velocity and acceleration</h3><p><strong>Displacement</strong> is the vector from start to finish; <strong>distance</strong> is the total path length. Average velocity = displacement / time; average speed = distance / time. <strong>Acceleration</strong> is the rate of change of velocity, a = Δv/Δt, in m s<sup>-2</sup>.</p><p>On a displacement–time graph the gradient is velocity; on a velocity–time graph the gradient is acceleration and the <strong>area under the curve is displacement</strong>. A curved displacement–time graph means changing velocity (non-uniform motion).</p><p>For uniform acceleration the equations of motion are: v = u + at, s = ut + ½at<sup>2</sup>, v<sup>2</sup> = u<sup>2</sup> + 2as, s = ½(u+v)t. They apply only when acceleration is constant.</p>'},
    {id:'al-phy-t2-l2', title:'Using the equations of motion', html:'<h3>Using the equations of motion</h3><p>Worked example: a stone is thrown vertically upward at 15 m s<sup>-1</sup>. Taking up as positive, a = −9.81 m s<sup>-2</sup>. Maximum height occurs when v = 0: v<sup>2</sup> = u<sup>2</sup> + 2as → 0 = 225 − 19.62s → s = 11.5 m. Time to reach it: v = u + at → 0 = 15 − 9.81t → t = 1.53 s. Total flight time (back to launch level) = 2 × 1.53 = 3.06 s, by symmetry.</p><p>Exam reasoning: for projectile motion, horizontal and vertical components are independent. With no air resistance the horizontal velocity is constant (a<sub>x</sub> = 0) while vertical motion has a<sub>y</sub> = −g. The trajectory is a parabola.</p><p>Worked example: a ball rolls off a 1.25 m high table at 3.0 m s<sup>-1</sup> horizontally. Time to fall: s = ut + ½at<sup>2</sup> → 1.25 = 0 + 4.905t<sup>2</sup> → t = 0.505 s. Horizontal range = 3.0 × 0.505 = 1.52 m.</p>'},
    {id:'al-phy-t2-l3', title:'Terminal velocity and air resistance', html:'<h3>Terminal velocity and air resistance</h3><p>An object falling through a fluid experiences drag, which increases with speed. The resultant force ma = mg − drag, so acceleration decreases as drag grows. Eventually drag = weight and the resultant force is zero: the object falls at constant <strong>terminal velocity</strong>.</p><p>Shape of the velocity–time graph: a curve whose gradient (acceleration) starts at g and flattens to zero. The curve is steep at first and levels off — an exponential-like approach to terminal velocity.</p><p>Practical consequence: a skydiver reaches terminal velocity (~60 m s<sup>-1</sup> spread out); opening the parachute increases drag hugely, giving a new, much lower terminal velocity (~6 m s<sup>-1</sup>). The velocity–time graph shows a sudden drop then levelling at the new value.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'A car travels 60 km east then 40 km west. Its displacement is', options:['100 km east','20 km east','100 km west','20 km west'], answer:1, explain:'Displacement is the vector from start to finish: 60 − 40 = 20 km east (final position is 20 km east of start).'},
    {q:'The area under a velocity–time graph gives', options:['acceleration','displacement','distance travelled only','average speed'], answer:1, explain:'Area under a velocity–time graph = velocity × time = displacement (signed distance).'},
    {q:'On a displacement–time graph, a horizontal straight line means the object is', options:['moving at constant speed','accelerating','stationary','moving backwards'], answer:2, explain:'Horizontal line = displacement not changing with time, so velocity is zero: stationary.'},
    {q:'A body accelerates uniformly from rest at 4.0 m s<sup>-2</sup> for 5.0 s. Its velocity is', options:['20 m s<sup>-1</sup>','50 m s<sup>-1</sup>','80 m s<sup>-1</sup>','0.8 m s<sup>-1</sup>'], answer:0, explain:'v = u + at = 0 + 4.0 × 5.0 = 20 m s<sup>-1</sup>.'},
    {q:'A ball is dropped from rest and hits the ground after 2.0 s. Taking g = 10 m s<sup>-2</sup>, the drop height is', options:['10 m','20 m','40 m','100 m'], answer:1, explain:'s = ut + ½at<sup>2</sup> = 0 + ½ × 10 × 4 = 20 m.'},
    {q:'In projectile motion without air resistance, the horizontal component of velocity is', options:['increasing','decreasing','constant','zero'], answer:2, explain:'No horizontal force means no horizontal acceleration, so horizontal velocity stays constant.'},
    {q:'An object reaches terminal velocity when', options:['drag = 0','drag = weight','acceleration is maximum','weight = 0'], answer:1, explain:'Terminal velocity occurs when drag equals weight, so the resultant force (and acceleration) is zero.'},
    {q:'Which equation of motion cannot be used when acceleration varies?', options:['v = u + at','all of them','none of them','s = ½(u+v)t'], answer:1, explain:'All four SUVAT equations are derived assuming constant acceleration; none apply when acceleration varies.'},
    {q:'A velocity–time graph with constant negative gradient represents', options:['constant speed','uniform deceleration','increasing acceleration','rest'], answer:1, explain:'Gradient = acceleration. A constant negative gradient means constant (negative) acceleration: uniform deceleration.'},
    {q:'A stone thrown up at 20 m s<sup>-1</sup> reaches maximum height when its velocity is', options:['20 m s<sup>-1</sup>','10 m s<sup>-1</sup>','0','−20 m s<sup>-1</sup>'], answer:2, explain:'At the top of its path the stone momentarily stops before falling back, so v = 0.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-phy-t3', title:'Dynamics (AS)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-phy-t3-l1', title:'Newton’s laws of motion', html:'<h3>Newton’s laws of motion</h3><p><strong>First law (inertia):</strong> a body remains at rest or in uniform motion unless acted on by a resultant force. It defines inertial frames and the concept of zero net force.</p><p><strong>Second law:</strong> the rate of change of momentum of a body is proportional to the resultant force and in its direction. Defining force via F = ma, momentum p = mv gives F = Δp/Δt. This is the operational definition of the newton: 1 N accelerates 1 kg at 1 m s<sup>-2</sup>.</p><p><strong>Third law:</strong> if body A exerts a force on body B, body B exerts an equal and opposite force on body A. The pair acts on <strong>different bodies</strong> — this is why weight and normal reaction are NOT a Newton third-law pair (both act on the same book).</p>'},
    {id:'al-phy-t3-l2', title:'Momentum and its conservation', html:'<h3>Momentum and its conservation</h3><p>Momentum p = mv is a vector. <strong>Impulse</strong> = force × time = Δp = Ft. This explains why airbags work: they extend the impact time t, reducing the force F for the same change in momentum.</p><p><strong>Conservation:</strong> when no external resultant force acts, total momentum is conserved. Derivation: the only forces are internal action–reaction pairs, which are equal, opposite and act for equal times, so their impulses cancel: total Δp = 0.</p><p>Worked example: a 2.0 kg trolley moving at 3.0 m s<sup>-1</sup> hits a stationary 1.0 kg trolley and they stick together. 2.0 × 3.0 = 3.0v → v = 2.0 m s<sup>-1</sup>. In collisions, momentum is conserved but kinetic energy generally is not (some becomes heat/sound) unless the collision is perfectly elastic.</p>'},
    {id:'al-phy-t3-l3', title:'Elastic and inelastic collisions', html:'<h3>Elastic and inelastic collisions</h3><p>In a <strong>perfectly elastic collision</strong>, both momentum and kinetic energy are conserved — no kinetic energy is lost to deformation. Real collisions are usually inelastic: kinetic energy is lost, but momentum is still conserved (as long as no external impulse acts).</p><p>Exam reasoning: in a perfectly elastic head-on collision between equal masses where one is initially stationary, the moving mass stops dead and the stationary one takes all the velocity — this conserves both momentum (mv = mv) and kinetic energy (½mv<sup>2</sup> = ½mv<sup>2</sup>).</p><p>Relative velocity approach: for elastic collisions, relative speed of approach = relative speed of separation. Combined with momentum conservation, this lets you solve one-dimensional elastic collisions without energy equations. Check answers: momentum conserved AND kinetic energy not exceeding the initial total.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'Newton’s first law is about', options:['action–reaction pairs','inertia','F = ma','momentum conservation'], answer:1, explain:'The first law states that bodies keep their state of motion unless a resultant force acts — the principle of inertia.'},
    {q:'The newton is defined as the force that', options:['gives 1 g an acceleration of 1 m s<sup>-2</sup>','gives 1 kg an acceleration of 1 m s<sup>-2</sup>','moves 1 kg at 1 m s<sup>-1</sup>','lifts 1 kg'], answer:1, explain:'From F = ma, 1 N = 1 kg × 1 m s<sup>-2</sup>.'},
    {q:'Which pair is a Newton’s third-law pair?', options:['weight of a book and normal force from the table','force of book on table and force of table on book','weight and friction','normal force and friction'], answer:1, explain:'Third-law pairs act on different bodies. The book pushes down on the table; the table pushes up on the book. Weight and normal both act on the book.'},
    {q:'Impulse equals', options:['F/v','Ft','mv<sup>2</sup>','F/t'], answer:1, explain:'Impulse = force × time = change in momentum (Ft = Δp).'},
    {q:'Airbags reduce injury mainly by', options:['reducing the change in momentum','increasing the impact time','absorbing all the energy','reducing the passenger’s mass'], answer:1, explain:'Δp is fixed by the crash, but F = Δp/t: a longer impact time means a smaller force on the passenger.'},
    {q:'A 3.0 kg mass at 4.0 m s<sup>-1</sup> collides with a stationary 2.0 kg mass and they stick. The combined speed is', options:['2.4 m s<sup>-1</sup>','1.2 m s<sup>-1</sup>','4.0 m s<sup>-1</sup>','6.0 m s<sup>-1</sup>'], answer:0, explain:'3.0 × 4.0 = 5.0v → v = 12/5 = 2.4 m s<sup>-1</sup>.'},
    {q:'Momentum is conserved in collisions provided', options:['the collision is elastic','no external impulse acts','kinetic energy is conserved','masses are equal'], answer:1, explain:'Momentum conservation needs only zero net external force (no external impulse). It holds for elastic and inelastic collisions alike.'},
    {q:'In a perfectly elastic collision, which is conserved?', options:['only momentum','only kinetic energy','both momentum and kinetic energy','neither'], answer:2, explain:'Perfectly elastic means no KE lost to deformation, so both momentum and kinetic energy are conserved.'},
    {q:'For two equal masses in a perfectly elastic head-on collision with one initially at rest, the moving mass', options:['bounces back at half speed','stops completely','continues at half speed','sticks to the other'], answer:1, explain:'Momentum mv must transfer fully; energy ½mv<sup>2</sup> also transfers fully only if the first mass stops and the second moves off at v.'},
    {q:'A force of 12 N acts for 0.50 s on a 4.0 kg object at rest. Its final speed is', options:['1.5 m s<sup>-1</sup>','3.0 m s<sup>-1</sup>','6.0 m s<sup>-1</sup>','24 m s<sup>-1</sup>'], answer:0, explain:'Ft = Δp = mv → 12 × 0.50 = 4.0v → v = 6.0/4.0 = 1.5 m s<sup>-1</sup>.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-phy-t4', title:'Forces, density and pressure (AS)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-phy-t4-l1', title:'Types of force and free-body diagrams', html:'<h3>Types of force and free-body diagrams</h3><p>Forces you must recognise: <strong>weight</strong> (mg, acting at the centre of gravity), <strong>normal reaction</strong> (perpendicular to a surface), <strong>tension</strong> (along a string), <strong>friction</strong> (parallel to surfaces, opposing relative motion), <strong>upthrust/drag</strong> (in fluids), and elastic forces.</p><p>A <strong>free-body diagram</strong> shows only the forces acting ON the chosen body, drawn as arrows from the centre of gravity (or point of application). Never mix in forces the body exerts on something else — those belong on the other body’s diagram.</p><p>Equilibrium: resultant force = 0. For a body on an incline at angle θ, resolve weight into components: mg sinθ down the slope and mg cosθ perpendicular into the slope. Friction up the slope balances mg sinθ for a stationary body, up to its maximum μN = μmg cosθ.</p>'},
    {id:'al-phy-t4-l2', title:'Density and pressure', html:'<h3>Density and pressure</h3><p><strong>Density</strong> ρ = m/V in kg m<sup>-3</sup>. Water is 1000 kg m<sup>-3</sup>; air about 1.2 kg m<sup>-3</sup>. A body floats in a fluid if its average density is less than the fluid’s, sinks if greater.</p><p><strong>Pressure</strong> p = F/A in pascals (1 Pa = 1 N m<sup>-2</sup>). In a fluid at rest, pressure acts in all directions and increases with depth: p = ρgh (gauge pressure — the pressure due to the fluid alone, excluding atmospheric pressure). Total pressure at depth = atmospheric pressure + ρgh.</p><p>Derivation of p = ρgh: consider a column of fluid of area A and height h. Its weight = ρAhg; pressure = weight/area = ρAhg/A = ρgh. Units check: kg m<sup>-3</sup> × m s<sup>-2</sup> × m = kg m<sup>-1</sup> s<sup>-2</sup> = Pa. ✓</p>'},
    {id:'al-phy-t4-l3', title:'Upthrust, Archimedes’ principle and moments', html:'<h3>Upthrust, Archimedes’ principle and moments</h3><p><strong>Archimedes’ principle:</strong> a body immersed in a fluid experiences an upthrust equal to the weight of fluid displaced. A floating body displaces a weight of fluid equal to its own weight — hence upthrust = weight for a floating body.</p><p>Worked example: a 0.60 kg block floats with 2/3 of its volume submerged. Upthrust = weight = 5.9 N. Volume submerged = 0.60/1000 = 6.0×10<sup>-4</sup> m<sup>3</sup>, so total volume = 9.0×10<sup>-4</sup> m<sup>3</sup> and block density = 0.60/9.0×10<sup>-4</sup> = 667 kg m<sup>-3</sup>. ✓ (< 1000, so it floats.)</p><p><strong>Moments:</strong> moment = force × perpendicular distance from pivot. In equilibrium, the <strong>principle of moments</strong> states that the sum of clockwise moments equals the sum of anticlockwise moments about any point. A couple (two equal, opposite, parallel forces) produces a pure turning effect: torque = F × distance between the forces.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'On an incline of angle θ, the component of weight acting down the slope is', options:['mg cosθ','mg sinθ','mg tanθ','mg'], answer:1, explain:'Resolving weight perpendicular and parallel to the slope: the parallel (down-slope) component is mg sinθ, and the perpendicular one is mg cosθ.'},
    {q:'The SI unit of density is', options:['g cm<sup>-3</sup>','kg m<sup>-3</sup>','N m<sup>-2</sup>','kg m<sup>2</sup>'], answer:1, explain:'Density ρ = m/V has SI unit kg m<sup>-3</sup>. g cm<sup>-3</sup> is a common non-SI unit (1 g cm<sup>-3</sup> = 1000 kg m<sup>-3</sup>).'},
    {q:'A 2.0 kg cube of side 0.10 m has density', options:['200 kg m<sup>-3</sup>','2000 kg m<sup>-3</sup>','20 kg m<sup>-3</sup>','20000 kg m<sup>-3</sup>'], answer:1, explain:'V = 0.10<sup>3</sup> = 0.001 m<sup>3</sup>; ρ = 2.0/0.001 = 2000 kg m<sup>-3</sup>.'},
    {q:'The gauge pressure at depth 5.0 m in water (ρ = 1000 kg m<sup>-3</sup>, g = 10 m s<sup>-2</sup>) is', options:['2.0×10<sup>3</sup> Pa','5.0×10<sup>4</sup> Pa','2.0×10<sup>5</sup> Pa','1.0×10<sup>5</sup> Pa'], answer:1, explain:'p = ρgh = 1000 × 10 × 5.0 = 5.0×10<sup>4</sup> Pa.'},
    {q:'Archimedes’ principle states that upthrust equals', options:['the weight of the object','the weight of fluid displaced','the pressure at the base','the density of the fluid'], answer:1, explain:'Upthrust = weight of displaced fluid. For a floating body this equals the body’s own weight, but the principle is about displaced fluid.'},
    {q:'A body floats when its average density is', options:['greater than the fluid’s','less than the fluid’s','equal to air’s','independent of the fluid'], answer:1, explain:'Floating needs upthrust (max = weight of fluid equal to body’s volume) to balance weight; this requires body density < fluid density.'},
    {q:'The principle of moments states that for equilibrium', options:['resultant force is zero','clockwise moments = anticlockwise moments','upthrust = weight','friction = applied force'], answer:1, explain:'For rotational equilibrium about any point, total clockwise moment equals total anticlockwise moment.'},
    {q:'A couple produces', options:['pure translation','a pure turning effect','zero net moment','acceleration only'], answer:1, explain:'A couple is two equal, opposite, parallel forces: net force zero, net moment = F × separation — pure turning.'},
    {q:'Friction on a stationary block on a slope acts', options:['down the slope','up the slope','perpendicular to the slope','vertically up'], answer:1, explain:'Friction opposes the tendency to slide, so it acts up the slope, balancing the mg sinθ component.'},
    {q:'1 Pa is equal to', options:['1 N m','1 N m<sup>-2</sup>','1 N m<sup>-3</sup>','1 kg m s<sup>-1</sup>'], answer:1, explain:'Pressure = force/area, so 1 Pa = 1 N m<sup>-2</sup>.'}
   ]}
,
  {id:'al-phy-t5', title:'Work, energy and power (AS)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-phy-t5-l1', title:'Work and kinetic energy', html:'<h3>Work and kinetic energy</h3><p><strong>Work</strong> W = Fd cosθ, where θ is the angle between force and displacement. Only the component of force along the displacement does work: a force perpendicular to motion (e.g. the normal force on a car on level ground) does zero work.</p><p><strong>Kinetic energy</strong> KE = ½mv<sup>2</sup>. Derivation from F = ma: W = Fd = ma × d. With constant acceleration from rest, v<sup>2</sup> = 2ad → d = v<sup>2</sup>/2a, so W = ma × v<sup>2</sup>/2a = ½mv<sup>2</sup>. The <strong>work–energy theorem</strong>: net work done on a body equals its change in kinetic energy.</p><p><strong>Gravitational potential energy</strong> GPE = mgh near the Earth’s surface, where h is the height above a chosen reference level. Elastic potential energy in a stretched spring = ½kx<sup>2</sup> (area under the F–x graph, a triangle: ½ × kx × x).</p>'},
    {id:'al-phy-t5-l2', title:'Conservation of energy', html:'<h3>Conservation of energy</h3><p>The <strong>principle of conservation of energy</strong>: energy cannot be created or destroyed, only transferred from one form to another. Total energy in a closed system is constant.</p><p>Worked example: a 2.0 kg block slides down a frictionless 5.0 m high slope. Loss of GPE = mgh = 2.0 × 9.81 × 5.0 = 98 J. All becomes KE: ½ × 2.0 × v<sup>2</sup> = 98 → v = √(98) ≈ 9.9 m s<sup>-1</sup>. Note the answer is independent of the slope’s angle — only the vertical drop matters.</p><p>With friction, some mechanical energy becomes thermal: mgh = ½mv<sup>2</sup> + W<sub>friction</sub>. Exam reasoning: always identify the system, list energy stores before and after, and equate total energy before = total after (closed system) or account for work done against friction/air resistance.</p>'},
    {id:'al-phy-t5-l3', title:'Power and efficiency', html:'<h3>Power and efficiency</h3><p><strong>Power</strong> P = W/t = rate of doing work, in watts (1 W = 1 J s<sup>-1</sup>). For a force moving an object at velocity v: P = Fv (from W = Fd, P = Fd/t = Fv). Useful for engines: power = driving force × speed at constant speed on level ground (driving force = drag).</p><p>Worked example: a car of mass 1200 kg climbs a 1 in 20 slope at 25 m s<sup>-1</sup> with drag 800 N. Component of weight down slope = mg/20 = 1200 × 9.81/20 = 588 N. Total resistive force = 800 + 588 = 1388 N. Power = Fv = 1388 × 25 ≈ 34.7 kW.</p><p><strong>Efficiency</strong> = useful energy (or power) output / total energy (or power) input × 100%. Sankey diagrams show energy flows with arrow widths proportional to amounts; efficiency = useful output arrow width / total input width.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'Work is defined as', options:['F × d × sinθ','F × d × cosθ','F/d','F × v'], answer:1, explain:'W = Fd cosθ where θ is the angle between force and displacement.'},
    {q:'The kinetic energy of a 4.0 kg mass moving at 5.0 m s<sup>-1</sup> is', options:['10 J','20 J','50 J','100 J'], answer:2, explain:'KE = ½mv<sup>2</sup> = 0.5 × 4.0 × 25 = 50 J.'},
    {q:'A 60 kg person climbs 3.0 m vertically. Taking g = 10 m s<sup>-2</sup>, the gain in GPE is', options:['180 J','1800 J','200 J','600 J'], answer:1, explain:'GPE = mgh = 60 × 10 × 3.0 = 1800 J.'},
    {q:'The work–energy theorem states that net work equals', options:['change in momentum','change in kinetic energy','change in potential energy','power × time<sup>2</sup>'], answer:1, explain:'Net work done on a body equals its change in kinetic energy — this is the work–energy theorem.'},
    {q:'Elastic potential energy in a spring of stiffness k stretched by x is', options:['kx','½kx<sup>2</sup>','kx<sup>2</sup>','mgx'], answer:1, explain:'EPE is the area under the F–x graph: a triangle of area ½ × (kx) × x = ½kx<sup>2</sup>.'},
    {q:'A motor lifts 200 kg at 0.50 m s<sup>-1</sup>. Taking g = 10 m s<sup>-2</sup>, the useful power is', options:['100 W','1000 W','2000 W','400 W'], answer:1, explain:'P = Fv = mgv = 200 × 10 × 0.50 = 1000 W.'},
    {q:'Efficiency is defined as', options:['input/output × 100%','useful output/total input × 100%','power × time','input − output'], answer:1, explain:'Efficiency = (useful energy or power output / total energy or power input) × 100%.'},
    {q:'A machine with efficiency 40% and input power 500 W has useful output power', options:['1250 W','200 W','460 W','20 W'], answer:1, explain:'Useful output = 0.40 × 500 = 200 W.'},
    {q:'When a ball falls freely, its GPE loss equals', options:['its KE gain, exactly, ignoring air resistance','half its KE gain','twice its KE gain','zero'], answer:0, explain:'Energy conservation: without air resistance all lost GPE becomes KE.'},
    {q:'The unit of power, the watt, in base units is', options:['kg m<sup>2</sup> s<sup>-2</sup>','kg m<sup>2</sup> s<sup>-3</sup>','kg m s<sup>-2</sup>','kg m<sup>3</sup> s<sup>-3</sup>'], answer:1, explain:'Power = energy/time = (kg m<sup>2</sup> s<sup>-2</sup>)/s = kg m<sup>2</sup> s<sup>-3</sup>.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-phy-t6', title:'Deformation of solids (AS)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-phy-t6-l1', title:'Stress, strain and the Young modulus', html:'<h3>Stress, strain and the Young modulus</h3><p><strong>Tensile stress</strong> σ = F/A (Pa), <strong>tensile strain</strong> ε = extension/original length (dimensionless). While Hooke’s law holds, stress ∝ strain and the ratio is the <strong>Young modulus</strong> E = stress/strain = Fl/Ax, in Pa.</p><p>Worked example: a wire of length 2.0 m, cross-section 1.0 mm<sup>2</sup>, stretches 4.0 mm under a 200 N load. Stress = 200/(1.0×10<sup>-6</sup>) = 2.0×10<sup>8</sup> Pa. Strain = 4.0×10<sup>-3</sup>/2.0 = 2.0×10<sup>-3</sup>. E = 2.0×10<sup>8</sup>/2.0×10<sup>-3</sup> = 1.0×10<sup>11</sup> Pa (steel-like value).</p><p>E depends only on the material, not the wire’s dimensions. Stress–strain graphs: gradient in the linear region = Young modulus; the area under the curve = energy stored per unit volume.</p>'},
    {id:'al-phy-t6-l2', title:'Hooke’s law and force–extension graphs', html:'<h3>Hooke’s law and force–extension graphs</h3><p><strong>Hooke’s law:</strong> F = kx — extension is proportional to load, provided the <strong>limit of proportionality</strong> is not exceeded. Beyond it the graph curves; the <strong>elastic limit</strong> is the point beyond which the material will not return to its original length.</p><p>Key points on a force–extension graph: limit of proportionality (linearity ends), elastic limit (permanent deformation begins), yield point (large extension for small extra load, in ductile metals), breaking point. <strong>Brittle</strong> materials (glass, cast iron) break with little plastic deformation; <strong>ductile</strong> materials (copper) draw out; <strong>polymeric</strong> materials (rubber, polythene) show large extensions.</p><p>The area under an F–x graph = work done stretching the material. For Hooke’s-law region this is ½Fx = ½kx<sup>2</sup> = elastic potential energy. Loading and unloading a stretched rubber band traces a loop — the loop’s area is energy lost as heat (hysteresis).</p>'},
    {id:'al-phy-t6-l3', title:'Experiments: measuring the Young modulus', html:'<h3>Experiments: measuring the Young modulus</h3><p>Standard experiment: a long thin wire clamped at one end, loaded with slotted masses, extension measured with a vernier scale or travelling microscope. Long wire + thin diameter minimises percentage uncertainty (Δx/x and Δd/d dominate).</p><p>Uncertainty reasoning: extension should be measured from a fiducial marker with a reference wire to allow for thermal drift; diameter measured at several points along the wire with a micrometer and averaged (percentage uncertainty in A is 2× that in d, since A ∝ d<sup>2</sup>). Plot F against x — gradient = k = EA/l, so E = gradient × l/A.</p><p>Safety/exam reasoning: use goggles (wire may snap under load), keep the load below the breaking point, and check the graph remains linear — if it curves, Hooke’s law (and E = Fl/Ax) no longer applies.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'Tensile stress is defined as', options:['F × A','F/A','x/l','A/F'], answer:1, explain:'Stress = force per unit cross-sectional area = F/A, in pascals.'},
    {q:'Tensile strain is', options:['F/A','x/l','E × σ','F × x'], answer:1, explain:'Strain = extension ÷ original length, a dimensionless ratio.'},
    {q:'The Young modulus is equal to', options:['stress × strain','stress/strain','strain/stress','F × x'], answer:1, explain:'E = stress/strain = Fl/Ax, in pascals.'},
    {q:'Hooke’s law states that, within the limit of proportionality,', options:['stress = strain','extension ∝ load','E is constant for all materials','strain = 1/stress'], answer:1, explain:'F = kx: extension is directly proportional to the applied load.'},
    {q:'Beyond the elastic limit, a material', options:['obeys Hooke’s law','will not return to its original length','has infinite E','becomes brittle'], answer:1, explain:'Past the elastic limit, deformation is plastic — the material keeps a permanent extension.'},
    {q:'The area under a force–extension graph gives', options:['the Young modulus','work done stretching','the breaking stress','the spring constant'], answer:1, explain:'Work = ∫F dx = area under the F–x curve.'},
    {q:'Elastic potential energy in a spring obeying Hooke’s law is', options:['kx','½kx<sup>2</sup>','kx<sup>2</sup>','½Fx<sup>2</sup>'], answer:1, explain:'Area of the triangular F–x graph: ½ × (kx) × x = ½kx<sup>2</sup>.'},
    {q:'A wire of cross-section 2.0 mm<sup>2</sup> under a 100 N load experiences stress', options:['5.0×10<sup>7</sup> Pa','2.0×10<sup>5</sup> Pa','50 Pa','5.0×10<sup>-5</sup> Pa'], answer:0, explain:'σ = F/A = 100/(2.0×10<sup>-6</sup>) = 5.0×10<sup>7</sup> Pa.'},
    {q:'Which material property means it can be drawn into a wire?', options:['brittleness','ductility','stiffness','hardness'], answer:1, explain:'Ductility is the ability to undergo large plastic deformation — drawn into wire. Brittle materials fracture instead.'},
    {q:'In the Young modulus experiment, measuring the wire diameter with a micrometer at several points and averaging reduces', options:['systematic error from a zero error','random error in d and its large effect on A','parallax error','thermal expansion'], answer:1, explain:'Averaging reduces random error; it matters because A ∝ d<sup>2</sup>, so the percentage uncertainty in A is twice that in d.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-phy-t7', title:'Waves (AS)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-phy-t7-l1', title:'Wave properties and types', html:'<h3>Wave properties and types</h3><p>Waves transfer <strong>energy without transferring matter</strong>. Key terms: <strong>displacement</strong> (distance from equilibrium), <strong>amplitude</strong> (maximum displacement), <strong>wavelength</strong> λ (distance between adjacent identical points), <strong>period</strong> T, <strong>frequency</strong> f = 1/T, <strong>wave speed</strong> v = fλ.</p><p><strong>Transverse</strong> waves oscillate perpendicular to the direction of energy transfer (light, water ripples, S-waves); <strong>longitudinal</strong> waves oscillate parallel to it (sound, P-waves). In a longitudinal wave, <strong>compressions</strong> are high-pressure regions and <strong>rarefactions</strong> low-pressure regions.</p><p>All electromagnetic waves travel at c = 3.00×10<sup>8</sup> m s<sup>-1</sup> in a vacuum. The EM spectrum from long to short wavelength: radio, microwave, infrared, visible, ultraviolet, X-ray, gamma. Know one use and one hazard for each band (e.g. microwaves — cooking/communications, heating of tissue; X-rays — medical imaging, ionisation damage).</p>'},
    {id:'al-phy-t7-l2', title:'Progressive waves and phase', html:'<h3>Progressive waves and phase</h3><p>A <strong>progressive (travelling) wave</strong> carries energy from one place to another. Its displacement–distance graph is a snapshot at one instant; its displacement–time graph shows one particle’s motion. Both are sinusoids for a pure wave.</p><p><strong>Phase difference</strong> measures how far through the oscillation cycle two points are, in degrees or radians: 360° (2π rad) = one full wavelength. Phase difference = (2π/λ) × path difference. Points half a wavelength apart are in antiphase (180° apart) and always move in opposite directions.</p><p>Worked example: two points 0.40 m apart on a wave of λ = 1.6 m have path difference 0.40 m = λ/4, so phase difference = 2π × ¼ = π/2 rad (90°). If one is at maximum displacement, the other is passing through equilibrium.</p>'},
    {id:'al-phy-t7-l3', title:'Polarisation and the Doppler effect', html:'<h3>Polarisation and the Doppler effect</h3><p><strong>Polarisation</strong> restricts oscillations to one plane. Only <strong>transverse</strong> waves can be polarised — this is how we know light is transverse. Longitudinal waves (sound) cannot be polarised. A polarising filter transmits only the component of the electric field parallel to its axis; crossed polaroids block all light (Malus’ law: I = I<sub>0</sub>cos<sup>2</sup>θ).</p><p>Applications: polarising sunglasses cut glare (reflected light is partially polarised), 3D cinema, stress analysis of transparent materials.</p><p><strong>Doppler effect:</strong> the observed frequency changes when source and observer move relative to each other. Approaching → higher observed frequency (shorter wavelength); receding → lower. Applications: police radar speed guns, redshift of distant galaxies (evidence for the expanding universe), medical ultrasound blood-flow measurement. For sound with a moving source: f′ = fv/(v ∓ v<sub>s</sub>).</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'Waves transfer', options:['matter','energy without net transfer of matter','only particles','charge'], answer:1, explain:'A wave transfers energy (and momentum) through a medium or field without net movement of the medium itself.'},
    {q:'Which wave is longitudinal?', options:['light','a water ripple','sound','a wave on a string'], answer:2, explain:'Sound oscillates parallel to its direction of travel (compressions and rarefactions) — longitudinal. The others are transverse.'},
    {q:'The speed of all EM waves in a vacuum is', options:['3.00×10<sup>6</sup> m s<sup>-1</sup>','3.00×10<sup>8</sup> m s<sup>-1</sup>','340 m s<sup>-1</sup>','1.50×10<sup>3</sup> m s<sup>-1</sup>'], answer:1, explain:'All electromagnetic waves travel at c = 3.00×10<sup>8</sup> m s<sup>-1</sup> in vacuum.'},
    {q:'Two points a quarter wavelength apart have phase difference', options:['π/4 rad','π/2 rad','π rad','2π rad'], answer:1, explain:'Phase difference = 2π × (λ/4)/λ = π/2 rad (90°).'},
    {q:'Polarisation proves that light is', options:['longitudinal','transverse','a particle stream','faster than sound'], answer:1, explain:'Only transverse waves can be polarised, since polarisation restricts the plane of oscillation.'},
    {q:'In the Doppler effect, an approaching source gives', options:['lower observed frequency','higher observed frequency','unchanged frequency','zero frequency'], answer:1, explain:'Wavefronts bunch up ahead of an approaching source: shorter observed wavelength, higher frequency.'},
    {q:'The unit of frequency is the hertz, equal to', options:['1 cycle per minute','1 cycle per second','2π rad s<sup>-1</sup>','1 m s<sup>-1</sup>'], answer:1, explain:'1 Hz = 1 oscillation per second. (2π rad s<sup>-1</sup> is angular frequency.)'},
    {q:'Which EM band has the shortest wavelength?', options:['radio','microwave','X-ray','gamma'], answer:3, explain:'Gamma rays have the shortest wavelengths (highest frequencies) in the EM spectrum.'},
    {q:'A wave of frequency 500 Hz and wavelength 0.68 m has speed', options:['340 m s<sup>-1</sup>','735 m s<sup>-1</sup>','0.00136 m s<sup>-1</sup>','500 m s<sup>-1</sup>'], answer:0, explain:'v = fλ = 500 × 0.68 = 340 m s<sup>-1</sup> (the speed of sound in air).'},
    {q:'Points half a wavelength apart are', options:['in phase','in antiphase','stationary','at nodes'], answer:1, explain:'λ/2 path difference = π rad = 180° phase difference: they oscillate in antiphase.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-phy-t8', title:'Superposition (AS)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-phy-t8-l1', title:'The principle of superposition', html:'<h3>The principle of superposition</h3><p>The <strong>principle of superposition</strong>: when two or more waves meet, the resultant displacement at any point is the vector sum of the individual displacements. After passing through each other, the waves continue unchanged.</p><p><strong>Constructive interference</strong>: waves arrive in phase → displacements add → larger amplitude. <strong>Destructive interference</strong>: waves arrive in antiphase (path difference = (n + ½)λ) → displacements cancel → minimum amplitude.</p><p>Coherence: for a stable interference pattern the sources must be <strong>coherent</strong> — same frequency and constant phase difference. Two separate light bulbs are incoherent, so no stable fringes form; a laser split into two beams is coherent.</p>'},
    {id:'al-phy-t8-l2', title:'Stationary waves', html:'<h3>Stationary waves</h3><p>A <strong>stationary (standing) wave</strong> forms when two identical progressive waves travel in opposite directions and superpose — e.g. a wave reflecting off a fixed end. It has fixed <strong>nodes</strong> (zero displacement) and <strong>antinodes</strong> (maximum displacement). Unlike a progressive wave, no net energy is transferred along it.</p><p>Modes of a stretched string fixed at both ends: fundamental has nodes at the ends and one antinode — length l = λ/2. Harmonics: l = nλ/2 for n = 1, 2, 3…, so f<sub>n</sub> = nv/2l. The first overtone (n = 2) has frequency twice the fundamental.</p><p>Nodes are λ/2 apart; adjacent node–antinode separation is λ/4. At a node, destructive interference occurs at all times; at an antinode, particles oscillate with maximum amplitude 2a (twice a single wave’s amplitude).</p>'},
    {id:'al-phy-t8-l3', title:'Two-source interference and diffraction', html:'<h3>Two-source interference and diffraction</h3><p>Young’s double-slit experiment: fringe spacing w = λD/s, where D is slit-to-screen distance and s is slit separation. Derivation: for the nth bright fringe, path difference = nλ; geometry gives nλ = sx/D (small angles), so fringe position x = nλD/s and spacing w = λD/s.</p><p>Worked example: λ = 600 nm, s = 0.50 mm, D = 2.0 m → w = (6.0×10<sup>-7</sup> × 2.0)/(5.0×10<sup>-4</sup>) = 2.4×10<sup>-3</sup> m = 2.4 mm. Using a diffraction grating gives sharper, brighter maxima: d sinθ = nλ.</p><p><strong>Diffraction:</strong> waves spread when passing through a gap or around an obstacle. Significant diffraction needs gap width ≈ λ. A diffraction grating with N lines per mm has slit spacing d = 1/N mm — know how to convert to metres. White light through a grating gives spectra at each order with violet deviated least.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'The principle of superposition states that the resultant displacement is', options:['the product of the displacements','the vector sum of the displacements','always zero','the difference of amplitudes'], answer:1, explain:'Resultant displacement = vector sum of the individual wave displacements at that point.'},
    {q:'Constructive interference occurs when waves meet', options:['in antiphase','in phase','at right angles','with different frequencies'], answer:1, explain:'In-phase arrival means displacements add — constructive interference, maximum amplitude.'},
    {q:'For a stable interference pattern, the sources must be', options:['incoherent','coherent','polarised','moving'], answer:1, explain:'Coherent sources (same frequency, constant phase difference) are needed for a stable, observable pattern.'},
    {q:'In a stationary wave, points of zero displacement are called', options:['antinodes','nodes','crests','troughs'], answer:1, explain:'Nodes are the fixed points of zero displacement; antinodes oscillate with maximum amplitude.'},
    {q:'Adjacent nodes in a stationary wave are separated by', options:['λ','λ/2','λ/4','2λ'], answer:1, explain:'Nodes are half a wavelength apart; node to adjacent antinode is λ/4.'},
    {q:'For a string of length l fixed at both ends, the fundamental wavelength is', options:['l','2l','l/2','4l'], answer:1, explain:'Fundamental: nodes at both ends with one antinode, so l = λ/2 → λ = 2l.'},
    {q:'In Young’s experiment, doubling the slit separation s makes the fringe spacing', options:['double','halve','unchanged','quadruple'], answer:1, explain:'w = λD/s, so w ∝ 1/s: doubling s halves the fringe spacing.'},
    {q:'A diffraction grating equation maximum satisfies', options:['d sinθ = nλ','λD = ws','v = fλ','sinθ = λd'], answer:0, explain:'d sinθ = nλ gives the angles of the nth-order maxima for a grating of spacing d.'},
    {q:'Significant diffraction occurs when the gap width is', options:['much larger than λ','approximately equal to λ','much smaller than λ','unrelated to λ'], answer:1, explain:'Maximum spreading happens when the aperture is comparable in size to the wavelength.'},
    {q:'White light through a diffraction grating shows', options:['a single white maximum at each order','a spectrum at each order, violet deviated least','only red light','no pattern'], answer:1, explain:'Each wavelength satisfies d sinθ = nλ at a different angle: spectra form, with shorter wavelengths (violet) deviated least.'}
   ]}
,
  {id:'al-phy-t9', title:'Electricity (AS)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-phy-t9-l1', title:'Current, charge and drift velocity', html:'<h3>Current, charge and drift velocity</h3><p><strong>Electric current</strong> I = Q/t: the rate of flow of charge, in amperes. Conventional current flows from positive to negative; electron flow is opposite. 1 A = 1 C s<sup>-1</sup>; the charge of one electron e = 1.60×10<sup>-19</sup> C.</p><p><strong>Drift velocity:</strong> electrons move randomly at ~10<sup>6</sup> m s<sup>-1</sup> but drift slowly (~10<sup>-4</sup> m s<sup>-1</sup>) under an electric field. Derivation: in time t, electrons in length vt of wire (area A) pass a point: number = nAvt, charge Q = nAvt e → I = Q/t = nAve, where n is the number density of free electrons.</p><p>Worked example: copper has n ≈ 8.5×10<sup>28</sup> m<sup>-3</sup>. For I = 2.0 A in a wire of area 1.0 mm<sup>2</sup>: v = I/(nAe) = 2.0/(8.5×10<sup>28</sup> × 1.0×10<sup>-6</sup> × 1.6×10<sup>-19</sup>) ≈ 1.5×10<sup>-4</sup> m s<sup>-1</sup> — about 0.15 mm per second.</p>'},
    {id:'al-phy-t9-l2', title:'Potential difference, e.m.f. and resistance', html:'<h3>Potential difference, e.m.f. and resistance</h3><p><strong>Potential difference</strong> V = W/Q: work done per unit charge moving between two points. <strong>E.m.f.</strong> is the energy per unit charge supplied by a source (chemical → electrical). The terminal p.d. across a real battery is e.m.f. minus the voltage lost across its internal resistance: V = E − Ir.</p><p><strong>Resistance</strong> R = V/I (definition, holds for any component). <strong>Ohm’s law</strong> (V = IR with constant R) applies only to <strong>ohmic</strong> conductors — I–V graph a straight line through the origin (metal wire at constant temperature).</p><p>Non-ohmic examples: filament lamp (I–V curve flattens as the filament heats and resistance rises); semiconductor diode (conducts one way only, with a threshold ~0.6 V); thermistor (resistance falls as temperature rises — NTC). Know the I–V shape for each.</p>'},
    {id:'al-phy-t9-l3', title:'Resistivity', html:'<h3>Resistivity</h3><p><strong>Resistivity</strong> ρ = RA/l, a property of the material (Ω m). Resistance of a uniform wire: R = ρl/A. Doubling the length doubles R; doubling the diameter quarters R (area ×4).</p><p>Derivation from the drift model: resistance arises from collisions between electrons and lattice ions. Longer wire → more collisions → higher R. Wider wire → more parallel paths → lower R. Heating a metal increases ion vibration → more collisions → higher resistivity (positive temperature coefficient).</p><p>Worked example: copper ρ = 1.7×10<sup>-8</sup> Ω m. A 50 m cable of diameter 2.0 mm: A = π(1.0×10<sup>-3</sup>)<sup>2</sup> = 3.14×10<sup>-6</sup> m<sup>2</sup>; R = ρl/A = (1.7×10<sup>-8</sup> × 50)/3.14×10<sup>-6</sup> ≈ 0.27 Ω. Superconductors have zero resistivity below a critical temperature.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'Electric current is defined as', options:['charge × time','charge/time','energy/charge','voltage × resistance'], answer:1, explain:'I = Q/t: current is the rate of flow of charge.'},
    {q:'The charge of an electron is', options:['1.60×10<sup>19</sup> C','1.60×10<sup>-19</sup> C','9.11×10<sup>-31</sup> C','1.00 C'], answer:1, explain:'The elementary charge e = 1.60×10<sup>-19</sup> C (negative for the electron).'},
    {q:'Drift velocity is given by', options:['I = nAve','I = nAv/e','v = neA/I','v = IR/ne'], answer:0, explain:'I = nAve: current = (electron number density)(area)(drift velocity)(electron charge).'},
    {q:'E.m.f. is best described as', options:['voltage across a resistor','energy per unit charge supplied by a source','lost volts','current × time'], answer:1, explain:'E.m.f. = work done per unit charge by the source (e.g. chemical → electrical energy).'},
    {q:'Terminal p.d. of a battery with e.m.f. E and internal resistance r is', options:['E + Ir','E − Ir','Ir − E','E/Ir'], answer:1, explain:'V = E − Ir: some e.m.f. is lost as voltage across the internal resistance.'},
    {q:'Which component is ohmic?', options:['filament lamp','diode','metal wire at constant temperature','thermistor'], answer:2, explain:'Only the metal wire at constant temperature has constant R (straight I–V through origin).'},
    {q:'The I–V characteristic of a filament lamp curves because', options:['it is a semiconductor','heating raises its resistance','it emits light','current saturates'], answer:1, explain:'Higher current heats the filament, increasing lattice vibrations and resistance, so the I–V curve flattens.'},
    {q:'Resistivity has units', options:['Ω','Ω m','Ω m<sup>-1</sup>','S m<sup>-1</sup>'], answer:1, explain:'ρ = RA/l → Ω × m<sup>2</sup>/m = Ω m.'},
    {q:'Doubling both the length and the diameter of a wire changes its resistance by a factor of', options:['1 (unchanged)','2','1/2','4'], answer:2, explain:'R = ρl/A. Length ×2 and area ×4 → R becomes 2/4 = 1/2 of the original.'},
    {q:'A thermistor’s resistance', options:['rises with temperature','falls with temperature','is constant','is zero'], answer:1, explain:'NTC thermistors: heating frees more charge carriers, so resistance falls as temperature rises.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-phy-t10', title:'D.C. circuits (AS)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-phy-t10-l1', title:'Kirchhoff’s laws', html:'<h3>Kirchhoff’s laws</h3><p><strong>First law (junction rule):</strong> the sum of currents entering a junction equals the sum leaving — conservation of charge. ΣI<sub>in</sub> = ΣI<sub>out</sub>.</p><p><strong>Second law (loop rule):</strong> the sum of e.m.f.s around a closed loop equals the sum of p.d.s: ΣE = ΣIR — conservation of energy. Apply a consistent sign convention: traverse the loop, +E when going from − to + terminal, −IR when moving with the current.</p><p>Worked example: a 12 V battery (r = 1 Ω) drives two parallel branches, 4 Ω and 6 Ω. Equivalent branch resistance = (4×6)/10 = 2.4 Ω. Total R = 3.4 Ω. Total current I = 12/3.4 = 3.53 A. Terminal p.d. = 12 − 3.53×1 = 8.47 V. Branch currents: 8.47/4 = 2.12 A and 8.47/6 = 1.41 A (sum = 3.53 A ✓).</p>'},
    {id:'al-phy-t10-l2', title:'Series, parallel and the potential divider', html:'<h3>Series, parallel and the potential divider</h3><p><strong>Series:</strong> same current through all; R<sub>total</sub> = R<sub>1</sub> + R<sub>2</sub> + …; voltage divides in the ratio of resistances. <strong>Parallel:</strong> same p.d. across all; 1/R<sub>total</sub> = 1/R<sub>1</sub> + 1/R<sub>2</sub> + …; current divides inversely with resistance.</p><p><strong>Potential divider:</strong> two resistors in series across a supply V. V<sub>out</sub> across R<sub>2</sub> = V × R<sub>2</sub>/(R<sub>1</sub> + R<sub>2</sub>). Replace R<sub>2</sub> with a thermistor or LDR to make a sensor circuit: e.g. with an LDR as R<sub>2</sub>, V<sub>out</sub> falls as light level rises (LDR resistance falls).</p><p>Exam reasoning: always redraw mixed circuits step by step, collapsing series/parallel pairs. The current through a component depends on the p.d. across IT and ITS resistance — in parallel branches, the smaller resistor carries the larger current.</p>'},
    {id:'al-phy-t10-l3', title:'Power in circuits and internal resistance', html:'<h3>Power in circuits and internal resistance</h3><p>Electrical power: P = VI = I<sup>2</sup>R = V<sup>2</sup>/R. Use I<sup>2</sup>R when comparing components in series (same I); V<sup>2</sup>/R when comparing components in parallel (same V).</p><p>Maximum power is delivered to a load when load resistance = internal resistance (maximum power theorem): P<sub>max</sub> = E<sup>2</sup>/4r. Derivation: P = I<sup>2</sup>R = E<sup>2</sup>R/(R+r)<sup>2</sup>; differentiate or complete the square to find the maximum at R = r.</p><p>Measuring internal resistance: plot terminal p.d. V against current I as load varies; V = E − Ir gives a straight line of gradient −r and intercept E. Short-circuit current = E/r. This is a standard Paper 3 practical.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'Kirchhoff’s first law expresses conservation of', options:['energy','charge','momentum','mass'], answer:1, explain:'Currents into a junction = currents out: charge cannot accumulate at the junction.'},
    {q:'Kirchhoff’s second law expresses conservation of', options:['charge','energy','momentum','current'], answer:1, explain:'ΣE = ΣIR around a loop: energy supplied per unit charge equals energy dissipated per unit charge.'},
    {q:'Two resistors 3 Ω and 6 Ω in parallel have equivalent resistance', options:['9 Ω','2 Ω','4.5 Ω','18 Ω'], answer:1, explain:'1/R = 1/3 + 1/6 = 1/2 → R = 2 Ω.'},
    {q:'In a potential divider with R<sub>1</sub> = 2 kΩ, R<sub>2</sub> = 3 kΩ across 10 V, V<sub>out</sub> across R<sub>2</sub> is', options:['4 V','6 V','2 V','10 V'], answer:1, explain:'V<sub>out</sub> = 10 × 3/(2+3) = 6 V.'},
    {q:'P = I<sup>2</sup>R is most convenient when comparing resistors in', options:['parallel','series','short circuit','open circuit'], answer:1, explain:'Series components share the same current, so I<sup>2</sup>R compares powers directly.'},
    {q:'Maximum power is transferred to a load when', options:['R<sub>load</sub> = 0','R<sub>load</sub> = r','R<sub>load</sub> = 2r','R<sub>load</sub> → ∞'], answer:1, explain:'Maximum power theorem: P is maximum when load resistance equals the source’s internal resistance.'},
    {q:'A V–I plot for a real battery has gradient', options:['+r','−r','E','E/r'], answer:1, explain:'V = E − Ir: gradient = −r, intercept = E.'},
    {q:'A 12 V battery with r = 2 Ω is short-circuited. The current is', options:['6 A','24 A','4 A','0.17 A'], answer:0, explain:'I = E/r = 12/2 = 6 A (load resistance zero).'},
    {q:'Three identical resistors in parallel across a supply each dissipate P. Rewired in series across the same supply, each dissipates', options:['P','P/3','P/9','3P'], answer:2, explain:'Series: each gets V/3 → power V<sub>1</sub><sup>2</sup>/R = (V/3)<sup>2</sup>/R = P/9, since P = V<sup>2</sup>/R.'},
    {q:'A 6 V, 60 W lamp has resistance', options:['0.6 Ω','6 Ω','0.1 Ω','360 Ω'], answer:0, explain:'R = V<sup>2</sup>/P = 36/60 = 0.6 Ω.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-phy-t11', title:'Particle physics (AS)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-phy-t11-l1', title:'Alpha, beta and gamma radiation', html:'<h3>Alpha, beta and gamma radiation</h3><p><strong>Alpha (α):</strong> a helium nucleus (<sup>4</sup><sub>2</sub>He), heavily ionising, range of a few cm in air, stopped by paper. <strong>Beta-minus (β<sup>−</sup>):</strong> a fast electron from neutron decay, moderately ionising, range of a few metres in air, stopped by aluminium. <strong>Gamma (γ):</strong> high-energy photon, weakly ionising, very penetrating, reduced by thick lead.</p><p>In β<sup>−</sup> decay a neutron becomes a proton, emitting an electron and an antineutrino: n → p + e<sup>−</sup> + ν̄<sub>e</sub>. In β<sup>+</sup> decay a proton becomes a neutron: p → n + e<sup>+</sup> + ν<sub>e</sub>. Charge, nucleon number and lepton number are conserved.</p><p><strong>Inverse-square law:</strong> intensity of γ radiation ∝ 1/distance<sup>2</sup> from a point source (spreading over sphere area 4πd<sup>2</sup>). Doubling distance quarters the count rate — the basis of safe handling distances.</p>'},
    {id:'al-phy-t11-l2', title:'Fundamental particles', html:'<h3>Fundamental particles</h3><p>Matter particles are <strong>quarks</strong> and <strong>leptons</strong>. Quarks: up (+2/3 e), down (−1/3 e) and heavier flavours. <strong>Proton</strong> = uud (charge +1), <strong>neutron</strong> = udd (charge 0). Leptons: electron, muon, tau and their neutrinos.</p><p><strong>Hadrons</strong> (made of quarks) divide into <strong>baryons</strong> (three quarks, e.g. proton, neutron) and <strong>mesons</strong> (quark–antiquark pairs, e.g. pion). Every particle has an <strong>antiparticle</strong> with opposite charge (and other quantum numbers): positron, antiproton, antineutrino.</p><p>When matter meets antimatter they <strong>annihilate</strong>, converting mass to energy: E = mc<sup>2</sup> (usually as two γ photons to conserve momentum). <strong>Pair production</strong> is the reverse: a high-energy photon creates a particle–antiparticle pair near a nucleus.</p>'},
    {id:'al-phy-t11-l3', title:'Quark model of beta decay', html:'<h3>Quark model of beta decay</h3><p>At quark level, β<sup>−</sup> decay is a <strong>down quark changing into an up quark</strong>: udd → uud. The emitted W<sup>−</sup> boson (a force carrier of the weak interaction) then decays into an electron and an antineutrino: d → u + e<sup>−</sup> + ν̄<sub>e</sub>.</p><p>Exam reasoning: check conservation laws in any decay equation — charge (e.g. −1/3 = +2/3 − 1 + 0 ✓), baryon number (1 = 1 + 0 + 0 ✓), lepton number (0 = 0 + 1 − 1 ✓). If a proposed decay violates one, it cannot occur.</p><p>The <strong>weak nuclear force</strong> is responsible for beta decay and has very short range (~10<sup>−18</sup> m). The <strong>strong force</strong> binds quarks inside hadrons and holds nuclei together; the <strong>electromagnetic force</strong> acts between charges; <strong>gravity</strong> is weakest but infinite in range.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'Which radiation is most ionising?', options:['gamma','beta','alpha','X-ray'], answer:2, explain:'Alpha particles are slow and doubly charged, so they ionise most densely along a short track.'},
    {q:'Beta-minus decay is', options:['p → n + e<sup>+</sup> + ν<sub>e</sub>','n → p + e<sup>−</sup> + ν̄<sub>e</sub>','p → p + γ','n → n + e<sup>−</sup>'], answer:1, explain:'A neutron converts to a proton, emitting an electron and an electron antineutrino.'},
    {q:'Doubling the distance from a γ point source changes the intensity by a factor of', options:['2','1/2','1/4','4'], answer:2, explain:'Inverse-square law: I ∝ 1/d<sup>2</sup>, so doubling d quarters I.'},
    {q:'The quark composition of a proton is', options:['udd','uud','ddd','uū'], answer:1, explain:'Proton = up + up + down: charge 2/3 + 2/3 − 1/3 = +1.'},
    {q:'A meson consists of', options:['three quarks','a quark and an antiquark','two quarks','three antiquarks'], answer:1, explain:'Mesons are quark–antiquark pairs; baryons are three quarks.'},
    {q:'The antiparticle of the electron is the', options:['proton','positron','neutrino','photon'], answer:1, explain:'The positron has the electron’s mass but +1 charge.'},
    {q:'In β<sup>−</sup> decay at quark level,', options:['an up quark becomes a down quark','a down quark becomes an up quark','a quark becomes a lepton','a gluon is emitted'], answer:1, explain:'udd (neutron) → uud (proton): one down quark changes to an up quark via the weak force.'},
    {q:'Which force is responsible for beta decay?', options:['strong','electromagnetic','weak','gravitational'], answer:2, explain:'Beta decay is mediated by W bosons — the weak nuclear interaction.'},
    {q:'Pair production is', options:['a photon creating a particle–antiparticle pair','two protons fusing','an electron absorbing a photon','a nucleus splitting'], answer:0, explain:'A γ photon (E > 2m<sub>e</sub>c<sup>2</sup>) near a nucleus converts into an electron–positron pair.'},
    {q:'Which is stopped by a sheet of paper?', options:['beta','gamma','alpha','neutron'], answer:2, explain:'Alpha particles are stopped by paper (or skin); beta needs aluminium; gamma needs thick lead.'}
   ]}
,
  {id:'al-phy-t12', title:'Motion in a circle (A2)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-phy-t12-l1', title:'Angular displacement and angular velocity', html:'<h3>Angular displacement and angular velocity</h3><p><strong>Radian measure:</strong> angle θ = arc length / radius, in radians. 360° = 2π rad. The radian is dimensionless but must be used in all circular-motion formulae.</p><p><strong>Angular velocity</strong> ω = Δθ/Δt in rad s<sup>-1</sup>. For one revolution per period T: ω = 2π/T = 2πf. <strong>Linear speed</strong> v = rω (from arc length s = rθ differentiated: v = ds/dt = r dθ/dt = rω).</p><p>Worked example: a car tyre of radius 0.30 m rotates at 600 rpm. f = 10 rev s<sup>-1</sup>, ω = 2π × 10 = 62.8 rad s<sup>-1</sup>. v = rω = 0.30 × 62.8 ≈ 18.8 m s<sup>-1</sup> (≈ 68 km h<sup>-1</sup>). ✓</p>'},
    {id:'al-phy-t12-l2', title:'Centripetal acceleration and force', html:'<h3>Centripetal acceleration and force</h3><p>Velocity is a vector: even at constant speed, direction changes, so there is acceleration <strong>towards the centre</strong>: a = v<sup>2</sup>/r = rω<sup>2</sup>. Derivation sketch: in time Δt the velocity vector rotates by Δθ, giving Δv ≈ vΔθ; a = Δv/Δt = vω = v<sup>2</sup>/r.</p><p><strong>Centripetal force</strong> F = mv<sup>2</sup>/r = mrω<sup>2</sup> is the <strong>resultant</strong> force towards the centre — not an extra force. Identify what provides it: tension (conical pendulum), friction (car cornering), gravity (satellite), normal reaction (banked track component).</p><p>Worked example: a 1500 kg car corners at 20 m s<sup>-1</sup> on radius 80 m. F = mv<sup>2</sup>/r = 1500 × 400/80 = 7500 N of friction needed. If μ<sub>s</sub>mg = 0.8 × 1500 × 9.81 = 11800 N > 7500 N, the car holds the corner.</p>'},
    {id:'al-phy-t12-l3', title:'Vertical circles and conical pendulum', html:'<h3>Vertical circles and conical pendulum</h3><p><strong>Vertical circle:</strong> at the top, both weight and tension act downwards, so T + mg = mv<sup>2</sup>/r. Minimum speed to stay on a circular path (T = 0): v<sub>min</sub> = √(gr). At the bottom, T − mg = mv<sup>2</sup>/r so tension is greatest there.</p><p>Worked example: a bucket of water whirled on a 1.0 m string. v<sub>min</sub> at top = √(9.81 × 1.0) ≈ 3.1 m s<sup>-1</sup>. Slower than this and the string goes slack — the water falls out.</p><p><strong>Conical pendulum:</strong> a mass on a string sweeps a horizontal circle of radius r = l sinθ. Vertical equilibrium: T cosθ = mg. Horizontal (centripetal): T sinθ = mrω<sup>2</sup>. Dividing: tanθ = rω<sup>2</sup>/g = ω<sup>2</sup>l sinθ/g → cosθ = g/(ω<sup>2</sup>l). A classic Paper 4 derivation.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'One complete revolution equals', options:['π rad','2π rad','360 rad','1 rad'], answer:1, explain:'360° = 2π radians by definition of the radian.'},
    {q:'Angular velocity is defined as', options:['Δθ/Δt','rθ','v/r<sup>2</sup>','2πf<sup>2</sup>'], answer:0, explain:'ω = rate of change of angular displacement = Δθ/Δt, in rad s<sup>-1</sup>.'},
    {q:'The relation between linear speed and angular velocity is', options:['v = r/ω','v = rω','v = ω/r','v = rω<sup>2</sup>'], answer:1, explain:'v = ds/dt = r dθ/dt = rω.'},
    {q:'Centripetal acceleration is directed', options:['along the velocity','away from the centre','towards the centre','vertically down'], answer:2, explain:'Direction of velocity changes towards the centre, so acceleration (and resultant force) point to the centre.'},
    {q:'Centripetal force on mass m at speed v, radius r is', options:['mv<sup>2</sup>/r','mvr','mr<sup>2</sup>ω','mv/r<sup>2</sup>'], answer:0, explain:'F = ma = m(v<sup>2</sup>/r) = mv<sup>2</sup>/r = mrω<sup>2</sup>.'},
    {q:'A 2.0 kg mass on a 0.50 m string whirls at 3.0 rev s<sup>-1</sup>. The tension is approximately', options:['36 N','110 N','350 N','18 N'], answer:2, explain:'ω = 2π×3 = 18.85 rad s<sup>-1</sup>; T = mrω<sup>2</sup> = 2.0×0.50×355 ≈ 355 N.'},
    {q:'Minimum speed at the top of a vertical circle of radius r is', options:['√(2gr)','√(gr)','gr','2πr'], answer:1, explain:'T = 0 at the top gives mg = mv<sup>2</sup>/r → v = √(gr).'},
    {q:'For a car cornering on a flat road, the centripetal force is provided by', options:['the engine','friction','air resistance','the normal force'], answer:1, explain:'Sideways friction between tyres and road supplies the inward resultant force.'},
    {q:'A satellite in circular orbit: the centripetal force is', options:['thrust','air drag','gravity','tension'], answer:2, explain:'Gravity provides the centripetal force: GMm/r<sup>2</sup> = mv<sup>2</sup>/r.'},
    {q:'Doubling the angular velocity of circular motion multiplies the centripetal force by', options:['2','4','1/2','√2'], answer:1, explain:'F = mrω<sup>2</sup> ∝ ω<sup>2</sup>: doubling ω quadruples F.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-phy-t13', title:'Gravitational fields (A2)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-phy-t13-l1', title:'Newton’s law of gravitation', html:'<h3>Newton’s law of gravitation</h3><p><strong>Newton’s law:</strong> the gravitational force between two point masses is proportional to the product of the masses and inversely proportional to the square of their separation: F = Gm<sub>1</sub>m<sub>2</sub>/r<sup>2</sup>, with G = 6.67×10<sup>-11</sup> N m<sup>2</sup> kg<sup>-2</sup>.</p><p>Worked example: Earth (5.97×10<sup>24</sup> kg) and Moon (7.35×10<sup>22</sup> kg), r = 3.84×10<sup>8</sup> m. F = 6.67×10<sup>-11</sup> × 5.97×10<sup>24</sup> × 7.35×10<sup>22</sup>/(3.84×10<sup>8</sup>)<sup>2</sup> ≈ 1.98×10<sup>20</sup> N.</p><p><strong>Gravitational field strength</strong> g = F/m: force per unit mass. For a point mass M, g = GM/r<sup>2</sup> directed towards M. Near Earth’s surface this gives g ≈ 9.81 N kg<sup>-1</sup> (also m s<sup>-2</sup> — same quantity).</p>'},
    {id:'al-phy-t13-l2', title:'Gravitational potential', html:'<h3>Gravitational potential</h3><p><strong>Gravitational potential</strong> V<sub>g</sub> = work done per unit mass bringing a mass from infinity to the point — always <strong>negative</strong> (work is done BY the field). For a point mass: V<sub>g</sub> = −GM/r. Potential is a scalar; field strength is the negative gradient: g = −dV/dr.</p><p>Equipotentials are surfaces of constant V<sub>g</sub> — spheres around a point mass. Field lines are perpendicular to equipotentials and point from high to low potential (towards the mass).</p><p>Energy: a mass m at potential V<sub>g</sub> has GPE = mV<sub>g</sub> = −GMm/r. Work done moving between two points = mΔV<sub>g</sub>. Escape velocity: set total energy zero at infinity: ½mv<sup>2</sup> − GMm/R = 0 → v = √(2GM/R) ≈ 11.2 km s<sup>-1</sup> for Earth.</p>'},
    {id:'al-phy-t13-l3', title:'Orbits and geostationary satellites', html:'<h3>Orbits and geostationary satellites</h3><p>Circular orbit: gravity provides centripetal force: GMm/r<sup>2</sup> = mv<sup>2</sup>/r → v = √(GM/r). Orbital period: T = 2πr/v = 2π√(r<sup>3</sup>/GM) — <strong>Kepler’s third law</strong>: T<sup>2</sup> ∝ r<sup>3</sup>.</p><p><strong>Geostationary orbit:</strong> T = 24 h, above the equator, orbiting in the same direction as Earth’s rotation — appears fixed in the sky. r<sup>3</sup> = GMT<sup>2</sup>/4π<sup>2</sup> → r ≈ 4.23×10<sup>7</sup> m from Earth’s centre (≈ 36000 km altitude). Uses: communications, weather, TV broadcasting.</p><p><strong>Low polar orbits</strong> (~few hundred km) sweep over the whole Earth as it rotates beneath — used for imaging, spy and weather-detail satellites. They have short periods (~90 min) from T<sup>2</sup> ∝ r<sup>3</sup> with small r.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'Newton’s law of gravitation: F is proportional to', options:['m<sub>1</sub>m<sub>2</sub>/r','m<sub>1</sub>m<sub>2</sub>/r<sup>2</sup>','(m<sub>1</sub>+m<sub>2</sub>)/r<sup>2</sup>','m<sub>1</sub>m<sub>2</sub>r<sup>2</sup>'], answer:1, explain:'F = Gm<sub>1</sub>m<sub>2</sub>/r<sup>2</sup>: inverse-square law.'},
    {q:'The value of G is approximately', options:['9.81 N m<sup>2</sup> kg<sup>-2</sup>','6.67×10<sup>-11</sup> N m<sup>2</sup> kg<sup>-2</sup>','6.02×10<sup>23</sup> N m<sup>2</sup> kg<sup>-2</sup>','1.60×10<sup>-19</sup> N m<sup>2</sup> kg<sup>-2</sup>'], answer:1, explain:'G = 6.67×10<sup>-11</sup> N m<sup>2</sup> kg<sup>-2</sup> (given in the data booklet).'},
    {q:'Gravitational field strength is defined as', options:['force × mass','force per unit mass','mass per unit force','potential × mass'], answer:1, explain:'g = F/m: the gravitational force per unit mass, in N kg<sup>-1</sup>.'},
    {q:'Gravitational potential at a point is negative because', options:['mass is negative','work is done by the field bringing mass from infinity','gravity repels','potential is a vector'], answer:1, explain:'Bringing a mass in from infinity, the field does the work — work done BY an external agent is negative, so V<sub>g</sub> < 0.'},
    {q:'The relation between g and V<sub>g</sub> is', options:['g = dV/dr','g = −dV/dr','g = V<sub>g</sub>/r','g = −V<sub>g</sub>'], answer:1, explain:'Field strength is the negative potential gradient: g = −dV/dr (points down the potential slope).'},
    {q:'Escape velocity from a planet of mass M and radius R is', options:['√(GM/R)','√(2GM/R)','2GM/R','GM/R<sup>2</sup>'], answer:1, explain:'½mv<sup>2</sup> = GMm/R → v = √(2GM/R).'},
    {q:'Kepler’s third law states', options:['T ∝ r','T<sup>2</sup> ∝ r<sup>3</sup>','T ∝ r<sup>3</sup>','T<sup>2</sup> ∝ r'], answer:1, explain:'From T = 2π√(r<sup>3</sup>/GM): T<sup>2</sup> = (4π<sup>2</sup>/GM)r<sup>3</sup>.'},
    {q:'A geostationary satellite must be', options:['over either pole','over the equator with T = 24 h','in low orbit','stationary relative to the Sun'], answer:1, explain:'It must orbit above the equator with a 24-hour period, in Earth’s rotation direction, to appear fixed.'},
    {q:'Geostationary orbit radius from Earth’s centre is about', options:['6.4×10<sup>6</sup> m','4.2×10<sup>7</sup> m','3.8×10<sup>8</sup> m','1.5×10<sup>11</sup> m'], answer:1, explain:'r<sup>3</sup> = GMT<sup>2</sup>/4π<sup>2</sup> with T = 86400 s gives r ≈ 4.23×10<sup>7</sup> m.'},
    {q:'Equipotential surfaces around a point mass are', options:['radial lines','concentric spheres','parallel planes','hyperbolas'], answer:1, explain:'V<sub>g</sub> = −GM/r depends only on r, so surfaces of constant V are spheres centred on the mass.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-phy-t14', title:'Temperature (A2)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-phy-t14-l1', title:'Thermal equilibrium and the kelvin scale', html:'<h3>Thermal equilibrium and the kelvin scale</h3><p><strong>Thermal equilibrium:</strong> two objects are in thermal equilibrium when no net heat flows between them — they are at the same temperature. Temperature is the property that determines the direction of heat flow (hot → cold).</p><p>The <strong>kelvin</strong> is the SI unit of temperature. T(K) = θ(°C) + 273.15. Absolute zero (0 K = −273.15 °C) is the temperature at which particles have minimum kinetic energy — unattainable but approachable.</p><p>Any temperature change is the same in K and °C (ΔT = Δθ), so specific heat calculations work in either — but gas laws and Boltzmann factors need kelvin.</p>'},
    {id:'al-phy-t14-l2', title:'Specific heat capacity and specific latent heat', html:'<h3>Specific heat capacity and specific latent heat</h3><p><strong>Specific heat capacity</strong> c: energy needed to raise 1 kg by 1 K: Q = mcΔT. Water’s c = 4200 J kg<sup>-1</sup> K<sup>-1</sup> is unusually high — it moderates coastal climates and makes water a good coolant.</p><p>Worked example: heating 0.50 kg of water from 20 °C to 80 °C: Q = 0.50 × 4200 × 60 = 1.26×10<sup>5</sup> J. A 200 W heater (100% efficient) takes 630 s ≈ 10.5 min.</p><p><strong>Specific latent heat</strong> l: energy to change the state of 1 kg without temperature change: Q = ml. Latent heat of vaporisation of water = 2.26×10<sup>6</sup> J kg<sup>-1</sup>; of fusion = 3.34×10<sup>5</sup> J kg<sup>-1</sup>. During a change of state, energy breaks/form intermolecular bonds, not raising kinetic energy — hence constant temperature.</p>'},
    {id:'al-phy-t14-l3', title:'Measuring specific heat capacity', html:'<h3>Measuring specific heat capacity</h3><p>Electrical method: a metal block with an immersion heater; Q = VIt (electrical energy supplied). Measure mass m and temperature rise ΔT; c = VIt/(mΔT). Insulate the block and correct for heat loss — e.g. measure the cooling rate and extrapolate, or use the method of mixtures.</p><p>Uncertainty reasoning: the largest percentage uncertainties are usually in ΔT (small rises) and in heat lost to surroundings. Using a large mass and large ΔT reduces percentage uncertainty; lagging reduces systematic loss.</p><p>Exam reasoning: if the calculated c exceeds the true value, the likely cause is unaccounted heat loss (energy supplied > energy heating the block). If it is too low, possible heat gain from surroundings or an overestimated temperature rise.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'Thermal equilibrium between two objects means', options:['they have equal mass','no net heat flows between them','they are at 0 K','they are insulated'], answer:1, explain:'Equal temperature → no net heat flow: the definition of thermal equilibrium.'},
    {q:'0 °C in kelvin is', options:['273 K','273.15 K','−273.15 K','0 K'], answer:1, explain:'T(K) = θ(°C) + 273.15, so 0 °C = 273.15 K.'},
    {q:'Absolute zero is the temperature at which', options:['water freezes','particles have minimum kinetic energy','gases liquefy','heat flows fastest'], answer:1, explain:'At 0 K particles have the minimum possible kinetic energy (zero-point energy remains).'},
    {q:'Specific heat capacity is defined as', options:['Q/(mΔT)','Q × m × ΔT','m/(QΔT)','Q/m<sup>2</sup>'], answer:0, explain:'c = Q/(mΔT): energy per unit mass per unit temperature rise.'},
    {q:'Energy to heat 2.0 kg of water (c = 4200 J kg<sup>-1</sup> K<sup>-1</sup>) by 10 K is', options:['8400 J','84000 J','420 J','21000 J'], answer:1, explain:'Q = mcΔT = 2.0 × 4200 × 10 = 84000 J.'},
    {q:'Specific latent heat of vaporisation is', options:['energy to raise 1 kg by 1 K','energy to change state of 1 kg at constant temperature','energy to melt 1 kg','always 4200 J kg<sup>-1</sup>'], answer:1, explain:'l = Q/m for a change of state with no temperature change.'},
    {q:'During melting, the temperature stays constant because', options:['no energy is supplied','energy breaks intermolecular bonds rather than raising KE','the thermometer is faulty','heat is lost as fast as supplied'], answer:1, explain:'Latent heat goes into potential energy of separation, not kinetic energy, so temperature is unchanged.'},
    {q:'In the electrical method, c = VIt/(mΔT). If heat is lost to the air, the calculated c will be', options:['too low','too high','correct','zero'], answer:1, explain:'VIt overestimates the energy actually heating the block, so c = VIt/(mΔT) comes out too high.'},
    {q:'Which has the highest specific heat capacity?', options:['copper','aluminium','water','oil'], answer:2, explain:'Water (4200 J kg<sup>-1</sup> K<sup>-1</sup>) is far above metals (~400–900) and oils (~2000).'},
    {q:'A temperature rise of 50 °C is the same as a rise of', options:['50 K','223 K','323 K','−223 K'], answer:0, explain:'Temperature CHANGES are identical in °C and K; only absolute values differ by 273.15.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-phy-t15', title:'Ideal gases (A2)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-phy-t15-l1', title:'The ideal gas equation', html:'<h3>The ideal gas equation</h3><p><strong>Ideal gas equation:</strong> pV = nRT, with R = 8.31 J mol<sup>-1</sup> K<sup>-1</sup>. Alternatively pV = NkT with Boltzmann constant k = 1.38×10<sup>-23</sup> J K<sup>-1</sup> and N molecules. T must be in kelvin.</p><p>Worked example: 2.0 mol of gas at 300 K in 0.050 m<sup>3</sup>: p = nRT/V = (2.0 × 8.31 × 300)/0.050 = 9.97×10<sup>4</sup> Pa ≈ 1.0×10<sup>5</sup> Pa (atmospheric pressure — sensible).</p><p>The three gas laws are special cases: Boyle’s (pV = constant at fixed T), Charles’s (V/T = constant at fixed p), pressure law (p/T = constant at fixed V). One mole contains N<sub>A</sub> = 6.02×10<sup>23</sup> particles.</p>'},
    {id:'al-phy-t15-l2', title:'Kinetic theory of gases', html:'<h3>Kinetic theory of gases</h3><p><strong>Kinetic model assumptions:</strong> large number of identical molecules in random motion; negligible molecular volume vs container; no intermolecular forces except during collisions; collisions with walls and each other are perfectly elastic; collision time negligible vs time between collisions.</p><p>Derivation of pV = (1/3)Nm&lt;c<sup>2</sup>&gt;: one molecule of mass m hitting a wall of cube side l rebounds with Δp = 2mv<sub>x</sub>; time between hits on that wall = 2l/v<sub>x</sub>; force = Δp/Δt = mv<sub>x</sub><sup>2</sup>/l. Summing over N molecules and averaging over three directions gives p = (1/3)Nm&lt;c<sup>2</sup>&gt;/V.</p><p>Combining with pV = NkT: (1/3)m&lt;c<sup>2</sup>&gt; = (3/2)kT → average molecular KE = (3/2)kT. Temperature is proportional to mean molecular kinetic energy — the kinetic interpretation of temperature.</p>'},
    {id:'al-phy-t15-l3', title:'Molecular speeds and the Boltzmann distribution', html:'<h3>Molecular speeds and the Boltzmann distribution</h3><p>Root-mean-square speed: from (1/2)m&lt;c<sup>2</sup>&gt; = (3/2)kT → c<sub>rms</sub> = √(3kT/m) = √(3RT/M), where M is molar mass. Lighter molecules move faster at the same temperature.</p><p>Worked example: nitrogen (M = 0.028 kg mol<sup>-1</sup>) at 300 K: c<sub>rms</sub> = √(3 × 8.31 × 300/0.028) ≈ 517 m s<sup>-1</sup>. Faster than sound — molecules are quick, but collisions make diffusion slow.</p><p>The <strong>Boltzmann distribution</strong> shows the spread of molecular energies: starts at zero, peaks, then tails off. Raising T flattens and shifts the peak right; the <strong>area under the curve is constant</strong> (fixed number of molecules). Fraction with energy > E<sub>a</sub> grows with T — explaining why reaction rates rise with temperature.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'The ideal gas equation is', options:['pV = nRT','pV = Nk','p/T = nR','V = nRpT'], answer:0, explain:'pV = nRT (or pV = NkT). T must be in kelvin.'},
    {q:'The value of the molar gas constant R is', options:['1.38×10<sup>-23</sup> J mol<sup>-1</sup> K<sup>-1</sup>','8.31 J mol<sup>-1</sup> K<sup>-1</sup>','6.02×10<sup>23</sup> J mol<sup>-1</sup> K<sup>-1</sup>','9.81 J mol<sup>-1</sup> K<sup>-1</sup>'], answer:1, explain:'R = 8.31 J mol<sup>-1</sup> K<sup>-1</sup>; k = R/N<sub>A</sub> = 1.38×10<sup>-23</sup> J K<sup>-1</sup>.'},
    {q:'Which is NOT an assumption of the kinetic theory?', options:['molecules move randomly','molecular volume negligible','collisions perfectly elastic','strong attractive forces between molecules'], answer:3, explain:'The ideal model assumes NO intermolecular forces except during collisions.'},
    {q:'From kinetic theory, pV equals', options:['Nm<c<sup>2</sup>>','(1/3)Nm<c<sup>2</sup>>','(3/2)NkT','3Nm<c<sup>2</sup>>'], answer:1, explain:'pV = (1/3)Nm<c<sup>2</sup>> — the 1/3 comes from averaging motion over three dimensions.'},
    {q:'The mean kinetic energy of a gas molecule is', options:['kT','(3/2)kT','3kT','(1/2)RT'], answer:1, explain:'Equating (1/3)m<c<sup>2</sup>> = kT per molecule per… gives mean KE = (3/2)kT.'},
    {q:'c<sub>rms</sub> is proportional to', options:['T','√T','1/T','T<sup>2</sup>'], answer:1, explain:'c<sub>rms</sub> = √(3RT/M) ∝ √T.'},
    {q:'At the same temperature, hydrogen molecules move faster than oxygen molecules because', options:['hydrogen is hotter','hydrogen molecules are lighter','oxygen is a liquid','pressure is higher'], answer:1, explain:'c<sub>rms</sub> = √(3RT/M): smaller M gives larger speed at fixed T.'},
    {q:'Raising the temperature of the Boltzmann distribution', options:['increases the area under the curve','shifts the peak right and flattens the curve','shifts the peak left','narrows the curve'], answer:1, explain:'Higher T: more molecules at higher energies; total area (number of molecules) stays constant.'},
    {q:'A gas at 300 K and 1.0×10<sup>5</sup> Pa in 2.0×10<sup>-3</sup> m<sup>3</sup> contains about', options:['0.080 mol','8.0 mol','0.80 mol','80 mol'], answer:0, explain:'n = pV/RT = (1.0×10<sup>5</sup> × 2.0×10<sup>-3</sup>)/(8.31 × 300) ≈ 0.080 mol.'},
    {q:'Boyle’s law (pV = constant) applies at constant', options:['pressure','temperature','volume','moles only'], answer:1, explain:'Boyle’s law is the isothermal (constant-T) special case of pV = nRT.'}
   ]}
,
  {id:'al-phy-t16', title:'Thermodynamics (A2)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-phy-t16-l1', title:'The first law of thermodynamics', html:'<h3>The first law of thermodynamics</h3><p><strong>First law:</strong> ΔU = q + w, where ΔU is the increase in internal energy, q is heat supplied TO the system, and w is work done ON the system. (Cambridge sign convention: work done ON the gas is positive.) Internal energy U of an ideal gas = sum of the random kinetic energies of its molecules.</p><p>Worked example: a gas is compressed (work 200 J done ON it) while losing 50 J of heat. ΔU = −50 + 200 = +150 J. Since U ∝ T for an ideal gas, the temperature rises.</p><p>Special cases: <strong>isothermal</strong> (ΔU = 0 for ideal gas → q = −w), <strong>adiabatic</strong> (q = 0 → ΔU = w), <strong>isochoric</strong> (w = 0 → ΔU = q), <strong>isobaric</strong> (w = −pΔV by the gas… careful with signs: work done BY the gas = pΔV).</p>'},
    {id:'al-phy-t16-l2', title:'p–V diagrams and work', html:'<h3>p–V diagrams and work</h3><p>The <strong>area under a p–V curve</strong> = work done BY the gas. For a <strong>cyclic process</strong>, the area enclosed by the loop = net work done per cycle. Clockwise cycle: net work done BY the gas (heat engine); anticlockwise: net work done ON the gas (refrigerator/heat pump).</p><p>Worked example: a gas expands isobarically at 2.0×10<sup>5</sup> Pa from 1.0 to 3.0 litres. W<sub>by</sub> = pΔV = 2.0×10<sup>5</sup> × 2.0×10<sup>-3</sup> = 400 J. If it absorbed 1000 J of heat, ΔU = 1000 − 400 = 600 J (work done BY gas is negative in the ΔU = q + w convention).</p><p>Adiabatic vs isothermal on p–V: the adiabatic curve is steeper (pV<sup>γ</sup> = constant, γ > 1) because in adiabatic expansion the gas cools (no heat inflow), so pressure falls faster than at constant T.</p>'},
    {id:'al-phy-t16-l3', title:'The second law and heat engines', html:'<h3>The second law and heat engines</h3><p><strong>Second law (Kelvin–Planck):</strong> no heat engine operating in a cycle can convert all heat into work — some heat must be rejected to a cold reservoir. <strong>Efficiency</strong> = W<sub>out</sub>/Q<sub>hot</sub> = 1 − Q<sub>cold</sub>/Q<sub>hot</sub>.</p><p>The <strong>Carnot cycle</strong> (two isotherms + two adiabats) is the most efficient possible between T<sub>hot</sub> and T<sub>cold</sub>: η<sub>max</sub> = 1 − T<sub>cold</sub>/T<sub>hot</sub> (kelvin). Worked example: steam at 500 K rejected to 300 K surroundings: η<sub>max</sub> = 1 − 300/500 = 40%. Real engines do worse (friction, non-ideal cycles).</p><p><strong>Entropy</strong> view: the second law says the entropy of an isolated system never decreases. Heat flowing from hot to cold increases total entropy; this defines the arrow of time for macroscopic processes.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'The first law of thermodynamics is', options:['ΔU = q − w','ΔU = q + w','ΔU = q/w','ΔU = w − q'], answer:1, explain:'ΔU = q + w with the Cambridge convention (w = work done ON the system).'},
    {q:'Internal energy of an ideal gas depends only on', options:['pressure','volume','temperature','mass'], answer:2, explain:'U = sum of random molecular KE = (3/2)NkT: a function of T only.'},
    {q:'In an adiabatic change', options:['ΔU = 0','q = 0','w = 0','T is constant'], answer:1, explain:'Adiabatic = no heat transfer (q = 0), so ΔU = w.'},
    {q:'The area enclosed by a cyclic process on a p–V diagram is', options:['the heat absorbed','net work per cycle','the change in internal energy','zero'], answer:1, explain:'Enclosed area = ∮p dV = net work done per cycle.'},
    {q:'A clockwise p–V cycle represents', options:['a refrigerator','a heat engine','an isothermal change','no work'], answer:1, explain:'Clockwise: expansion at high p, compression at low p → net work done BY the gas: a heat engine.'},
    {q:'Maximum (Carnot) efficiency between 600 K and 300 K is', options:['25%','50%','100%','67%'], answer:1, explain:'η = 1 − 300/600 = 0.50 = 50%.'},
    {q:'The Kelvin–Planck statement says', options:['heat flows hot to cold','no engine can convert all heat to work in a cycle','entropy always increases','absolute zero is unreachable'], answer:1, explain:'Some heat must always be rejected to a cold reservoir: 100% thermal efficiency is impossible.'},
    {q:'On a p–V diagram the adiabatic curve is steeper than the isothermal because', options:['pressure is higher','the gas cools during adiabatic expansion','volume changes faster','heat is added'], answer:1, explain:'Adiabatic expansion cools the gas (work done with no heat inflow), so pressure falls faster than at constant T.'},
    {q:'A gas expands against a constant external pressure of 1.0×10<sup>5</sup> Pa, volume increasing by 0.020 m<sup>3</sup>. Work done BY the gas is', options:['2000 J','5000 J','200 J','0'], answer:0, explain:'W = pΔV = 1.0×10<sup>5</sup> × 0.020 = 2000 J.'},
    {q:'For an ideal gas undergoing isothermal expansion', options:['ΔU > 0','ΔU = 0','ΔU < 0','q = 0'], answer:1, explain:'Isothermal → T constant → U (function of T only) unchanged: ΔU = 0, so heat absorbed = work done by gas.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-phy-t17', title:'Oscillations (A2)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-phy-t17-l1', title:'Simple harmonic motion', html:'<h3>Simple harmonic motion</h3><p><strong>SHM definition:</strong> acceleration is proportional to displacement from equilibrium and directed towards it: a = −ω<sup>2</sup>x. The negative sign is essential — it is the restoring character.</p><p>Solutions: x = x<sub>0</sub> sin(ωt) or x = x<sub>0</sub> cos(ωt). Velocity v = ±ω√(x<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> − x<sup>2</sup>) — maximum at equilibrium (v<sub>max</sub> = ωx<sub>0</sub>), zero at extremes. Acceleration maximum at extremes: a<sub>max</sub> = ω<sup>2</sup>x<sub>0</sub>.</p><p>Period T = 2π/ω. For a mass–spring system, ω = √(k/m) so T = 2π√(m/k). For a simple pendulum (small angles), T = 2π√(l/g) — independent of mass and (small) amplitude: <strong>isochronous</strong>.</p>'},
    {id:'al-phy-t17-l2', title:'Energy in SHM', html:'<h3>Energy in SHM</h3><p>Total energy in undamped SHM is constant: E = ½mω<sup>2</sup>x<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup>. It swaps between kinetic and potential: KE = ½mω<sup>2</sup>(x<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> − x<sup>2</sup>), PE = ½mω<sup>2</sup>x<sup>2</sup>.</p><p>Derivation: at the extreme (x = x<sub>0</sub>), all energy is potential: E = ½kx<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> = ½mω<sup>2</sup>x<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> (using k = mω<sup>2</sup>). At displacement x, PE = ½kx<sup>2</sup>, so KE = E − PE.</p><p>Graph shapes: E–x: total energy is a horizontal line; KE is an inverted parabola (max at x = 0); PE is an upright parabola (max at x = ±x<sub>0</sub>). KE and PE curves cross at x = ±x<sub>0</sub>/√2.</p>'},
    {id:'al-phy-t17-l3', title:'Damping and resonance', html:'<h3>Damping and resonance</h3><p><strong>Damping</strong> removes energy (usually as heat via friction/air resistance), so amplitude decays. <strong>Light damping:</strong> oscillations continue with slowly decreasing amplitude (period barely changes). <strong>Heavy/critical damping:</strong> system returns to equilibrium without oscillating — critical damping is the fastest non-oscillatory return (car suspension, galvanometers). <strong>Overdamping:</strong> slow creep back.</p><p><strong>Resonance:</strong> driving a system at its natural frequency gives large amplitude — energy is fed in efficiently each cycle. Resonance curve: amplitude vs driver frequency peaks at f<sub>0</sub>; light damping → tall narrow peak; heavy damping → short broad peak.</p><p>Examples: a child on a swing pushed at the right moment; a wine glass shattered by sound at its natural frequency; bridges and buildings designed to avoid resonance with wind/earthquake frequencies (Tacoma Narrows). Damping is deliberately added to reduce resonance damage.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'The defining equation of SHM is', options:['a = ω<sup>2</sup>x','a = −ω<sup>2</sup>x','v = ωx','x = ω<sup>2</sup>t'], answer:1, explain:'a = −ω<sup>2</sup>x: acceleration proportional to displacement, directed towards equilibrium.'},
    {q:'In SHM, velocity is maximum at', options:['maximum displacement','equilibrium position','half amplitude','the turning points'], answer:1, explain:'v = ω√(x<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> − x<sup>2</sup>) is max at x = 0 (equilibrium).'},
    {q:'The period of a mass–spring system is', options:['2π√(k/m)','2π√(m/k)','2π√(g/l)','2π√(l/g)'], answer:1, explain:'ω = √(k/m), T = 2π/ω = 2π√(m/k).'},
    {q:'The period of a simple pendulum (small angles) is independent of', options:['length','gravity','mass and amplitude','nothing'], answer:2, explain:'T = 2π√(l/g): no m, and independent of (small) amplitude — isochronous.'},
    {q:'Total energy in SHM is', options:['½mω<sup>2</sup>x<sub>0</sub>','½mω<sup>2</sup>x<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup>','mω<sup>2</sup>x<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup>','½kx'], answer:1, explain:'E = ½kx<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> = ½mω<sup>2</sup>x<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup>, constant for undamped SHM.'},
    {q:'At x = x<sub>0</sub>/√2, KE and PE are', options:['KE = 2×PE','equal','PE = 2×KE','both zero'], answer:1, explain:'KE = ½mω<sup>2</sup>(x<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> − x<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup>/2) = PE = ¼mω<sup>2</sup>x<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup>.'},
    {q:'Critical damping is used when', options:['oscillation must continue','the fastest return without oscillation is needed','amplitude must grow','resonance is wanted'], answer:1, explain:'Critical damping returns to equilibrium in the shortest time without oscillating (e.g. car suspension).'},
    {q:'Resonance occurs when the driving frequency', options:['is zero','equals the natural frequency','is double the natural frequency','is random'], answer:1, explain:'Driving at f<sub>0</sub> feeds energy in phase each cycle → large amplitude.'},
    {q:'Light damping makes the resonance curve', options:['short and broad','tall and narrow','flat','inverted'], answer:1, explain:'Less energy lost per cycle → sharper, taller resonance peak.'},
    {q:'A 0.25 kg mass on a spring (k = 100 N m<sup>-1</sup>) has period', options:['0.31 s','0.63 s','0.10 s','1.26 s'], answer:0, explain:'T = 2π√(m/k) = 2π√(0.25/100) = 2π × 0.05 ≈ 0.314 s.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-phy-t18', title:'Electric fields (A2)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-phy-t18-l1', title:'Coulomb’s law', html:'<h3>Coulomb’s law</h3><p><strong>Coulomb’s law:</strong> F = Q<sub>1</sub>Q<sub>2</sub>/(4πε<sub>0</sub>r<sup>2</sup>), with 1/(4πε<sub>0</sub>) = 9.0×10<sup>9</sup> N m<sup>2</sup> C<sup>-2</sup> and ε<sub>0</sub> = 8.85×10<sup>-12</sup> F m<sup>-1</sup>. Like charges repel, unlike attract, along the line joining them.</p><p>Worked example: two 1.0 μC charges 0.10 m apart: F = 9.0×10<sup>9</sup> × (1.0×10<sup>-6</sup>)<sup>2</sup>/0.01 = 0.90 N repulsion. Compare with gravity between the same masses — the electric force is ~10<sup>20</sup> times stronger for elementary particles.</p><p><strong>Electric field strength</strong> E = F/Q: force per unit positive charge, in N C<sup>-1</sup> (= V m<sup>-1</sup>). Field lines: start on + and end on −, never cross, density ∝ field strength.</p>'},
    {id:'al-phy-t18-l2', title:'Field of a point charge and uniform fields', html:'<h3>Field of a point charge and uniform fields</h3><p>For a point charge Q: E = Q/(4πε<sub>0</sub>r<sup>2</sup>) radially. For a charged sphere, outside it the field is as if all charge were at the centre.</p><p><strong>Uniform field</strong> between parallel plates: E = V/d (field lines parallel, equally spaced). Derivation: work moving charge Q across p.d. V is QV; also work = Fd = EQd → E = V/d.</p><p>Worked example: 200 V across plates 5.0 mm apart: E = 200/5.0×10<sup>-3</sup> = 4.0×10<sup>4</sup> V m<sup>-1</sup>. An electron (1.6×10<sup>-19</sup> C) feels F = eE = 6.4×10<sup>-15</sup> N, accelerating at F/m ≈ 7.0×10<sup>15</sup> m s<sup>-2</sup>.</p>'},
    {id:'al-phy-t18-l3', title:'Electric potential', html:'<h3>Electric potential</h3><p><strong>Electric potential</strong> V = work done per unit positive charge bringing it from infinity to the point (joules per coulomb = volts). For a point charge: V = Q/(4πε<sub>0</sub>r) — a scalar, positive near + charges.</p><p>Equipotentials: for a point charge, concentric spheres; for a uniform field, planes perpendicular to field lines. Moving along an equipotential does no work. E = −dV/dr (field points down the potential gradient).</p><p>Energy: charge Q at potential V has EPE = QV. Electronvolt: 1 eV = 1.60×10<sup>-19</sup> J — the energy of an electron accelerated through 1 V. A proton through 500 V gains 500 eV = 8.0×10<sup>-17</sup> J.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'Coulomb’s law: the force between two point charges is proportional to', options:['Q<sub>1</sub>Q<sub>2</sub>/r','Q<sub>1</sub>Q<sub>2</sub>/r<sup>2</sup>','(Q<sub>1</sub>+Q<sub>2</sub>)/r<sup>2</sup>','Q<sub>1</sub>Q<sub>2</sub>r<sup>2</sup>'], answer:1, explain:'F = Q<sub>1</sub>Q<sub>2</sub>/(4πε<sub>0</sub>r<sup>2</sup>): inverse-square law.'},
    {q:'1/(4πε<sub>0</sub>) is approximately', options:['8.85×10<sup>-12</sup>','9.0×10<sup>9</sup>','1.6×10<sup>-19</sup>','6.67×10<sup>-11</sup>'], answer:1, explain:'The electrostatic constant k = 1/(4πε<sub>0</sub>) ≈ 9.0×10<sup>9</sup> N m<sup>2</sup> C<sup>-2</sup>.'},
    {q:'Electric field strength is defined as', options:['force × charge','force per unit charge','charge per unit force','potential × distance'], answer:1, explain:'E = F/Q: force per unit positive charge, in N C<sup>-1</sup>.'},
    {q:'Between parallel plates with p.d. V and separation d, the field is', options:['V/d','Vd','V<sup>2</sup>/d','d/V'], answer:0, explain:'E = V/d for a uniform field (from QV = EQd).'},
    {q:'Electric potential at distance r from point charge Q is', options:['Q/(4πε<sub>0</sub>r<sup>2</sup>)','Q/(4πε<sub>0</sub>r)','Qr/(4πε<sub>0</sub>)','4πε<sub>0</sub>Q/r'], answer:1, explain:'V = Q/(4πε<sub>0</sub>r): scalar, falls as 1/r.'},
    {q:'1 eV equals', options:['1.60×10<sup>19</sup> J','1.60×10<sup>-19</sup> J','9.11×10<sup>-31</sup> J','1.00 J'], answer:1, explain:'1 eV = e × 1 V = 1.60×10<sup>-19</sup> J.'},
    {q:'Equipotential surfaces are always', options:['parallel to field lines','perpendicular to field lines','radial lines','at zero potential'], answer:1, explain:'No work is done moving along an equipotential, so field lines (direction of force) are perpendicular to them.'},
    {q:'The field inside a hollow charged conducting sphere is', options:['maximum at the centre','zero','Q/(4πε<sub>0</sub>r<sup>2</sup>)','uniform'], answer:1, explain:'Charge resides on the outer surface; by symmetry (Gauss’s law) the field inside is zero.'},
    {q:'Two 2.0 μC charges 0.20 m apart repel with force about', options:['0.90 N','0.45 N','1.8 N','9.0 N'], answer:0, explain:'F = 9.0×10<sup>9</sup> × (2.0×10<sup>-6</sup>)<sup>2</sup>/0.04 = 0.90 N.'},
    {q:'An electron accelerated from rest through 100 V gains kinetic energy', options:['100 J','1.6×10<sup>-17</sup> J','100 eV = 1.6×10<sup>-17</sup> J','1.6×10<sup>-19</sup> eV'], answer:2, explain:'KE = eV = 100 eV = 100 × 1.6×10<sup>-19</sup> = 1.6×10<sup>-17</sup> J.'}
   ]}
,
  {id:'al-phy-t19', title:'Capacitance (A2)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-phy-t19-l1', title:'Capacitors and capacitance', html:'<h3>Capacitors and capacitance</h3><p><strong>Capacitance</strong> C = Q/V: charge stored per unit p.d., in farads (1 F = 1 C V<sup>-1</sup>). A parallel-plate capacitor: C = ε<sub>0</sub>A/d — larger area or smaller separation stores more charge.</p><p>Combinations: <strong>parallel:</strong> C<sub>total</sub> = C<sub>1</sub> + C<sub>2</sub> (same V, charges add). <strong>Series:</strong> 1/C<sub>total</sub> = 1/C<sub>1</sub> + 1/C<sub>2</sub> (same Q, voltages add).</p><p>Worked example: 4 μF and 6 μF in series: 1/C = 1/4 + 1/6 = 5/12 → C = 2.4 μF. Across 12 V, Q = CV = 28.8 μC on each; V<sub>4</sub> = 7.2 V, V<sub>6</sub> = 4.8 V (smaller capacitor gets larger p.d.).</p>'},
    {id:'al-phy-t19-l2', title:'Energy stored in a capacitor', html:'<h3>Energy stored in a capacitor</h3><p>Energy stored W = ½QV = ½CV<sup>2</sup> = Q<sup>2</sup>/2C. Derivation: charging moves small charge dq against rising p.d. q/C; W = ∫(q/C)dq from 0 to Q = Q<sup>2</sup>/2C. Graphically: area under the Q–V line (a triangle).</p><p>Worked example: a 470 μF capacitor charged to 12 V stores W = ½ × 470×10<sup>-6</sup> × 144 ≈ 0.034 J. A camera flash capacitor (100 μF at 300 V) stores 4.5 J — released in milliseconds for a bright flash.</p><p>Exam reasoning: when a capacitor discharges through a resistor, the energy becomes heat in the resistor. If two capacitors are connected, use conservation of charge (not energy — some is lost as heat/spark).</p>'},
    {id:'al-phy-t19-l3', title:'Charging and discharging curves', html:'<h3>Charging and discharging curves</h3><p>Discharging through resistance R: Q = Q<sub>0</sub>e<sup>−t/RC</sup>, V = V<sub>0</sub>e<sup>−t/RC</sup>, I = I<sub>0</sub>e<sup>−t/RC</sup>. <strong>Time constant</strong> τ = RC: after one τ, values fall to 1/e ≈ 37% of initial (63% discharged).</p><p>Derivation sketch: I = −dQ/dt = V/R = Q/(RC) → dQ/Q = −dt/(RC) → ln Q = −t/(RC) + const → Q = Q<sub>0</sub>e<sup>−t/RC</sup>.</p><p>Charging: Q = Q<sub>0</sub>(1 − e<sup>−t/RC</sup>). Worked example: 100 μF discharging through 50 kΩ: τ = 5.0 s. Time to fall to 10%: e<sup>−t/5</sup> = 0.1 → t = 5 ln 10 ≈ 11.5 s. Uses: timing circuits, smoothing rectified a.c., camera flashes.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'Capacitance is defined as', options:['Q × V','Q/V','V/Q','Q<sup>2</sup>/V'], answer:1, explain:'C = Q/V: charge stored per unit potential difference, in farads.'},
    {q:'For a parallel-plate capacitor, C equals', options:['ε<sub>0</sub>d/A','ε<sub>0</sub>A/d','A/(ε<sub>0</sub>d)','ε<sub>0</sub>Ad'], answer:1, explain:'C = ε<sub>0</sub>A/d: proportional to plate area, inversely to separation.'},
    {q:'Two capacitors in parallel: total capacitance is', options:['C<sub>1</sub> + C<sub>2</sub>','(C<sub>1</sub>C<sub>2</sub>)/(C<sub>1</sub>+C<sub>2</sub>)','C<sub>1</sub>C<sub>2</sub>','|C<sub>1</sub> − C<sub>2</sub>|'], answer:0, explain:'Parallel: same V, charges add → C<sub>total</sub> = C<sub>1</sub> + C<sub>2</sub>.'},
    {q:'Energy stored in a capacitor is', options:['QV','½QV','Q<sup>2</sup>V','CV'], answer:1, explain:'W = ½QV = ½CV<sup>2</sup> = Q<sup>2</sup>/2C (area under the Q–V graph).'},
    {q:'A 200 μF capacitor at 50 V stores', options:['0.25 J','0.50 J','5.0 J','10 J'], answer:0, explain:'W = ½CV<sup>2</sup> = 0.5 × 200×10<sup>-6</sup> × 2500 = 0.25 J.'},
    {q:'The time constant of an RC circuit is', options:['R/C','RC','C/R','1/(RC)'], answer:1, explain:'τ = RC: the time for charge/voltage/current to fall to 1/e of initial value.'},
    {q:'After one time constant of discharging, the charge remaining is about', options:['63%','37%','50%','0%'], answer:1, explain:'Q = Q<sub>0</sub>e<sup>−1</sup> ≈ 0.37Q<sub>0</sub>: 37% remains (63% discharged).'},
    {q:'Charging a capacitor: Q as a function of time is', options:['Q<sub>0</sub>e<sup>−t/RC</sup>','Q<sub>0</sub>(1 − e<sup>−t/RC</sup>)','Q<sub>0</sub>(1 + e<sup>−t/RC</sup>)','Q<sub>0</sub>t/(RC)'], answer:1, explain:'Charging approaches Q<sub>0</sub> asymptotically: Q = Q<sub>0</sub>(1 − e<sup>−t/RC</sup>).'},
    {q:'A 3 μF and 6 μF capacitor in series have total capacitance', options:['9 μF','2 μF','4.5 μF','18 μF'], answer:1, explain:'1/C = 1/3 + 1/6 = 1/2 → C = 2 μF.'},
    {q:'In series, the p.d. across each capacitor', options:['is equal','divides inversely with capacitance','divides directly with capacitance','is zero'], answer:1, explain:'Same Q on each: V = Q/C, so the smaller capacitor takes the larger share of voltage.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-phy-t20', title:'Magnetic fields (A2)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-phy-t20-l1', title:'Magnetic force on a moving charge', html:'<h3>Magnetic force on a moving charge</h3><p>A charge Q moving at velocity v perpendicular to field B feels F = BQv. Generally F = BQv sinθ. <strong>Fleming’s left-hand rule:</strong> First finger = Field, seCond finger = Current, thuMb = Motion (force).</p><p><strong>Magnetic flux density</strong> B is defined by F = BIl sinθ for a current-carrying wire: B = F/(Il) for perpendicular, in tesla (1 T = 1 N A<sup>-1</sup> m<sup>-1</sup>).</p><p>Direction: the force is perpendicular to both v and B. A positive charge moving perpendicular to a uniform B follows a circular path (F provides centripetal force): BQv = mv<sup>2</sup>/r → r = mv/(BQ). This is the basis of cyclotrons and mass spectrometers.</p>'},
    {id:'al-phy-t20-l2', title:'The Hall effect and velocity selector', html:'<h3>The Hall effect and velocity selector</h3><p><strong>Hall effect:</strong> a current-carrying conductor in a perpendicular B field develops a transverse voltage. Electrons are pushed to one side by BQv until the electric field of the accumulated charge balances it: eE = evB → Hall voltage V<sub>H</sub> = vBd (d = width).</p><p>Derivation of carrier density: I = nAve and V<sub>H</sub> = vBd with A = (width d)(thickness t)… careful: for a slab of width w (across field) and thickness t: V<sub>H</sub> = IB/(nqt). Measuring V<sub>H</sub> gives n, the charge-carrier density — and its sign reveals whether carriers are electrons or holes.</p><p><strong>Velocity selector:</strong> crossed E and B fields; only charges with v = E/B pass undeflected (electric force qE balances magnetic force qvB). Used before mass spectrometers to select one speed.</p>'},
    {id:'al-phy-t20-l3', title:'Electromagnetic induction', html:'<h3>Electromagnetic induction</h3><p><strong>Faraday’s law:</strong> induced e.m.f. ∝ rate of change of magnetic flux linkage: E = −N dΦ/dt. <strong>Lenz’s law</strong> (the minus sign): the induced current opposes the change producing it — conservation of energy (otherwise free energy).</p><p><strong>Magnetic flux</strong> Φ = BA (for perpendicular field), <strong>flux linkage</strong> = NΦ. Worked example: a 200-turn coil of area 5.0×10<sup>-3</sup> m<sup>2</sup> in a field falling from 0.40 T to 0 in 0.10 s: E = N A ΔB/Δt = 200 × 5.0×10<sup>-3</sup> × 0.40/0.10 = 4.0 V.</p><p>Moving conductor: a rod of length l moving at v perpendicular to B cuts flux at rate Blv → induced e.m.f. = Blv. Generators rotate a coil in a field: e.m.f. = BANω sin(ωt) — sinusoidal a.c. output.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'Force on charge Q moving at v perpendicular to B is', options:['BQ/v','BQv','Qv/B','B/Qv'], answer:1, explain:'F = BQv (perpendicular); generally F = BQv sinθ.'},
    {q:'The tesla is equal to', options:['N A<sup>-1</sup> m<sup>-1</sup>','N A m','Wb m','V m<sup>-1</sup>'], answer:0, explain:'B = F/(Il) → 1 T = 1 N A<sup>-1</sup> m<sup>-1</sup>.'},
    {q:'In Fleming’s left-hand rule, the first finger represents', options:['current','field','motion/force','voltage'], answer:1, explain:'First = Field, seCond = Current, thuMb = Motion.'},
    {q:'A charged particle entering a uniform B field perpendicularly follows a', options:['parabola','circle','straight line','spiral inward'], answer:1, explain:'Perpendicular F = BQv provides centripetal force: circular path of radius r = mv/(BQ).'},
    {q:'The Hall voltage is given by', options:['V<sub>H</sub> = vBd','V<sub>H</sub> = B/(vd)','V<sub>H</sub> = vd/B','V<sub>H</sub> = IBd'], answer:0, explain:'Balancing eE = evB across width d gives V<sub>H</sub> = vBd.'},
    {q:'A velocity selector passes charges with speed', options:['E/B','B/E','EB','E<sup>2</sup>/B'], answer:0, explain:'qE = qvB → v = E/B undeflected.'},
    {q:'Faraday’s law states induced e.m.f. is proportional to', options:['magnetic flux','rate of change of flux linkage','field strength','number of turns only'], answer:1, explain:'E = −N dΦ/dt: proportional to the rate of change of flux linkage.'},
    {q:'Lenz’s law is a consequence of conservation of', options:['charge','energy','momentum','mass'], answer:1, explain:'Opposing the change prevents runaway energy gain — energy conservation.'},
    {q:'E.m.f. induced in a rod of length l moving at v perpendicular to B is', options:['Bl/v','Blv','Bv/l','lv/B'], answer:1, explain:'Rate of cutting flux: E = Blv.'},
    {q:'Magnetic flux has unit', options:['tesla','weber','henry','farad'], answer:1, explain:'Φ = BA: 1 Wb = 1 T m<sup>2</sup>.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-phy-t21', title:'Alternating currents (A2)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-phy-t21-l1', title:'Sinusoidal a.c. and rms values', html:'<h3>Sinusoidal a.c. and rms values</h3><p>Mains a.c.: V = V<sub>0</sub> sin(ωt), I = I<sub>0</sub> sin(ωt) for a resistive load. <strong>Root-mean-square</strong> values: V<sub>rms</sub> = V<sub>0</sub>/√2, I<sub>rms</sub> = I<sub>0</sub>/√2. The rms value is the equivalent d.c. that delivers the same heating power.</p><p>Derivation: mean of sin<sup>2</sup> over a cycle = ½, so mean power = V<sub>0</sub>I<sub>0</sub>/2 = (V<sub>0</sub>/√2)(I<sub>0</sub>/√2) = V<sub>rms</sub>I<sub>rms</sub>. UK mains: 230 V rms → peak ≈ 325 V.</p><p>Power in a.c.: P = V<sub>rms</sub>I<sub>rms</sub> for resistive loads. An oscilloscope (CRO) displays the waveform: peak voltage from vertical deflection, period/frequency from the horizontal timebase.</p>'},
    {id:'al-phy-t21-l2', title:'The transformer', html:'<h3>The transformer</h3><p>Ideal transformer: V<sub>s</sub>/V<sub>p</sub> = N<sub>s</sub>/N<sub>p</sub> (voltages in turns ratio — from Faraday’s law, same dΦ/dt links both coils). For 100% efficiency, power in = power out: V<sub>p</sub>I<sub>p</sub> = V<sub>s</sub>I<sub>s</sub> → I<sub>s</sub>/I<sub>p</sub> = N<sub>p</sub>/N<sub>s</sub> (currents in inverse ratio).</p><p>Worked example: 230 V primary, 2000 turns; secondary 100 turns → V<sub>s</sub> = 230 × 100/2000 = 11.5 V. If the secondary delivers 2.0 A, primary current = 2.0 × 100/2000 = 0.10 A (ideal).</p><p>Why high-voltage transmission: P<sub>loss</sub> = I<sup>2</sup>R. Stepping up to 400 kV cuts current (for the same power) hugely, slashing I<sup>2</sup>R losses. Step-down transformers near users return to safe 230 V.</p>'},
    {id:'al-phy-t21-l3', title:'Rectification and smoothing', html:'<h3>Rectification and smoothing</h3><p><strong>Half-wave rectification:</strong> a single diode passes only one half-cycle — output is pulsed d.c. <strong>Full-wave (bridge) rectification:</strong> four diodes steer both half-cycles the same way — output pulses at twice the mains frequency with no gaps.</p><p><strong>Smoothing:</strong> a large capacitor in parallel with the load charges on peaks and discharges through the load between them, reducing <strong>ripple</strong>. Larger C or smaller load current → smaller ripple voltage.</p><p>CRO traces to recognise: raw a.c. sine wave; half-wave (alternate half-cycles zeroed); full-wave (all humps same direction); smoothed (nearly flat d.c. with small ripple). Ripple frequency = mains frequency (half-wave) or double (full-wave).</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'V<sub>rms</sub> for a sinusoidal supply relates to peak V<sub>0</sub> by', options:['V<sub>rms</sub> = V<sub>0</sub>','V<sub>rms</sub> = V<sub>0</sub>/√2','V<sub>rms</sub> = V<sub>0</sub>/2','V<sub>rms</sub> = √2 V<sub>0</sub>'], answer:1, explain:'V<sub>rms</sub> = V<sub>0</sub>/√2 — the equivalent d.c. for the same heating effect.'},
    {q:'UK mains 230 V rms has peak voltage about', options:['230 V','163 V','325 V','460 V'], answer:2, explain:'V<sub>0</sub> = 230 × √2 ≈ 325 V.'},
    {q:'Mean power in a resistive a.c. circuit is', options:['V<sub>0</sub>I<sub>0</sub>','V<sub>rms</sub>I<sub>rms</sub>','V<sub>0</sub>I<sub>0</sub>/4','zero'], answer:1, explain:'Mean of sin<sup>2</sup> = ½ gives P = V<sub>0</sub>I<sub>0</sub>/2 = V<sub>rms</sub>I<sub>rms</sub>.'},
    {q:'In an ideal transformer, V<sub>s</sub>/V<sub>p</sub> equals', options:['N<sub>p</sub>/N<sub>s</sub>','N<sub>s</sub>/N<sub>p</sub>','I<sub>s</sub>/I<sub>p</sub>','1 always'], answer:1, explain:'Voltages are in the turns ratio: V<sub>s</sub>/V<sub>p</sub> = N<sub>s</sub>/N<sub>p</sub>.'},
    {q:'In an ideal transformer the currents satisfy', options:['I<sub>s</sub> = I<sub>p</sub>','I<sub>s</sub>/I<sub>p</sub> = N<sub>p</sub>/N<sub>s</sub>','I<sub>s</sub>/I<sub>p</sub> = N<sub>s</sub>/N<sub>p</sub>','I<sub>s</sub>I<sub>p</sub> = 1'], answer:1, explain:'Power in = power out: V<sub>p</sub>I<sub>p</sub> = V<sub>s</sub>I<sub>s</sub> → currents in inverse turns ratio.'},
    {q:'High-voltage transmission reduces losses because', options:['voltage destroys resistance','P<sub>loss</sub> = I<sup>2</sup>R and current is smaller','cables are thicker','a.c. cannot lose energy'], answer:1, explain:'For fixed transmitted power, higher V means lower I, and loss = I<sup>2</sup>R falls sharply.'},
    {q:'A bridge rectifier gives', options:['half-wave output','full-wave output','smooth d.c.','a.c. output'], answer:1, explain:'Four diodes conduct on alternate half-cycles, giving full-wave pulsed d.c.'},
    {q:'Smoothing in a power supply is done with', options:['a resistor in series','a large capacitor in parallel with the load','an inductor','a second transformer'], answer:1, explain:'The capacitor charges at peaks and supplies current between them, reducing ripple.'},
    {q:'Increasing the smoothing capacitance', options:['increases ripple','decreases ripple','changes frequency','stops rectification'], answer:1, explain:'Larger C discharges less between peaks → smaller ripple voltage.'},
    {q:'On a CRO, doubling the timebase setting makes the displayed wave appear', options:['taller','twice as wide (fewer cycles shown)','half as wide','unchanged'], answer:1, explain:'Longer time per division stretches the trace horizontally.'}
   ]}
,
  {id:'al-phy-t22', title:'Quantum physics (A2)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-phy-t22-l1', title:'The photoelectric effect', html:'<h3>The photoelectric effect</h3><p><strong>Photoelectric effect:</strong> electrons are emitted from a metal surface when light above a <strong>threshold frequency</strong> f<sub>0</sub> shines on it. Key observations the wave theory cannot explain: no emission below f<sub>0</sub> (however intense); emission is instantaneous; maximum KE depends on frequency, not intensity.</p><p><strong>Einstein’s photon model:</strong> light arrives as photons of energy E = hf. One photon gives all its energy to one electron: hf = Φ + KE<sub>max</sub>, where Φ = hf<sub>0</sub> is the <strong>work function</strong> (minimum escape energy).</p><p>Worked example: light of λ = 400 nm on a metal with Φ = 2.0 eV. Photon energy = hc/λ = (6.63×10<sup>-34</sup> × 3.0×10<sup>8</sup>)/(4.0×10<sup>-7</sup>) = 4.97×10<sup>-19</sup> J = 3.1 eV. KE<sub>max</sub> = 3.1 − 2.0 = 1.1 eV.</p>'},
    {id:'al-phy-t22-l2', title:'Stopping potential and photon momentum', html:'<h3>Stopping potential and photon momentum</h3><p><strong>Stopping potential</strong> V<sub>s</sub>: the reverse p.d. that just stops the fastest photoelectrons: eV<sub>s</sub> = KE<sub>max</sub> = hf − Φ. Plotting V<sub>s</sub> against f gives a straight line of gradient h/e and intercept −Φ/e — a classic determination of Planck’s constant.</p><p>Photons carry <strong>momentum</strong> p = E/c = h/λ (from E = hf and c = fλ). Though massless, photons transfer momentum — demonstrated by Compton scattering and solar sails.</p><p><strong>Wave–particle duality:</strong> light shows wave behaviour (interference, diffraction) and particle behaviour (photoelectric effect). Electrons show particle behaviour (charge, mass) and wave behaviour (diffraction). <strong>de Broglie wavelength:</strong> λ = h/p = h/(mv) for any moving particle.</p>'},
    {id:'al-phy-t22-l3', title:'Electron diffraction and energy levels', html:'<h3>Electron diffraction and energy levels</h3><p><strong>Electron diffraction:</strong> firing electrons at a thin graphite film produces diffraction rings — proof that electrons have wave properties. Faster electrons (shorter λ = h/mv) give smaller ring diameters, confirming λ = h/p quantitatively.</p><p><strong>Discrete energy levels:</strong> line spectra show atoms emit/absorb only certain photon energies. An electron dropping from level E<sub>2</sub> to E<sub>1</sub> emits a photon: hf = E<sub>2</sub> − E<sub>1</sub>. Each element’s line spectrum is unique — used to identify elements in stars.</p><p>Worked example: hydrogen’s Lyman-α line (n=2 → n=1) has λ = 121.6 nm. Photon energy = hc/λ = 1.63×10<sup>-18</sup> J = 10.2 eV — the energy difference between the levels. Absorption lines (dark) correspond to photons absorbed jumping electrons UP.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'The photoelectric effect cannot be explained by the wave theory because', options:['light is too fast','emission depends on frequency, not intensity, with a threshold','electrons are negative','metals reflect light'], answer:1, explain:'Wave theory predicts energy ∝ intensity with no threshold; experiment shows a threshold frequency and instantaneous emission.'},
    {q:'Einstein’s photoelectric equation is', options:['hf = Φ − KE<sub>max</sub>','hf = Φ + KE<sub>max</sub>','KE<sub>max</sub> = Φ + hf','hf = Φ × KE<sub>max</sub>'], answer:1, explain:'Photon energy = work function + max kinetic energy of the emitted electron.'},
    {q:'The work function Φ is', options:['the photon energy','the minimum energy to liberate an electron','the stopping potential','hf<sub>0</sub> × e'], answer:1, explain:'Φ = hf<sub>0</sub>: the minimum energy needed to free an electron from the surface.'},
    {q:'Stopping potential V<sub>s</sub> satisfies', options:['eV<sub>s</sub> = KE<sub>max</sub>','V<sub>s</sub> = hf','eV<sub>s</sub> = Φ','V<sub>s</sub> = KE<sub>max</sub>/h'], answer:0, explain:'The retarding field does work eV<sub>s</sub> to stop the fastest electrons: eV<sub>s</sub> = KE<sub>max</sub>.'},
    {q:'A V<sub>s</sub>–f graph has gradient', options:['h','h/e','e/h','Φ'], answer:1, explain:'V<sub>s</sub> = (h/e)f − Φ/e: gradient = h/e.'},
    {q:'Photon momentum equals', options:['hf','h/λ','hc','hλ'], answer:1, explain:'p = E/c = hf/c = h/λ.'},
    {q:'de Broglie wavelength of a particle is', options:['h/p','hp','p/h','h<sup>2</sup>/p'], answer:0, explain:'λ = h/p = h/(mv): every moving particle has an associated wavelength.'},
    {q:'Electron diffraction through graphite proves', options:['electrons are particles only','electrons have wave properties','graphite is magnetic','electrons have no mass'], answer:1, explain:'Diffraction is a wave phenomenon — the rings show electrons behaving as waves.'},
    {q:'A photon emitted in an atomic transition has energy', options:['E<sub>1</sub> + E<sub>2</sub>','E<sub>2</sub> − E<sub>1</sub>','E<sub>2</sub>/E<sub>1</sub>','√(E<sub>1</sub>E<sub>2</sub>)'], answer:1, explain:'hf = ΔE = E<sub>upper</sub> − E<sub>lower</sub>.'},
    {q:'Increasing the intensity of above-threshold light', options:['increases KE<sub>max</sub>','increases the number of photoelectrons per second','changes the threshold frequency','stops emission'], answer:1, explain:'More photons per second → more electrons emitted; each photon’s energy (hence KE<sub>max</sub>) is unchanged.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-phy-t23', title:'Nuclear physics (A2)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-phy-t23-l1', title:'Mass defect and binding energy', html:'<h3>Mass defect and binding energy</h3><p><strong>Mass defect</strong> Δm = (mass of separate nucleons) − (mass of nucleus). The “missing” mass became <strong>binding energy</strong> E = Δmc<sup>2</sup> holding the nucleus together.</p><p><strong>Binding energy per nucleon</strong> measures nuclear stability. The curve rises steeply to iron-56 (most stable, ~8.8 MeV/nucleon) then slowly falls. Nuclei can release energy by moving towards iron: heavy nuclei split (<strong>fission</strong>), light nuclei join (<strong>fusion</strong>).</p><p>Worked example: <sup>4</sup>He nucleus mass = 4.0015 u; 2p + 2n = 2(1.0073) + 2(1.0087) = 4.0319 u. Δm = 0.0304 u = 0.0304 × 1.66×10<sup>-27</sup> = 5.05×10<sup>-29</sup> kg. E = Δmc<sup>2</sup> ≈ 4.5×10<sup>-12</sup> J ≈ 28 MeV total, ~7.1 MeV per nucleon.</p>'},
    {id:'al-phy-t23-l2', title:'Radioactive decay', html:'<h3>Radioactive decay</h3><p>Radioactive decay is <strong>random</strong> (cannot predict which nucleus decays) and <strong>spontaneous</strong> (unaffected by temperature, pressure, chemical state). <strong>Activity</strong> A = −dN/dt = λN, where λ is the <strong>decay constant</strong> (probability per unit time).</p><p>Solving dN/dt = −λN gives N = N<sub>0</sub>e<sup>−λt</sup> and A = A<sub>0</sub>e<sup>−λt</sup>. <strong>Half-life</strong> T<sub>½</sub> = ln 2/λ. Worked example: λ = 0.0231 s<sup>-1</sup> → T<sub>½</sub> = 0.693/0.0231 = 30 s.</p><p>Determining λ: plot ln A against t — straight line of gradient −λ. Background count must be subtracted first. After n half-lives, fraction remaining = (1/2)<sup>n</sup>.</p>'},
    {id:'al-phy-t23-l3', title:'Fission, fusion and nuclear energy', html:'<h3>Fission, fusion and nuclear energy</h3><p><strong>Fission:</strong> a heavy nucleus (e.g. <sup>235</sup>U) absorbs a neutron and splits into two lighter nuclei + neutrons + energy (~200 MeV per fission). The released neutrons can trigger further fissions — a <strong>chain reaction</strong>, controlled in reactors by control rods (absorb neutrons) and a moderator (slows neutrons to thermal speeds).</p><p><strong>Fusion:</strong> light nuclei join, e.g. in the Sun: 4p → <sup>4</sup>He + 2e<sup>+</sup> + 2ν<sub>e</sub> (proton–proton chain), releasing ~27 MeV. Requires extreme temperature/pressure to overcome Coulomb repulsion — the subject of fusion reactor research (tokamaks).</p><p>Exam reasoning: energy released = (mass of reactants − mass of products)c<sup>2</sup>. Always include ALL particles (neutrinos carry away some energy in the Sun’s chain).</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'Mass defect is', options:['mass of nucleus − mass of nucleons','mass of nucleons − mass of nucleus','mass lost as radiation','zero for all nuclei'], answer:1, explain:'Δm = (separate nucleon masses) − (nucleus mass) > 0; it equals the binding energy via E = Δmc<sup>2</sup>.'},
    {q:'The most stable nucleus (highest binding energy per nucleon) is around', options:['hydrogen','helium','iron-56','uranium-235'], answer:2, explain:'The binding-energy-per-nucleon curve peaks at iron-56 (~8.8 MeV/nucleon).'},
    {q:'Activity is defined as', options:['λ/N','−dN/dt','N<sub>0</sub>e<sup>λt</sup>','dN/dt × N'], answer:1, explain:'A = −dN/dt = λN: decays per unit time.'},
    {q:'Half-life relates to the decay constant by', options:['T<sub>½</sub> = λ/ln 2','T<sub>½</sub> = ln 2/λ','T<sub>½</sub> = 1/λ<sup>2</sup>','T<sub>½</sub> = e<sup>λ</sup>'], answer:1, explain:'From (1/2) = e<sup>−λT½</sup>: T<sub>½</sub> = ln 2/λ.'},
    {q:'Radioactive decay is described as random and spontaneous, meaning', options:['it can be predicted exactly','it is unaffected by temperature and pressure','it always emits alpha','it needs a trigger'], answer:1, explain:'Spontaneous: no external trigger and unaffected by physical/chemical conditions; random: individual decays unpredictable.'},
    {q:'After 3 half-lives, the fraction of undecayed nuclei is', options:['1/3','1/8','1/6','3/4'], answer:1, explain:'(1/2)<sup>3</sup> = 1/8 remains.'},
    {q:'In a fission reactor, the moderator’s job is to', options:['absorb neutrons','slow neutrons to thermal speeds','cool the fuel','contain radiation'], answer:1, explain:'Slow (thermal) neutrons are far more likely to cause further fission of U-235.'},
    {q:'The energy released in a nuclear reaction equals', options:['(products − reactants)c<sup>2</sup>','(reactants − products)c<sup>2</sup>','reactants × c<sup>2</sup>','zero'], answer:1, explain:'ΔE = Δmc<sup>2</sup> with Δm = mass(reactants) − mass(products).'},
    {q:'Fusion in the Sun mainly converts', options:['helium to carbon','hydrogen to helium','uranium to lead','electrons to photons'], answer:1, explain:'The proton–proton chain fuses 4 protons into helium-4, releasing ~27 MeV.'},
    {q:'A ln A vs t plot for a decaying sample has gradient', options:['λ','−λ','ln 2','−ln 2'], answer:1, explain:'ln A = −λt + ln A<sub>0</sub>: gradient = −λ.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-phy-t24', title:'Medical physics (A2)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-phy-t24-l1', title:'Ultrasound imaging', html:'<h3>Ultrasound imaging</h3><p><strong>Ultrasound:</strong> sound above 20 kHz; medical scanners use 1–20 MHz. Produced by a <strong>piezoelectric transducer</strong> (quartz crystal vibrates when alternating p.d. applied; echoes generate p.d. on return).</p><p><strong>Specific acoustic impedance</strong> Z = ρc. At a boundary, fraction reflected = ((Z<sub>2</sub> − Z<sub>1</sub>)/(Z<sub>2</sub> + Z<sub>1</sub>))<sup>2</sup>. Large Z mismatch (tissue–air) reflects almost everything — hence coupling gel. Distance to boundary: s = ct/2 (pulse–echo, t = round-trip time).</p><p><strong>A-scan:</strong> echo amplitude vs time (one line); <strong>B-scan:</strong> brightness dots build a 2D image as the probe sweeps. Higher frequency → better resolution but less penetration (more attenuation).</p>'},
    {id:'al-phy-t24-l2', title:'X-ray imaging and CT scans', html:'<h3>X-ray imaging and CT scans</h3><p><strong>X-rays</strong> are produced when fast electrons strike a metal target (bremsstrahlung + characteristic lines). Attenuation: I = I<sub>0</sub>e<sup>−μx</sup>, where μ is the <strong>linear attenuation coefficient</strong> (depends on material and photon energy).</p><p>Contrast: bone (high Z, high μ) absorbs strongly → white on film; soft tissue → grey; air → black. <strong>Contrast media</strong> (barium meal, iodine) highlight soft structures.</p><p><strong>CT scan:</strong> an X-ray tube rotates around the patient, taking many projections; a computer reconstructs cross-sectional slices. Much better soft-tissue contrast than plain X-ray, but higher dose. <strong>PET scans</strong> detect γ pairs from positron annihilation to map metabolic activity.</p>'},
    {id:'al-phy-t24-l3', title:'Dose, safety and MRI basics', html:'<h3>Dose, safety and MRI basics</h3><p>Ionising radiation damages tissue: X-rays/CT involve a <strong>dose</strong> trade-off — diagnostic benefit vs cancer risk. Ultrasound and MRI use non-ionising radiation and are safer for foetuses/children.</p><p><strong>MRI:</strong> strong magnetic field aligns hydrogen nuclei spins; radio-frequency pulses flip them; as they relax they emit RF signals detected to build images. Superb soft-tissue contrast (brain, joints) with no ionising radiation. Drawbacks: cost, time, and incompatibility with metal implants/pacemakers.</p><p>Exam reasoning: compare modalities by (1) ionising or not, (2) resolution vs penetration, (3) what each images best — ultrasound: foetus, moving structures (Doppler blood flow); X-ray: bone fractures, chest; CT: 3D trauma/tumour detail; MRI: brain/spinal soft tissue.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'Medical ultrasound typically uses frequencies of', options:['20 Hz – 20 kHz','1 – 20 MHz','100 GHz','50 Hz'], answer:1, explain:'1–20 MHz: high enough for millimetre resolution, low enough to penetrate tissue.'},
    {q:'Ultrasound is generated using', options:['a microphone','a piezoelectric transducer','an X-ray tube','a magnet'], answer:1, explain:'Piezoelectric crystals convert alternating p.d. to mechanical vibration and back.'},
    {q:'Specific acoustic impedance Z equals', options:['ρ/c','ρc','c/ρ','ρ + c'], answer:1, explain:'Z = ρc: density × speed of sound in the medium.'},
    {q:'Coupling gel is used in ultrasound because', options:['it cools the skin','tissue–air mismatch would reflect almost all the ultrasound','it conducts electricity','it amplifies echoes'], answer:1, explain:'Air’s tiny Z vs tissue would reflect ~99.9% at the boundary; gel bridges the impedance gap.'},
    {q:'X-ray intensity through material follows', options:['I = I<sub>0</sub>e<sup>μx</sup>','I = I<sub>0</sub>e<sup>−μx</sup>','I = I<sub>0</sub>/x','I = I<sub>0</sub> − μx'], answer:1, explain:'Exponential attenuation: I = I<sub>0</sub>e<sup>−μx</sup>.'},
    {q:'Bones appear white on an X-ray because', options:['they emit X-rays','they strongly attenuate X-rays','they are hollow','film inverts the image'], answer:1, explain:'High-Z calcium gives large μ: little radiation reaches the film → white (unexposed) areas.'},
    {q:'A CT scan differs from a plain X-ray in that it', options:['uses no radiation','reconstructs cross-sectional slices from many projections','uses ultrasound','images only bones'], answer:1, explain:'CT rotates the tube/detectors and computers build 3D slice images.'},
    {q:'PET scans locate tumours by detecting', options:['X-rays','gamma photon pairs from positron annihilation','ultrasound echoes','magnetic resonance'], answer:1, explain:'A positron-emitting tracer annihilates with electrons, giving back-to-back γ pairs.'},
    {q:'MRI is preferred for brain imaging mainly because', options:['it is cheap','superb soft-tissue contrast with no ionising radiation','it is very fast','it uses X-rays'], answer:1, explain:'MRI gives excellent soft-tissue detail without ionising radiation.'},
    {q:'In pulse–echo ultrasound, the depth of a boundary is', options:['ct','ct/2','2ct','c/t'], answer:1, explain:'The pulse travels there and back: distance = c × (round-trip time)/2.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-phy-t25', title:'Astronomy and cosmology (A2)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-phy-t25-l1', title:'Distances and the parsec', html:'<h3>Distances and the parsec</h3><p><strong>Parallax:</strong> nearby stars appear to shift against distant background as Earth orbits. Parallax angle p (arcseconds) gives distance d = 1/p <strong>parsecs</strong>. 1 pc = 3.26 light-years = 3.09×10<sup>16</sup> m — the distance at which 1 AU subtends 1 arcsecond.</p><p><strong>Luminosity</strong> L = total power output of a star. <strong>Radiant flux</strong> F = L/(4πd<sup>2</sup>): power per unit area at Earth — the inverse-square law again. Measuring F and knowing L (from spectral class) gives d = √(L/(4πF)) — <strong>standard candles</strong>.</p><p>Type Ia supernovae are excellent standard candles (uniform peak luminosity), used to measure distances to far galaxies.</p>'},
    {id:'al-phy-t25-l2', title:'Stellar evolution and the Hertzsprung–Russell diagram', html:'<h3>Stellar evolution and the Hertzsprung–Russell diagram</h3><p>The <strong>H–R diagram</strong> plots luminosity vs surface temperature (hot/blue on the left). <strong>Main sequence:</strong> hydrogen fusing to helium (Sun’s current state). Position depends on mass: massive stars are hotter, brighter, bluer — and die young.</p><p>Life cycles: Sun-like star → red giant (H exhausted, He fuses in shell) → planetary nebula → <strong>white dwarf</strong> (electron degeneracy supports it; Chandrasekhar limit 1.4 M<sub>☉</sub>). Massive star → red supergiant → <strong>supernova</strong> → <strong>neutron star</strong> or <strong>black hole</strong> (beyond ~3 M<sub>☉</sub>, nothing stops collapse).</p><p><strong>Stefan–Boltzmann law:</strong> L = 4πR<sup>2</sup>σT<sup>4</sup> — from a star’s luminosity and temperature you can find its radius. <strong>Wien’s displacement law:</strong> λ<sub>max</sub>T = 2.9×10<sup>-3</sup> m K gives temperature from colour.</p>'},
    {id:'al-phy-t25-l3', title:'The expanding universe and the Big Bang', html:'<h3>The expanding universe and the Big Bang</h3><p><strong>Hubble’s law:</strong> v = H<sub>0</sub>d — recession velocity proportional to distance (H<sub>0</sub> ≈ 70 km s<sup>-1</sup> Mpc<sup>-1</sup>). Measured via <strong>cosmological redshift</strong>: spectral lines shifted to longer wavelengths, z = Δλ/λ ≈ v/c for v ≪ c.</p><p>Evidence for the <strong>Big Bang</strong>: (1) Hubble expansion — galaxies receding; extrapolating back gives an age ~1/H<sub>0</sub> ≈ 14 billion years. (2) <strong>Cosmic microwave background</strong>: 2.7 K black-body radiation from all directions — the cooled remnant of the hot early universe. (3) Abundance of light elements (H, He) matches Big Bang nucleosynthesis.</p><p><strong>Dark matter</strong> (inferred from galaxy rotation curves — outer stars orbit too fast for visible mass) and <strong>dark energy</strong> (accelerating expansion, from distant supernovae) dominate the universe’s energy budget; ordinary matter is only ~5%.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'One parsec is defined as the distance at which', options:['light travels in one year','1 AU subtends 1 arcsecond','a star has zero parallax','the H–R diagram is calibrated'], answer:1, explain:'1 pc: parallax angle of 1 arcsecond for a baseline of 1 AU = 3.26 ly.'},
    {q:'Radiant flux F at distance d from a star of luminosity L is', options:['L/(4πd)','L/(4πd<sup>2</sup>)','Ld<sup>2</sup>/(4π)','4πL/d<sup>2</sup>'], answer:1, explain:'F = L/(4πd<sup>2</sup>): luminosity spread over a sphere — inverse-square law.'},
    {q:'On the H–R diagram, the hottest stars are on the', options:['right','left','top right','bottom'], answer:1, explain:'Temperature increases right → left; hot blue stars sit on the left.'},
    {q:'A Sun-like star ends its life as a', options:['neutron star','white dwarf','black hole','supernova remnant only'], answer:1, explain:'Below ~1.4 M<sub>☉</sub> core: planetary nebula → white dwarf.'},
    {q:'The Chandrasekhar limit (1.4 solar masses) is the maximum mass of a', options:['main-sequence star','white dwarf','neutron star','red giant'], answer:1, explain:'Above 1.4 M<sub>☉</sub>, electron degeneracy pressure cannot support a white dwarf.'},
    {q:'Stefan–Boltzmann law: L equals', options:['4πR<sup>2</sup>σT<sup>2</sup>','4πR<sup>2</sup>σT<sup>4</sup>','2πRσT<sup>4</sup>','σT<sup>4</sup>/R<sup>2</sup>'], answer:1, explain:'L = 4πR<sup>2</sup>σT<sup>4</sup>: luminosity from radius and surface temperature.'},
    {q:'Hubble’s law is', options:['v = H<sub>0</sub>/d','v = H<sub>0</sub>d','d = H<sub>0</sub>/v<sup>2</sup>','v = d<sup>2</sup>H<sub>0</sub>'], answer:1, explain:'Recession velocity proportional to distance: v = H<sub>0</sub>d.'},
    {q:'Which is NOT evidence for the Big Bang?', options:['Hubble expansion','cosmic microwave background','light-element abundances','stellar parallax'], answer:3, explain:'Parallax measures nearby distances; it is not cosmological evidence for the Big Bang.'},
    {q:'Dark matter is inferred mainly from', options:['black holes','galaxy rotation curves','the CMB temperature','solar neutrinos'], answer:1, explain:'Outer stars orbit too fast for the visible mass — extra unseen mass (dark matter) is needed.'},
    {q:'Wien’s displacement law relates', options:['luminosity and radius','peak wavelength and temperature','redshift and distance','mass and luminosity'], answer:1, explain:'λ<sub>max</sub>T = 2.9×10<sup>-3</sup> m K: hotter bodies peak at shorter wavelengths.'}
   ]}]});
/* ================= CAMBRIDGE A LEVEL CHEMISTRY (9701) ================= */
window.__saMergeCourse({id:'al-chemistry-9701', board:'alevel', title:'Chemistry (9701)', urdu:'', class:'A Level', icon:'🧪', desc:'Cambridge A Level Chemistry 9701 — full AS & A2 syllabus with lessons and quizzes.', chapters:[
  {id:'al-che-t1', title:'Atomic structure (AS)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-che-t1-l1', title:'Protons, neutrons and electrons', html:'<h3>Protons, neutrons and electrons</h3><p>Atoms consist of <strong>protons</strong> (+1, ~1 u) and <strong>neutrons</strong> (0, ~1 u) in a tiny nucleus, with <strong>electrons</strong> (−1, ~1/1836 u) in orbitals around it. <strong>Atomic (proton) number Z</strong> = number of protons; <strong>mass (nucleon) number A</strong> = protons + neutrons.</p><p><strong>Isotopes</strong> are atoms of the same element (same Z) with different numbers of neutrons (different A). E.g. <sup>12</sup>C, <sup>13</sup>C, <sup>14</sup>C. Isotopes have identical chemistry (same electron arrangement) but different masses and nuclear stability.</p><p><strong>Relative isotopic mass</strong> compares one isotope to 1/12 of <sup>12</sup>C. <strong>Relative atomic mass A<sub>r</sub></strong> is the weighted mean of isotopic masses: A<sub>r</sub> = Σ(isotopic mass × abundance)/100. Chlorine: (35 × 75 + 37 × 25)/100 = 35.5.</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t1-l2', title:'Electron configuration', html:'<h3>Electron configuration</h3><p>Electrons fill orbitals in order of increasing energy: 1s, 2s, 2p, 3s, 3p, 4s, 3d, 4p… Each orbital holds max 2 electrons (opposite spins). s holds 2, p holds 6, d holds 10.</p><p>Write configurations as e.g. Fe: 1s<sup>2</sup> 2s<sup>2</sup> 2p<sup>6</sup> 3s<sup>2</sup> 3p<sup>6</sup> 4s<sup>2</sup> 3d<sup>6</sup>, or [Ar] 4s<sup>2</sup> 3d<sup>6</sup>. Exceptions: <strong>Cr = [Ar] 4s<sup>1</sup> 3d<sup>5</sup></strong> and <strong>Cu = [Ar] 4s<sup>1</sup> 3d<sup>10</sup></strong> — half-filled and full d subshells are extra stable.</p><p>Electrons are removed from 4s before 3d when forming ions: Fe<sup>2+</sup> = [Ar] 3d<sup>6</sup> (lose the 4s electrons first). Shells n = 1–4 with subshell capacities: know the shapes — s spherical, p dumbbell (three orientations).</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t1-l3', title:'Mass spectrometry', html:'<h3>Mass spectrometry</h3><p>A <strong>mass spectrometer</strong> vaporises the sample, ionises it (electron bombardment knocks off an electron → +1 ions), accelerates the ions, deflects them in a magnetic field (lighter ions deflected more), and detects them. The <strong>mass spectrum</strong> plots abundance vs m/z.</p><p>Uses: (1) find A<sub>r</sub> from isotopic peaks and abundances; (2) identify molecules from the <strong>molecular ion peak</strong> M<sup>+</sup> (highest m/z, the whole molecule); (3) deduce structure from <strong>fragmentation</strong> peaks.</p><p>Worked example: a spectrum shows peaks at m/z 35 (75%) and 37 (25%) → A<sub>r</sub> = 35.5 → chlorine. For Br<sub>2</sub>, three molecular ion peaks at 158, 160, 162 in ratio 1:2:1 (<sup>79</sup>Br<sup>79</sup>Br, <sup>79</sup>Br<sup>81</sup>Br, <sup>81</sup>Br<sup>81</sup>Br).</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'The mass number of an atom is', options:['protons only','protons + neutrons','protons + electrons','neutrons only'], answer:1, explain:'A = number of nucleons = protons + neutrons.'},
    {q:'Isotopes of an element differ in', options:['proton number','electron number','neutron number','chemical properties'], answer:2, explain:'Isotopes: same Z (protons), different A (neutrons). Chemistry is identical.'},
    {q:'The electron configuration of chromium (Z = 24) is', options:['[Ar] 4s<sup>2</sup> 3d<sup>4</sup>','[Ar] 4s<sup>1</sup> 3d<sup>5</sup>','[Ar] 4s<sup>2</sup> 3d<sup>6</sup>','[Ar] 3d<sup>6</sup>'], answer:1, explain:'Cr is the exception: [Ar] 4s<sup>1</sup> 3d<sup>5</sup> (half-filled d subshell).'},
    {q:'Fe<sup>2+</sup> has electron configuration', options:['[Ar] 4s<sup>2</sup> 3d<sup>4</sup>','[Ar] 4s<sup>1</sup> 3d<sup>5</sup>','[Ar] 3d<sup>6</sup>','[Ar] 4s<sup>2</sup> 3d<sup>6</sup>'], answer:2, explain:'Fe = [Ar] 4s<sup>2</sup> 3d<sup>6</sup>; the 4s electrons are lost first → [Ar] 3d<sup>6</sup>.'},
    {q:'In a mass spectrometer, ions are deflected by', options:['an electric field only','a magnetic field','gravity','a laser'], answer:1, explain:'The magnetic field deflects moving ions; lighter (smaller m/z) ions deflect more.'},
    {q:'The molecular ion peak in a mass spectrum is', options:['the tallest peak','the peak at highest m/z','the first peak','always at m/z 12'], answer:1, explain:'M<sup>+</sup> is the intact molecule — the highest m/z peak (fragments are lighter).'},
    {q:'A sample has isotopes of mass 35 (75%) and 37 (25%). A<sub>r</sub> is', options:['36.0','35.5','35.0','37.0'], answer:1, explain:'(35×75 + 37×25)/100 = (2625 + 925)/100 = 35.5.'},
    {q:'How many electrons can a p subshell hold?', options:['2','6','10','14'], answer:1, explain:'Three p orbitals × 2 electrons = 6.'},
    {q:'The shape of a p orbital is', options:['spherical','dumbbell','cloverleaf','torus'], answer:1, explain:'p orbitals are dumbbell-shaped, oriented along x, y, z axes.'},
    {q:'Br<sub>2</sub> shows molecular ion peaks at 158, 160, 162 in ratio', options:['1:1:1','1:2:1','2:1:2','3:1:3'], answer:1, explain:'<sup>79</sup>Br<sup>79</sup>Br (158), <sup>79</sup>Br<sup>81</sup>Br (160, two combinations), <sup>81</sup>Br<sup>81</sup>Br (162) → 1:2:1.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-che-t2', title:'Atoms, molecules and stoichiometry (AS)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-che-t2-l1', title:'The mole and molar mass', html:'<h3>The mole and molar mass</h3><p>One <strong>mole</strong> contains the Avogadro constant N<sub>A</sub> = 6.02×10<sup>23</sup> particles. <strong>Molar mass</strong> M = mass of one mole (g mol<sup>-1</sup>). Moles n = m/M.</p><p>Worked example: moles in 4.9 g of H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> (M = 98 g mol<sup>-1</sup>): n = 4.9/98 = 0.050 mol. Number of molecules = 0.050 × 6.02×10<sup>23</sup> = 3.0×10<sup>22</sup>.</p><p><strong>Empirical formula</strong> = simplest whole-number ratio; <strong>molecular formula</strong> = actual numbers. Find empirical formula from % composition: divide each % by A<sub>r</sub>, then divide by the smallest to get the ratio.</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t2-l2', title:'Concentration and gas volumes', html:'<h3>Concentration and gas volumes</h3><p><strong>Concentration</strong> c = n/V in mol dm<sup>-3</sup>. Moles = c × V(dm<sup>3</sup>). Remember: 1 dm<sup>3</sup> = 1000 cm<sup>3</sup>, so divide cm<sup>3</sup> by 1000.</p><p><strong>Molar gas volume:</strong> at room temperature and pressure (r.t.p.: 20 °C, 1 atm) one mole of any gas occupies 24.0 dm<sup>3</sup>. Volume = n × 24.0 dm<sup>3</sup>.</p><p><strong>Titration calculations:</strong> work in moles. Example: 25.0 cm<sup>3</sup> of 0.100 mol dm<sup>-3</sup> NaOH neutralised by 20.0 cm<sup>3</sup> of HCl. Moles NaOH = 0.100 × 0.0250 = 0.00250 mol = moles HCl. [HCl] = 0.00250/0.0200 = 0.125 mol dm<sup>-3</sup>.</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t2-l3', title:'Limiting reactants and percentage yield', html:'<h3>Limiting reactants and percentage yield</h3><p>The <strong>limiting reactant</strong> is the reactant that runs out first, capping the product. Worked example: 4.0 g H<sub>2</sub> (2.0 mol) + 32 g O<sub>2</sub> (1.0 mol) → 2H<sub>2</sub> + O<sub>2</sub> → 2H<sub>2</sub>O. O<sub>2</sub> needs 2.0 mol H<sub>2</sub> — exactly enough; neither is limiting here. If only 1.0 mol H<sub>2</sub> were present, H<sub>2</sub> would limit: max H<sub>2</sub>O = 1.0 mol.</p><p><strong>Percentage yield</strong> = (actual yield/theoretical yield) × 100%. Yields < 100% because reactions may not go to completion, side reactions occur, or product is lost in separation.</p><p><strong>Atom economy</strong> = (mass of desired product/total mass of reactants) × 100%. High atom economy = greener. Addition reactions have 100% atom economy.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'The Avogadro constant is', options:['6.02×10<sup>23</sup> mol<sup>-1</sup>','1.60×10<sup>-19</sup> mol<sup>-1</sup>','8.31 mol<sup>-1</sup>','12.00 g mol<sup>-1</sup>'], answer:0, explain:'N<sub>A</sub> = 6.02×10<sup>23</sup> particles per mole.'},
    {q:'Moles in 8.0 g of NaOH (M = 40 g mol<sup>-1</sup>)', options:['0.20 mol','5.0 mol','320 mol','0.020 mol'], answer:0, explain:'n = m/M = 8.0/40 = 0.20 mol.'},
    {q:'Molar gas volume at r.t.p. is', options:['22.4 dm<sup>3</sup>','24.0 dm<sup>3</sup>','24.0 cm<sup>3</sup>','1.0 dm<sup>3</sup>'], answer:1, explain:'At r.t.p. (20 °C, 1 atm), 1 mol of gas = 24.0 dm<sup>3</sup>.'},
    {q:'Moles in 50 cm<sup>3</sup> of 2.0 mol dm<sup>-3</sup> solution', options:['100 mol','0.10 mol','1.0 mol','0.025 mol'], answer:1, explain:'n = cV = 2.0 × 0.050 = 0.10 mol (50 cm<sup>3</sup> = 0.050 dm<sup>3</sup>).'},
    {q:'The empirical formula of a compound with 40% C, 6.7% H, 53.3% O is', options:['CH<sub>2</sub>O','C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>4</sub>O<sub>2</sub>','CHO','C<sub>3</sub>H<sub>6</sub>O<sub>3</sub>'], answer:0, explain:'40/12 : 6.7/1 : 53.3/16 = 3.33 : 6.7 : 3.33 = 1:2:1 → CH<sub>2</sub>O.'},
    {q:'Percentage yield is', options:['actual/theoretical × 100%','theoretical/actual × 100%','actual × theoretical','always 100%'], answer:0, explain:'% yield = (actual yield ÷ theoretical maximum) × 100%.'},
    {q:'Atom economy of an addition reaction is', options:['0%','50%','100%','variable'], answer:2, explain:'All reactant atoms end up in the single product: 100% atom economy.'},
    {q:'In 2Mg + O<sub>2</sub> → 2MgO, 6.0 g Mg (0.25 mol) reacts with excess O<sub>2</sub>. Max mass of MgO is', options:['10 g','5.0 g','20 g','2.5 g'], answer:0, explain:'0.25 mol Mg → 0.25 mol MgO (M = 40) = 10 g.'},
    {q:'The limiting reactant is', options:['the most expensive reactant','the reactant used up first','the reactant in excess','the catalyst'], answer:1, explain:'It runs out first and limits the amount of product.'},
    {q:'A compound has empirical formula CH<sub>2</sub> and M<sub>r</sub> = 42. Its molecular formula is', options:['CH<sub>2</sub>','C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>4</sub>','C<sub>3</sub>H<sub>6</sub>','C<sub>4</sub>H<sub>8</sub>'], answer:2, explain:'CH<sub>2</sub> = 14; 42/14 = 3 → C<sub>3</sub>H<sub>6</sub>.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-che-t3', title:'Chemical bonding (AS)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-che-t3-l1', title:'Ionic and covalent bonding', html:'<h3>Ionic and covalent bonding</h3><p><strong>Ionic bonding:</strong> transfer of electrons from metal to non-metal, forming oppositely charged ions held by electrostatic attraction. E.g. Na → Na<sup>+</sup> + e<sup>−</sup>; Cl + e<sup>−</sup> → Cl<sup>−</sup>. Giant lattice structures: high melting points, conduct electricity when molten/aqueous (mobile ions) but not solid.</p><p><strong>Covalent bonding:</strong> sharing of electron pairs. <strong>Dative (coordinate) bonding:</strong> both shared electrons come from one atom — e.g. NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup> (N donates to H<sup>+</sup>), CO (C donates to O), AlCl<sub>3</sub> dimer.</p><p><strong>Metallic bonding:</strong> lattice of positive ions in a sea of delocalised electrons — explains conductivity, malleability and high melting points of metals.</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t3-l2', title:'Shapes of molecules', html:'<h3>Shapes of molecules</h3><p><strong>VSEPR:</strong> electron pairs repel to maximise separation. Count bonding + lone pairs around the central atom: 2 pairs → linear (180°); 3 → trigonal planar (120°); 4 → tetrahedral (109.5°); 5 → trigonal bipyramidal; 6 → octahedral (90°).</p><p>Lone pairs repel more strongly, squeezing bond angles: CH<sub>4</sub> 109.5° → NH<sub>3</sub> (1 lone pair) 107° pyramidal → H<sub>2</sub>O (2 lone pairs) 104.5° bent. BF<sub>3</sub> trigonal planar 120°; PCl<sub>5</sub> trigonal bipyramidal; SF<sub>6</sub> octahedral.</p><p>Exam reasoning: always state the number of bonding and lone pairs first, then name the shape, then the bond angle. E.g. CO<sub>2</sub>: 2 bonding pairs, 0 lone pairs → linear, 180°.</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t3-l3', title:'Intermolecular forces', html:'<h3>Intermolecular forces</h3><p><strong>London (dispersion) forces:</strong> present in ALL molecules — temporary dipoles induce dipoles in neighbours. Strength increases with number of electrons (M<sub>r</sub>). This is why boiling points rise down Group 17 and along the alkanes.</p><p><strong>Permanent dipole–dipole:</strong> between polar molecules (e.g. HCl). <strong>Hydrogen bonding:</strong> the strongest IMF — H bonded to N, O or F attracted to a lone pair on N/O/F of another molecule. Explains water’s high boiling point, ice floating (open lattice), and solubility of alcohols/ammonia.</p><p>Order of strength: hydrogen bonding > permanent dipole–dipole > London forces. All are far weaker than covalent/ionic bonds. Boiling breaks IMFs, not covalent bonds.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'Ionic bonding involves', options:['sharing electrons','transfer of electrons','delocalised electrons','overlapping orbitals'], answer:1, explain:'Electrons transfer from metal to non-metal, forming ions held by electrostatic attraction.'},
    {q:'A dative covalent bond differs because', options:['it is weaker','both shared electrons come from one atom','it forms only between metals','it has no direction'], answer:1, explain:'Dative: one atom donates both electrons of the shared pair (e.g. NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup>).'},
    {q:'The shape of NH<sub>3</sub> is', options:['tetrahedral','trigonal pyramidal','bent','linear'], answer:1, explain:'3 bonding pairs + 1 lone pair → trigonal pyramidal, 107°.'},
    {q:'The bond angle in CH<sub>4</sub> is', options:['107°','104.5°','109.5°','120°'], answer:2, explain:'4 bonding pairs, no lone pairs → tetrahedral 109.5°.'},
    {q:'SF<sub>6</sub> has shape', options:['tetrahedral','octahedral','trigonal bipyramidal','square planar'], answer:1, explain:'6 bonding pairs → octahedral, 90°.'},
    {q:'Hydrogen bonding occurs when H is bonded to', options:['C, H or O','N, O or F','any metal','Cl, Br or I'], answer:1, explain:'H attached to small, highly electronegative N, O or F, attracted to a lone pair on N/O/F.'},
    {q:'Boiling points rise down Group 17 mainly because of stronger', options:['covalent bonds','London forces','hydrogen bonds','ionic bonds'], answer:1, explain:'More electrons → stronger temporary dipoles → stronger London forces.'},
    {q:'Ice floats on water because', options:['ice is warmer','hydrogen bonding creates an open lattice with lower density','ice contains air','water contracts'], answer:1, explain:'H-bonds hold water molecules in an open hexagonal lattice in ice — less dense than liquid water.'},
    {q:'Which substance conducts electricity when molten but not solid?', options:['copper','sodium chloride','diamond','polythene'], answer:1, explain:'Ionic lattice: ions mobile when molten, fixed when solid.'},
    {q:'Metallic bonding best explains', options:['brittleness','electrical conductivity','low melting point','solubility in water'], answer:1, explain:'Delocalised electrons move freely — conductivity (and malleability).'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-che-t4', title:'States of matter (AS)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-che-t4-l1', title:'The gaseous state', html:'<h3>The gaseous state</h3><p>Gases: particles far apart, random motion, negligible IMFs (ideal). <strong>Ideal gas equation</strong> pV = nRT (p in Pa, V in m<sup>3</sup>, T in K, R = 8.31 J mol<sup>-1</sup> K<sup>-1</sup>).</p><p>Worked example: mass of 0.50 dm<sup>3</sup> of gas at 100 kPa and 300 K with M = 44 g mol<sup>-1</sup>. V = 5.0×10<sup>-4</sup> m<sup>3</sup>; n = pV/RT = (1.0×10<sup>5</sup> × 5.0×10<sup>-4</sup>)/(8.31 × 300) = 0.020 mol; m = 0.020 × 44 = 0.88 g.</p><p>Real gases deviate at high pressure (molecular volume significant) and low temperature (IMFs significant). <strong>Assumptions of kinetic theory:</strong> random motion, elastic collisions, negligible molecular volume, no IMFs, collision time negligible.</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t4-l2', title:'Liquids and solids', html:'<h3>Liquids and solids</h3><p>Liquids: particles close but disordered, can flow; IMFs significant. <strong>Vapour pressure</strong> rises with temperature; boiling occurs when vapour pressure = external pressure.</p><p>Solids: <strong>giant covalent</strong> (diamond, graphite, SiO<sub>2</sub> — very high mp), <strong>giant ionic</strong> (NaCl — high mp, brittle), <strong>giant metallic</strong>, and <strong>simple molecular</strong> (I<sub>2</sub>, ice — low mp; only weak IMFs between molecules).</p><p>Diamond vs graphite: diamond — each C bonded tetrahedrally to 4 others, hard, insulator. Graphite — layers of hexagonal rings (3 bonds each), delocalised electrons between layers → conducts, layers slide → soft/lubricant.</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t4-l3', title:'Lattice structures and properties', html:'<h3>Lattice structures and properties</h3><p><strong>Sodium chloride lattice:</strong> each Na<sup>+</sup> surrounded by 6 Cl<sup>−</sup> (6:6 coordination). Strong electrostatic forces → high mp (801 °C), brittle (shifting layers brings like charges together → repulsion).</p><p><strong>Predicting properties from structure:</strong> giant covalent/ionic/metallic → high mp; simple molecular → low mp. Conductivity: mobile charged particles needed — delocalised electrons (metals, graphite) or mobile ions (molten/aqueous ionic).</p><p>Exam reasoning: to explain a property, link structure → bonding → particle mobility. E.g. “diamond is hard because every carbon forms four strong covalent bonds in a rigid 3D network; it does not conduct because all electrons are localised in bonds.”</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'In the ideal gas equation, temperature must be in', options:['°C','K','°F','any unit'], answer:1, explain:'T must be absolute (kelvin) in pV = nRT.'},
    {q:'Which is NOT a kinetic theory assumption?', options:['random motion','elastic collisions','strong intermolecular forces','negligible molecular volume'], answer:2, explain:'Ideal gases assume NO intermolecular forces except during collisions.'},
    {q:'Real gases deviate most from ideal at', options:['high T, low p','low T, high p','high T, high p','low T, low p'], answer:1, explain:'Low T: IMFs matter; high p: molecular volume matters.'},
    {q:'Graphite conducts electricity because', options:['it has mobile ions','it has delocalised electrons between layers','it is a metal','it has free protons'], answer:1, explain:'Each carbon contributes a delocalised electron free to move along the layers.'},
    {q:'Diamond is hard because', options:['it has hydrogen bonds','each C forms 4 strong covalent bonds in a 3D network','it has ionic bonds','it is dense'], answer:1, explain:'The rigid tetrahedral covalent network resists deformation.'},
    {q:'NaCl is brittle because', options:['it is soft','shifting layers brings like charges together, causing repulsion','it dissolves','it has weak bonds'], answer:1, explain:'Displacing the lattice aligns + with + and − with −: repulsion shatters the crystal.'},
    {q:'Which has the lowest melting point?', options:['diamond','NaCl','iodine','magnesium'], answer:2, explain:'I<sub>2</sub> is simple molecular — only weak London forces between molecules.'},
    {q:'Boiling occurs when', options:['temperature reaches 100 °C','vapour pressure equals external pressure','all IMFs break','bubbles form'], answer:1, explain:'Boiling = vapour pressure matching external pressure (so 100 °C only at 1 atm).'},
    {q:'Coordination number of Na<sup>+</sup> in NaCl is', options:['4','6','8','12'], answer:1, explain:'Rock-salt structure: 6:6 coordination.'},
    {q:'Moles of gas in 12 dm<sup>3</sup> at r.t.p.', options:['0.50 mol','2.0 mol','12 mol','288 mol'], answer:0, explain:'n = V/24.0 = 12/24.0 = 0.50 mol.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-che-t5', title:'Chemical energetics (AS)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-che-t5-l1', title:'Enthalpy changes', html:'<h3>Enthalpy changes</h3><p><strong>Enthalpy change ΔH:</strong> heat energy change at constant pressure. <strong>Exothermic</strong> (ΔH negative): heat released — e.g. combustion. <strong>Endothermic</strong> (ΔH positive): heat absorbed — e.g. thermal decomposition.</p><p><strong>Standard enthalpy of combustion ΔH<sub>c</sub><sup>⊖</sup>:</strong> 1 mol of substance completely burned in oxygen under standard conditions (100 kPa, 298 K). <strong>Standard enthalpy of formation ΔH<sub>f</sub><sup>⊖</sup>:</strong> 1 mol of compound formed from its elements in standard states.</p><p>Reaction profiles: exothermic — products lower than reactants; endothermic — products higher. <strong>Activation energy E<sub>a</sub></strong> = energy needed to start the reaction (reach the transition state).</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t5-l2', title:'Hess’s law', html:'<h3>Hess’s law</h3><p><strong>Hess’s law:</strong> enthalpy change is independent of the route — ΔH depends only on initial and final states. Use enthalpy cycles with combustion or formation data.</p><p>From formation: ΔH<sub>r</sub><sup>⊖</sup> = ΣΔH<sub>f</sub><sup>⊖</sup>(products) − ΣΔH<sub>f</sub><sup>⊖</sup>(reactants). Worked example: CH<sub>4</sub> + 2O<sub>2</sub> → CO<sub>2</sub> + 2H<sub>2</sub>O. ΔH<sub>r</sub> = [−394 + 2(−286)] − [−75] = −966 + 75 = −891 kJ mol<sup>-1</sup>.</p><p>From combustion: ΔH<sub>r</sub><sup>⊖</sup> = ΣΔH<sub>c</sub><sup>⊖</sup>(reactants) − ΣΔH<sub>c</sub><sup>⊖</sup>(products) — note the reversed order. Draw the cycle with arrows; the sum around a closed loop is zero.</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t5-l3', title:'Bond enthalpies', html:'<h3>Bond enthalpies</h3><p><strong>Bond enthalpy:</strong> energy needed to break 1 mol of bonds in the gas phase (mean values). ΔH<sub>r</sub> ≈ Σ(bonds broken) − Σ(bonds formed). Breaking is endothermic (+), forming is exothermic (−).</p><p>Worked example: H<sub>2</sub> + Cl<sub>2</sub> → 2HCl. Broken: H–H (436) + Cl–Cl (242) = 678. Formed: 2 × H–Cl (2 × 431) = 862. ΔH = 678 − 862 = −184 kJ mol<sup>-1</sup>.</p><p>Limitations: mean bond enthalpies are averaged over many compounds, and the equation assumes all species are gases — so bond-enthalpy ΔH values are approximate. Hess’s law with formation/combustion data is more accurate.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'An exothermic reaction has', options:['ΔH > 0','ΔH < 0','ΔH = 0','no activation energy'], answer:1, explain:'Exothermic: heat released to surroundings → ΔH negative.'},
    {q:'Standard conditions for ΔH<sup>⊖</sup> are', options:['0 °C, 1 atm','298 K, 100 kPa','273 K, 100 kPa','25 °C, 1 Pa'], answer:1, explain:'Standard: 100 kPa, 298 K (25 °C), 1 mol dm<sup>-3</sup> for solutions.'},
    {q:'ΔH<sub>f</sub><sup>⊖</sup> of an element in its standard state is', options:['always negative','zero','always positive','undefined'], answer:1, explain:'Forming an element from itself involves no change: ΔH<sub>f</sub><sup>⊖</sup> = 0.'},
    {q:'Using formation data, ΔH<sub>r</sub><sup>⊖</sup> equals', options:['ΣΔH<sub>f</sub>(reactants) − ΣΔH<sub>f</sub>(products)','ΣΔH<sub>f</sub>(products) − ΣΔH<sub>f</sub>(reactants)','ΣΔH<sub>f</sub>(products) + ΣΔH<sub>f</sub>(reactants)','zero'], answer:1, explain:'Products minus reactants (formation cycle).'},
    {q:'Using combustion data, ΔH<sub>r</sub><sup>⊖</sup> equals', options:['ΣΔH<sub>c</sub>(products) − ΣΔH<sub>c</sub>(reactants)','ΣΔH<sub>c</sub>(reactants) − ΣΔH<sub>c</sub>(products)','the sum of both','zero'], answer:1, explain:'Reactants minus products for the combustion cycle — reversed vs formation.'},
    {q:'Bond enthalpy calculation: ΔH equals', options:['formed − broken','broken − formed','broken + formed','formed/broken'], answer:1, explain:'ΔH = Σ(bonds broken) − Σ(bonds formed).'},
    {q:'For H<sub>2</sub> + Cl<sub>2</sub> → 2HCl with bond enthalpies 436, 242, 431 kJ mol<sup>-1</sup>, ΔH is', options:['+184 kJ mol<sup>-1</sup>','−184 kJ mol<sup>-1</sup>','−92 kJ mol<sup>-1</sup>','+92 kJ mol<sup>-1</sup>'], answer:1, explain:'(436+242) − 2(431) = 678 − 862 = −184 kJ mol<sup>-1</sup>.'},
    {q:'Activation energy is', options:['the overall ΔH','the minimum energy to start reaction','the energy of products','always zero with a catalyst'], answer:1, explain:'E<sub>a</sub>: energy needed to reach the transition state; catalysts lower it but not to zero.'},
    {q:'Bond breaking is', options:['exothermic','endothermic','athermic','spontaneous'], answer:1, explain:'Breaking bonds requires energy input: endothermic (+).'},
    {q:'Why are bond-enthalpy ΔH values approximate?', options:['Hess’s law is wrong','mean bond enthalpies are averaged and assume gaseous species','bond enthalpies are exact','temperature is ignored'], answer:1, explain:'Mean values from many compounds + gas-phase assumption → approximate results.'}
   ]}
,
  {id:'al-che-t6', title:'Electrochemistry (AS)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-che-t6-l1', title:'Oxidation numbers', html:'<h3>Oxidation numbers</h3><p><strong>Oxidation number</strong> rules: uncombined elements = 0; Group 1 = +1; Group 2 = +2; O = −2 (except peroxides −1, OF<sub>2</sub> +2); H = +1 (except metal hydrides −1); F = −1; sum = charge of species.</p><p>Worked example: S in H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>: 2(+1) + x + 4(−2) = 0 → x = +6. Mn in MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>−</sup>: x + 4(−2) = −1 → x = +7. Cr in Cr<sub>2</sub>O<sub>7</sub><sup>2−</sup>: 2x − 14 = −2 → x = +6.</p><p><strong>Oxidation</strong> = increase in oxidation number (loss of electrons); <strong>reduction</strong> = decrease (gain of electrons). OILRIG. <strong>Disproportionation:</strong> the same element is both oxidised and reduced — e.g. Cl<sub>2</sub> + 2OH<sup>−</sup> → Cl<sup>−</sup> + ClO<sup>−</sup> + H<sub>2</sub>O (Cl: 0 → −1 and 0 → +1).</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t6-l2', title:'Redox half-equations', html:'<h3>Redox half-equations</h3><p>Balance redox equations in steps: (1) write oxidation numbers; (2) balance atoms of the element changing; (3) balance O with H<sub>2</sub>O, H with H<sup>+</sup>; (4) balance charge with electrons; (5) combine half-equations so electrons cancel.</p><p>Worked example: MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>−</sup> → Mn<sup>2+</sup>. Mn: +7 → +2 (gain 5e<sup>−</sup>). Add 4 H<sub>2</sub>O right for O; 8 H<sup>+</sup> left for H: MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>−</sup> + 8H<sup>+</sup> + 5e<sup>−</sup> → Mn<sup>2+</sup> + 4H<sub>2</sub>O. Charge check: −1 + 8 − 5 = +2 ✓.</p><p>With Fe<sup>2+</sup> → Fe<sup>3+</sup> + e<sup>−</sup> (×5): overall MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>−</sup> + 8H<sup>+</sup> + 5Fe<sup>2+</sup> → Mn<sup>2+</sup> + 4H<sub>2</sub>O + 5Fe<sup>3+</sup> — the basis of manganate(VII) titrations (self-indicating: purple → colourless).</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t6-l3', title:'Redox titrations', html:'<h3>Redox titrations</h3><p><strong>Manganate(VII) titrations:</strong> KMnO<sub>4</sub> oxidises Fe<sup>2+</sup> (or ethanedioate) in acid. No indicator needed — the purple MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>−</sup> decolourises; the end point is the first permanent pale pink.</p><p>Worked example: 25.0 cm<sup>3</sup> of Fe<sup>2+</sup> solution needs 20.0 cm<sup>3</sup> of 0.0200 mol dm<sup>-3</sup> MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>−</sup>. Moles MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>−</sup> = 0.0200 × 0.0200 = 4.00×10<sup>-4</sup>. Ratio 1:5 → moles Fe<sup>2+</sup> = 2.00×10<sup>-3</sup>. [Fe<sup>2+</sup>] = 2.00×10<sup>-3</sup>/0.0250 = 0.0800 mol dm<sup>-3</sup>.</p><p><strong>Iodine–thiosulfate titrations:</strong> I<sub>2</sub> + 2S<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub><sup>2−</sup> → 2I<sup>−</sup> + S<sub>4</sub>O<sub>6</sub><sup>2−</sup>. Starch indicator added near the end point (blue-black → colourless). Used to find oxidising agents (e.g. Cu<sup>2+</sup>) that liberate I<sub>2</sub> from KI.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'The oxidation number of S in SO<sub>4</sub><sup>2−</sup> is', options:['+4','+6','−2','+2'], answer:1, explain:'x + 4(−2) = −2 → x = +6.'},
    {q:'The oxidation number of Cr in Cr<sub>2</sub>O<sub>7</sub><sup>2−</sup> is', options:['+7','+6','+3','+12'], answer:1, explain:'2x − 14 = −2 → x = +6.'},
    {q:'Reduction is', options:['loss of electrons','gain of electrons','gain of oxygen','increase in oxidation number'], answer:1, explain:'Reduction = gain of electrons = decrease in oxidation number.'},
    {q:'Which is a disproportionation reaction?', options:['Zn + Cu<sup>2+</sup> → Zn<sup>2+</sup> + Cu','Cl<sub>2</sub> + 2OH<sup>−</sup> → Cl<sup>−</sup> + ClO<sup>−</sup> + H<sub>2</sub>O','H<sub>2</sub> + Cl<sub>2</sub> → 2HCl','NaOH + HCl → NaCl + H<sub>2</sub>O'], answer:1, explain:'Cl goes 0 → −1 and 0 → +1: simultaneously oxidised and reduced.'},
    {q:'The half-equation for MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>−</sup> → Mn<sup>2+</sup> in acid needs', options:['3 electrons','5 electrons','7 electrons','1 electron'], answer:1, explain:'Mn +7 → +2: gain of 5 electrons.'},
    {q:'In manganate(VII) titrations the end point is', options:['first permanent pink','blue-black colour','colourless solution','purple precipitate'], answer:0, explain:'Excess MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>−</sup> gives the first permanent pale pink — self-indicating.'},
    {q:'Starch indicator is used in', options:['acid–base titrations','iodine–thiosulfate titrations','manganate titrations','precipitation titrations'], answer:1, explain:'Starch forms a blue-black complex with iodine, sharpening the end point.'},
    {q:'The iodine–thiosulfate reaction ratio I<sub>2</sub> : S<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub><sup>2−</sup> is', options:['1:1','1:2','2:1','1:5'], answer:1, explain:'I<sub>2</sub> + 2S<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub><sup>2−</sup> → 2I<sup>−</sup> + S<sub>4</sub>O<sub>6</sub><sup>2−</sup>.'},
    {q:'Oxidation number of N in NO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup>', options:['+3','+5','−1','+1'], answer:1, explain:'x + 3(−2) = −1 → x = +5.'},
    {q:'Which species is the oxidising agent in Zn + Cu<sup>2+</sup> → Zn<sup>2+</sup> + Cu?', options:['Zn','Cu<sup>2+</sup>','Zn<sup>2+</sup>','Cu'], answer:1, explain:'Cu<sup>2+</sup> gains electrons (is reduced), so it oxidises Zn — the oxidising agent.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-che-t7', title:'Equilibria (AS)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-che-t7-l1', title:'Dynamic equilibrium', html:'<h3>Dynamic equilibrium</h3><p><strong>Dynamic equilibrium</strong> occurs in a closed system when forward and reverse reactions proceed at equal rates — concentrations stay constant, but reactions continue. Established only when the system is closed (no substances added/removed).</p><p>Features: (1) forward rate = reverse rate; (2) concentrations constant (not equal); (3) no net change observable; (4) requires a closed system. At the molecular level, particles constantly react in both directions.</p><p>Example: N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>4</sub>(g) ⇌ 2NO<sub>2</sub>(g) in a sealed flask — brown colour intensity becomes constant. Opening the flask destroys equilibrium (NO<sub>2</sub> escapes).</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t7-l2', title:'Le Chatelier’s principle', html:'<h3>Le Chatelier’s principle</h3><p><strong>Le Chatelier:</strong> if a change is applied to a system in equilibrium, the position shifts to oppose the change.</p><p><strong>Concentration:</strong> adding reactant shifts right (uses it up); removing product shifts right. <strong>Pressure</strong> (gases): increasing pressure shifts towards fewer moles of gas. N<sub>2</sub> + 3H<sub>2</sub> ⇌ 2NH<sub>3</sub>: higher pressure → right (4 mol → 2 mol).</p><p><strong>Temperature:</strong> increasing T shifts in the endothermic direction. For the exothermic Haber process, higher T shifts LEFT (less NH<sub>3</sub>) — the compromise 450 °C balances rate and yield. <strong>Catalysts</strong> do NOT shift position — they speed both directions equally, reaching equilibrium faster.</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t7-l3', title:'The equilibrium constant Kc', html:'<h3>The equilibrium constant Kc</h3><p><strong>Kc = [products]<sup>coeff</sup>/[reactants]<sup>coeff</sup></strong> using equilibrium concentrations. For aA + bB ⇌ cC + dD: Kc = [C]<sup>c</sup>[D]<sup>d</sup>/([A]<sup>a</sup>[B]<sup>b</sup>). Units depend on the expression (may be none).</p><p>Worked example: H<sub>2</sub> + I<sub>2</sub> ⇌ 2HI. At equilibrium [H<sub>2</sub>] = 0.10, [I<sub>2</sub>] = 0.10, [HI] = 0.80 mol dm<sup>-3</sup>: Kc = 0.80<sup>2</sup>/(0.10 × 0.10) = 64 (no units).</p><p>Kc is constant at fixed temperature only. Large Kc → equilibrium far right. ICE tables organise “initial, change, equilibrium” moles for calculations — convert to concentrations before substituting.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'Dynamic equilibrium requires', options:['equal concentrations','a closed system with equal forward and reverse rates','an open system','a catalyst'], answer:1, explain:'Closed system + forward rate = reverse rate; concentrations are constant but not equal.'},
    {q:'For N<sub>2</sub> + 3H<sub>2</sub> ⇌ 2NH<sub>3</sub>, increasing pressure shifts equilibrium', options:['left','right','nowhere','to completion'], answer:1, explain:'Higher pressure favours fewer gas moles: 4 → 2, so right.'},
    {q:'For an exothermic forward reaction, raising temperature shifts equilibrium', options:['right','left','nowhere','to completion'], answer:1, explain:'Higher T favours the endothermic direction — the reverse here — so left.'},
    {q:'A catalyst affects equilibrium by', options:['shifting it right','shifting it left','reaching it faster with no shift','changing Kc'], answer:2, explain:'Catalysts lower E<sub>a</sub> both ways equally: faster, same position, same Kc.'},
    {q:'Kc for aA + bB ⇌ cC + dD is', options:['[A]<sup>a</sup>[B]<sup>b</sup>/[C]<sup>c</sup>[D]<sup>d</sup>','[C]<sup>c</sup>[D]<sup>d</sup>/[A]<sup>a</sup>[B]<sup>b</sup>','[A][B]/[C][D]','[C] + [D] − [A] − [B]'], answer:1, explain:'Products over reactants, each raised to its stoichiometric coefficient.'},
    {q:'If [H<sub>2</sub>] = [I<sub>2</sub>] = 0.20 and [HI] = 1.60 mol dm<sup>-3</sup> at equilibrium, Kc is', options:['8','64','16','4'], answer:1, explain:'Kc = 1.60<sup>2</sup>/(0.20×0.20) = 2.56/0.04 = 64.'},
    {q:'A very large Kc means', options:['equilibrium lies far to the right','equilibrium lies far left','reaction is slow','Kc has no units'], answer:0, explain:'Large Kc: products dominate at equilibrium.'},
    {q:'Kc changes when', options:['pressure changes','temperature changes','a catalyst is added','concentration changes'], answer:1, explain:'Only temperature changes Kc; other changes shift position but Kc stays constant.'},
    {q:'Adding more reactant to an equilibrium mixture', options:['decreases Kc','shifts position right, Kc unchanged','shifts position left','stops the reaction'], answer:1, explain:'Position shifts to use up added reactant; Kc (T constant) is unchanged.'},
    {q:'In the Haber process the 450 °C compromise is chosen because', options:['it gives maximum yield','it balances reasonable rate with acceptable yield','ammonia decomposes below 450 °C','catalysts melt above it'], answer:1, explain:'Lower T gives better yield (exothermic) but too slow; 450 °C with an iron catalyst is the economic compromise.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-che-t8', title:'Reaction kinetics (AS)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-che-t8-l1', title:'Collision theory', html:'<h3>Collision theory</h3><p><strong>Collision theory:</strong> particles must collide with (1) energy ≥ <strong>activation energy E<sub>a</sub></strong> and (2) correct orientation. <strong>Boltzmann distribution:</strong> plots number of molecules vs energy; area under the curve = total molecules; only the area beyond E<sub>a</sub> can react.</p><p><strong>Temperature:</strong> higher T shifts the distribution right and flattens it — many more molecules exceed E<sub>a</sub> (area beyond E<sub>a</sub> grows disproportionately). A 10 K rise roughly doubles rate.</p><p><strong>Concentration/pressure:</strong> more particles per unit volume → more frequent collisions → faster rate. <strong>Surface area:</strong> smaller pieces → larger surface area → more collision sites. <strong>Catalyst:</strong> provides an alternative route of lower E<sub>a</sub> — more molecules can react at the same T.</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t8-l2', title:'Measuring rates', html:'<h3>Measuring rates</h3><p><strong>Rate = change in concentration (or mass/volume) per unit time.</strong> Methods: (1) gas collection in a syringe (volume vs time); (2) mass loss on a balance (CO<sub>2</sub> escaping); (3) titration of samples quenched at intervals; (4) colorimetry for coloured species.</p><p>From a concentration–time graph, rate = gradient of the tangent at that time. Initial rate (tangent at t = 0) avoids complications from changing concentrations.</p><p>Worked example: Mg + 2HCl → H<sub>2</sub>. Measuring H<sub>2</sub> volume every 10 s gives a curve levelling off when Mg is used up. Steeper initial gradient = faster rate (e.g. with higher [HCl]).</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t8-l3', title:'Catalysts', html:'<h3>Catalysts</h3><p>A <strong>catalyst</strong> increases rate without being used up, by providing an alternative pathway of lower activation energy. It does not change ΔH or the equilibrium position/yield.</p><p><strong>Homogeneous catalysts</strong> are in the same phase as reactants (e.g. Fe<sup>2+</sup> in S<sub>2</sub>O<sub>8</sub><sup>2−</sup>/I<sup>−</sup> reaction — via Fe<sup>3+</sup> intermediate). <strong>Heterogeneous catalysts</strong> are a different phase (e.g. Fe in Haber, Ni in hydrogenation, Pt/Rh in catalytic converters) — reaction occurs on the surface: adsorption → reaction → desorption.</p><p>On the Boltzmann curve, a catalyst shifts E<sub>a</sub> left (lower), so a larger area lies beyond it. <strong>Catalytic poisoning:</strong> impurities (e.g. sulfur, lead) block active sites permanently.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'For a successful collision, particles need', options:['low energy and any orientation','energy ≥ E<sub>a</sub> and correct orientation','high pressure only','a catalyst present'], answer:1, explain:'Collision theory: sufficient energy AND correct orientation.'},
    {q:'Raising temperature increases rate mainly because', options:['collisions are more violent only','more molecules exceed E<sub>a</sub>','E<sub>a</sub> decreases','concentration rises'], answer:1, explain:'The Boltzmann tail beyond E<sub>a</sub> grows disproportionately with T.'},
    {q:'A catalyst increases rate by', options:['raising E<sub>a</sub>','providing a lower-E<sub>a</sub> pathway','increasing ΔH','shifting equilibrium'], answer:1, explain:'Alternative route of lower activation energy; ΔH and equilibrium unchanged.'},
    {q:'Rate from a concentration–time graph is', options:['the area under the curve','the gradient of the tangent','the intercept','1/time'], answer:1, explain:'Rate = d[conc]/dt = gradient of the tangent at that time.'},
    {q:'The initial rate is preferred because', options:['it is always fastest','concentrations are known and complications avoided','it needs no graph','it equals the average rate'], answer:1, explain:'At t = 0 concentrations are known exactly and reverse reaction/product effects are negligible.'},
    {q:'Increasing surface area of a solid reactant', options:['lowers E<sub>a</sub>','increases collision frequency at the surface','changes ΔH','has no effect'], answer:1, explain:'More exposed particles → more frequent effective collisions.'},
    {q:'A homogeneous catalyst is', options:['a solid in a gas reaction','in the same phase as the reactants','always a transition metal','never used up chemically'], answer:1, explain:'Same phase (e.g. aqueous Fe<sup>2+</sup> catalysing aqueous ions).'},
    {q:'Catalytic poisoning is', options:['catalyst dissolving','impurities blocking active sites','catalyst melting','excess catalyst'], answer:1, explain:'Poisons (S, Pb, CO) adsorb strongly and permanently block heterogeneous active sites.'},
    {q:'On the Boltzmann distribution, the total area under the curve represents', options:['molecules with E > E<sub>a</sub>','total number of molecules','the rate','E<sub>a</sub>'], answer:1, explain:'Area = all molecules; the fraction beyond E<sub>a</sub> can react.'},
    {q:'For Mg + 2HCl, doubling [HCl] will', options:['double the total H<sub>2</sub> volume','increase the initial rate','decrease the rate','change E<sub>a</sub>'], answer:1, explain:'Higher concentration → more frequent collisions → faster initial rate (total H<sub>2</sub> limited by Mg).'}
   ]}
,
  {id:'al-che-t9', title:'The Periodic Table: chemical periodicity (AS)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-che-t9-l1', title:'Period 3 elements: structure and bonding', html:'<h3>Period 3 elements: structure and bonding</h3><p>Period 3: Na, Mg (giant metallic) → Al (giant metallic) → Si (giant covalent) → P<sub>4</sub>, S<sub>8</sub>, Cl<sub>2</sub>, Ar (simple molecular). This structural change drives the <strong>melting point trend</strong>: rises Na → Mg → Al (stronger metallic bonding, more electrons/charge) → Si highest (giant covalent) → falls sharply to P, S, Cl (weak IMFs) → Ar lowest.</p><p>Sulfur’s mp is higher than phosphorus’s because S<sub>8</sub> has more electrons than P<sub>4</sub> → stronger London forces. Chlorine is a gas (Cl<sub>2</sub> small), argon monatomic with the weakest IMFs.</p><p>Electrical conductivity: Na, Mg, Al conduct (delocalised electrons); Si is a <strong>semiconductor</strong> (conductivity rises with T/light — basis of electronics); P, S, Cl<sub>2</sub>, Ar are insulators.</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t9-l2', title:'Period 3 oxides', html:'<h3>Period 3 oxides</h3><p>Oxides: Na<sub>2</sub>O, MgO <strong>basic</strong> (react with acids; Na<sub>2</sub>O also with water → NaOH); Al<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub> <strong>amphoteric</strong> (reacts with acids AND bases); SiO<sub>2</sub> <strong>weakly acidic</strong> (reacts with hot concentrated NaOH); P<sub>4</sub>O<sub>10</sub>, SO<sub>2</sub>/SO<sub>3</sub> <strong>acidic</strong> (dissolve to give acids: H<sub>3</sub>PO<sub>4</sub>, H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>3</sub>/H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>).</p><p>Acid–base character changes from basic to acidic across the period as bonding changes from ionic to covalent and the oxide’s ability to accept/donate O<sup>2−</sup>/H<sup>+</sup> shifts.</p><p>Reactions to know: MgO + 2HCl → MgCl<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O; Al<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub> + 6HCl → 2AlCl<sub>3</sub> + 3H<sub>2</sub>O AND Al<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub> + 2NaOH + 3H<sub>2</sub>O → 2NaAl(OH)<sub>4</sub>; SO<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O → H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>3</sub>.</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t9-l3', title:'Chlorides and ionisation energy trends', html:'<h3>Chlorides and ionisation energy trends</h3><p>Period 3 chlorides: NaCl, MgCl<sub>2</sub> <strong>ionic</strong> — dissolve in water giving neutral solutions. AlCl<sub>3</sub>, SiCl<sub>4</sub>, PCl<sub>5</sub> <strong>covalent</strong> — hydrolyse in water giving <strong>acidic</strong> solutions (HCl fumes): SiCl<sub>4</sub> + 2H<sub>2</sub>O → SiO<sub>2</sub> + 4HCl.</p><p><strong>First ionisation energy</strong> generally rises across a period (greater nuclear charge, similar shielding, smaller radius) but with dips: Mg → Al (electron removed from higher-energy 3p vs 3s) and P → S (paired 3p electrons repel, easier to remove).</p><p>Down a group, IE falls (more shielding, larger radius despite higher nuclear charge). Successive IEs jump after the outer shell is emptied — revealing the shell structure and group number.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'Melting point is highest for silicon in Period 3 because', options:['it is metallic','it has a giant covalent structure','it has the most electrons','it forms SiO<sub>2</sub>'], answer:1, explain:'Giant covalent lattice: many strong C–C… Si–Si bonds must break.'},
    {q:'Which Period 3 element is a semiconductor?', options:['aluminium','silicon','phosphorus','sodium'], answer:1, explain:'Si: giant covalent with a small band gap — conductivity increases with temperature.'},
    {q:'Al<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub> is described as', options:['basic','acidic','amphoteric','neutral'], answer:2, explain:'Reacts with both acids and bases.'},
    {q:'SO<sub>2</sub> dissolves in water to give', options:['a neutral solution','an acidic solution','an alkaline solution','no reaction'], answer:1, explain:'SO<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O → H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>3</sub> (acidic).'},
    {q:'SiCl<sub>4</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O gives', options:['a neutral solution','white fumes of HCl (acidic)','a precipitate','no reaction'], answer:1, explain:'Covalent chloride hydrolyses: SiCl<sub>4</sub> + 2H<sub>2</sub>O → SiO<sub>2</sub> + 4HCl.'},
    {q:'First ionisation energy dips from Mg to Al because', options:['Al is larger','the electron is removed from a higher-energy 3p orbital','Al has fewer protons','shielding decreases'], answer:1, explain:'Al’s outer electron is in 3p (higher energy, more shielded) vs Mg’s 3s.'},
    {q:'First IE dips from P to S because', options:['S is smaller','paired 3p electrons repel, making removal easier','S has fewer shells','nuclear charge falls'], answer:1, explain:'Spin-pair repulsion in S’s paired 3p electrons outweighs the extra proton.'},
    {q:'Down a group, first ionisation energy', options:['rises','falls','stays constant','rises then falls'], answer:1, explain:'More shielding and greater radius dominate over increased nuclear charge.'},
    {q:'Na<sub>2</sub>O reacts with water to give', options:['an acidic solution','NaOH (alkaline)','hydrogen gas','no reaction'], answer:1, explain:'Na<sub>2</sub>O + H<sub>2</sub>O → 2NaOH — basic oxide.'},
    {q:'A large jump between the 2nd and 3rd ionisation energies indicates the element is in', options:['Group 1','Group 2','Group 3','Group 0'], answer:1, explain:'After removing 2 outer electrons, the next comes from a full inner shell (much harder) → Group 2.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-che-t10', title:'Group 2 (AS)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-che-t10-l1', title:'Reactions of Group 2 metals', html:'<h3>Reactions of Group 2 metals</h3><p>Group 2: Be, Mg, Ca, Sr, Ba. <strong>Reactivity increases down the group</strong> — outer electrons further from the nucleus and more shielded, so more easily lost (lower ionisation energy).</p><p>With oxygen: 2Mg + O<sub>2</sub> → 2MgO (bright white flame). With chlorine: Mg + Cl<sub>2</sub> → MgCl<sub>2</sub>. With dilute acids: Mg + 2HCl → MgCl<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub> (faster down the group).</p><p>With water: Mg reacts very slowly with cold water (Mg + 2H<sub>2</sub>O → Mg(OH)<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>) but burns in steam (Mg + H<sub>2</sub>O → MgO + H<sub>2</sub>); Ca reacts steadily with cold water; Ba reacts vigorously. Hydroxides formed are alkaline.</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t10-l2', title:'Solubility trends', html:'<h3>Solubility trends</h3><p><strong>Hydroxides:</strong> solubility INCREASES down Group 2 (Mg(OH)<sub>2</sub> sparingly soluble → Ba(OH)<sub>2</sub> soluble). <strong>Sulfates:</strong> solubility DECREASES down Group 2 (MgSO<sub>4</sub> soluble → BaSO<sub>4</sub> insoluble).</p><p>Explanation: dissolving = breaking lattice (endothermic lattice energy) + hydrating ions (exothermic hydration). Down the group both fall, but hydration enthalpy falls faster for hydroxides while lattice energy dominates for sulfates — know the qualitative argument, not numbers.</p><p>Test: Ba<sup>2+</sup> + SO<sub>4</sub><sup>2−</sup> → BaSO<sub>4</sub>(s) white precipitate — the test for sulfates (acidify with HCl first to remove carbonate/sulfite).</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t10-l3', title:'Uses: antacids, lime and BaSO<sub>4</sub>', html:'<h3>Uses: antacids, lime and BaSO<sub>4</sub></h3><p><strong>Mg(OH)<sub>2</sub> and CaCO<sub>3</sub></strong> are used as antacids/indigestion remedies: bases neutralise excess stomach HCl. Mg(OH)<sub>2</sub> + 2HCl → MgCl<sub>2</sub> + 2H<sub>2</sub>O.</p><p><strong>Calcium hydroxide (slaked lime)</strong> neutralises acidic soil; <strong>calcium oxide (quicklime)</strong> from thermal decomposition of limestone: CaCO<sub>3</sub> → CaO + CO<sub>2</sub>.</p><p><strong>Barium sulfate</strong> is given as a “barium meal” before X-rays: BaSO<sub>4</sub> is insoluble (so Ba<sup>2+</sup>, which is toxic, is not absorbed) yet opaque to X-rays, outlining the gut.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'Reactivity of Group 2 metals down the group', options:['decreases','increases','stays the same','increases then decreases'], answer:1, explain:'Lower ionisation energy down the group: outer electrons lost more easily.'},
    {q:'Mg + steam gives', options:['Mg(OH)<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>','MgO + H<sub>2</sub>','MgO + O<sub>2</sub>','no reaction'], answer:1, explain:'Mg + H<sub>2</sub>O(steam) → MgO + H<sub>2</sub> (burns with white light).'},
    {q:'Solubility of Group 2 hydroxides down the group', options:['decreases','increases','is constant','increases then decreases'], answer:1, explain:'Mg(OH)<sub>2</sub> sparingly soluble → Ba(OH)<sub>2</sub> soluble.'},
    {q:'Solubility of Group 2 sulfates down the group', options:['increases','decreases','is constant','increases then decreases'], answer:1, explain:'MgSO<sub>4</sub> soluble → BaSO<sub>4</sub> insoluble.'},
    {q:'The test for sulfate ions uses', options:['AgNO<sub>3</sub>','BaCl<sub>2</sub> acidified with HCl','NaOH','limewater'], answer:1, explain:'Ba<sup>2+</sup> + SO<sub>4</sub><sup>2−</sup> → white BaSO<sub>4</sub> precipitate; HCl removes carbonate interference.'},
    {q:'BaSO<sub>4</sub> is safe as a barium meal because', options:['barium is non-toxic','it is insoluble so Ba<sup>2+</sup> is not absorbed','it dissolves quickly','it is radioactive'], answer:1, explain:'Insoluble → passes through unabsorbed; Ba<sup>2+</sup> ions are toxic.'},
    {q:'CaCO<sub>3</sub> on strong heating gives', options:['CaO + CO','CaO + CO<sub>2</sub>','Ca + CO<sub>2</sub>','Ca(OH)<sub>2</sub>'], answer:1, explain:'Thermal decomposition: CaCO<sub>3</sub> → CaO + CO<sub>2</sub>.'},
    {q:'Which reacts most vigorously with cold water?', options:['Mg','Ca','Sr','Ba'], answer:3, explain:'Reactivity increases down the group: Ba most vigorous.'},
    {q:'Mg(OH)<sub>2</sub> is used as an antacid because', options:['it is acidic','it neutralises excess stomach acid','it is insoluble','it contains magnesium'], answer:1, explain:'Base + HCl → salt + water, relieving indigestion.'},
    {q:'Slaked lime used to neutralise acidic soil is', options:['CaO','Ca(OH)<sub>2</sub>','CaCO<sub>3</sub>','CaCl<sub>2</sub>'], answer:1, explain:'Calcium hydroxide — Ca(OH)<sub>2</sub>.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-che-t11', title:'Group 17 (AS)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-che-t11-l1', title:'Physical properties and volatility', html:'<h3>Physical properties and volatility</h3><p>Group 17: F, Cl, Br, I. Colours darken down the group: pale yellow-green Cl<sub>2</sub> gas → red-brown Br<sub>2</sub> liquid → grey-black I<sub>2</sub> solid (purple vapour).</p><p><strong>Volatility decreases down the group</strong> (boiling points rise): more electrons → stronger London forces between the diatomic molecules. F<sub>2</sub> and Cl<sub>2</sub> gases, Br<sub>2</sub> liquid, I<sub>2</sub> solid at room temperature.</p><p><strong>Oxidising power decreases down the group:</strong> Cl<sub>2</sub> > Br<sub>2</sub> > I<sub>2</sub>. Smaller atoms gain electrons more readily (higher electron affinity, less shielding). A more reactive halogen displaces a less reactive halide: Cl<sub>2</sub> + 2Br<sup>−</sup> → 2Cl<sup>−</sup> + Br<sub>2</sub> (solution turns orange).</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t11-l2', title:'Reactions of the halogens', html:'<h3>Reactions of the halogens</h3><p>With hydrogen: H<sub>2</sub> + X<sub>2</sub> → 2HX. Vigour decreases down the group (F<sub>2</sub> explosive even in the dark; I<sub>2</sub> needs heating). Thermal stability of HX also decreases: HI decomposes most readily.</p><p>With NaOH: Cl<sub>2</sub> + 2NaOH → NaCl + NaClO + H<sub>2</sub>O (cold, dilute — bleach); 3Cl<sub>2</sub> + 6NaOH → 5NaCl + NaClO<sub>3</sub> + 3H<sub>2</sub>O (hot, concentrated). Both are <strong>disproportionation</strong> (Cl: 0 → −1 and +1/+5).</p><p>Halide tests: add AgNO<sub>3</sub> → AgCl white, AgBr cream, AgI yellow precipitates. Then add NH<sub>3</sub>: AgCl dissolves in dilute NH<sub>3</sub>, AgBr in concentrated, AgI insoluble.</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t11-l3', title:'Reactions of halides and uses of chlorine', html:'<h3>Reactions of halides and uses of chlorine</h3><p>Concentrated H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> with solid halides: NaCl → HCl (white fumes, acid–base only); NaBr → HBr then oxidised to Br<sub>2</sub> (brown fumes) + SO<sub>2</sub>; NaI → HI oxidised to I<sub>2</sub> (purple fumes) + H<sub>2</sub>S + S. <strong>Reducing power: I<sup>−</sup> > Br<sup>−</sup> > Cl<sup>−</sup></strong> (larger ions lose electrons more easily).</p><p>Uses of chlorine: killing microbes in drinking water and swimming pools (Cl<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O ⇌ HCl + HClO; HClO kills bacteria), making bleach (NaClO), PVC and other organochlorines.</p><p>Benefits vs risks: chlorine prevents waterborne disease (huge benefit); risks include formation of chlorinated hydrocarbons and the toxicity of Cl<sub>2</sub> itself — dose control matters.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'At room temperature, bromine is a', options:['gas','liquid','solid','plasma'], answer:1, explain:'Br<sub>2</sub>: red-brown fuming liquid.'},
    {q:'Volatility of the halogens down the group', options:['increases','decreases','is constant','increases then decreases'], answer:1, explain:'Stronger London forces (more electrons) → higher boiling points → less volatile.'},
    {q:'The strongest oxidising halogen is', options:['iodine','bromine','chlorine','fluorine'], answer:3, explain:'Oxidising power: F<sub>2</sub> > Cl<sub>2</sub> > Br<sub>2</sub> > I<sub>2</sub>.'},
    {q:'Cl<sub>2</sub> + 2Br<sup>−</sup> → 2Cl<sup>−</sup> + Br<sub>2</sub> shows', options:['bromine is a stronger oxidiser','chlorine displaces bromide','no reaction','reduction of chlorine'], answer:1, explain:'The more reactive halogen (Cl<sub>2</sub>) oxidises/displaces the less reactive halide.'},
    {q:'AgBr precipitate is', options:['white','cream','yellow','black'], answer:1, explain:'AgCl white, AgBr cream, AgI yellow.'},
    {q:'AgCl dissolves in', options:['concentrated NH<sub>3</sub> only','dilute NH<sub>3</sub>','no ammonia','water'], answer:1, explain:'AgCl dissolves in dilute NH<sub>3</sub>; AgBr needs concentrated; AgI is insoluble.'},
    {q:'With conc. H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>, NaI gives purple fumes of', options:['HI','I<sub>2</sub>','H<sub>2</sub>S','SO<sub>2</sub>'], answer:1, explain:'I<sup>−</sup> is the strongest reductant: HI is oxidised to I<sub>2</sub> (purple).'},
    {q:'Chlorine kills microbes in water via', options:['Cl<sub>2</sub> directly','HClO formed with water','NaCl','ozone'], answer:1, explain:'Cl<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O ⇌ HCl + HClO; chloric(I) acid kills bacteria.'},
    {q:'Cl<sub>2</sub> + cold dilute NaOH gives', options:['NaClO<sub>3</sub>','NaCl + NaClO + H<sub>2</sub>O','only NaCl','no reaction'], answer:1, explain:'Cold dilute: bleach (NaClO); hot concentrated: chlorate(V).'},
    {q:'Reducing power of halides increases in the order', options:['I<sup>−</sup> > Br<sup>−</sup> > Cl<sup>−</sup>','Cl<sup>−</sup> > Br<sup>−</sup> > I<sup>−</sup>','Br<sup>−</sup> > I<sup>−</sup> > Cl<sup>−</sup>','all equal'], answer:0, explain:'Larger ions lose electrons more easily: I<sup>−</sup> strongest reductant.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-che-t12', title:'Nitrogen and sulfur (AS)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-che-t12-l1', title:'Nitrogen: ammonia and the Haber process', html:'<h3>Nitrogen: ammonia and the Haber process</h3><p>N<sub>2</sub> is unreactive due to the very strong N≡N triple bond (941 kJ mol<sup>-1</sup>). Ammonia NH<sub>3</sub> is made by the <strong>Haber process</strong>: N<sub>2</sub> + 3H<sub>2</sub> ⇌ 2NH<sub>3</sub>, ΔH negative.</p><p>Conditions: <strong>450 °C, 200 atm, iron catalyst</strong>. High pressure shifts right (4 → 2 gas moles); low temperature would give better yield (exothermic) but too slow — 450 °C is the compromise; iron catalyst speeds equilibrium attainment. NH<sub>3</sub> is liquefied and removed, shifting equilibrium right and allowing recycling of unreacted N<sub>2</sub>/H<sub>2</sub>.</p><p>Ammonia uses: fertilisers (ammonium nitrate, urea), nitric acid (Ostwald process), nylon. Environmental cost: fertiliser runoff → eutrophication.</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t12-l2', title:'Nitrogen oxides and pollution', html:'<h3>Nitrogen oxides and pollution</h3><p>NO forms at high temperatures (car engines, lightning): N<sub>2</sub> + O<sub>2</sub> → 2NO, then 2NO + O<sub>2</sub> → 2NO<sub>2</sub>. <strong>Acid rain:</strong> 2NO<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O → HNO<sub>2</sub> + HNO<sub>3</sub> — damages buildings, lakes and forests.</p><p><strong>Catalytic converters</strong> (Pt/Rh) remove pollutants: 2CO + 2NO → 2CO<sub>2</sub> + N<sub>2</sub>; also oxidise unburned hydrocarbons. They need unleaded petrol (lead poisons the catalyst).</p><p>NO<sub>x</sub> also contributes to photochemical smog. Remediation: lean-burn engines, catalytic converters, flue-gas treatment.</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t12-l3', title:'Sulfur and sulfuric acid', html:'<h3>Sulfur and sulfuric acid</h3><p>Sulfur burns: S + O<sub>2</sub> → SO<sub>2</sub>. <strong>Contact process</strong> for H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>: (1) 2SO<sub>2</sub> + O<sub>2</sub> ⇌ 2SO<sub>3</sub> (450 °C, 2 atm, V<sub>2</sub>O<sub>5</sub> catalyst); (2) SO<sub>3</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> → H<sub>2</sub>S<sub>2</sub>O<sub>7</sub> (oleum) — NOT dissolved directly in water (uncontrollable mist); (3) H<sub>2</sub>S<sub>2</sub>O<sub>7</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O → 2H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>.</p><p>450 °C compromise: forward reaction exothermic, so lower T gives better yield but slower rate; V<sub>2</sub>O<sub>5</sub> catalyst. Pressure ~2 atm (small effect, 3 → 2 moles).</p><p><strong>Acid rain from SO<sub>2</sub>:</strong> SO<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O → H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>3</sub>, oxidised to H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>. Reduced by flue-gas desulfurisation (CaCO<sub>3</sub> scrubbing) and low-sulfur fuels.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'N<sub>2</sub> is unreactive mainly because of', options:['its low mass','the very strong N≡N triple bond','its full outer shell','hydrogen bonding'], answer:1, explain:'N≡N bond enthalpy 941 kJ mol<sup>-1</sup>: huge activation barrier.'},
    {q:'Haber process conditions are', options:['200 °C, 1 atm, Pt','450 °C, 200 atm, Fe','1000 °C, 500 atm, Ni','25 °C, 1 atm, V<sub>2</sub>O<sub>5</sub>'], answer:1, explain:'450 °C (compromise), 200 atm (high pressure), iron catalyst.'},
    {q:'In the Haber process, unreacted N<sub>2</sub>/H<sub>2</sub> are', options:['vented','recycled','burned','stored'], answer:1, explain:'Recycled over the catalyst; NH<sub>3</sub> is condensed out to shift equilibrium right.'},
    {q:'Catalytic converters convert CO and NO into', options:['CO<sub>2</sub> and N<sub>2</sub>','C and NO<sub>2</sub>','CH<sub>4</sub> and N<sub>2</sub>','nothing'], answer:0, explain:'2CO + 2NO → 2CO<sub>2</sub> + N<sub>2</sub> (Pt/Rh catalyst).'},
    {q:'Acid rain from NO<sub>2</sub> forms', options:['H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>','HNO<sub>3</sub> (and HNO<sub>2</sub>)','HCl','H<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub>'], answer:1, explain:'2NO<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O → HNO<sub>2</sub> + HNO<sub>3</sub>.'},
    {q:'The catalyst in the Contact process is', options:['Fe','Pt','V<sub>2</sub>O<sub>5</sub>','Ni'], answer:2, explain:'Vanadium(V) oxide for 2SO<sub>2</sub> + O<sub>2</sub> ⇌ 2SO<sub>3</sub>.'},
    {q:'SO<sub>3</sub> is absorbed into concentrated H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> (not water) because', options:['water is expensive','direct absorption in water gives an uncontrollable mist','SO<sub>3</sub> is insoluble','it is faster'], answer:1, explain:'SO<sub>3</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O forms a mist that is hard to condense; oleum route is controllable.'},
    {q:'Oleum has formula', options:['H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>','H<sub>2</sub>S<sub>2</sub>O<sub>7</sub>','SO<sub>3</sub>','H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>3</sub>'], answer:1, explain:'H<sub>2</sub>S<sub>2</sub>O<sub>7</sub>: disulfuric acid, then diluted to H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>.'},
    {q:'Flue-gas desulfurisation typically uses', options:['NaOH spray','CaCO<sub>3</sub> (limestone)','activated carbon','electrostatic filters'], answer:1, explain:'CaCO<sub>3</sub> + SO<sub>2</sub> → CaSO<sub>3</sub>/CaSO<sub>4</sub> + CO<sub>2</sub>.'},
    {q:'Ammonia is mainly used for', options:['plastics','fertilisers','fuels','glass'], answer:1, explain:'Most NH<sub>3</sub> becomes ammonium nitrate/urea fertilisers.'}
   ]}
,
  {id:'al-che-t13', title:'An introduction to AS Level organic chemistry (AS)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-che-t13-l1', title:'Representing organic molecules', html:'<h3>Representing organic molecules</h3><p><strong>Display formula:</strong> every atom and bond shown. <strong>Structural formula:</strong> condensed groups, e.g. CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>OH. <strong>Skeletal formula:</strong> carbon skeleton as lines, H on carbons omitted (each vertex = C with enough H). <strong>Molecular formula:</strong> atom counts only.</p><p><strong>Functional group:</strong> the atom/group responsible for characteristic reactions — e.g. C=C (alkene), –OH (alcohol), C=O (carbonyl), –COOH (carboxylic acid), C–X (halogenoalkane).</p><p><strong>Homologous series:</strong> family with the same functional group and general formula, e.g. alkanes C<sub>n</sub>H<sub>2n+2</sub>, alkenes C<sub>n</sub>H<sub>2n</sub>, alcohols C<sub>n</sub>H<sub>2n+1</sub>OH. Each member differs by CH<sub>2</sub>; properties show trends.</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t13-l2', title:'Nomenclature', html:'<h3>Nomenclature</h3><p>IUPAC naming: find the longest chain containing the principal functional group → parent alkane; number to give substituents lowest locants; name substituents alphabetically (di-, tri- for repeats).</p><p>Examples: CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>CH(CH<sub>3</sub>)CH<sub>3</sub> = 2-methylbutane; CH<sub>3</sub>CH(OH)CH<sub>3</sub> = propan-2-ol; CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>CHO = propanal; CH<sub>3</sub>COCH<sub>3</sub> = propanone.</p><p>Exam reasoning: the suffix shows the principal group (–ol, –al, –one, –oic acid); prefixes show substituents (methyl, chloro, bromo). Number the chain so the functional group gets the lowest number.</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t13-l3', title:'Isomerism and reaction types', html:'<h3>Isomerism and reaction types</h3><p><strong>Structural isomerism:</strong> same molecular formula, different connectivity — chain (butane vs methylpropane), positional (butan-1-ol vs butan-2-ol), functional group (ethanol vs methoxymethane).</p><p><strong>Stereoisomerism — cis–trans (E/Z):</strong> restricted rotation about C=C; each C has two different groups. Cis: same groups on the same side; trans: opposite sides. Trans isomers are usually more stable (less steric hindrance) and have higher melting points.</p><p><strong>Reaction types:</strong> addition (alkenes), substitution (alkanes with Cl<sub>2</sub>/UV; halogenoalkanes), elimination (dehydration of alcohols), hydrolysis, oxidation, reduction. <strong>Homolytic fission:</strong> bond splits evenly → two radicals (·). <strong>Heterolytic fission:</strong> uneven → ions (nucleophile/electrophile attack).</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'A skeletal formula shows', options:['all atoms','carbon skeleton as lines with C–H omitted','only hydrogens','3D shape'], answer:1, explain:'Lines = C–C bonds; each vertex/end is a carbon with implicit hydrogens.'},
    {q:'The functional group of an alkene is', options:['–OH','C=C','C=O','C–X'], answer:1, explain:'The carbon–carbon double bond defines alkenes.’ reactivity.'},
    {q:'General formula of alkanes is', options:['C<sub>n</sub>H<sub>2n</sub>','C<sub>n</sub>H<sub>2n+2</sub>','C<sub>n</sub>H<sub>2n−2</sub>','C<sub>n</sub>H<sub>n</sub>'], answer:1, explain:'Alkanes: C<sub>n</sub>H<sub>2n+2</sub> (saturated).'},
    {q:'CH<sub>3</sub>CH(OH)CH<sub>3</sub> is named', options:['propan-1-ol','propan-2-ol','ethanol','propanone'], answer:1, explain:'OH on carbon 2 of propane → propan-2-ol.'},
    {q:'Butane and methylpropane are', options:['stereoisomers','chain isomers','identical','functional group isomers'], answer:1, explain:'Same formula C<sub>4</sub>H<sub>10</sub>, different carbon skeleton: chain isomerism.'},
    {q:'Cis–trans isomerism requires', options:['a single bond','a C=C with two different groups on each carbon','any double bond','a ring only'], answer:1, explain:'Restricted rotation + two different substituents on each C of the double bond.'},
    {q:'Homolytic fission produces', options:['ions','free radicals','protons','neutrons'], answer:1, explain:'Even splitting: one electron to each fragment → two radicals.'},
    {q:'A nucleophile is', options:['electron-deficient','electron-rich (donates a pair)','a radical','positively charged'], answer:1, explain:'Nucleophiles donate an electron pair to electron-deficient centres.'},
    {q:'Ethanol and methoxymethane (same formula C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>6</sub>O) are', options:['chain isomers','functional group isomers','stereoisomers','identical'], answer:1, explain:'Different functional groups (–OH vs ether): functional group isomerism.'},
    {q:'An electrophile is attracted to', options:['nuclei','electron-rich centres','protons','neutrons'], answer:1, explain:'Electrophiles accept electron pairs — they attack electron-rich sites (e.g. C=C).'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-che-t14', title:'Hydrocarbons (AS)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-che-t14-l1', title:'Alkanes: properties and combustion', html:'<h3>Alkanes: properties and combustion</h3><p><strong>Alkanes</strong> C<sub>n</sub>H<sub>2n+2</sub>: saturated, tetrahedral carbons, σ bonds only. Unreactive (strong C–C and C–H bonds, non-polar) except <strong>combustion</strong> and <strong>free-radical substitution</strong>.</p><p>Complete combustion: CH<sub>4</sub> + 2O<sub>2</sub> → CO<sub>2</sub> + 2H<sub>2</sub>O. Incomplete: 2CH<sub>4</sub> + 3O<sub>2</sub> → 2CO + 4H<sub>2</sub>O (CO is toxic — binds haemoglobin). Trends: boiling point rises with chain length (stronger London forces); branching lowers boiling point (less surface contact).</p><p>Alkanes are obtained from <strong>fractional distillation of crude oil</strong> and <strong>cracking</strong> (breaking long alkanes: thermal or catalytic) to make petrol-range alkanes and alkenes.</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t14-l2', title:'Free-radical substitution', html:'<h3>Free-radical substitution</h3><p>CH<sub>4</sub> + Cl<sub>2</sub> → CH<sub>3</sub>Cl + HCl (UV light). <strong>Initiation:</strong> Cl<sub>2</sub> → 2Cl· (homolytic fission). <strong>Propagation:</strong> Cl· + CH<sub>4</sub> → HCl + CH<sub>3</sub>·; CH<sub>3</sub>· + Cl<sub>2</sub> → CH<sub>3</sub>Cl + Cl·. <strong>Termination:</strong> two radicals combine (Cl· + Cl· → Cl<sub>2</sub>; CH<sub>3</sub>· + CH<sub>3</sub>· → C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>6</sub>).</p><p>Further substitution gives CH<sub>2</sub>Cl<sub>2</sub>, CHCl<sub>3</sub>, CCl<sub>4</sub> — a mixture, hard to control. Limitation: multiple products; use excess alkane to favour mono-substitution.</p><p>Exam reasoning: radicals shown with a dot (·); each propagation step consumes one radical and makes one new radical — the chain continues. Termination removes radicals.</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t14-l3', title:'Alkenes: electrophilic addition', html:'<h3>Alkenes: electrophilic addition</h3><p><strong>Alkenes</strong> C<sub>n</sub>H<sub>2n</sub>: the C=C π bond is electron-rich and exposed → attacked by <strong>electrophiles</strong>. <strong>Electrophilic addition</strong> mechanism: π bond donates to E<sup>+</sup> → carbocation intermediate → nucleophile attacks.</p><p>Reactions: + H<sub>2</sub>/Ni → alkane (hydrogenation); + X<sub>2</sub> → dihalogenoalkane (Br<sub>2</sub> decolourised — test for C=C); + HX → halogenoalkane; + H<sub>2</sub>O/H<sub>3</sub>PO<sub>4</sub>, 300 °C, 60 atm → alcohol (e.g. ethene → ethanol).</p><p><strong>Markovnikov’s rule:</strong> with unsymmetrical alkenes/HX, H adds to the carbon with more H’s already — via the more stable carbocation (tertiary > secondary > primary, due to positive inductive effect of alkyl groups).</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'Alkanes are unreactive because', options:['they are gases','C–C and C–H bonds are strong and non-polar','they have double bonds','they dissolve in water'], answer:1, explain:'Strong, non-polar σ bonds with no polarised site for attack.'},
    {q:'Incomplete combustion of methane can produce', options:['only CO<sub>2</sub>','CO (toxic)','O<sub>2</sub>','H<sub>2</sub>'], answer:1, explain:'Limited O<sub>2</sub>: 2CH<sub>4</sub> + 3O<sub>2</sub> → 2CO + 4H<sub>2</sub>O; CO binds haemoglobin.'},
    {q:'Branching in alkanes', options:['raises boiling point','lowers boiling point','has no effect','raises melting point only'], answer:1, explain:'Branched molecules have less surface contact → weaker London forces → lower bp.'},
    {q:'Initiation in CH<sub>4</sub> + Cl<sub>2</sub> is', options:['Cl· + CH<sub>4</sub> → HCl + CH<sub>3</sub>·','Cl<sub>2</sub> → 2Cl· (UV)','CH<sub>3</sub>· + Cl<sub>2</sub> → CH<sub>3</sub>Cl + Cl·','2Cl· → Cl<sub>2</sub>'], answer:1, explain:'UV homolytically splits Cl<sub>2</sub> into two chlorine radicals.'},
    {q:'A propagation step must', options:['consume two radicals','produce a new radical','need UV light','form only HCl'], answer:1, explain:'Propagation: radical + molecule → product + new radical — the chain continues.'},
    {q:'The test for an alkene uses', options:['limewater','bromine water (decolourised)','Tollens’ reagent','2,4-DNPH'], answer:1, explain:'Orange Br<sub>2</sub> → colourless on addition across C=C.'},
    {q:'Electrophilic addition to alkenes starts with', options:['nucleophilic attack','π bond attacking an electrophile','radical formation','carbocation rearrangement'], answer:1, explain:'The electron-rich π bond donates to E<sup>+</sup>, forming a carbocation.'},
    {q:'Markovnikov’s rule: HBr + propene gives mainly', options:['1-bromopropane','2-bromopropane','propane','propene dimer'], answer:1, explain:'H adds to C1 (more H’s); Br<sup>−</sup> attacks the more stable secondary carbocation → 2-bromopropane.'},
    {q:'Carbocation stability order is', options:['primary > secondary > tertiary','tertiary > secondary > primary','all equal','methyl > tertiary'], answer:1, explain:'Alkyl groups donate electron density (positive inductive effect), stabilising the positive charge.'},
    {q:'Ethene to ethanol industrially uses', options:['H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> cold','H<sub>3</sub>PO<sub>4</sub> catalyst, 300 °C, 60 atm','Ni, 25 °C','UV light'], answer:1, explain:'Steam + ethene over phosphoric acid catalyst (hydration).'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-che-t15', title:'Halogen compounds (AS)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-che-t15-l1', title:'Nucleophilic substitution', html:'<h3>Nucleophilic substitution</h3><p>Halogenoalkanes: C–X bond is polar (C<sup>δ+</sup>–X<sup>δ−</sup>) → attacked by <strong>nucleophiles</strong>. <strong>Hydrolysis:</strong> R–X + OH<sup>−</sup> → R–OH + X<sup>−</sup> (warm aqueous NaOH).</p><p>Reactivity: R–I > R–Br > R–Cl (C–I weakest bond, easiest to break). Test: warm with NaOH, acidify with HNO<sub>3</sub>, add AgNO<sub>3</sub> → AgX precipitate (white/cream/yellow). Acidification removes OH<sup>−</sup> that would precipitate Ag<sub>2</sub>O.</p><p>Mechanism (S<sub>N</sub>2 for primary): OH<sup>−</sup> attacks C from the side opposite C–X as X<sup>−</sup> leaves — one step, <strong>inversion of configuration</strong>.</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t15-l2', title:'Elimination vs substitution', html:'<h3>Elimination vs substitution</h3><p><strong>Elimination:</strong> R–CH<sub>2</sub>–CH<sub>2</sub>X + KOH (ethanolic, heat) → alkene + KX + H<sub>2</sub>O. Ethanolic KOH favours elimination; aqueous NaOH favours substitution. Saytzeff: the more substituted alkene predominates.</p><p>Worked example: 2-bromobutane + hot ethanolic KOH → mainly but-2-ene (more substituted) + some but-1-ene.</p><p>Exam reasoning: identify conditions first — “ethanolic KOH, heat” = elimination to alkene; “aqueous NaOH, warm” = substitution to alcohol. Both need the halogenoalkane; the solvent chooses the pathway.</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t15-l3', title:'Uses and environmental issues', html:'<h3>Uses and environmental issues</h3><p>Uses: solvents, refrigerants, flame retardants, pharmaceuticals, PTFE (non-stick). <strong>CFCs</strong> (e.g. CCl<sub>2</sub>F<sub>2</sub>) were ideal refrigerants/aerosol propellants — non-toxic, non-flammable.</p><p>Problem: CFCs diffuse to the stratosphere where UV releases Cl· radicals: Cl· + O<sub>3</sub> → ClO· + O<sub>2</sub>; ClO· + O → Cl· + O<sub>2</sub> — <strong>catalytic ozone destruction</strong> (one Cl· destroys thousands of O<sub>3</sub>). The <strong>Montreal Protocol</strong> phased out CFCs; replacements: HCFCs, HFCs (no Cl).</p><p>Green chemistry: develop alternatives, avoid persistent organic pollutants; halogenoalkanes’ toxicity and persistence drive regulation.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'Nucleophilic substitution of R–X is fastest for', options:['R–Cl','R–Br','R–I','all equal'], answer:2, explain:'C–I is the weakest bond → easiest to break: R–I > R–Br > R–Cl.'},
    {q:'The test for a halogenoalkane uses NaOH then', options:['AgNO<sub>3</sub> directly','HNO<sub>3</sub> then AgNO<sub>3</sub>','HCl then AgNO<sub>3</sub>','litmus'], answer:1, explain:'Acidify with HNO<sub>3</sub> (removes OH<sup>−</sup>) then AgNO<sub>3</sub> → AgX precipitate.'},
    {q:'Ethanolic KOH with a halogenoalkane gives', options:['an alcohol','an alkene','an alkane','a ketone'], answer:1, explain:'Ethanolic KOH, heat → elimination of HX → alkene.'},
    {q:'Aqueous NaOH with a halogenoalkane gives', options:['an alkene','an alcohol','an alkane','a carboxylic acid'], answer:1, explain:'Aqueous conditions → nucleophilic substitution → alcohol.'},
    {q:'2-bromobutane + ethanolic KOH gives mainly', options:['but-1-ene','but-2-ene','butane','butan-2-ol'], answer:1, explain:'Saytzeff: the more substituted alkene (but-2-ene) predominates.'},
    {q:'CFCs destroy ozone because', options:['they block sunlight','UV releases Cl· which catalytically destroys O<sub>3</sub>','they are acidic','they freeze the stratosphere'], answer:1, explain:'Cl· + O<sub>3</sub> → ClO· + O<sub>2</sub>, regenerated in a catalytic cycle.'},
    {q:'The Montreal Protocol addressed', options:['acid rain','CFCs and ozone depletion','global warming','plastic waste'], answer:1, explain:'International treaty phasing out ozone-depleting CFCs.'},
    {q:'In S<sub>N</sub>2, the nucleophile attacks', options:['from the same side as X','from the opposite side to X','the halogen atom','a hydrogen'], answer:1, explain:'Backside attack as X<sup>−</sup> leaves → inversion of configuration.'},
    {q:'Why must the NaOH be acidified before AgNO<sub>3</sub> in the halogenoalkane test?', options:['to speed the reaction','to prevent Ag<sub>2</sub>O precipitating with OH<sup>−</sup>','to dissolve AgX','it is not needed'], answer:1, explain:'OH<sup>−</sup> would give brown Ag<sub>2</sub>O, masking the halide result.'},
    {q:'PTFE (Teflon) is made from', options:['chloroethene','tetrafluoroethene','bromoethane','dichloromethane'], answer:1, explain:'Polymerisation of CF<sub>2</sub>=CF<sub>2</sup> gives poly(tetrafluoroethene).'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-che-t16', title:'Hydroxy compounds (AS)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-che-t16-l1', title:'Alcohols: structure and preparation', html:'<h3>Alcohols: structure and preparation</h3><p><strong>Alcohols</strong> contain –OH. Classification: <strong>primary</strong> (C–OH carbon attached to 1 other C), <strong>secondary</strong> (2), <strong>tertiary</strong> (3). E.g. butan-1-ol (1°), butan-2-ol (2°), 2-methylpropan-2-ol (3°).</p><p>Preparation: (1) <strong>hydration of ethene</strong> (H<sub>3</sub>PO<sub>4</sub>, 300 °C, 60 atm); (2) <strong>fermentation</strong> of glucose (yeast, ~37 °C, anaerobic): C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>12</sub>O<sub>6</sub> → 2C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH + 2CO<sub>2</sub>; (3) <strong>nucleophilic substitution</strong> of halogenoalkanes with aqueous NaOH.</p><p>Fermentation is batch, renewable, but slow and gives dilute (~15%) ethanol; hydration is continuous, fast, from non-renewable ethene. Compare sustainability in exam answers.</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t16-l2', title:'Reactions of alcohols', html:'<h3>Reactions of alcohols</h3><p><strong>Combustion:</strong> C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH + 3O<sub>2</sub> → 2CO<sub>2</sub> + 3H<sub>2</sub>O (clean fuel). <strong>Substitution:</strong> with Na → alkoxide + H<sub>2</sub> (2C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH + 2Na → 2C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>ONa + H<sub>2</sub>); with PCl<sub>5</sub> → chloroalkane (steamy HCl fumes); with conc. H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> → chloroalkane.</p><p><strong>Dehydration (elimination):</strong> conc. H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> or Al<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub>, heat → alkene + H<sub>2</sub>O. Saytzeff applies: butan-2-ol → mainly but-2-ene.</p><p><strong>Oxidation</strong> (acidified K<sub>2</sub>Cr<sub>2</sub>O<sub>7</sub>, heat): primary → aldehyde (distil off) → carboxylic acid (reflux); secondary → ketone; tertiary → no reaction (resists oxidation). Orange → green colour change.</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t16-l3', title:'Phenol', html:'<h3>Phenol</h3><p><strong>Phenol</strong> C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH: –OH directly on a benzene ring. Weakly acidic (pK<sub>a</sub> ~10): reacts with NaOH → sodium phenoxide + H<sub>2</sub>O, and with Na → H<sub>2</sub>, but NOT with Na<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub> (weaker acid than carbonic acid) — unlike carboxylic acids.</p><p>The ring’s π system delocalises the O lone pair, weakening the O–H bond (more acidic than alcohols) but deactivating the ring less… actually the –OH <strong>activates</strong> the ring: phenol undergoes <strong>bromination</strong> with Br<sub>2</sub>(aq) instantly at room temperature → white precipitate of 2,4,6-tribromophenol (test for phenol).</p><p>Nitration with conc. HNO<sub>3</sub> gives 2,4,6-trinitrophenol (picric acid). Phenol is used in disinfectants, resins and pharmaceuticals; it is toxic/corrosive — handle with care.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'2-methylpropan-2-ol is a', options:['primary alcohol','secondary alcohol','tertiary alcohol','phenol'], answer:2, explain:'The C–OH carbon is attached to 3 other carbons: tertiary.'},
    {q:'Fermentation of glucose is carried out', options:['aerobically at 100 °C','anaerobically with yeast at ~37 °C','with H<sub>3</sub>PO<sub>4</sub>','by distillation'], answer:1, explain:'Yeast enzymes work anaerobically near 37 °C; ethanol is toxic to yeast above ~15%.'},
    {q:'Acidified K<sub>2</sub>Cr<sub>2</sub>O<sub>7</sub> with a primary alcohol under reflux gives', options:['an aldehyde','a carboxylic acid','a ketone','no reaction'], answer:1, explain:'Reflux oxidises fully: 1° alcohol → aldehyde → carboxylic acid.'},
    {q:'The oxidising agent turning orange to green indicates', options:['an alkene','an oxidisable alcohol (1° or 2°)','a tertiary alcohol','a phenol'], answer:1, explain:'Cr<sub>2</sub>O<sub>7</sub><sup>2−</sup> (orange) → Cr<sup>3+</sup> (green) when it oxidises 1°/2° alcohols.'},
    {q:'Dehydration of butan-2-ol gives mainly', options:['but-1-ene','but-2-ene','butane','butanone'], answer:1, explain:'Saytzeff: the more substituted alkene predominates.'},
    {q:'Phenol reacts with NaOH but not Na<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub> because', options:['it is neutral','it is a weaker acid than carbonic acid','it is insoluble','it is a base'], answer:1, explain:'Only acids stronger than H<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub> liberate CO<sub>2</sub>; phenol (pK<sub>a</sub> ~10) is weaker.'},
    {q:'The test for phenol uses bromine water giving', options:['an orange solution','a white precipitate of 2,4,6-tribromophenol','a purple solution','no change'], answer:1, explain:'Activated ring brominates instantly at room temperature → white ppt.'},
    {q:'Ethanol + Na gives', options:['sodium ethanoate','sodium ethoxide + H<sub>2</sub>','ethane','no reaction'], answer:1, explain:'2C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH + 2Na → 2C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>ONa + H<sub>2</sub>.'},
    {q:'A tertiary alcohol with acidified dichromate', options:['gives a ketone','gives no reaction (stays orange)','gives an acid','dehydrates'], answer:1, explain:'3° alcohols resist oxidation: no C–H on the C–OH carbon to remove.'},
    {q:'To obtain ethanal (not ethanoic acid) from ethanol, you should', options:['reflux','distil off the aldehyde as it forms','use cold dilute oxidant','add water'], answer:1, explain:'Distillation removes volatile ethanal before further oxidation to the acid.'}
   ]}
,
  {id:'al-che-t17', title:'Carbonyl compounds (AS)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-che-t17-l1', title:'Aldehydes and ketones', html:'<h3>Aldehydes and ketones</h3><p><strong>Aldehydes</strong> RCHO (C=O at the end of a chain, –al suffix); <strong>ketones</strong> RCOR′ (C=O in the middle, –one). The C=O bond is polar (C<sup>δ+</sup>), so carbonyls undergo <strong>nucleophilic addition</strong>.</p><p>Preparation: oxidation of 1° alcohols (distillation) → aldehydes; 2° alcohols → ketones. Reduction: NaBH<sub>4</sub> reduces aldehydes → 1° alcohols, ketones → 2° alcohols.</p><p>Reactivity difference: aldehydes are easily oxidised (to acids); ketones resist oxidation — the basis of distinguishing tests.</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t17-l2', title:'Nucleophilic addition: HCN', html:'<h3>Nucleophilic addition: HCN</h3><p>HCN (+ trace NaCN/NaOH catalyst) adds to carbonyls → <strong>hydroxynitriles</strong>. Mechanism: CN<sup>−</sup> attacks C<sup>δ+</sup> → alkoxide intermediate → protonated by HCN: R<sub>2</sub>C=O + HCN → R<sub>2</sub>C(OH)(CN).</p><p>The CN<sup>−</sup> catalyst is regenerated. Product has one more carbon — useful in synthesis (hydroxynitrile → hydrolyse to hydroxy acid; reduce to amino alcohol).</p><p>Worked example: ethanal + HCN → 2-hydroxypropanenitrile, CH<sub>3</sub>CH(OH)CN. The product is chiral (racemic mixture — attack from either face equally likely).</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t17-l3', title:'Tests: Tollens’, Fehling’s and 2,4-DNPH', html:'<h3>Tests: Tollens’, Fehling’s and 2,4-DNPH</h3><p><strong>2,4-DNPH:</strong> orange solution + carbonyl → orange/yellow precipitate of 2,4-dinitrophenylhydrazone — confirms a carbonyl group (both aldehydes and ketones). Melting point of the derivative identifies the specific carbonyl.</p><p><strong>Tollens’ reagent</strong> [Ag(NH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>]<sup>+</sup>: aldehyde → silver mirror (Ag<sup>+</sup> reduced); ketone → no reaction. <strong>Fehling’s/Benedict’s:</strong> aldehyde → brick-red Cu<sub>2</sub>O precipitate on warming; ketone → stays blue.</p><p><strong>Iodoform test</strong> for CH<sub>3</sub>CO– group (ethanal, methyl ketones): I<sub>2</sub>/NaOH → yellow CHI<sub>3</sub> precipitate with antiseptic smell.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'The functional group of a ketone is', options:['–CHO','C=O in the middle of a chain','–COOH','–OH'], answer:1, explain:'Ketones: carbonyl carbon bonded to two carbons.'},
    {q:'HCN adds to carbonyl compounds by', options:['electrophilic addition','nucleophilic addition','free-radical addition','substitution'], answer:1, explain:'CN<sup>−</sup> (nucleophile) attacks the δ+ carbonyl carbon.'},
    {q:'The product of ethanal + HCN is', options:['CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>CN','CH<sub>3</sub>CH(OH)CN','CH<sub>3</sub>COCN','CH<sub>2</sub>(OH)CH<sub>2</sub>CN'], answer:1, explain:'2-hydroxypropanenitrile: HCN adds across C=O.'},
    {q:'Tollens’ reagent with an aldehyde gives', options:['brick-red precipitate','silver mirror','orange precipitate','no change'], answer:1, explain:'Ag<sup>+</sup> reduced to Ag metal coating the tube.'},
    {q:'Fehling’s solution with a ketone', options:['gives a red precipitate','stays blue (no reaction)','gives a silver mirror','turns green'], answer:1, explain:'Ketones resist oxidation: no reaction.'},
    {q:'2,4-DNPH is used to', options:['distinguish aldehydes from ketones','confirm a carbonyl group (orange ppt)','test for alcohols','test for acids'], answer:1, explain:'Forms orange 2,4-dinitrophenylhydrazone with any aldehyde/ketone.'},
    {q:'The iodoform test is positive for', options:['all aldehydes','CH<sub>3</sub>CO– group compounds','all ketones','carboxylic acids'], answer:1, explain:'Ethanal and methyl ketones give yellow CHI<sub>3</sub> with I<sub>2</sub>/NaOH.'},
    {q:'NaBH<sub>4</sub> reduces a ketone to a', options:['primary alcohol','secondary alcohol','carboxylic acid','alkane'], answer:1, explain:'Ketone → 2° alcohol; aldehyde → 1° alcohol.'},
    {q:'The HCN addition product from ethanal is chiral because', options:['HCN is chiral','attack from either face gives enantiomers','the catalyst is chiral','ethanal is chiral'], answer:1, explain:'Planar carbonyl attacked equally from both faces → racemic mixture.'},
    {q:'Which can be oxidised by acidified dichromate?', options:['propanone','propanal','tert-butanol','benzene'], answer:1, explain:'Aldehydes oxidise to acids; ketones and 3° alcohols do not.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-che-t18', title:'Carboxylic acids and derivatives (AS)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-che-t18-l1', title:'Carboxylic acids', html:'<h3>Carboxylic acids</h3><p><strong>Carboxylic acids</strong> RCOOH (–oic acid). Weak acids: RCOOH ⇌ RCOO<sup>−</sup> + H<sup>+</sup> (partial dissociation). Acidity increases with electron-withdrawing substituents: chloroethanoic acid > ethanoic acid (Cl pulls electron density, stabilising the anion).</p><p>Preparation: oxidation of 1° alcohols/aldehydes (reflux, acidified dichromate); hydrolysis of nitriles: R–CN + 2H<sub>2</sub>O/H<sup>+</sup> → RCOOH + NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup>.</p><p>Reactions: with metals, bases, carbonates (2CH<sub>3</sub>COOH + Na<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub> → 2CH<sub>3</sub>COONa + H<sub>2</sub>O + CO<sub>2</sub> — effervescence, test for acids); with alcohols → esters (conc. H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> catalyst); with PCl<sub>5</sub> → acyl chlorides (steamy HCl fumes).</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t18-l2', title:'Esters', html:'<h3>Esters</h3><p><strong>Esters</strong> RCOOR′: from carboxylic acid + alcohol (conc. H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>, warm). Named alkyl alkanoate: CH<sub>3</sub>COOCH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>3</sub> = ethyl ethanoate. Sweet fruity smells — flavourings, perfumes, solvents.</p><p><strong>Hydrolysis:</strong> acid hydrolysis → acid + alcohol (reverse of formation); <strong>alkaline hydrolysis (saponification)</strong> → carboxylate salt + alcohol: CH<sub>3</sub>COOCH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>3</sub> + NaOH → CH<sub>3</sub>COONa + C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH. Saponification of fats gives soap.</p><p>Exam reasoning: esterification is reversible (use excess of one reactant / remove water to drive right); saponification goes to completion (carboxylate not electrophilic).</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t18-l3', title:'Acyl chlorides', html:'<h3>Acyl chlorides</h3><p><strong>Acyl chlorides</strong> RCOCl: made from acids + SOCl<sub>2</sub> (or PCl<sub>5</sub>). Very reactive — the C<sup>δ+</sup> is attacked by nucleophiles with Cl<sup>−</sup> leaving. Fumes in moist air (hydrolysis: RCOCl + H<sub>2</sub>O → RCOOH + HCl).</p><p>Reactions (nucleophilic addition–elimination): + H<sub>2</sub>O → acid; + alcohol → ester + HCl; + conc. NH<sub>3</sub> → amide + NH<sub>4</sub>Cl; + 1° amine → N-substituted amide. All vigorous at room temperature — no heating needed (unlike acids).</p><p>Uses: making esters/amides fast at room temperature; in polyamide/polyester synthesis. Handle in a fume cupboard (HCl fumes, lachrymator).</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'Chloroethanoic acid is stronger than ethanoic acid because', options:['Cl is bigger','Cl withdraws electron density, stabilising the anion','it has more H','Cl donates electrons'], answer:1, explain:'Electron-withdrawing Cl stabilises RCOO<sup>−</sup>: equilibrium lies further right.'},
    {q:'Hydrolysis of a nitrile with aqueous acid gives', options:['an amide','a carboxylic acid','an amine','an alcohol'], answer:1, explain:'R–CN + 2H<sub>2</sub>O → RCOOH + NH<sub>3</sub> (as NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup> in acid).'},
    {q:'Ethyl ethanoate is made from', options:['ethanal + ethanol','ethanoic acid + ethanol (conc. H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>)','ethanoic acid + ethene','ethanol + NaOH'], answer:1, explain:'Acid + alcohol, conc. H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> catalyst, warm → ester + water.'},
    {q:'Saponification is', options:['acid hydrolysis of an ester','alkaline hydrolysis of an ester','forming soap from alkanes','ester formation'], answer:1, explain:'Ester + NaOH → carboxylate salt (soap) + alcohol.'},
    {q:'Acyl chlorides are made from carboxylic acids using', options:['NaOH','SOCl<sub>2</sub>','H<sub>2</sub>/Ni','Br<sub>2</sub>'], answer:1, explain:'RCOOH + SOCl<sub>2</sub> → RCOCl + SO<sub>2</sub> + HCl.'},
    {q:'RCOCl + conc. NH<sub>3</sub> gives', options:['a nitrile','an amide','an amine','an ester'], answer:1, explain:'RCOCl + 2NH<sub>3</sub> → RCONH<sub>2</sub> + NH<sub>4</sub>Cl.'},
    {q:'The test for a carboxylic acid with Na<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub> gives', options:['no change','effervescence of CO<sub>2</sub>','a white precipitate','a sweet smell'], answer:1, explain:'Acid + carbonate → salt + water + CO<sub>2</sub> (fizzing).'},
    {q:'Acyl chlorides fume in moist air because', options:['they evaporate','they hydrolyse releasing HCl','they oxidise','they sublime'], answer:1, explain:'RCOCl + H<sub>2</sub>O → RCOOH + HCl (steamy fumes).'},
    {q:'Esterification is best driven to completion by', options:['cooling','removing water / using excess reactant','adding water','dilution'], answer:1, explain:'Reversible reaction: Le Chatelier — remove a product or add excess reactant.'},
    {q:'PCl<sub>5</sub> with a carboxylic acid gives', options:['an ester','an acyl chloride','an alkane','no reaction'], answer:1, explain:'RCOOH + PCl<sub>5</sub> → RCOCl + POCl<sub>3</sub> + HCl (steamy fumes).'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-che-t19', title:'Nitrogen compounds (AS)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-che-t19-l1', title:'Amines', html:'<h3>Amines</h3><p><strong>Amines</strong> RNH<sub>2</sub> (1°), R<sub>2</sub>NH (2°), R<sub>3</sub>N (3°). Made by: (1) <strong>nucleophilic substitution</strong> of halogenoalkanes with excess ethanolic NH<sub>3</sub> (heat, sealed tube): CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>Br + 2NH<sub>3</sub> → CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>NH<sub>2</sub> + NH<sub>4</sub>Br; (2) <strong>reduction of nitriles</strong>: R–CN + 4[H] (LiAlH<sub>4</sub> or H<sub>2</sub>/Ni) → RCH<sub>2</sub>NH<sub>2</sub>.</p><p>Amines are <strong>bases</strong> (lone pair on N accepts H<sup>+</sup>): CH<sub>3</sub>NH<sub>2</sub> + HCl → CH<sub>3</sub>NH<sub>3</sub><sup>+</sup>Cl<sup>−</sup>. <strong>Basicity:</strong> ethylamine > ammonia > phenylamine. Alkyl groups donate electrons (stronger base); phenyl ring withdraws the lone pair into delocalisation (weaker base).</p><p>With acyl chlorides → N-substituted amides: RCOCl + R′NH<sub>2</sub> → RCONHR′ + HCl. Phenylamine + Br<sub>2</sub>(aq) → white precipitate (activated ring, like phenol).</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t19-l2', title:'Amides', html:'<h3>Amides</h3><p><strong>Amides</strong> RCONH<sub>2</sub>: made from acyl chlorides + conc. NH<sub>3</sub> (room temp) or acids + NH<sub>3</sub> (heat). <strong>Neutral</strong> compounds — the N lone pair is delocalised into the C=O, so amides are NOT basic (unlike amines).</p><p><strong>Hydrolysis:</strong> acid: RCONH<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>3</sub>O<sup>+</sup> → RCOOH + NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup>; alkali: RCONH<sub>2</sub> + OH<sup>−</sup> → RCOO<sup>−</sup> + NH<sub>3</sub>. Requires heating (amides are unreactive).</p><p>Proteins are polyamides — hydrolysed by enzymes/acids to amino acids. Nylon is a synthetic polyamide (see polymerisation).</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t19-l3', title:'Amino acids', html:'<h3>Amino acids</h3><p><strong>Amino acids:</strong> H<sub>2</sub>N–CHR–COOH. <strong>Amphoteric</strong>: –NH<sub>2</sub> accepts H<sup>+</sup>, –COOH donates H<sup>+</sup>. In solution they exist as <strong>zwitterions</strong>: H<sub>3</sub>N<sup>+</sup>–CHR–COO<sup>−</sup>.</p><p>At low pH (excess H<sup>+</sup>): cation H<sub>3</sub>N<sup>+</sup>–CHR–COOH; at high pH: anion H<sub>2</sub>N–CHR–COO<sup>−</sup>. This pH-dependent charge is exploited in <strong>electrophoresis</strong> to separate amino acids.</p><p>Proteins form by <strong>condensation polymerisation</strong> between –NH<sub>2</sub> and –COOH of amino acids, making <strong>peptide (amide) links</strong> with loss of water. Hydrolysis reverses this.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'Ethanamine is made from bromoethane using', options:['aqueous NaOH','excess ethanolic NH<sub>3</sub>, heat','H<sub>2</sub>/Ni','KCN'], answer:1, explain:'Nucleophilic substitution: R–Br + 2NH<sub>3</sub> → RNH<sub>2</sub> + NH<sub>4</sub>Br.'},
    {q:'Reduction of CH<sub>3</sub>CN with LiAlH<sub>4</sub> gives', options:['CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>NH<sub>2</sub>','CH<sub>3</sub>NH<sub>2</sub>','CH<sub>3</sub>CONH<sub>2</sub>','CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>3</sub>'], answer:0, explain:'Nitrile → primary amine with one extra carbon: CH<sub>3</sub>CN → CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>NH<sub>2</sub>.'},
    {q:'Order of basicity (strongest first) is', options:['phenylamine > ammonia > ethylamine','ethylamine > ammonia > phenylamine','ammonia > ethylamine > phenylamine','all equal'], answer:1, explain:'Alkyl donates electrons (stronger); phenyl delocalises the lone pair (weaker).'},
    {q:'Amides are neutral because', options:['they contain oxygen','the N lone pair is delocalised into C=O','they are insoluble','they have no N'], answer:1, explain:'Delocalisation removes the lone pair’s availability → not basic.'},
    {q:'Acid hydrolysis of ethanamide gives', options:['ethanoic acid + NH<sub>3</sub>','ethanoic acid + NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup>','ethanoate + NH<sub>3</sub>','methane + CO<sub>2</sub>'], answer:1, explain:'CH<sub>3</sub>CONH<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>3</sub>O<sup>+</sup> → CH<sub>3</sub>COOH + NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup>.'},
    {q:'An amino acid at low pH exists mainly as', options:['a zwitterion','a cation','an anion','neutral molecule'], answer:1, explain:'Excess H<sup>+</sup> protonates –COO<sup>−</sup>: H<sub>3</sub>N<sup>+</sup>–CHR–COOH (net +1).'},
    {q:'A zwitterion has', options:['no charge','both + and − charges, net zero','only + charge','only − charge'], answer:1, explain:'H<sub>3</sub>N<sup>+</sup>–CHR–COO<sup>−</sup>: internal charges cancel.'},
    {q:'The peptide link is', options:['an ester link','an amide link','a hydrogen bond','an ionic bond'], answer:1, explain:'–CO–NH– between amino acids: an amide (peptide) bond.'},
    {q:'Proteins are hydrolysed to', options:['amines','amino acids','nitriles','amides'], answer:1, explain:'Hydrolysis breaks peptide links, regenerating amino acids.'},
    {q:'Phenylamine with bromine water gives', options:['an orange solution','a white precipitate','no reaction','a purple solution'], answer:1, explain:'Activated ring → 2,4,6-tribromophenylamine white ppt (like phenol).'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-che-t20', title:'Polymerisation (AS)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-che-t20-l1', title:'Addition polymerisation', html:'<h3>Addition polymerisation</h3><p><strong>Addition polymers:</strong> from alkene monomers — the C=C opens and monomers join: nCH<sub>2</sub>=CH<sub>2</sub> → –[CH<sub>2</sub>–CH<sub>2</sub>]<sub>n</sub>–. Conditions: high pressure/temperature with catalyst (Ziegler–Natta) or peroxides.</p><p>Examples: <strong>poly(ethene)</strong> (bags, bottles), <strong>poly(propene)</strong> (crates, fibres), <strong>poly(chloroethene) PVC</strong> (pipes, window frames), <strong>PTFE</strong> (non-stick), <strong>poly(phenylethene) polystyrene</strong> (packaging).</p><p>Draw the repeat unit by opening the double bond: propene CH<sub>2</sub>=CHCH<sub>3</sub> → –[CH<sub>2</sub>–CH(CH<sub>3</sub>)]<sub>n</sub>–. To find the monomer, locate the repeating 2-carbon unit and restore C=C.</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t20-l2', title:'Condensation polymerisation', html:'<h3>Condensation polymerisation</h3><p><strong>Condensation polymers:</strong> monomers join with loss of a small molecule (H<sub>2</sub>O or HCl). Two types: <strong>polyesters</strong> (diol + dicarboxylic acid → ester links) and <strong>polyamides</strong> (diamine + dicarboxylic acid → amide links).</p><p>Example: <strong>Terylene/PET</strong>: benzene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid + ethane-1,2-diol → polyester + H<sub>2</sub>O. <strong>Nylon-6,6</strong>: hexane-1,6-diamine + hexanedioic acid → polyamide + H<sub>2</sub>O (or use the diacyl chloride, releasing HCl).</p><p>Unlike addition polymers, condensation polymers can be <strong>hydrolysed</strong> back to monomers (acid/alkali, heat) — relevant for recycling and biodegradability.</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t20-l3', title:'Disposal and green polymers', html:'<h3>Disposal and green polymers</h3><p>Problems: addition polymers are <strong>non-biodegradable</strong> (persistent in landfill), release toxic gases if burned (HCl from PVC, HCN from nylon), and come from finite crude oil.</p><p>Solutions: <strong>recycling</strong> (sorting by polymer type — difficult for mixed waste), <strong>feedstock recycling</strong> (cracking back to monomers), <strong>biodegradable polymers</strong> (e.g. polylactic acid from corn starch; condensation polymers that hydrolyse), incineration with energy recovery.</p><p>Exam reasoning: compare disposal methods on energy, pollution and practicality. Degradable polymers help litter but need correct composting conditions; “biodegradable” ≠ disappears anywhere.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'Addition polymerisation involves', options:['loss of water','opening of C=C bonds, no small molecule lost','loss of HCl','ester formation'], answer:1, explain:'Alkene monomers add across the double bond; nothing is eliminated.'},
    {q:'The repeat unit of poly(propene) is', options:['–[CH<sub>2</sub>=CH(CH<sub>3</sub>)]–','–[CH<sub>2</sub>–CH(CH<sub>3</sub>)]<sub>n</sub>–','–[CH(CH<sub>3</sub>)]<sub>n</sub>–','–[CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>]<sub>n</sub>–'], answer:1, explain:'Open the C=C: CH<sub>2</sub>=CHCH<sub>3</sub> → –[CH<sub>2</sub>–CH(CH<sub>3</sub>)]–.'},
    {q:'PVC monomer is', options:['ethene','chloroethene','tetrafluoroethene','propene'], answer:1, explain:'Poly(chloroethene) from CH<sub>2</sub>=CHCl.'},
    {q:'A polyester is formed from', options:['alkene + alkene','diol + dicarboxylic acid','diamine + diol','two alkenes'], answer:1, explain:'–OH + –COOH condense → ester links + H<sub>2</sub>O.'},
    {q:'Nylon-6,6 monomers are', options:['ethene and water','hexane-1,6-diamine and hexanedioic acid','lactic acid only','styrene'], answer:1, explain:'Diamine + dicarboxylic acid → polyamide + H<sub>2</sub>O.'},
    {q:'Condensation polymers differ from addition polymers in that they', options:['cannot be recycled','can be hydrolysed to monomers','are always biodegradable','contain C=C'], answer:1, explain:'Ester/amide links hydrolyse back (acid/alkali, heat); addition polymers’ C–C backbone resists.'},
    {q:'Burning PVC is problematic because', options:['it melts','it releases HCl (toxic)','it is expensive','it produces water'], answer:1, explain:'Chlorine → HCl fumes; also dioxins possible.'},
    {q:'Terylene is a', options:['polyamide','polyester','addition polymer','protein'], answer:1, explain:'PET: ester links from diol + dicarboxylic acid.'},
    {q:'To draw the monomer from an addition polymer repeat unit,', options:['add water','restore the C=C in each 2-carbon unit','remove HCl','halve the chain'], answer:1, explain:'Reverse polymerisation: find the repeating –C–C– and put back the double bond.'},
    {q:'Polylactic acid is attractive because', options:['it is cheap oil-based plastic','it is biodegradable (from renewable starch)','it never degrades','it is a metal'], answer:1, explain:'PLA from corn starch: renewable and compostable under the right conditions.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-che-t21', title:'Organic synthesis (AS)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-che-t21-l1', title:'Planning a synthesis: two-step routes', html:'<h3>Planning a synthesis: two-step routes</h3><p>Design syntheses by working <strong>backwards (retrosynthesis)</strong> from the target: what functional group do I need, and what makes it? Key interconversions: alkene ↔ alkane (H<sub>2</sub>/Ni), halogenoalkane → alcohol (aq. NaOH) / alkene (ethanolic KOH) / amine (NH<sub>3</sub>) / nitrile (KCN), alcohol → alkene (dehydration) / halogenoalkane (PCl<sub>5</sub>), nitrile → amine (LiAlH<sub>4</sub>) / acid (hydrolysis).</p><p>Example: ethene → ethylamine. Step 1: HBr → bromoethane (electrophilic addition). Step 2: excess NH<sub>3</sub> (ethanolic, heat) → ethylamine (nucleophilic substitution).</p><p>Example: propene → propanoic acid. Step 1: HBr → 2-bromopropane (Markovnikov). Step 2: KCN (ethanolic) → propanenitrile. Step 3: acid hydrolysis → propanoic acid. (Three steps — show all reagents/conditions.)</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t21-l2', title:'Choosing reagents and conditions', html:'<h3>Choosing reagents and conditions</h3><p>State <strong>reagent + condition</strong> precisely: “K<sub>2</sub>Cr<sub>2</sub>O<sub>7</sub>/H<sup>+</sup>, reflux” vs “distil”. “Ethanolic KOH, heat” vs “aqueous NaOH, warm”. Examiners award marks for conditions as well as reagents.</p><p>Carbon-chain extension: <strong>KCN</strong> adds one carbon (R–X → R–CN). Carbon-chain shortening: not directly — plan around it (e.g. via cleavage is beyond AS).</p><p>Common traps: oxidising a 1° alcohol with reflux gives the acid, not the aldehyde; phenylamine needs the ring already present (no Friedel–Crafts at AS); HCN needs alkaline (CN<sup>−</sup>) conditions.</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t21-l3', title:'Purification and yield in synthesis', html:'<h3>Purification and yield in synthesis</h3><p>After reaction: <strong>separating funnel</strong> for immiscible liquids; <strong>distillation</strong> for volatile products; <strong>recrystallisation</strong> for solids. Dry with anhydrous MgSO<sub>4</sub>/CaCl<sub>2</sub>.</p><p>Check purity: <strong>boiling/melting point</strong> vs data book; chromatography; IR/mass spec. Percentage yield per step multiplies: two 80% steps → 64% overall — plan short routes.</p><p>Green considerations: atom economy, renewable feedstocks, fewer steps, less hazardous reagents — mention where relevant for “suggest improvements” questions.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'Retrosynthesis means', options:['synthesising backwards','working backwards from the target molecule','using retrosynthetic solvents','reversing a reaction'], answer:1, explain:'Plan from target → precursors → starting material.'},
    {q:'Bromoethane → ethylamine needs', options:['aqueous NaOH','excess ethanolic NH<sub>3</sub>, heat','KCN','H<sub>2</sub>/Ni'], answer:1, explain:'Nucleophilic substitution by ammonia gives the amine.'},
    {q:'To extend a carbon chain by one, use', options:['HBr','KCN (then hydrolysis/reduction)','NaOH','Br<sub>2</sub>'], answer:1, explain:'R–X + CN<sup>−</sup> → R–CN: one extra carbon.'},
    {q:'Propene → propanoic acid: the middle step after HBr addition is', options:['NaOH','KCN in ethanol','H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>','NH<sub>3</sub>'], answer:1, explain:'2-bromopropane + KCN → propanenitrile (chain extended), then hydrolyse.'},
    {q:'“Ethanolic KOH, heat” vs “aqueous NaOH, warm” give', options:['the same product','alkene vs alcohol','alcohol vs alkene','no reaction either way'], answer:1, explain:'Ethanolic → elimination (alkene); aqueous → substitution (alcohol).'},
    {q:'To stop oxidation of a 1° alcohol at the aldehyde,', options:['reflux','distil off the aldehyde as it forms','use excess oxidant','cool to 0 °C'], answer:1, explain:'Remove volatile aldehyde before it oxidises further to the acid.'},
    {q:'Two synthetic steps of 90% and 80% yield give overall', options:['85%','72%','170%','70%'], answer:1, explain:'0.90 × 0.80 = 0.72 = 72%.'},
    {q:'An involatile solid product is best purified by', options:['distillation','recrystallisation','filtration only','decanting'], answer:1, explain:'Dissolve in hot solvent, cool to crystallise pure solid.'},
    {q:'Drying an organic liquid uses', options:['filter paper','anhydrous MgSO<sub>4</sub>','wet cloth','heating'], answer:1, explain:'Anhydrous salts absorb water from organic layers.'},
    {q:'A good synthetic route minimises', options:['yield','number of steps','use of catalysts','purification'], answer:1, explain:'Fewer steps → higher overall yield, less waste, lower cost.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-che-t22', title:'Analytical techniques (AS)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-che-t22-l1', title:'Infrared spectroscopy', html:'<h3>Infrared spectroscopy</h3><p><strong>IR spectroscopy</strong> identifies functional groups: bonds absorb IR at characteristic wavenumbers, causing increased vibration. Key absorptions (cm<sup>-1</sup>): O–H (alcohol) 3200–3600 (broad); O–H (acid) 2500–3000 (very broad); N–H 3300–3500; C=O 1680–1750; C=C 1620–1680 (weak).</p><p>The <strong>fingerprint region</strong> (< 1500 cm<sup>-1</sup>) is unique to each molecule — match against a database to identify the compound.</p><p>Worked example: an IR spectrum with a broad 2500–3000 band + 1710 peak → carboxylic acid. A 3350 broad band + no C=O → alcohol.</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t22-l2', title:'Mass spectrometry in organic analysis', html:'<h3>Mass spectrometry in organic analysis</h3><p><strong>M<sup>+</sup> peak</strong> (highest m/z) gives M<sub>r</sub>. <strong>Fragment peaks</strong> reveal structure: e.g. m/z 29 (C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub><sup>+</sup>), 43 (CH<sub>3</sub>CO<sup>+</sup> or C<sub>3</sub>H<sub>7</sub><sup>+</sup>), 15 (CH<sub>3</sub><sup>+</sup>).</p><p>Worked example: M<sup>+</sup> at 74 with fragments 59 (loss of 15, CH<sub>3</sub>), 45, 31 (CH<sub>2</sub>OH<sup>+</sup>) → consistent with propanoic acid or methyl ethanoate — combine with IR to decide.</p><p><strong>[M+1] peak</strong> (from <sup>13</sup>C, ~1.1% per carbon) can count carbons: intensity ratio [M+1]/[M] ≈ 0.011 × n(C).</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t22-l3', title:'Combining techniques', html:'<h3>Combining techniques</h3><p>Structure elucidation strategy: (1) <strong>mass spec</strong> → M<sub>r</sub> (M<sup>+</sup>) and fragments; (2) <strong>IR</strong> → functional groups present/absent; (3) <strong>chemical tests</strong> (2,4-DNPH, Tollens’, Br<sub>2</sub> water, Na<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub>) → narrow down; (4) propose structure consistent with ALL data.</p><p>Worked example: M<sub>r</sub> 60, IR shows broad O–H (2500–3000) + C=O 1710 → carboxylic acid; fragments at 45 (–CH<sub>3</sub>) → ethanoic acid CH<sub>3</sub>COOH (M<sub>r</sub> 60 ✓).</p><p>Exam reasoning: never rely on one technique. Absence of a peak is also evidence (e.g. no C=O rules out carbonyls/carboxylic acids/esters).</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'An O–H stretch in an alcohol appears at', options:['1680–1750 cm<sup>-1</sup>','3200–3600 cm<sup>-1</sup> (broad)','1620–1680 cm<sup>-1</sup>','2500–3000 cm<sup>-1</sup>'], answer:1, explain:'Alcohol O–H: broad 3200–3600 cm<sup>-1</sup> (hydrogen-bonded).'},
    {q:'A C=O stretch appears at', options:['3200–3600 cm<sup>-1</sup>','2500–3000 cm<sup>-1</sup>','1680–1750 cm<sup>-1</sup>','3300–3500 cm<sup>-1</sup>'], answer:2, explain:'Carbonyl C=O: strong sharp 1680–1750 cm<sup>-1</sup>.'},
    {q:'The fingerprint region is used to', options:['find functional groups','identify a compound by matching a database','measure M<sub>r</sub>','count carbons'], answer:1, explain:'Below 1500 cm<sup>-1</sup>: unique pattern per molecule.'},
    {q:'The M<sup>+</sup> peak gives', options:['the base peak','M<sub>r</sub> of the molecule','the number of carbons','the functional group'], answer:1, explain:'Molecular ion = intact molecule: highest m/z → M<sub>r</sub>.'},
    {q:'A fragment at m/z 31 suggests', options:['CH<sub>3</sub><sup>+</sup>','CH<sub>2</sub>OH<sup>+</sup>','C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub><sup>+</sup>','CH<sub>3</sub>CO<sup>+</sup>'], answer:1, explain:'31 = 12 + 2 + 16 + 1: CH<sub>2</sub>OH<sup>+</sup>.'},
    {q:'IR shows broad 2500–3000 + C=O at 1710 cm<sup>-1</sup>. The compound is', options:['an alcohol','a carboxylic acid','a ketone','an ester'], answer:1, explain:'Very broad O–H + C=O = carboxylic acid.'},
    {q:'The [M+1] peak arises mainly from', options:['<sup>13</sup>C isotope','fragmentation','double ionisation','impurities'], answer:0, explain:'~1.1% natural <sup>13</sup>C: [M+1]/[M] ≈ 0.011 × number of C atoms.'},
    {q:'Absence of a C=O peak in IR rules out', options:['alcohols','aldehydes, ketones, acids, esters','alkenes','amines'], answer:1, explain:'All contain C=O; its absence eliminates them all.'},
    {q:'Which combination best identifies an unknown carbonyl compound?', options:['IR only','mass spec M<sub>r</sub> + IR + 2,4-DNPH derivative mp','melting point only','smell'], answer:1, explain:'Multiple independent techniques + derivative mp for confirmation.'},
    {q:'A strong peak at 1715 cm<sup>-1</sup> with no broad O–H suggests', options:['carboxylic acid','aldehyde or ketone','alcohol','alkene'], answer:1, explain:'C=O present, no acid O–H → aldehyde/ketone (distinguish with Tollens’).'}
   ]}
,
  {id:'al-che-t23', title:'Chemical energetics (A Level)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-che-t23-l1', title:'Lattice energy and Born–Haber cycles', html:'<h3>Lattice energy and Born–Haber cycles</h3><p><strong>Lattice energy ΔH<sub>latt</sub><sup>⊖</sup>:</strong> enthalpy change when 1 mol of solid ionic compound forms from its gaseous ions: Na<sup>+</sup>(g) + Cl<sup>−</sup>(g) → NaCl(s) — exothermic (negative). Magnitude depends on ionic charge and size: higher charge and smaller ions → more exothermic.</p><p><strong>Born–Haber cycle</strong> for NaCl: ΔH<sub>f</sub> = ΔH<sub>at</sub>(Na) + IE<sub>1</sub>(Na) + ½ΔH<sub>at</sub>(Cl<sub>2</sub>) + EA<sub>1</sub>(Cl) + ΔH<sub>latt</sub>. Worked example: −411 = 109 + 494 + 121 + (−349) + ΔH<sub>latt</sub> → ΔH<sub>latt</sub> = −786 kJ mol<sup>-1</sup>.</p><p>Construct the cycle carefully with correct state symbols and electron affinity signs (EA<sub>1</sub> usually exothermic, EA<sub>2</sub> endothermic). The cycle must close: sum = ΔH<sub>f</sub>.</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t23-l2', title:'Enthalpy of solution and hydration', html:'<h3>Enthalpy of solution and hydration</h3><p><strong>Enthalpy of hydration ΔH<sub>hyd</sub><sup>⊖</sup>:</strong> 1 mol of gaseous ions dissolves in water → hydrated ions (exothermic). <strong>Enthalpy of solution ΔH<sub>sol</sub><sup>⊖</sup>:</strong> 1 mol of solid dissolves: ΔH<sub>sol</sub> = −ΔH<sub>latt</sub> + ΣΔH<sub>hyd</sub> (break lattice, then hydrate).</p><p>Worked example: NaCl: ΔH<sub>latt</sub> = −786, ΔH<sub>hyd</sub>(Na<sup>+</sup>) = −406, ΔH<sub>hyd</sub>(Cl<sup>−</sup>) = −364. ΔH<sub>sol</sub> = +786 − 406 − 364 = +16 kJ mol<sup>-1</sup> (slightly endothermic — yet it dissolves: entropy drives it).</p><p>Trends: ΔH<sub>hyd</sub> more exothermic for smaller, more highly charged ions (greater charge density polarises water more).</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t23-l3', title:'Entropy and Gibbs free energy', html:'<h3>Entropy and Gibbs free energy</h3><p><strong>Entropy S:</strong> measure of disorder (J K<sup>-1</sup> mol<sup>-1</sup>). S increases: solid → liquid → gas; more moles of gas; more complex molecules. <strong>ΔS = ΣS(products) − ΣS(reactants)</strong> using standard values.</p><p><strong>Gibbs free energy: ΔG = ΔH − TΔS.</strong> ΔG < 0 → feasible (spontaneous); ΔG > 0 → not feasible; ΔG = 0 at equilibrium. Worked example: ΔH = −100 kJ mol<sup>-1</sup>, ΔS = −200 J K<sup>-1</sup> mol<sup>-1</sup> at 298 K: ΔG = −100000 − 298(−200) = −40400 J = −40.4 kJ mol<sup>-1</sup> → feasible.</p><p>Temperature dependence: if ΔH < 0 and ΔS > 0, feasible at all T; if ΔH > 0 and ΔS < 0, never feasible; otherwise feasible above/below T = ΔH/ΔS. Feasible ≠ fast — kinetics (E<sub>a</sub>) may still block it.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'Lattice energy is defined for', options:['1 mol of solid from gaseous ions','1 mol of gas from solid','dissolving 1 mol','melting 1 mol'], answer:0, explain:'ΔH<sub>latt</sub>: gaseous ions → 1 mol solid lattice (exothermic).'},
    {q:'Lattice energy is most exothermic for', options:['NaCl','MgO','KBr','CsI'], answer:1, explain:'Mg<sup>2+</sup>/O<sup>2−</sup>: higher charges and small ions → strongest attraction.'},
    {q:'In the Born–Haber cycle, the second electron affinity is', options:['exothermic','endothermic','zero','equal to the first'], answer:1, explain:'Adding an electron to an already negative ion needs energy: EA<sub>2</sub> > 0.'},
    {q:'ΔH<sub>sol</sub> equals', options:['ΔH<sub>latt</sub> + ΣΔH<sub>hyd</sub>','−ΔH<sub>latt</sub> + ΣΔH<sub>hyd</sub>','ΔH<sub>latt</sub> − ΣΔH<sub>hyd</sub>','ΣΔH<sub>hyd</sub> only'], answer:1, explain:'Break the lattice (−ΔH<sub>latt</sub>, endothermic) then hydrate ions (exothermic).'},
    {q:'Entropy increases when', options:['gas condenses','solid melts','gas dissolves','vapour deposits'], answer:1, explain:'Melting: ordered solid → disordered liquid: ΔS > 0.'},
    {q:'ΔG = ΔH − TΔS. A reaction is feasible when', options:['ΔG > 0','ΔG < 0','ΔG = 0','ΔS < 0'], answer:1, explain:'Negative ΔG → thermodynamically feasible (spontaneous).'},
    {q:'With ΔH = +50 kJ mol<sup>-1</sup> and ΔS = +200 J K<sup>-1</sup> mol<sup>-1</sup>, the reaction becomes feasible above', options:['250 K','2500 K','25 K','never'], answer:0, explain:'T > ΔH/ΔS = 50000/200 = 250 K.'},
    {q:'NaCl dissolves despite ΔH<sub>sol</sub> > 0 because', options:['lattice energy is small','entropy of the system increases','hydration is endothermic','ΔG is positive'], answer:1, explain:'TΔS term makes ΔG negative: disorder increases on dissolving.'},
    {q:'Feasible (ΔG < 0) does not guarantee reaction because', options:['ΔH is wrong','kinetics: E<sub>a</sub> may be too high','entropy is zero','catalysts are absent'], answer:1, explain:'Thermodynamics says “can happen”; kinetics (activation energy) says “how fast”.'},
    {q:'ΔS for 2SO<sub>2</sub> + O<sub>2</sub> → 2SO<sub>3</sub> is', options:['positive','negative','zero','cannot tell'], answer:1, explain:'3 gas moles → 2: fewer, more ordered → ΔS < 0.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-che-t24', title:'Electrochemistry (A Level)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-che-t24-l1', title:'Standard electrode potentials', html:'<h3>Standard electrode potentials</h3><p><strong>Standard electrode potential E<sup>⊖</sup>:</strong> the e.m.f. of a half-cell vs the <strong>standard hydrogen electrode</strong> (SHE, 0.00 V) under standard conditions (298 K, 1 mol dm<sup>-3</sup> ions, 100 kPa gases). More positive E<sup>⊖</sup> → stronger oxidising agent (the oxidised species).</p><p>Cell notation: Zn(s)|Zn<sup>2+</sup>(aq)||Cu<sup>2+</sup>(aq)|Cu(s). <strong>E<sub>cell</sub><sup>⊖</sup> = E<sup>⊖</sup>(positive electrode) − E<sup>⊖</sup>(negative electrode)</strong> = E<sup>⊖</sup>(cathode) − E<sup>⊖</sup>(anode).</p><p>Worked example: Zn<sup>2+</sup>/Zn E<sup>⊖</sup> = −0.76 V; Cu<sup>2+</sup>/Cu = +0.34 V. E<sub>cell</sub> = 0.34 − (−0.76) = +1.10 V. Positive → feasible: Zn oxidised, Cu<sup>2+</sup> reduced.</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t24-l2', title:'Predicting feasibility', html:'<h3>Predicting feasibility</h3><p>A redox reaction is feasible if <strong>E<sub>cell</sub><sup>⊖</sup> > 0</strong>: the half-equation with the more positive E<sup>⊖</sup> runs as reduction. Combine half-equations accordingly and check electrons balance.</p><p>Worked example: will Cl<sub>2</sub> oxidise Fe<sup>2+</sup> to Fe<sup>3+</sup>? E<sup>⊖</sup>(Cl<sub>2</sub>/Cl<sup>−</sup>) = +1.36 V; E<sup>⊖</sup>(Fe<sup>3+</sup>/Fe<sup>2+</sup>) = +0.77 V. Cl<sub>2</sub> half-equation more positive → Cl<sub>2</sub> reduced, Fe<sup>2+</sup> oxidised: E<sub>cell</sub> = 1.36 − 0.77 = +0.59 V → feasible.</p><p>Limitations: standard conditions rarely apply (concentrations change E); kinetics may prevent reaction despite E<sub>cell</sub> > 0; E<sup>⊖</sup> says nothing about rate.</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t24-l3', title:'Batteries and fuel cells', html:'<h3>Batteries and fuel cells</h3><p><strong>Rechargeable batteries</strong> (lead–acid, lithium-ion): redox reactions reversed on charging. Lead–acid: Pb + PbO<sub>2</sub> + 2H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> ⇌ 2PbSO<sub>4</sub> + 2H<sub>2</sub>O.</p><p><strong>Hydrogen fuel cell:</strong> 2H<sub>2</sub> + O<sub>2</sub> → 2H<sub>2</sub>O. Alkaline version: anode: H<sub>2</sub> + 2OH<sup>−</sup> → 2H<sub>2</sub>O + 2e<sup>−</sup>; cathode: ½O<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O + 2e<sup>−</sup> → 2OH<sup>−</sup>. E<sub>cell</sub> ≈ +1.23 V. Only water as product — clean at point of use.</p><p>Advantages vs petrol: no CO<sub>2</sub>/NO<sub>x</sub> at use; higher efficiency. Challenges: H<sub>2</sub> storage/transport, production energy cost, infrastructure, fuel-cell cost (Pt catalysts).</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'The standard hydrogen electrode is assigned', options:['+1.00 V','0.00 V','−0.76 V','+0.34 V'], answer:1, explain:'SHE is the reference: E<sup>⊖</sup> = 0.00 V by definition.'},
    {q:'E<sub>cell</sub><sup>⊖</sup> equals', options:['E<sup>⊖</sup>(anode) − E<sup>⊖</sup>(cathode)','E<sup>⊖</sup>(cathode) − E<sup>⊖</sup>(anode)','sum of both','product of both'], answer:1, explain:'E<sub>cell</sub> = E<sup>⊖</sup>(positive/right-hand electrode) − E<sup>⊖</sup>(negative/left-hand).'},
    {q:'For Zn (−0.76 V) and Cu (+0.34 V), E<sub>cell</sub><sup>⊖</sup> is', options:['−1.10 V','+1.10 V','−0.42 V','+0.42 V'], answer:1, explain:'0.34 − (−0.76) = +1.10 V.'},
    {q:'A redox reaction is feasible (standard conditions) when', options:['E<sub>cell</sub><sup>⊖</sup> < 0','E<sub>cell</sub><sup>⊖</sup> > 0','E<sub>cell</sub><sup>⊖</sup> = 0','ΔG > 0'], answer:1, explain:'Positive E<sub>cell</sub> ↔ negative ΔG: feasible.'},
    {q:'The more positive E<sup>⊖</sup> half-equation', options:['is oxidised','is reduced (stronger oxidising agent)','has no reaction','is the anode'], answer:1, explain:'Greater tendency to gain electrons → reduced; its oxidised form is the stronger oxidiser.'},
    {q:'In cell notation, the salt bridge is shown as', options:['|','||',',','/'], answer:1, explain:'|| = salt bridge; single | = phase boundary.'},
    {q:'A limitation of E<sup>⊖</sup> predictions is', options:['they are always wrong','non-standard conditions and kinetic barriers','they ignore electrons','they need catalysts'], answer:1, explain:'Real concentrations differ from standard; a feasible reaction may be immeasurably slow.'},
    {q:'The hydrogen fuel cell overall reaction is', options:['2H<sub>2</sub> + O<sub>2</sub> → 2H<sub>2</sub>O','H<sub>2</sub> + O<sub>2</sub> → H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>','2H<sub>2</sub>O → 2H<sub>2</sub> + O<sub>2</sub>','H<sub>2</sub> + 2OH<sup>−</sup> → 2H<sub>2</sub>O'], answer:0, explain:'Hydrogen + oxygen → water (+ electrical energy).'},
    {q:'Lead–acid battery discharge produces', options:['PbO','PbSO<sub>4</sub>','PbCl<sub>2</sub>','Pb(NO<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>'], answer:1, explain:'Pb + PbO<sub>2</sub> + 2H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> → 2PbSO<sub>4</sub> + 2H<sub>2</sub>O.'},
    {q:'A fuel cell differs from a battery in that', options:['it stores reactants internally','reactants are supplied continuously from outside','it cannot be recharged','it produces CO<sub>2</sub>'], answer:1, explain:'Fuel cells run as long as fuel/oxidant are fed in; batteries contain fixed reactants.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-che-t25', title:'Equilibria (A Level)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-che-t25-l1', title:'Acid–base equilibria and Ka', html:'<h3>Acid–base equilibria and Ka</h3><p><strong>Brønsted–Lowry:</strong> acid = proton donor; base = proton acceptor. <strong>Conjugate pairs:</strong> CH<sub>3</sub>COOH/CH<sub>3</sub>COO<sup>−</sup>, NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup>/NH<sub>3</sub>, H<sub>2</sub>O/OH<sup>−</sup>.</p><p><strong>Acid dissociation constant:</strong> HA ⇌ H<sup>+</sup> + A<sup>−</sup>; Ka = [H<sup>+</sup>][A<sup>−</sup>]/[HA]. Larger Ka → stronger acid. <strong>pKa = −log Ka</strong>; pH = −log[H<sup>+</sup>]. For a weak acid, [H<sup>+</sup>] ≈ √(Ka × [HA]).</p><p>Worked example: 0.10 mol dm<sup>-3</sup> ethanoic acid, Ka = 1.7×10<sup>-5</sup>. [H<sup>+</sup>] = √(1.7×10<sup>-5</sup> × 0.10) = 1.30×10<sup>-3</sup>; pH = 2.88.</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t25-l2', title:'pH curves and indicators', html:'<h3>pH curves and indicators</h3><p><strong>pH curves:</strong> strong acid–strong base: long vertical around pH 7 (equivalence pH 7). Weak acid–strong base: vertical shorter, equivalence pH > 7. Strong acid–weak base: equivalence pH < 7. Weak–weak: no sharp vertical — no suitable indicator.</p><p><strong>Indicator choice:</strong> the indicator’s range must lie on the vertical section. Methyl orange (3.1–4.4) for strong acid–weak base; phenolphthalein (8.3–10.0) for weak acid–strong base; either for strong–strong.</p><p>Indicators are weak acids: HIn ⇌ H<sup>+</sup> + In<sup>−</sup> with different colours. Added acid/base shifts the equilibrium (Le Chatelier), changing colour.</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t25-l3', title:'Buffer solutions and Kw', html:'<h3>Buffer solutions and Kw</h3><p><strong>Buffer:</strong> resists pH change on adding small amounts of acid/base — a weak acid + its conjugate base (e.g. CH<sub>3</sub>COOH/CH<sub>3</sub>COONa) or a weak base + its conjugate acid.</p><p>How it works: added H<sup>+</sup> + A<sup>−</sup> → HA (removed); added OH<sup>−</sup> + HA → A<sup>−</sup> + H<sub>2</sub>O. Both components present in large amounts, so ratios barely change. pH of a buffer: [H<sup>+</sup>] = Ka × [HA]/[A<sup>−</sup>].</p><p><strong>Ionic product of water:</strong> Kw = [H<sup>+</sup>][OH<sup>−</sup>] = 1.0×10<sup>-14</sup> mol<sup>2</sup> dm<sup>-6</sup> at 298 K. Pure water: [H<sup>+</sup>] = [OH<sup>−</sup>] = 1.0×10<sup>-7</sup> → pH 7. Blood (pH 7.4) is a carbonic acid/hydrogencarbonate buffer.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'A Brønsted–Lowry base is a', options:['proton donor','proton acceptor','electron donor','hydroxide producer'], answer:1, explain:'Base = proton (H<sup>+</sup>) acceptor.'},
    {q:'The conjugate base of NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup> is', options:['NH<sub>3</sub>','NH<sub>2</sub><sup>−</sup>','N<sup>3−</sup>','NH<sub>5</sub><sup>2+</sup>'], answer:0, explain:'NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup> − H<sup>+</sup> = NH<sub>3</sub>.'},
    {q:'Ka is defined as', options:['[HA]/([H<sup>+</sup>][A<sup>−</sup>])','[H<sup>+</sup>][A<sup>−</sup>]/[HA]','[H<sup>+</sup>][HA]/[A<sup>−</sup>]','pH + pOH'], answer:1, explain:'Ka = [H<sup>+</sup>][A<sup>−</sup>]/[HA] for HA ⇌ H<sup>+</sup> + A<sup>−</sup>.'},
    {q:'pH of 0.10 mol dm<sup>-3</sup> ethanoic acid (Ka = 1.7×10<sup>-5</sup>) is about', options:['1.00','2.88','4.77','3.77'], answer:1, explain:'[H<sup>+</sup>] = √(Ka·c) = 1.30×10<sup>-3</sup>; pH = 2.88.'},
    {q:'Equivalence pH of a weak acid–strong base titration is', options:['7','> 7','< 7','0'], answer:1, explain:'Conjugate base present → slightly alkaline at equivalence.'},
    {q:'Suitable indicator for strong acid–weak base titration', options:['phenolphthalein','methyl orange','universal','litmus'], answer:1, explain:'Methyl orange range (3.1–4.4) lies on the vertical (equivalence pH < 7).'},
    {q:'A buffer solution contains', options:['strong acid + strong base','weak acid + its conjugate base','two strong acids','water only'], answer:1, explain:'Both HA and A<sup>−</sup> in quantity to mop up added H<sup>+</sup>/OH<sup>−</sup>.'},
    {q:'Adding OH<sup>−</sup> to an acidic buffer', options:['pH plummets','OH<sup>−</sup> + HA → A<sup>−</sup> + H<sub>2</sub>O; pH barely changes','precipitate forms','buffer destroyed'], answer:1, explain:'The weak acid neutralises added base; ratio [HA]/[A<sup>−</sup>] barely shifts.'},
    {q:'Kw at 298 K is', options:['1.0×10<sup>-7</sup>','1.0×10<sup>-14</sup> mol<sup>2</sup> dm<sup>-6</sup>','14.0','7.0'], answer:1, explain:'Kw = [H<sup>+</sup>][OH<sup>−</sup>] = 1.0×10<sup>-14</sup> mol<sup>2</sup> dm<sup>-6</sup>.'},
    {q:'Blood pH is maintained by the', options:['phosphate buffer only','carbonic acid/hydrogencarbonate buffer','pure water','HCl buffer'], answer:1, explain:'H<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub>/HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup> buffers blood at pH ~7.4.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-che-t26', title:'Reaction kinetics (A Level)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-che-t26-l1', title:'Rate equations and orders', html:'<h3>Rate equations and orders</h3><p><strong>Rate equation:</strong> rate = k[A]<sup>m</sup>[B]<sup>n</sup>. <strong>Orders</strong> m, n are found by experiment, NOT from the stoichiometric equation. <strong>Overall order</strong> = m + n. Units of k depend on overall order.</p><p>Finding orders: <strong>initial rates method</strong> — double [A], rate doubles → first order in A; quadruples → second order; unchanged → zero order. Or <strong>concentration–time graphs</strong>: constant half-life → first order.</p><p>Worked example: doubling [NO] quadruples rate and doubling [O<sub>2</sub>] doubles rate for 2NO + O<sub>2</sub> → 2NO<sub>2</sub>: rate = k[NO]<sup>2</sup>[O<sub>2</sub>], third order overall.</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t26-l2', title:'The rate constant and half-life', html:'<h3>The rate constant and half-life</h3><p>Units of k: rearrange k = rate/[conc]<sup>order</sup>. First order: s<sup>-1</sup>; second order: dm<sup>3</sup> mol<sup>-1</sup> s<sup>-1</sup>; zero order: mol dm<sup>-3</sup> s<sup>-1</sup>.</p><p><strong>Half-life t<sub>½</sub>:</strong> time for concentration to halve. First order: t<sub>½</sub> = ln 2/k — CONSTANT (independent of concentration). Plot ln[A] vs t: straight line, gradient = −k.</p><p>Second order: 1/[A] vs t is linear (gradient = k); half-life increases as concentration falls. Zero order: [A] vs t linear (gradient = −k); rate independent of [A].</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t26-l3', title:'Rate-determining step', html:'<h3>Rate-determining step</h3><p>The <strong>rate-determining step (rds)</strong> is the slowest step. Species in the rate equation (and their orders) appear in/are involved up to and including the rds. Species reacting AFTER the rds do not appear.</p><p>Worked example: (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>CBr + OH<sup>−</sup> → (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>COH + Br<sup>−</sup>; rate = k[(CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>CBr] (first order, zero order in OH<sup>−</sup>). Mechanism (S<sub>N</sub>1): step 1 (slow): (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>CBr → (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>C<sup>+</sup> + Br<sup>−</sup>; step 2 (fast): carbocation + OH<sup>−</sup> → product. OH<sup>−</sup> reacts after the rds → absent from rate equation.</p><p>Exam reasoning: propose mechanisms consistent with the rate equation — the rds must include exactly the species (with correct orders) from the rate law.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'Reaction orders are determined by', options:['the balanced equation','experiment','the catalyst','temperature'], answer:1, explain:'Orders come from experimental data (initial rates), never from stoichiometry.'},
    {q:'If doubling [A] quadruples the rate, the order in A is', options:['0','1','2','3'], answer:2, explain:'Rate ∝ [A]<sup>2</sup>: 2<sup>2</sup> = 4.'},
    {q:'Units of k for a second-order reaction', options:['s<sup>-1</sup>','mol dm<sup>-3</sup> s<sup>-1</sup>','dm<sup>3</sup> mol<sup>-1</sup> s<sup>-1</sup>','mol<sup>2</sup> dm<sup>-6</sup> s<sup>-1</sup>'], answer:2, explain:'k = rate/[A]<sup>2</sup>: (mol dm<sup>-3</sup> s<sup>-1</sup>)/(mol<sup>2</sup> dm<sup>-6</sup>) = dm<sup>3</sup> mol<sup>-1</sup> s<sup>-1</sup>.'},
    {q:'Constant half-life indicates', options:['zero order','first order','second order','third order'], answer:1, explain:'t<sub>½</sub> = ln 2/k constant only for first order.'},
    {q:'A plot of ln[A] vs t with gradient −k indicates', options:['zero order','first order','second order','equilibrium'], answer:1, explain:'Integrated first-order law: ln[A] = −kt + ln[A]<sub>0</sub>.'},
    {q:'The rate-determining step is', options:['the fastest step','the slowest step','the last step','the catalysed step'], answer:1, explain:'The slowest step bottlenecks the whole reaction.'},
    {q:'If rate = k[(CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>CBr], OH<sup>−</sup> is', options:['in the rds','not in the rds (reacts after it)','a catalyst','zero order overall'], answer:1, explain:'Zero order in OH<sup>−</sup> → it participates after the slow step (S<sub>N</sub>1).'},
    {q:'For rate = k[A][B]<sup>2</sup>, the overall order is', options:['2','3','4','1'], answer:1, explain:'1 + 2 = 3.'},
    {q:'A zero-order reactant means', options:['it is absent','rate is independent of its concentration','it is a catalyst','reaction is slow'], answer:1, explain:'Rate ∝ [X]<sup>0</sup> = 1: changing [X] does not change rate.'},
    {q:'1/[A] vs t linear with positive gradient indicates', options:['first order','second order','zero order','no reaction'], answer:1, explain:'Second-order integrated law: 1/[A] = kt + 1/[A]<sub>0</sub>.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-che-t27', title:'Group 2 (A Level)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-che-t27-l1', title:'Thermal stability of carbonates and nitrates', html:'<h3>Thermal stability of carbonates and nitrates</h3><p><strong>Thermal stability increases down Group 2</strong> (and Group 1): MgCO<sub>3</sub> decomposes on gentle heating; BaCO<sub>3</sub> needs strong heating. Same for nitrates: 2Mg(NO<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub> → 2MgO + 4NO<sub>2</sub> + O<sub>2</sub>; Ba(NO<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub> needs much higher temperature.</p><p>Explanation: the <strong>polarising power</strong> of the cation distorts the carbonate/nitrate ion, weakening C–O/N–O bonds. Smaller, more highly charged cations (Mg<sup>2+</sup>) polarise more → easier decomposition. Down the group, larger cations polarise less → more stable.</p><p>Group 1 carbonates (except Li<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub>) do not decompose: +1 cations polarise too weakly. Li<sup>+</sup> is small enough to decompose Li<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub>.</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t27-l2', title:'Flame tests and uses revisited', html:'<h3>Flame tests and uses revisited</h3><p><strong>Flame colours:</strong> Ca<sup>2+</sup> brick-red/orange-red, Sr<sup>2+</sup> crimson/red, Ba<sup>2+</sup> apple-green/pale green, Mg<sup>2+</sup> no colour (colourless). Use a nichrome wire cleaned in conc. HCl.</p><p>Explanation: heat excites electrons; on falling back they emit photons of characteristic wavelengths (line spectra — links to atomic emission).</p><p>Uses: Sr and Ba compounds in fireworks (red/green); Mg in flares (bright white); CaO/Ca(OH)<sub>2</sub> in agriculture and construction; BaSO<sub>4</sub> in X-ray imaging.</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t27-l3', title:'Lattice energy and solubility: quantitative reasoning', html:'<h3>Lattice energy and solubility: quantitative reasoning</h3><p>Down Group 2, cation radius increases → <strong>lattice energy</strong> less exothermic (ions further apart) and <strong>hydration enthalpy</strong> less exothermic (lower charge density).</p><p>For <strong>hydroxides</strong>: ΔH<sub>hyd</sub> falls faster than ΔH<sub>latt</sub> → ΔH<sub>sol</sub> becomes more exothermic → solubility increases. For <strong>sulfates</strong>: the large SO<sub>4</sub><sup>2−</sup> means lattice energy falls slowly while hydration still falls fast → ΔH<sub>sol</sub> less favourable → solubility decreases.</p><p>Exam reasoning: always frame solubility trends as a competition between lattice-breaking and hydration, identifying which term dominates for the anion size involved.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'Thermal stability of Group 2 carbonates down the group', options:['decreases','increases','is constant','increases then decreases'], answer:1, explain:'Larger cations polarise CO<sub>3</sub><sup>2−</sup> less → harder to decompose.'},
    {q:'MgCO<sub>3</sub> decomposes more easily than BaCO<sub>3</sub> because', options:['Mg is heavier','Mg<sup>2+</sup> has greater polarising power','BaCO<sub>3</sub> is covalent','MgCO<sub>3</sub> is soluble'], answer:1, explain:'Small Mg<sup>2+</sup> distorts the carbonate ion more, weakening C–O bonds.'},
    {q:'Heating Mg(NO<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub> gives', options:['Mg(NO<sub>2</sub>)<sub>2</sub>','MgO + NO<sub>2</sub> + O<sub>2</sub>','Mg + N<sub>2</sub>','Mg(OH)<sub>2</sub>'], answer:1, explain:'2Mg(NO<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub> → 2MgO + 4NO<sub>2</sub> + O<sub>2</sub>.'},
    {q:'Sr<sup>2+</sup> flame colour is', options:['brick-red','crimson','apple-green','lilac'], answer:1, explain:'Strontium: crimson/red flame.'},
    {q:'Ba<sup>2+</sup> flame colour is', options:['crimson','brick-red','apple-green','yellow'], answer:2, explain:'Barium: pale apple-green.'},
    {q:'Flame colours arise from', options:['burning the metal','electrons falling to lower levels emitting photons','oxidation','ionisation'], answer:1, explain:'Excited electrons emit characteristic wavelengths on de-excitation.'},
    {q:'Group 1 carbonates (except lithium) do not decompose on heating because', options:['they are covalent','M<sup>+</sup> polarising power is too low','they melt first','they are insoluble'], answer:1, explain:'+1 charge and larger size → insufficient distortion of CO<sub>3</sub><sup>2−</sup>.'},
    {q:'Solubility of hydroxides increases down Group 2 because', options:['lattice energy increases','hydration enthalpy falls faster than lattice energy','ions get smaller','water gets hotter'], answer:1, explain:'ΔH<sub>sol</sub> = −ΔH<sub>latt</sub> + ΣΔH<sub>hyd</sub>: hydration term dominates the trend.'},
    {q:'Which is most thermally stable?', options:['MgCO<sub>3</sub>','CaCO<sub>3</sub>','SrCO<sub>3</sub>','BaCO<sub>3</sub>'], answer:3, explain:'Largest cation → least polarising → most stable.'},
    {q:'Polarising power depends on', options:['mass only','charge density (charge/size)','colour','melting point'], answer:1, explain:'High charge + small radius = strong distortion of anions.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-che-t28', title:'Chemistry of transition elements', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-che-t28-l1', title:'Properties of transition metals', html:'<h3>Properties of transition metals</h3><p><strong>Transition element:</strong> a d-block element forming at least one ion with a partially filled d subshell (so Zn and Sc are excluded). Characteristic properties: <strong>variable oxidation states</strong> (4s and 3d energies similar), <strong>coloured ions</strong>, <strong>catalytic activity</strong>, <strong>complex formation</strong>, paramagnetism.</p><p>Examples: Fe (+2, +3), Cu (+1, +2), Mn (+2, +4, +7), Cr (+3, +6), V (+2 to +5). Colours: Cu<sup>2+</sup>(aq) blue, Fe<sup>2+</sup> pale green, Fe<sup>3+</sup> yellow/brown, MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>−</sup> purple, Cr<sub>2</sub>O<sub>7</sub><sup>2−</sup> orange, CrO<sub>4</sub><sup>2−</sup> yellow.</p><p>Catalysts: Fe (Haber), V<sub>2</sub>O<sub>5</sub> (Contact), Ni (hydrogenation), Pt/Rh (catalytic converters). Transition metals provide alternative routes via variable oxidation states (homogeneous) or surface adsorption (heterogeneous).</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t28-l2', title:'Complex ions: shapes and bonding', html:'<h3>Complex ions: shapes and bonding</h3><p><strong>Complex:</strong> central metal ion bonded to <strong>ligands</strong> (electron-pair donors) by dative bonds. <strong>Coordination number:</strong> number of dative bonds — commonly 6 (octahedral, e.g. [Cu(H<sub>2</sub>O)<sub>6</sub>]<sup>2+</sup>) or 4 (tetrahedral, e.g. [CoCl<sub>4</sub>]<sup>2−</sup>; square planar for Pt<sup>2+</sup>).</p><p><strong>Ligands:</strong> monodentate (H<sub>2</sub>O, NH<sub>3</sub>, Cl<sup>−</sup>, CN<sup>−</sup>), bidentate (ethane-1,2-diamine “en”, ethanedioate C<sub>2</sub>O<sub>4</sub><sup>2−</sup>), hexadentate (EDTA). <strong>Chelates</strong> (multidentate) are extra stable — the <strong>chelate effect</strong> (entropy: one ligand replaces several).</p><p><strong>Ligand exchange:</strong> [Cu(H<sub>2</sub>O)<sub>6</sub>]<sup>2+</sup> + 4NH<sub>3</sub> → [Cu(NH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>4</sub>(H<sub>2</sub>O)<sub>2</sub>]<sup>2+</sup> (deep blue) + 4H<sub>2</sub>O; + conc. HCl → [CuCl<sub>4</sub>]<sup>2−</sup> (yellow). Colour changes track the ligand.</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t28-l3', title:'Colour, redox and precipitation reactions', html:'<h3>Colour, redox and precipitation reactions</h3><p><strong>Origin of colour:</strong> ligands split the d orbitals into two energy levels; electrons absorb visible light to jump the gap (d–d transitions) — the transmitted/reflected colour is the complement of absorbed. Different ligands → different splitting (ΔE) → different colours. Zn<sup>2+</sup> (d<sup>10</sup>) and Sc<sup>3+</sup> (d<sup>0</sup>) are colourless — no d–d transition possible.</p><p><strong>Redox:</strong> Fe<sup>2+</sup>/Fe<sup>3+</sup> interconvert (manganate titration); Cu<sup>2+</sup> + I<sup>−</sup> → CuI + I<sub>2</sub> (iodometric copper determination). <strong>Stability constants K<sub>stab</sub>:</strong> larger = more stable complex; ligand exchange favours the more stable complex.</p><p><strong>With NaOH/NH<sub>3</sub>:</strong> Cu<sup>2+</sup> → blue Cu(OH)<sub>2</sub> ppt (dissolves in excess NH<sub>3</sub> → deep blue complex); Fe<sup>2+</sup> → dirty green Fe(OH)<sub>2</sub> (darkens on standing — oxidised to Fe<sup>3+</sup>); Fe<sup>3+</sup> → brown Fe(OH)<sub>3</sub> ppt.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'A transition element must form an ion with', options:['a full d subshell','a partially filled d subshell','no d electrons','a full s subshell'], answer:1, explain:'Definition: at least one ion with partially filled d orbitals (excludes Zn, Sc).'},
    {q:'Which is NOT a transition element?', options:['Fe','Cu','Zn','Mn'], answer:2, explain:'Zn<sup>2+</sup> is d<sup>10</sup> (full) — never partially filled.'},
    {q:'Coordination number 6 gives shape', options:['tetrahedral','octahedral','square planar','linear'], answer:1, explain:'Six ligands arrange octahedrally.'},
    {q:'EDTA is a', options:['monodentate ligand','hexadentate ligand','metal ion','precipitate'], answer:1, explain:'EDTA donates 6 electron pairs — hexadentate chelate.'},
    {q:'Transition metal ions are coloured because', options:['they reflect all light','d–d electron transitions absorb visible light','they are radioactive','ligands are coloured'], answer:1, explain:'Ligand-field splitting: absorbed wavelengths removed, complement seen.'},
    {q:'Zn<sup>2+</sup> complexes are colourless because', options:['Zn is not a metal','d<sup>10</sup>: no d–d transition possible','ligands are absent','they absorb all light'], answer:1, explain:'Full d subshell: no vacancy for the excited electron.'},
    {q:'[Cu(H<sub>2</sub>O)<sub>6</sub>]<sup>2+</sup> + excess NH<sub>3</sub> gives', options:['a pale blue ppt','a deep blue solution','a yellow solution','no change'], answer:1, explain:'Ligand exchange → [Cu(NH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>4</sub>(H<sub>2</sub>O)<sub>2</sub>]<sup>2+</sup>, deep blue.'},
    {q:'Fe<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + NaOH gives a precipitate that', options:['stays green','darkens on standing (oxidised to Fe<sup>3+</sup>)','dissolves','turns white'], answer:1, explain:'Dirty-green Fe(OH)<sub>2</sub> oxidises in air to brown Fe(OH)<sub>3</sub>.'},
    {q:'A larger stability constant K<sub>stab</sub> means', options:['a less stable complex','a more stable complex','faster exchange','a weaker ligand'], answer:1, explain:'K<sub>stab</sub> = [complex]/([metal][ligand]<sup>n</sup>): larger → more stable.'},
    {q:'Catalytic activity of transition metals is due to', options:['their colour','variable oxidation states and surface adsorption','high density','magnetism'], answer:1, explain:'They cycle oxidation states (homogeneous) or adsorb reactants (heterogeneous).'}
   ]}
,
  {id:'al-che-t29', title:'An introduction to A Level organic chemistry (A Level)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-che-t29-l1', title:'Benzene: structure and stability', html:'<h3>Benzene: structure and stability</h3><p><strong>Benzene</strong> C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>6</sub>: planar hexagonal ring with <strong>delocalised π electrons</strong> (six p orbitals overlapping above and below the ring). Evidence: (1) all C–C bonds equal length (intermediate between single and double); (2) hydrogenation enthalpy less exothermic than expected for three double bonds (~150 kJ mol<sup>-1</sup> stabilisation); (3) undergoes substitution, not addition.</p><p>The Kekulé structure (alternating double bonds) is a useful drawing but the delocalised model explains the chemistry. Represent benzene as a hexagon with a circle.</p><p>Consequence: benzene resists addition (would destroy delocalisation) and undergoes <strong>electrophilic substitution</strong> instead — preserving the aromatic π system.</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t29-l2', title:'Electrophilic substitution of benzene', html:'<h3>Electrophilic substitution of benzene</h3><p><strong>Nitration:</strong> conc. HNO<sub>3</sub> + conc. H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>, 50 °C → nitrobenzene. Electrophile: NO<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup> (from 2H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> + HNO<sub>3</sub> → 2HSO<sub>4</sub><sup>−</sup> + NO<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup> + H<sub>3</sub>O<sup>+</sup>).</p><p><strong>Bromination:</strong> Br<sub>2</sub>/FeBr<sub>3</sub> (Fe catalyst polarises Br<sub>2</sub>) → bromobenzene. <strong>Friedel–Crafts alkylation/acylation:</strong> RCl/AlCl<sub>3</sub> → alkylbenzene (electrophile R<sup>+</sup>).</p><p>Mechanism: electrophile attacks π system → <strong>arenium (Wheland) intermediate</strong> (positive charge delocalised, aromaticity temporarily lost) → base removes H<sup>+</sup> → aromaticity restored. The slow step is attack on the ring.</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t29-l3', title:'Phenol and phenylamine revisited', html:'<h3>Phenol and phenylamine revisited</h3><p>–OH and –NH<sub>2</sub> <strong>activate</strong> the ring (lone pair donated into π system): phenol brominates with Br<sub>2</sub>(aq) at room temperature without catalyst → 2,4,6-tribromophenol (white ppt). Nitro group <strong>deactivates</strong> (withdraws electrons).</p><p>Directing effects: activators are 2,4-directing (ortho/para); deactivators (NO<sub>2</sub>, and –NH<sub>3</sub><sup>+</sup>) are 3-directing (meta). Methylbenzene nitration gives ortho/para mixture.</p><p>Uses: nitrobenzene → phenylamine (Sn/HCl reduction) → dyes, pharmaceuticals; phenol → resins, disinfectants. Benzene itself is carcinogenic — avoid direct use; methylbenzene is a safer solvent.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'Benzene’s extra stability is shown by', options:['its boiling point','lower-than-expected hydrogenation enthalpy','its smell','its density'], answer:1, explain:'Hydrogenation releases ~150 kJ mol<sup>-1</sup> less than three isolated C=C: delocalisation stabilisation.'},
    {q:'All C–C bonds in benzene are', options:['alternating single/double','equal length (intermediate)','double','triple'], answer:1, explain:'Delocalised π system: bond order 1.5 throughout.'},
    {q:'Benzene undergoes substitution rather than addition to', options:['save energy','preserve aromatic delocalisation','avoid catalysts','form polymers'], answer:1, explain:'Addition would destroy the stable delocalised π system.'},
    {q:'Nitration of benzene needs', options:['HNO<sub>3</sub> alone','conc. HNO<sub>3</sub> + conc. H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>, 50 °C','Br<sub>2</sub>/Fe','UV light'], answer:1, explain:'Mixed acid generates the NO<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup> electrophile.'},
    {q:'The electrophile in nitration is', options:['NO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup>','NO<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>','H<sup>+</sup>','NO'], answer:1, explain:'Nitronium ion from HNO<sub>3</sub> + 2H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>.'},
    {q:'Bromination of benzene needs FeBr<sub>3</sub> because', options:['benzene is too reactive','it polarises Br<sub>2</sub> to generate Br<sup>+</sup>','it absorbs HBr','it cools the mixture'], answer:1, explain:'Halogen carrier: polarises Br–Br, creating the electrophile.'},
    {q:'The intermediate in electrophilic substitution is the', options:['carbocation','arenium (Wheland) intermediate','radical','carbanion'], answer:1, explain:'Delocalised positive σ-complex, then deprotonation restores aromaticity.'},
    {q:'–OH on benzene is', options:['deactivating, meta-directing','activating, ortho/para-directing','deactivating, ortho-directing','inert'], answer:1, explain:'Lone-pair donation activates; positions 2,4,6 favoured.'},
    {q:'Nitrobenzene is reduced to phenylamine using', options:['H<sub>2</sub>/Ni','Sn + conc. HCl (then NaOH)','K<sub>2</sub>Cr<sub>2</sub>O<sub>7</sub>','Br<sub>2</sub>'], answer:1, explain:'Sn/HCl reduces –NO<sub>2</sub> → –NH<sub>2</sub> (via the ammonium salt).'},
    {q:'Phenol gives a white precipitate with bromine water because the ring is', options:['deactivated','highly activated by –OH','saturated','basic'], answer:1, explain:'Activation allows tribromination at room temperature without a catalyst.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-che-t30', title:'Hydrocarbons (A Level)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-che-t30-l1', title:'Alkane reactions in depth', html:'<h3>Alkane reactions in depth</h3><p>Free-radical substitution: initiation, propagation, termination (see AS). <strong>Further substitution</strong> gives a mixture of mono-, di-, tri-substituted products — poor for synthesis unless one product dominates (excess alkane, or equivalent H’s).</p><p><strong>Cracking:</strong> long alkanes → shorter alkanes + alkenes + H<sub>2</sub>. <strong>Catalytic cracking:</strong> zeolite catalyst, ~450 °C, slight pressure (makes branched alkanes/alkenes for petrol). <strong>Steam cracking:</strong> ~900 °C (makes ethene for polymers). <strong>Thermal cracking:</strong> high T, high p.</p><p><strong>Reforming:</strong> straight-chain alkanes → branched/cyclic/aromatic (Pt catalyst) — raises octane number of petrol.</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t30-l2', title:'Alkene stereoisomerism and addition', html:'<h3>Alkene stereoisomerism and addition</h3><p><strong>Cis–trans (E/Z):</strong> but-2-ene exists as cis (CH<sub>3</sub> groups same side) and trans. Trans is more stable (less steric repulsion), higher melting point; cis often higher boiling point (polar). Distinguish by: trans decolourises Br<sub>2</sub> identically — chemical tests cannot distinguish; use physical properties or NMR.</p><p><strong>E/Z notation:</strong> assign priority (Cahn–Ingold–Prelog) to each substituent on each C; E = high-priority groups opposite, Z = same side. Needed when cis/trans is ambiguous.</p><p>Electrophilic addition to conjugated dienes and Markovnikov regioselectivity via carbocation stability (see AS topic 14) — apply to exam mechanisms with curly arrows: arrow from π bond to E<sup>+</sup>, then from Nu<sup>−</sup> to carbocation.</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t30-l3', title:'Fuels, octane and the environment', html:'<h3>Fuels, octane and the environment</h3><p><strong>Petrol:</strong> C<sub>5</sub>–C<sub>10</sub> fraction. <strong>Octane number:</strong> resistance to premature ignition (knocking); branched/cyclic/aromatic hydrocarbons have higher octane numbers — hence reforming and oxygenates (ethanol blending).</p><p>Pollutants: CO (incomplete combustion), unburned hydrocarbons, NO<sub>x</sub> (high-T N<sub>2</sub> + O<sub>2</sub>), SO<sub>2</sub> (sulfur impurities), particulates (diesel). <strong>Catalytic converters</strong> (Pt/Rh/Pd): oxidise CO/hydrocarbons, reduce NO<sub>x</sub>.</p><p>Alternatives: hydrogen fuel (only H<sub>2</sub>O product), biofuels (carbon-neutral cycle), electric vehicles (zero tailpipe emissions; lifecycle depends on electricity source).</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'Catalytic cracking uses', options:['900 °C steam','zeolite catalyst, ~450 °C','high pressure H<sub>2</sub>','UV light'], answer:1, explain:'Zeolite, ~450 °C, slight pressure → branched alkanes + alkenes for petrol.'},
    {q:'Steam cracking mainly produces', options:['diesel','ethene for polymers','methane','lubricants'], answer:1, explain:'~900 °C steam: makes ethene/propene feedstock.'},
    {q:'Trans-but-2-ene vs cis-but-2-ene: trans has', options:['lower melting point','higher melting point (better packing)','identical properties','no dipole'], answer:1, explain:'Trans packs better in a lattice → higher mp; cis is polar → higher bp.'},
    {q:'E/Z notation is needed when', options:['molecules are small','cis/trans labels are ambiguous','there is no double bond','rings are present'], answer:1, explain:'Cahn–Ingold–Prelog priorities resolve ambiguous cases.'},
    {q:'A high octane number means', options:['more energy','greater resistance to knocking','higher boiling point','more pollution'], answer:1, explain:'Octane rating = anti-knock quality.'},
    {q:'Reforming converts straight-chain alkanes into', options:['shorter alkanes','branched/cyclic/aromatics (higher octane)','alkenes','alcohols'], answer:1, explain:'Pt-catalysed isomerisation/cyclisation raises octane number.'},
    {q:'In free-radical substitution, using excess alkane', options:['speeds initiation','favours mono-substitution','causes explosion','forms polymers'], answer:1, explain:'Excess RH makes Cl· most likely to meet unsubstituted alkane.'},
    {q:'Curly arrows in mechanisms show', options:['atom movement','movement of electron pairs','reaction rates','energy changes'], answer:1, explain:'Arrows track electron pairs: from bond/lone pair to new bond/atom.'},
    {q:'Catalytic converters reduce NO<sub>x</sub> to', options:['NO<sub>2</sub>','N<sub>2</sub>','NH<sub>3</sub>','N<sub>2</sub>O'], answer:1, explain:'2NO + 2CO → N<sub>2</sub> + 2CO<sub>2</sub> (Rh).'},
    {q:'Biofuels are considered carbon-neutral because', options:['they contain no carbon','the CO<sub>2</sub> released was recently absorbed growing the crop','they burn cold','they are synthetic'], answer:1, explain:'Closed carbon cycle: growth absorbs what combustion releases.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-che-t31', title:'Halogen compounds (A Level)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-che-t31-l1', title:'S<sub>N</sub>1 vs S<sub>N</sub>2 mechanisms', html:'<h3>S<sub>N</sub>1 vs S<sub>N</sub>2 mechanisms</h3><p><strong>S<sub>N</sub>2</strong> (primary halogenoalkanes): one step — nucleophile attacks as X<sup>−</sup> leaves; rate = k[RX][Nu<sup>−</sup>]; <strong>inversion</strong> of configuration. Steric hindrance slows it.</p><p><strong>S<sub>N</sub>1</strong> (tertiary): two steps — slow ionisation to a planar <strong>carbocation</strong>, then fast nucleophilic attack; rate = k[RX]; <strong>racemic mixture</strong> (attack from either face). Favoured by stable carbocations (3° > 2° > 1°) and polar protic solvents.</p><p>Evidence: (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>CBr hydrolysis rate is independent of [OH<sup>−</sup>] (first order overall) → S<sub>N</sub>1; CH<sub>3</sub>Br rate depends on both → S<sub>N</sub>2.</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t31-l2', title:'Reactivity and the C–X bond', html:'<h3>Reactivity and the C–X bond</h3><p>Hydrolysis rate: R–I > R–Br > R–Cl >> R–F. <strong>Bond enthalpy</strong> dominates: C–I (228 kJ mol<sup>-1</sup>) < C–Br (276) < C–Cl (346) < C–F (484). Bond polarity also matters but bond strength is the main trend.</p><p>Practical comparison: warm each halogenoalkane with aqueous AgNO<sub>3</sub> in ethanol; the fastest precipitate (AgI yellow fastest) = most reactive. Ethanol dissolves both the halogenoalkane and AgNO<sub>3</sub>.</p><p><strong>Fluorocarbons</strong> (C–F very strong) are extremely unreactive — used as inert coatings (PTFE), but CFCs caused ozone damage (see AS).</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t31-l3', title:'Organohalogen synthesis routes', html:'<h3>Organohalogen synthesis routes</h3><p>Making halogenoalkanes: alkene + HX/X<sub>2</sub> (electrophilic addition); alcohol + PCl<sub>5</sub>/SOCl<sub>2</sub>/conc. H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> + NaX (substitution); alkane + X<sub>2</sub>/UV (free radical — mixtures).</p><p>Using halogenoalkanes: → amines (NH<sub>3</sub>), → nitriles (KCN, chain extension), → alcohols (aq. NaOH), → alkenes (ethanolic KOH). Halogenoalkanes are key <strong>intermediates</strong> in multi-step synthesis.</p><p>Exam reasoning: choose the route giving the purest product — addition to alkenes and substitution of alcohols beat free-radical substitution (mixtures).</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'S<sub>N</sub>1 is favoured by', options:['primary halogenoalkanes','tertiary halogenoalkanes','strong nucleophiles','non-polar solvents'], answer:1, explain:'Stable 3° carbocation intermediate; polar protic solvents stabilise ions.'},
    {q:'S<sub>N</sub>2 gives', options:['a racemic mixture','inversion of configuration','retention','rearrangement'], answer:1, explain:'Backside attack in one step inverts the stereocentre.'},
    {q:'S<sub>N</sub>1 gives a racemic mixture because', options:['two products form','planar carbocation attacked from either face','the nucleophile is chiral','inversion happens twice'], answer:1, explain:'Flat carbocation: equal attack above/below → enantiomers 50:50.'},
    {q:'Rate = k[RX][OH<sup>−</sup>] indicates', options:['S<sub>N</sub>1','S<sub>N</sub>2','E1','no reaction'], answer:1, explain:'Both species in the rate equation → bimolecular slow step: S<sub>N</sub>2.'},
    {q:'Hydrolysis is fastest for', options:['R–F','R–Cl','R–Br','R–I'], answer:3, explain:'Weakest C–X bond (C–I) breaks most easily.'},
    {q:'C–F bond enthalpy is about', options:['228 kJ mol<sup>-1</sup>','484 kJ mol<sup>-1</sup>','346 kJ mol<sup>-1</sup>','150 kJ mol<sup>-1</sup>'], answer:1, explain:'C–F is exceptionally strong: 484 kJ mol<sup>-1</sup>.'},
    {q:'Comparing R–X reactivity with AgNO<sub>3</sub>/ethanol, the solvent ethanol is used to', options:['catalyse','dissolve both R–X and AgNO<sub>3</sub>','precipitate AgX','cool the mixture'], answer:1, explain:'A common solvent bringing organic halide and inorganic salt together.'},
    {q:'Alcohol → chloroalkane best uses', options:['Cl<sub>2</sub>/UV','SOCl<sub>2</sub>','HCl gas only','NaCl'], answer:1, explain:'SOCl<sub>2</sub> gives clean RCl + SO<sub>2</sub> + HCl (gases escape, pure product).'},
    {q:'The slow step of S<sub>N</sub>1 is', options:['nucleophilic attack','ionisation to the carbocation','deprotonation','solvent attack'], answer:1, explain:'R–X → R<sup>+</sup> + X<sup>−</sup>: breaking the bond is slow; capture of R<sup>+</sup> is fast.'},
    {q:'Tertiary halogenoalkanes undergo elimination most readily because', options:['they are polar','the alkene product is more substituted (stable)','they are soluble','S<sub>N</sub>2 is fast'], answer:1, explain:'Saytzeff: more substituted alkenes form faster; 3° gives the most substituted alkene.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-che-t32', title:'Hydroxy compounds (A Level)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-che-t32-l1', title:'Acidity of alcohols and phenol', html:'<h3>Acidity of alcohols and phenol</h3><p>Alcohols are very weakly acidic (pK<sub>a</sub> ~16–18): react with Na (→ alkoxide + H<sub>2</sub>) but not NaOH. <strong>Phenol</strong> (pK<sub>a</sub> ~10) reacts with NaOH: the phenoxide ion is stabilised by delocalisation of the negative charge into the ring.</p><p>Electron-withdrawing groups increase acidity; electron-donating decrease it. Compare: 2,4,6-trinitrophenol (picric acid) is a strong acid — three NO<sub>2</sub> groups stabilise the anion hugely.</p><p>Test distinction: phenol + Na<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub> → no CO<sub>2</sub> (weaker than carbonic acid); carboxylic acid → CO<sub>2</sub> evolved.</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t32-l2', title:'Oxidation in depth and distinguishing tests', html:'<h3>Oxidation in depth and distinguishing tests</h3><p>1° alcohol → aldehyde (distil) → acid (reflux); 2° → ketone; 3° → no reaction. <strong>Mechanism insight:</strong> oxidation removes H from the C–OH carbon and from O; tertiary carbons have no C–H to remove.</p><p>Distinguishing: <strong>acidified dichromate</strong> (orange→green for 1°/2°, no change for 3°); <strong>Lucas test</strong> (ZnCl<sub>2</sub>/HCl: 3° → immediate cloudiness of chloroalkane, 2° slow, 1° no reaction at room temp).</p><p>Worked exam answer: “Warm with acidified K<sub>2</sub>Cr<sub>2</sub>O<sub>7</sub>: propan-1-ol turns the solution green (oxidised); 2-methylpropan-2-ol leaves it orange (no reaction).”</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t32-l3', title:'Phenol reactions and uses', html:'<h3>Phenol reactions and uses</h3><p><strong>Nitration:</strong> conc. HNO<sub>3</sub> → 2,4,6-trinitrophenol (picric acid, explosive). <strong>Bromination:</strong> Br<sub>2</sub>(aq) → white ppt of 2,4,6-tribromophenol (test). <strong>With NaOH</strong> → sodium phenoxide; <strong>with Na</strong> → sodium phenoxide + H<sub>2</sub>.</p><p>Phenol is <strong>not</strong> an alcohol for naming/reactions: it does not undergo substitution of –OH by Cl (no PCl<sub>5</sub> reaction giving chlorobenzene simply), and the C–O bond has partial double-bond character.</p><p>Uses: antiseptics/disinfectants (carbolic acid), resins (Bakelite), pharmaceuticals (aspirin synthesis), dyes. Toxic and corrosive — causes chemical burns.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'Phenol is more acidic than ethanol because', options:['it has more H','the phenoxide charge delocalises into the ring','it is aromatic','it dissolves in water'], answer:1, explain:'Delocalisation stabilises the conjugate base, shifting dissociation right.'},
    {q:'Which reacts with NaOH?', options:['ethanol','phenol','2-methylpropan-2-ol','cyclohexane'], answer:1, explain:'Phenol (pK<sub>a</sub> ~10) is acidic enough; alcohols are not.'},
    {q:'Oxidation of a 2° alcohol removes', options:['only the O–H hydrogen','H from C–OH carbon and from O','two carbons','water'], answer:1, explain:'Forms C=O: loses the hydroxyl H and the C–H hydrogen.'},
    {q:'3° alcohols resist oxidation because', options:['they are basic','no H on the C–OH carbon','they are gases','they lack oxygen'], answer:1, explain:'No C–H to remove on the carbon bearing –OH.'},
    {q:'Lucas test: fastest cloudiness with', options:['1° alcohol','2° alcohol','3° alcohol','phenol'], answer:2, explain:'3° → immediate RCl formation (stable carbocation, S<sub>N</sub>1).'},
    {q:'Picric acid is', options:['a weak base','2,4,6-trinitrophenol (strong acid)','an alcohol','a ketone'], answer:1, explain:'Three NO<sub>2</sub> groups make it strongly acidic; also explosive.'},
    {q:'Phenol + PCl<sub>5</sub> gives', options:['chlorobenzene readily','no simple substitution (C–O has partial double-bond character)','benzene','benzyl chloride'], answer:1, explain:'Unlike alcohols, phenol’s C–O bond resists nucleophilic substitution.'},
    {q:'Distinguishing ethanol from phenol: add Na<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub> —', options:['both fizz','neither fizzes','phenol fizzes','ethanol fizzes'], answer:1, explain:'Neither is stronger than carbonic acid: no CO<sub>2</sub> from either (use NaOH/Br<sub>2</sub> water instead).'},
    {q:'Bakelite is made from', options:['ethene','phenol + methanal','styrene','urea only'], answer:1, explain:'Phenol–methanal condensation polymer.'},
    {q:'Acidified dichromate stays orange with', options:['propan-1-ol','propan-2-ol','2-methylpropan-2-ol','ethanol'], answer:2, explain:'Tertiary alcohol: no oxidation → no colour change.'}
   ]}
,
  {id:'al-che-t33', title:'Carboxylic acids and derivatives (A Level)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-che-t33-l1', title:'Acyl chloride reactions in depth', html:'<h3>Acyl chloride reactions in depth</h3><p>Mechanism: <strong>nucleophilic addition–elimination</strong>. Nucleophile attacks C<sup>δ+</sup> → tetrahedral intermediate → Cl<sup>−</sup> leaves → C=O reforms. All fast at room temperature.</p><p>With alcohols → esters (RCOCl + R′OH → RCOOR′ + HCl); with phenols → phenyl esters (aspirin synthesis: salicylic acid derivative + ethanoic anhydride); with ammonia/amines → amides; with water → acids (vigorous).</p><p>Advantage over direct acid reactions: no heating, no equilibrium, no catalyst needed — ideal for temperature-sensitive molecules like pharmaceuticals.</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t33-l2', title:'Relative reactivity of derivatives', html:'<h3>Relative reactivity of derivatives</h3><p>Reactivity towards nucleophiles: <strong>acyl chloride > acid anhydride > ester > amide</strong>. Reason: the leaving group ability and electron withdrawal — Cl<sup>−</sup> is the best leaving group; the amide’s N lone pair donates into C=O, deactivating it.</p><p>Hydrolysis rates mirror this: RCOCl fumes in moist air; anhydrides hydrolyse readily; esters need acid/alkali + heat; amides need vigorous heating with acid/alkali.</p><p>Exam reasoning: choose the derivative to match the conditions — acyl chlorides for fast room-temperature acylation; esters for slower, controlled release (prodrugs, fragrances).</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t33-l3', title:'Aspirin and polyesters', html:'<h3>Aspirin and polyesters</h3><p><strong>Aspirin</strong> (2-ethanoyloxybenzenecarboxylic acid): salicylic acid + ethanoic anhydride → aspirin + ethanoic acid (warm). An ester of a phenol — made via the anhydride (less vigorous than the acyl chloride).</p><p><strong>Terylene (PET):</strong> condensation of benzene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid (or its dimethyl ester) with ethane-1,2-diol → polyester + H<sub>2</sub>O/CH<sub>3</sub>OH. Draw repeat unit: –[O–CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>–O–CO–C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>4</sub>–CO]–.</p><p>Hydrolysis of PET (acid/alkali, heat) regenerates monomers — chemical recycling. Uses: fibres, bottles, film.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'Acyl chloride + nucleophile mechanism is', options:['S<sub>N</sub>1','nucleophilic addition–elimination','electrophilic addition','free radical'], answer:1, explain:'Attack at C=O → tetrahedral intermediate → Cl<sup>−</sup> expelled.'},
    {q:'Order of reactivity towards nucleophiles', options:['amide > ester > acyl chloride','acyl chloride > anhydride > ester > amide','ester > amide > acyl chloride','all equal'], answer:1, explain:'Leaving-group ability and C=O activation: RCOCl most reactive.'},
    {q:'Amides are least reactive because', options:['they are insoluble','N lone pair donates into C=O, deactivating it','they have no C=O','they are basic'], answer:1, explain:'Delocalisation reduces the δ+ on carbon.'},
    {q:'Aspirin is made from salicylic acid using', options:['ethanoyl chloride','ethanoic anhydride (warm)','ethanoic acid, reflux','NaOH'], answer:1, explain:'The anhydride esterifies the phenol –OH controllably.'},
    {q:'PET monomers are', options:['ethene + water','benzene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid + ethane-1,2-diol','lactic acid','styrene'], answer:1, explain:'Diacid + diol → polyester (Terylene).'},
    {q:'Chemical recycling of PET uses', options:['melting','acid/alkali hydrolysis to monomers','burning','dissolving in water'], answer:1, explain:'Hydrolysis breaks ester links, regenerating monomers.'},
    {q:'RCOCl + CH<sub>3</sub>OH gives', options:['an acid','an ester + HCl','an amide','an alkane'], answer:1, explain:'RCOCl + CH<sub>3</sub>OH → RCOOCH<sub>3</sub> + HCl.'},
    {q:'Why use an acyl chloride rather than the acid to make an ester?', options:['cheaper','fast at room temperature, no equilibrium limitation','safer','more soluble'], answer:1, explain:'No heating/catalyst needed and the reaction goes to completion.'},
    {q:'Hydrolysis of an ester with NaOH is irreversible because', options:['it is fast','the carboxylate ion is not electrophilic','NaOH is strong','water evaporates'], answer:1, explain:'RCOO<sup>−</sup> repels nucleophiles: no back-reaction.'},
    {q:'The repeat unit of PET contains', options:['amide links','ester links','ether links','C=C bonds'], answer:1, explain:'Polyester: –CO–O– links between aromatic diacid and diol.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-che-t34', title:'Nitrogen compounds (A Level)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-che-t34-l1', title:'Diazonium salts and azo dyes', html:'<h3>Diazonium salts and azo dyes</h3><p><strong>Diazotisation:</strong> phenylamine + NaNO<sub>2</sub>/HCl below 10 °C → <strong>benzenediazonium chloride</strong> C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>N<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>Cl<sup>−</sup>. Keep cold — diazonium salts decompose above ~10 °C (N<sub>2</sub> evolved).</p><p><strong>Coupling:</strong> diazonium + phenol (alkaline) or phenylamine → <strong>azo compound</strong> (–N=N–), e.g. bright orange/yellow dyes. Coupling occurs para to –OH/–NH<sub>2</sub> (activated positions).</p><p>Uses: azo dyes for textiles, indicators (methyl orange is an azo dye). The extended conjugation gives intense colour.</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t34-l2', title:'Reactions of diazonium salts', html:'<h3>Reactions of diazonium salts</h3><p>The –N<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup> group is an excellent leaving group (N<sub>2</sub>): warm with water → <strong>phenol</strong> + N<sub>2</sub>; with CuCN → benzonitrile; with KI → iodobenzene; with H<sub>3</sub>PO<sub>2</sub> → benzene (replaces –N<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup> with H).</p><p>This gives routes to compounds not directly accessible: e.g. nitrobenzene → phenylamine → diazonium → phenol (phenol cannot be made by direct –OH substitution).</p><p>Exam reasoning: diazonium chemistry is the bridge between –NO<sub>2</sub>/–NH<sub>2</sub> and –OH/–CN/–I on the ring — memorise the five transformations.</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t34-l3', title:'Amino acids, proteins and electrophoresis', html:'<h3>Amino acids, proteins and electrophoresis</h3><p><strong>Primary structure:</strong> amino acid sequence. <strong>Secondary:</strong> α-helix/β-pleated sheet (hydrogen bonds between C=O and N–H). <strong>Tertiary:</strong> 3D folding (H-bonds, ionic, disulfide bridges, hydrophobic interactions). <strong>Quaternary:</strong> multiple polypeptides (e.g. haemoglobin).</p><p><strong>Electrophoresis:</strong> at a given pH, amino acids have characteristic charges → migrate differently in an electric field. At the <strong>isoelectric point</strong> (net charge zero) an amino acid does not move.</p><p><strong>Hydrolysis</strong> of proteins (6M HCl, reflux) → amino acids, separated by chromatography/electrophoresis and identified (ninhydrin → purple).</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'Diazotisation requires', options:['NaNO<sub>2</sub>/HCl below 10 °C','HNO<sub>3</sub>/H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>','Br<sub>2</sub>/Fe','Sn/HCl'], answer:0, explain:'Phenylamine + nitrous acid (NaNO<sub>2</sub>/HCl) cold → diazonium salt.'},
    {q:'Diazonium salts must be kept cold because', options:['they freeze','they decompose releasing N<sub>2</sub> above ~10 °C','they evaporate','they polymerise'], answer:1, explain:'Thermal decomposition: C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>N<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup> → C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub><sup>+</sup> + N<sub>2</sub>.'},
    {q:'Coupling a diazonium salt with phenol gives', options:['an alkane','an azo dye (–N=N–)','a polymer','an ester'], answer:1, explain:'Electrophilic substitution para to –OH: coloured azo compound.'},
    {q:'Benzenediazonium chloride + warm water gives', options:['benzene','phenol + N<sub>2</sub>','phenylamine','chlorobenzene'], answer:1, explain:'–N<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup> replaced by –OH from water.'},
    {q:'Methyl orange is', options:['a pH meter','an azo dye used as indicator','a protein','a polymer'], answer:1, explain:'Azo (–N=N–) dye changing colour with pH.'},
    {q:'Protein secondary structure is held by', options:['peptide bonds','hydrogen bonds between C=O and N–H','ionic bonds','disulfide bridges'], answer:1, explain:'α-helix/β-sheet: H-bonding between backbone groups.'},
    {q:'Disulfide bridges stabilise', options:['primary structure','tertiary structure','peptide bonds','zwitterions'], answer:1, explain:'S–S covalent links between cysteine residues fold the chain.'},
    {q:'At its isoelectric point, an amino acid', options:['moves fastest','does not move in electrophoresis','is fully protonated','decomposes'], answer:1, explain:'Net charge zero → no migration in the electric field.'},
    {q:'Acid hydrolysis of a protein gives', options:['amines','amino acids','nitriles','sugars'], answer:1, explain:'Peptide bonds hydrolyse (6M HCl, reflux) → free amino acids.'},
    {q:'Ninhydrin is used to', options:['hydrolyse proteins','detect amino acids (purple)','denature enzymes','measure pH'], answer:1, explain:'Ninhydrin + amino acids → purple (Ruhemann’s purple).'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-che-t35', title:'Polymerisation (A Level)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-che-t35-l1', title:'Deducing monomers and repeat units', html:'<h3>Deducing monomers and repeat units</h3><p><strong>Addition polymer → monomer:</strong> find the 2-carbon repeating unit, restore C=C. <strong>Condensation polymer → monomers:</strong> locate the ester/amide links, add H<sub>2</sub>O (or HCl) across each link to regenerate diol/diacid or diamine/diacid.</p><p>Worked example: –[O–CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>–O–CO–CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>–CO]–<sub>n</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O at each ester → ethane-1,2-diol + butanedioic acid.</p><p><strong>Kevlar</strong> (polyamide): benzene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid + benzene-1,4-diamine → strong H-bonding between chains → bulletproof vests, ropes.</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t35-l2', title:'Polymer properties and structure', html:'<h3>Polymer properties and structure</h3><p>Properties depend on <strong>chain length</strong> (longer → stronger London forces → higher mp/strength), <strong>branching</strong> (LDPE branched → flexible; HDPE linear → rigid), <strong>cross-linking</strong> (thermosets rigid, do not melt; vulcanised rubber elastic), and <strong>intermolecular forces</strong> (H-bonding in nylon/Kevlar → strong fibres).</p><p><strong>Thermoplastics</strong> (polyethene, PVC): soften on heating — recyclable. <strong>Thermosets</strong> (Bakelite, epoxy): cross-linked — do not melt, char on heating.</p><p><strong>Plasticisers:</strong> small molecules between chains reduce IMFs → flexible PVC. Leaching of plasticisers (phthalates) is a health concern.</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t35-l3', title:'Degradable polymers and sustainability', html:'<h3>Degradable polymers and sustainability</h3><p><strong>Polylactic acid (PLA):</strong> from lactic acid (fermented corn starch) → polyester; compostable. <strong>Starch-based</strong> packaging; <strong>polyhydroxyalkanoates (PHA)</strong> made by bacteria.</p><p>Life-cycle assessment: consider feedstock (renewable?), energy of manufacture, use phase, disposal (landfill/incineration/recycling/composting). Incineration recovers energy but releases CO<sub>2</sub>; landfill wastes resources.</p><p>Exam reasoning: “suggest a sustainable alternative” — propose a condensation (hydrolysable) polymer from renewable feedstock, and note the trade-off (cost, properties, composting infrastructure).</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'To find monomers of a condensation polymer,', options:['restore C=C','add H<sub>2</sub>O across each ester/amide link','remove HCl','halve the chain'], answer:1, explain:'Reverse condensation: hydrolyse each link.'},
    {q:'Kevlar’s strength comes from', options:['C=C bonds','H-bonding between polyamide chains','cross-linking','ionic bonds'], answer:1, explain:'Aligned chains with extensive H-bonding → immense tensile strength.'},
    {q:'LDPE is flexible because', options:['it is short','branching prevents close packing','it has cross-links','it contains plasticiser'], answer:1, explain:'Branches keep chains apart → weaker IMFs → flexible.'},
    {q:'Thermosets differ from thermoplastics in that they', options:['melt on heating','are cross-linked and do not melt','are always soft','dissolve easily'], answer:1, explain:'3D cross-linked network: heating chars rather than melts.'},
    {q:'Plasticisers make PVC flexible by', options:['cross-linking','reducing IMFs between chains','shortening chains','adding colour'], answer:1, explain:'Small molecules space chains apart, weakening attractions.'},
    {q:'PLA is made from', options:['crude oil','lactic acid from fermented starch','ethene','styrene'], answer:1, explain:'Renewable corn-starch route → biodegradable polyester.'},
    {q:'Vulcanisation of rubber', options:['melts it','adds sulfur cross-links → elastic strength','dissolves it','bleaches it'], answer:1, explain:'S bridges between poly(isoprene) chains give elasticity.'},
    {q:'HDPE vs LDPE: HDPE is more rigid because', options:['it is branched','its linear chains pack closely → stronger IMFs','it is cross-linked','it is shorter'], answer:1, explain:'Linear chains crystallise: close packing, strong London forces.'},
    {q:'A life-cycle assessment should include', options:['only cost','feedstock, manufacture, use, disposal','only disposal','only energy'], answer:1, explain:'Full cradle-to-grave analysis.'},
    {q:'Condensation polymers are generally easier to recycle chemically because', options:['they melt easily','ester/amide links hydrolyse to monomers','they are cheap','they burn cleanly'], answer:1, explain:'Hydrolysis regenerates monomers for repolymerisation.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-che-t36', title:'Organic synthesis (A Level)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-che-t36-l1', title:'Multi-step synthesis with benzene', html:'<h3>Multi-step synthesis with benzene</h3><p>Benzene routes: nitration (HNO<sub>3</sub>/H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>) → nitrobenzene; reduction (Sn/HCl) → phenylamine; diazotisation → diazonium → phenol (warm H<sub>2</sub>O) / benzonitrile (CuCN) / iodobenzene (KI).</p><p>Worked example: benzene → 2,4,6-tribromophenylamine. Step 1: nitration → nitrobenzene (deactivates ring for controlled chemistry). Step 2: Sn/HCl → phenylamine. Step 3: Br<sub>2</sub>(aq) → white ppt of 2,4,6-tribromophenylamine. Order matters: brominate AFTER making the amine (activation) — direct bromination of benzene needs FeBr<sub>3</sub> and gives monobromination.</p><p>Worked example: benzene → benzoic acid. Step 1: Friedel–Crafts CH<sub>3</sub>Cl/AlCl<sub>3</sub> → methylbenzene. Step 2: reflux with alkaline KMnO<sub>4</sub> → benzoate; acidify → benzoic acid (side-chain oxidation).</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t36-l2', title:'Chiral synthesis and resolution', html:'<h3>Chiral synthesis and resolution</h3><p>Reactions creating a chiral centre from achiral reactants give a <strong>racemic mixture</strong> (e.g. HCN + ethanal) — planar intermediate attacked equally from both faces. Racemates are optically inactive (rotations cancel).</p><p><strong>Resolution:</strong> react with a chiral reagent → diastereomers (different physical properties) → separate → regenerate. Or use chiral chromatography/enzymes.</p><p>Importance: enantiomers can have very different biological effects (thalidomide tragedy) — pharmaceutical synthesis must control chirality.</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t36-l3', title:'Designing complete routes', html:'<h3>Designing complete routes</h3><p>Strategy: (1) identify target functional groups; (2) work backwards; (3) check each step’s reagents/conditions; (4) count carbons — extend with KCN or Friedel–Crafts; (5) protect sensitive groups if needed (beyond AS: consider order — e.g. nitrate before reducing).</p><p>Worked example: ethene → ethanoic acid. 1: HBr → bromoethane. 2: KCN (ethanol) → propanenitrile. 3: HCl(aq), heat → propanoic acid… wait, that gives propanoic acid (3 carbons). For ethanoic acid (2 carbons): ethene → (H<sub>3</sub>PO<sub>4</sub>) ethanol → (K<sub>2</sub>Cr<sub>2</sub>O<sub>7</sub>/H<sup>+</sup>, reflux) ethanoic acid. Always verify the carbon count!</p><p>Present routes as numbered steps with reagents, conditions and structures — examiners mark each correct transformation.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'Benzene → phenylamine needs', options:['HNO<sub>3</sub> then H<sub>2</sub>/Ni','nitration then Sn/HCl reduction','Br<sub>2</sub>/Fe then NH<sub>3</sub>','direct NH<sub>3</sub>'], answer:1, explain:'Nitration → nitrobenzene; Sn/conc. HCl reduces –NO<sub>2</sub> to –NH<sub>2</sub>.'},
    {q:'Methylbenzene → benzoic acid uses', options:['acidified dichromate, cold','alkaline KMnO<sub>4</sub>, reflux, then acidify','Br<sub>2</sub>/Fe','conc. HNO<sub>3</sub>'], answer:1, explain:'Side-chain oxidation: alkylbenzene → benzoate → benzoic acid.'},
    {q:'HCN + ethanal gives a racemic mixture because', options:['HCN is chiral','planar carbonyl attacked equally from both faces','the product is achiral','CN<sup>−</sup> is chiral'], answer:1, explain:'Equal probability of attack above/below the plane → 50:50 enantiomers.'},
    {q:'A racemic mixture is optically inactive because', options:['it has no chiral centres','equal and opposite rotations cancel','it is a meso compound','light is absorbed'], answer:1, explain:'50:50 enantiomers: rotations cancel exactly.'},
    {q:'Resolution of enantiomers can use', options:['distillation','a chiral reagent forming separable diastereomers','filtration','recrystallisation from water'], answer:1, explain:'Diastereomers have different physical properties → separable.'},
    {q:'Why does chirality matter in drugs?', options:['cost','enantiomers can have very different biological effects','solubility','colour'], answer:1, explain:'Thalidomide: one enantiomer therapeutic, the other teratogenic.'},
    {q:'Benzene → phenol via diazonium: the middle step is', options:['nitration','reduction to phenylamine then diazotisation','direct hydrolysis','bromination'], answer:1, explain:'NO<sub>2</sub> → NH<sub>2</sub> → N<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup> → OH (warm water).'},
    {q:'To extend an aromatic side chain, use', options:['KCN on the ring','Friedel–Crafts alkylation','nitration','sulfonation'], answer:1, explain:'RCl/AlCl<sub>3</sub> adds alkyl groups to the ring.'},
    {q:'Ethene → ethanoic acid: correct sequence', options:['HBr, KCN, hydrolysis','hydration, then K<sub>2</sub>Cr<sub>2</sub>O<sub>7</sub>/H<sup>+</sup> reflux','Br<sub>2</sub>, NaOH','H<sub>2</sub>, KMnO<sub>4</sub>'], answer:1, explain:'Ethene → ethanol (H<sub>3</sub>PO<sub>4</sub>), then oxidise fully under reflux. (KCN would add a carbon → propanoic acid.)'},
    {q:'In a written synthesis, you must state', options:['only reagents','reagents, conditions and structures for each step','only the product','yields'], answer:1, explain:'Marks for each correct transformation including conditions.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-che-t37', title:'Analytical techniques (A Level)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-che-t37-l1', title:'NMR: proton NMR', html:'<h3>NMR: proton NMR</h3><p><strong><sup>1</sup>H NMR:</strong> protons in different environments absorb at different <strong>chemical shifts δ (ppm)</strong> vs TMS (0 ppm). Number of signals = number of proton environments. <strong>Integration</strong> (area ratio) = relative numbers of protons.</p><p><strong>Spin–spin splitting (n+1 rule):</strong> a signal splits into n+1 peaks where n = equivalent protons on adjacent carbons. Singlet (no adjacent H), doublet, triplet, quartet patterns identify neighbouring groups: CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>– gives triplet + quartet.</p><p>Key shifts: –CH<sub>3</sub> ~0.9, –CH<sub>2</sub>– ~1.3, –OCH<sub>3</sub> ~3.7, –OH variable 1–6 (disappears with D<sub>2</sub>O shake), aldehyde –CHO ~9.7, aromatic ~7.2, –COOH ~11.</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t37-l2', title:'Carbon-13 NMR and combined analysis', html:'<h3>Carbon-13 NMR and combined analysis</h3><p><strong><sup>13</sup>C NMR:</strong> number of signals = number of carbon environments (no splitting — low abundance of <sup>13</sup>C). Key shifts: C=O (acids/esters) ~170, C=O (aldehyde/ketone) ~200, aromatic ~130, C–O ~60, alkyl 10–40.</p><p>Combined strategy: M<sub>r</sub> (mass spec) → molecular formula; IR → functional groups; <sup>13</sup>C NMR → carbon skeleton symmetry; <sup>1</sup>H NMR → proton environments, splitting, integration → propose and verify structure.</p><p>Worked example: M<sub>r</sub> 88, IR C=O 1740 (no broad O–H) → ester. <sup>1</sup>H NMR: triplet (3H) + quartet (2H) + singlet (3H) → CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>COO–CH<sub>3</sub>? Check: ethyl (triplet+quartet) + OCH<sub>3</sub> singlet → methyl propanoate… verify M<sub>r</sub>: C<sub>4</sub>H<sub>8</sub>O<sub>2</sub> = 88 ✓. Actually CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>COOCH<sub>3</sub> = methyl propanoate ✓.</p>'},
    {id:'al-che-t37-l3', title:'Chromatography', html:'<h3>Chromatography</h3><p><strong>Chromatography</strong> separates mixtures: components partition between a <strong>stationary phase</strong> and a <strong>mobile phase</strong>. <strong>R<sub>f</sub> = distance moved by spot / distance moved by solvent front</strong> — characteristic under fixed conditions.</p><p><strong>TLC:</strong> silica plate; visualise with UV or iodine/ninhydrin. <strong>Paper chromatography:</strong> for amino acids/sugars. <strong>GC:</strong> gases/volatiles separated by boiling point/affinity; <strong>HPLC:</strong> liquids under pressure — quantitative purity analysis.</p><p>Uses: checking reaction completion, identifying unknowns (compare R<sub>f</sub> with standards), forensic/drug testing. <strong>Retention time</strong> in GC/HPLC identifies components vs known standards.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'TMS is used in NMR as', options:['a solvent','the reference standard (δ = 0)','a reactant','an impurity'], answer:1, explain:'Tetramethylsilane: single sharp peak set to 0 ppm; inert and volatile.'},
    {q:'The n+1 rule predicts', options:['chemical shift','splitting by n adjacent equivalent protons','integration','number of signals'], answer:1, explain:'n equivalent neighbours → n+1 peaks.'},
    {q:'CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>– group shows', options:['two singlets','triplet (CH<sub>3</sub>) + quartet (CH<sub>2</sub>)','two doublets','quartet + triplet reversed'], answer:1, explain:'CH<sub>3</sub> (2 neighbours → triplet) + CH<sub>2</sub> (3 neighbours → quartet).'},
    {q:'An aldehyde proton appears at about', options:['1 ppm','3.7 ppm','7.2 ppm','9.7 ppm'], answer:3, explain:'–CHO: strongly deshielded, δ ~9–10.'},
    {q:'D<sub>2</sub>O shake removes signals from', options:['CH<sub>3</sub>','O–H and N–H protons','aromatic H','CH<sub>2</sub>'], answer:1, explain:'Exchangeable protons swap with deuterium (invisible in <sup>1</sup>H NMR).'},
    {q:'<sup>13</sup>C NMR signals are not split because', options:['carbons are identical','<sup>13</sup>C is low abundance (neighbours rarely <sup>13</sup>C)','no magnetic field','carbons have no spin'], answer:1, explain:'~1.1% abundance: adjacent <sup>13</sup>C pairs are vanishingly rare.'},
    {q:'A carboxylic acid C=O in <sup>13</sup>C NMR appears near', options:['200 ppm','170 ppm','60 ppm','20 ppm'], answer:1, explain:'Acid/ester carbonyl ~160–175 ppm; aldehyde/ketone ~190–210.'},
    {q:'R<sub>f</sub> is defined as', options:['solvent distance/spot distance','spot distance/solvent-front distance','time/volume','mass/charge'], answer:1, explain:'R<sub>f</sub> = distance spot moved ÷ distance solvent front moved.'},
    {q:'In GC, components are mainly separated by', options:['colour','boiling point and column affinity','mass','charge'], answer:1, explain:'Volatility + interaction with stationary phase determine retention time.'},
    {q:'Two compounds with the same R<sub>f</sub> under identical conditions', options:['are definitely identical','may be the same (needs a second technique)','are definitely different','cannot be separated'], answer:1, explain:'Same R<sub>f</sub> is consistent with identity but not proof — confirm independently.'}
   ]}
]});
