window.__saMergeCourse({id:'al-maths-9709', board:'alevel', title:'Mathematics (9709)', urdu:'', class:'A Level', icon:'📐', desc:'Cambridge A Level Mathematics 9709 — Pure, Mechanics and Statistics with lessons and quizzes.', chapters:[
  {id:'al-mat-t1', title:'Quadratics (P1)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-mat-t1-l1', title:'Solving quadratics', html:'<h3>Solving quadratics</h3><p>Solve ax<sup>2</sup> + bx + c = 0 by <strong>factorising</strong>, <strong>completing the square</strong>, or the <strong>formula</strong> x = (−b ± √(b<sup>2</sup> − 4ac))/2a. Completing the square: x<sup>2</sup> + 6x + 5 = (x + 3)<sup>2</sup> − 4 = 0 → x = −3 ± 2.</p><p>The <strong>discriminant</strong> b<sup>2</sup> − 4ac determines roots: > 0 two distinct real; = 0 one repeated; < 0 no real roots. For “two distinct real roots” questions, set discriminant > 0 and solve the inequality.</p><p>Worked example: kx<sup>2</sup> + 4x + k = 0 has equal roots when 16 − 4k<sup>2</sup> = 0 → k = ±2.</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t1-l2', title:'Quadratic graphs and inequalities', html:'<h3>Quadratic graphs and inequalities</h3><p>y = ax<sup>2</sup> + bx + c: parabola opening up (a > 0) or down (a < 0). <strong>Vertex</strong> at x = −b/2a (completing the square gives the exact turning point). y-intercept = c; x-intercepts = roots.</p><p><strong>Quadratic inequalities:</strong> sketch the graph. For x<sup>2</sup> − 3x − 4 < 0: roots −1 and 4, parabola up → negative between roots: −1 < x < 4. For ≥ 0 with parabola down: between roots inclusive.</p><p>Exam reasoning: always find roots first, sketch, then read off the required region. “< 0” = below the x-axis.</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t1-l3', title:'Simultaneous equations with a quadratic', html:'<h3>Simultaneous equations with a quadratic</h3><p>Solve one linear + one quadratic by <strong>substitution</strong>: e.g. y = 2x + 1 and y = x<sup>2</sup> − 3x + 5 → x<sup>2</sup> − 3x + 5 = 2x + 1 → x<sup>2</sup> − 5x + 4 = 0 → x = 1 or 4; y = 3 or 9.</p><p>Geometrically: intersections of a line and a curve. The discriminant of the resulting quadratic tells you: 2 intersections (line cuts), 1 (tangent), 0 (misses).</p><p>Worked example: for what k is y = kx + 2 tangent to y = x<sup>2</sup> + 1? x<sup>2</sup> + 1 = kx + 2 → x<sup>2</sup> − kx − 1 = 0; discriminant k<sup>2</sup> + 4 > 0 always → never tangent (always cuts twice).</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'The discriminant of ax<sup>2</sup> + bx + c = 0 is', options:['b<sup>2</sup> + 4ac','b<sup>2</sup> − 4ac','−b ± √(b<sup>2</sup>−4ac)','4ac − b<sup>2</sup>'], answer:1, explain:'b<sup>2</sup> − 4ac determines the nature of the roots.'},
    {q:'x<sup>2</sup> + 6x + 9 = 0 has', options:['two distinct roots','one repeated root','no real roots','three roots'], answer:1, explain:'Discriminant 36 − 36 = 0: repeated root x = −3.'},
    {q:'kx<sup>2</sup> + 4x + k = 0 has equal roots when k equals', options:['±1','±2','4','0'], answer:1, explain:'16 − 4k<sup>2</sup> = 0 → k<sup>2</sup> = 4 → k = ±2.'},
    {q:'Completing the square: x<sup>2</sup> + 8x + 7 =', options:['(x+4)<sup>2</sup> − 9','(x+4)<sup>2</sup> + 9','(x+8)<sup>2</sup> − 57','(x+2)<sup>2</sup> + 3'], answer:0, explain:'(x+4)<sup>2</sup> = x<sup>2</sup>+8x+16; subtract 9 to get +7.'},
    {q:'The vertex of y = 2x<sup>2</sup> − 8x + 5 is at', options:['x = 2','x = −2','x = 4','x = 8'], answer:0, explain:'x = −b/2a = 8/4 = 2.'},
    {q:'Solution of x<sup>2</sup> − 3x − 4 < 0 is', options:['x < −1 or x > 4','−1 < x < 4','x > 4','x < −1'], answer:1, explain:'Roots −1, 4; parabola opens up; negative between roots.'},
    {q:'y = x<sup>2</sup> − 5x + 6 meets y = 0 at', options:['x = 5 only','x = 2 and x = 3','x = −2 and −3','never'], answer:1, explain:'Factorises: (x−2)(x−3) = 0.'},
    {q:'If a line is tangent to a parabola, the combined equation has', options:['two solutions','one repeated solution','no solution','three solutions'], answer:1, explain:'Tangent: discriminant = 0, one repeated root.'},
    {q:'The quadratic formula gives x =', options:['(−b + √(b<sup>2</sup>−4ac))/2','(−b ± √(b<sup>2</sup>−4ac))/2a','(b ± √(b<sup>2</sup>+4ac))/2a','−b/2a only'], answer:1, explain:'Standard formula.'},
    {q:'For y = −x<sup>2</sup> + 4, the maximum value is', options:['0','4','2','−4'], answer:1, explain:'Parabola opens down, vertex at x = 0: y = 4.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-mat-t2', title:'Functions (P1)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-mat-t2-l1', title:'Domain, range and notation', html:'<h3>Domain, range and notation</h3><p>A <strong>function</strong> maps each x in the <strong>domain</strong> to exactly one y in the <strong>range</strong>. Notation: f(x) = x<sup>2</sup> − 3, with domain x ∈ ℝ. A mapping is a function only if it is <strong>one-to-one or many-to-one</strong> (never one-to-many).</p><p>Restricted domains: f(x) = √(x − 2) needs x ≥ 2; f(x) = 1/(x − 1) needs x ≠ 1. The range follows: for f(x) = x<sup>2</sup> + 1, range is f(x) ≥ 1.</p><p>Worked example: f(x) = 3x + 2, x ≥ 0. Domain: x ≥ 0; range: f(x) ≥ 2.</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t2-l2', title:'Composite and inverse functions', html:'<h3>Composite and inverse functions</h3><p><strong>Composite:</strong> gf(x) = g(f(x)) — apply f first. E.g. f(x) = x + 2, g(x) = x<sup>2</sup>: gf(x) = (x+2)<sup>2</sup>; fg(x) = x<sup>2</sup> + 2. Order matters! For gf to exist, the range of f must be within the domain of g.</p><p><strong>Inverse:</strong> f<sup>−1</sup> reverses f; exists only if f is <strong>one-to-one</strong>. Method: y = f(x), swap x and y, solve for y. E.g. f(x) = 2x + 3 → x = 2y + 3 → f<sup>−1</sup>(x) = (x − 3)/2.</p><p>Graphs of f and f<sup>−1</sup> are reflections in y = x. Check: ff<sup>−1</sup>(x) = x.</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t2-l3', title:'Graph transformations', html:'<h3>Graph transformations</h3><p>y = f(x) + a: translate up a. y = f(x + a): translate left a (inside = opposite). y = af(x): stretch vertically by a. y = f(ax): stretch horizontally by 1/a. y = −f(x): reflect in x-axis. y = f(−x): reflect in y-axis. y = |f(x)|: reflect negative parts above the x-axis.</p><p>Worked example: y = x<sup>2</sup> → y = 2(x − 3)<sup>2</sup> + 1: translate right 3, stretch vertically ×2, translate up 1. Vertex moves (0,0) → (3,1).</p><p>Exam reasoning: apply transformations in order; for “describe the transformation” state type + vector/scale factor.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'A relation is a function if it is', options:['one-to-many','one-to-one or many-to-one','many-to-many','undefined'], answer:1, explain:'Each domain element maps to exactly one range element.'},
    {q:'Domain of f(x) = √(x − 5) is', options:['x > 0','x ≥ 5','all real x','x ≤ 5'], answer:1, explain:'Need x − 5 ≥ 0.'},
    {q:'Range of f(x) = x<sup>2</sup> − 4 is', options:['f(x) ≥ 0','f(x) ≥ −4','all real','f(x) ≤ −4'], answer:1, explain:'Minimum at x = 0: −4.'},
    {q:'If f(x) = x + 1 and g(x) = 2x, gf(3) equals', options:['7','8','14','6'], answer:1, explain:'f(3) = 4; g(4) = 8.'},
    {q:'Inverse of f(x) = 3x − 2 is', options:['(x+2)/3','(x−2)/3','x/3 + 2','3x + 2'], answer:0, explain:'x = 3y − 2 → y = (x+2)/3.'},
    {q:'The graphs of f and f<sup>−1</sup> are reflections in', options:['y = 0','y = x','x = 0','y = −x'], answer:1, explain:'Swapping x and y reflects in the line y = x.'},
    {q:'y = f(x + 2) is the graph of y = f(x)', options:['shifted right 2','shifted left 2','shifted up 2','stretched'], answer:1, explain:'Inside the bracket: opposite direction.'},
    {q:'y = −f(x) reflects the graph in', options:['the y-axis','the x-axis','y = x','the origin'], answer:1, explain:'Negating outputs flips vertically.'},
    {q:'f(x) = x<sup>2</sup>, x ≥ 0 has an inverse because', options:['it is a parabola','it is one-to-one on this domain','it is even','it passes through origin'], answer:1, explain:'Restricted to x ≥ 0, each y has one x: invertible.'},
    {q:'y = |f(x)| is obtained by', options:['reflecting left half','reflecting parts below the x-axis above it','shifting up','doubling'], answer:1, explain:'Modulus makes all outputs non-negative.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-mat-t3', title:'Coordinate geometry (P1)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-mat-t3-l1', title:'Lines', html:'<h3>Lines</h3><p><strong>Gradient</strong> m = (y<sub>2</sub> − y<sub>1</sub>)/(x<sub>2</sub> − x<sub>1</sub>). <strong>Midpoint</strong> = ((x<sub>1</sub>+x<sub>2</sub>)/2, (y<sub>1</sub>+y<sub>2</sub>)/2). <strong>Length</strong> = √((x<sub>2</sub>−x<sub>1</sub>)<sup>2</sup> + (y<sub>2</sub>−y<sub>1</sub>)<sup>2</sup>).</p><p>Line through (x<sub>1</sub>, y<sub>1</sub>) with gradient m: y − y<sub>1</sub> = m(x − x<sub>1</sub>). <strong>Parallel:</strong> same gradient. <strong>Perpendicular:</strong> m<sub>1</sub>m<sub>2</sub> = −1.</p><p>Worked example: line through (1, 2) perpendicular to y = 3x + 1 has gradient −1/3: y − 2 = −(1/3)(x − 1).</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t3-l2', title:'Circles', html:'<h3>Circles</h3><p>Circle centre (a, b), radius r: <strong>(x − a)<sup>2</sup> + (y − b)<sup>2</sup> = r<sup>2</sup></strong>. Complete the square to find centre/radius: x<sup>2</sup> + y<sup>2</sup> − 4x + 6y − 3 = 0 → (x−2)<sup>2</sup> + (y+3)<sup>2</sup> = 16: centre (2, −3), r = 4.</p><p><strong>Tangent to a circle:</strong> radius to point of contact is perpendicular to the tangent. Find gradient of radius, then perpendicular gradient for the tangent equation.</p><p>Worked example: tangent to x<sup>2</sup> + y<sup>2</sup> = 25 at (3, 4): radius gradient 4/3 → tangent gradient −3/4: y − 4 = −(3/4)(x − 3).</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t3-l3', title:'Intersections of lines and curves', html:'<h3>Intersections of lines and curves</h3><p>Solve simultaneously (substitution). The <strong>discriminant</strong> of the resulting quadratic gives the number of intersections: > 0 two (secant), = 0 one (tangent), < 0 none.</p><p>Worked example: does y = 2x + 1 meet x<sup>2</sup> + y<sup>2</sup> = 10? Substitute: x<sup>2</sup> + (2x+1)<sup>2</sup> = 10 → 5x<sup>2</sup> + 4x − 9 = 0; discriminant 16 + 180 = 196 > 0 → two intersections.</p><p>For “find the tangent from an external point”, use discriminant = 0 to find the unknown gradient.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'Gradient of the line through (2, 3) and (6, 11) is', options:['2','1/2','8','4'], answer:0, explain:'(11−3)/(6−2) = 8/4 = 2.'},
    {q:'Midpoint of (1, 5) and (7, 3) is', options:['(4, 4)','(3, 4)','(4, 8)','(8, 8)'], answer:0, explain:'((1+7)/2, (5+3)/2) = (4, 4).'},
    {q:'A line perpendicular to y = 2x + 5 has gradient', options:['2','−2','−1/2','1/2'], answer:2, explain:'m<sub>1</sub>m<sub>2</sub> = −1 → m = −1/2.'},
    {q:'Centre and radius of (x−1)<sup>2</sup> + (y+2)<sup>2</sup> = 9', options:['(1, 2), 3','(1, −2), 3','(−1, 2), 9','(1, −2), 9'], answer:1, explain:'Centre (a, b) = (1, −2); r<sup>2</sup> = 9 → r = 3.'},
    {q:'x<sup>2</sup> + y<sup>2</sup> + 6x − 8y = 0 has centre', options:['(3, −4)','(−3, 4)','(6, −8)','(3, 4)'], answer:1, explain:'(x+3)<sup>2</sup> + (y−4)<sup>2</sup> = 25: centre (−3, 4).'},
    {q:'The tangent to a circle at a point is', options:['parallel to the radius','perpendicular to the radius','through the centre','a chord'], answer:1, explain:'Radius–tangent perpendicularity.'},
    {q:'Tangent to x<sup>2</sup> + y<sup>2</sup> = 25 at (3, 4) has equation', options:['4y = 3x','3x + 4y = 25','y = x + 1','3x − 4y = 0'], answer:1, explain:'Radius gradient 4/3; tangent −3/4: y−4 = −3/4(x−3) → 3x + 4y = 25.'},
    {q:'Line y = x + 5 and circle x<sup>2</sup> + y<sup>2</sup> = 25: discriminant of combined equation', options:['> 0','< 0','= 0','= 25'], answer:1, explain:'x<sup>2</sup>+(x+5)<sup>2</sup>=25 → 2x<sup>2</sup>+10x+25=0; Δ = 100−200 < 0: no intersection.'},
    {q:'Distance between (0, 0) and (3, 4) is', options:['7','5','25','12'], answer:1, explain:'√(9+16) = 5.'},
    {q:'Two lines are parallel if', options:['gradients multiply to −1','gradients are equal','intercepts are equal','they cross'], answer:1, explain:'Equal gradients → parallel (distinct lines).'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-mat-t4', title:'Circular measure (P1)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-mat-t4-l1', title:'Radians', html:'<h3>Radians</h3><p><strong>Radian:</strong> angle subtended by an arc equal in length to the radius. 360° = 2π rad; 180° = π rad. Convert: degrees × π/180 = radians.</p><p>Common: 30° = π/6, 45° = π/4, 60° = π/3, 90° = π/2. Always check calculator mode (radian) for trig calculus and these formulae.</p><p>Worked example: 150° = 150π/180 = 5π/6 rad.</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t4-l2', title:'Arc length and sector area', html:'<h3>Arc length and sector area</h3><p><strong>Arc length</strong> s = rθ (θ in radians). <strong>Sector area</strong> A = ½r<sup>2</sup>θ. Derivation: fraction θ/2π of the full circle: (θ/2π) × 2πr = rθ; (θ/2π) × πr<sup>2</sup> = ½r<sup>2</sup>θ.</p><p>Worked example: r = 6 cm, θ = 1.2 rad: s = 7.2 cm; A = ½ × 36 × 1.2 = 21.6 cm<sup>2</sup>.</p><p><strong>Segment area</strong> = sector − triangle = ½r<sup>2</sup>θ − ½r<sup>2</sup>sinθ = ½r<sup>2</sup>(θ − sinθ).</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t4-l3', title:'Solving problems with sectors', html:'<h3>Solving problems with sectors</h3><p>Perimeter of a segment = arc + 2 × (chord or radii as appropriate). For a sector: perimeter = 2r + rθ.</p><p>Worked example: sector area 20 cm<sup>2</sup>, radius 5 cm: 20 = ½ × 25 × θ → θ = 1.6 rad; arc = 8 cm; perimeter = 10 + 8 = 18 cm.</p><p>Exam reasoning: keep θ in radians throughout; only convert to degrees if asked. Watch for “minor/major” sector — use θ or 2π − θ.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'π radians equals', options:['90°','180°','360°','45°'], answer:1, explain:'180° = π rad.'},
    {q:'60° in radians is', options:['π/6','π/3','π/4','2π/3'], answer:1, explain:'60 × π/180 = π/3.'},
    {q:'Arc length formula (θ in radians)', options:['s = r<sup>2</sup>θ','s = rθ','s = θ/r','s = 2rθ'], answer:1, explain:'s = rθ by definition of the radian.'},
    {q:'Sector area formula', options:['A = r<sup>2</sup>θ','A = ½r<sup>2</sup>θ','A = 2πrθ','A = πrθ'], answer:1, explain:'A = (θ/2π)πr<sup>2</sup> = ½r<sup>2</sup>θ.'},
    {q:'r = 4 cm, θ = 2 rad: arc length is', options:['4 cm','8 cm','16 cm','2 cm'], answer:1, explain:'s = 4 × 2 = 8 cm.'},
    {q:'r = 4 cm, θ = 2 rad: sector area is', options:['8 cm<sup>2</sup>','16 cm<sup>2</sup>','32 cm<sup>2</sup>','4 cm<sup>2</sup>'], answer:1, explain:'A = ½ × 16 × 2 = 16 cm<sup>2</sup>.'},
    {q:'Segment area (minor) equals', options:['sector + triangle','sector − triangle','2 × sector','triangle − sector'], answer:1, explain:'Segment = ½r<sup>2</sup>(θ − sinθ).'},
    {q:'Perimeter of a sector (r, θ) is', options:['rθ','2r + rθ','2rθ','πr + 2r'], answer:1, explain:'Two radii + arc.'},
    {q:'A sector has area 15 cm<sup>2</sup> and r = 5 cm. θ is', options:['0.6 rad','1.2 rad','3 rad','6 rad'], answer:1, explain:'15 = ½ × 25θ → θ = 1.2.'},
    {q:'Which must be true to use s = rθ?', options:['θ in degrees','θ in radians','r in cm','θ acute'], answer:1, explain:'The formulae require radian measure.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-mat-t5', title:'Trigonometry (P1)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-mat-t5-l1', title:'Sine and cosine rules', html:'<h3>Sine and cosine rules</h3><p><strong>Sine rule:</strong> a/sinA = b/sinB = c/sinC (use for opposite pairs). <strong>Cosine rule:</strong> a<sup>2</sup> = b<sup>2</sup> + c<sup>2</sup> − 2bc cosA (use for SSS or SAS). <strong>Area</strong> = ½ab sinC.</p><p>Worked example: a = 7, b = 9, C = 60°: c<sup>2</sup> = 49 + 81 − 2×7×9×cos60° = 130 − 63 = 67 → c ≈ 8.19.</p><p><strong>Ambiguous case:</strong> SSA with sine rule can give two solutions (θ and 180° − θ) — check both against the triangle.</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t5-l2', title:'Trig graphs and equations', html:'<h3>Trig graphs and equations</h3><p>Know y = sin x, cos x, tan x shapes, periods (360°/2π for sin/cos; 180°/π for tan), and symmetries. <strong>Exact values:</strong> sin/cos/tan of 30°, 45°, 60° — memorise via triangles.</p><p>Solving: e.g. 2 sin x = 1 → sin x = 0.5 → x = 30°, 150° (in 0°–360°). Use CAST or symmetry: sin θ = sin(180° − θ); cos θ = cos(−θ); tan period 180°.</p><p>Worked example: cos 2x = 0.5 for 0 ≤ x ≤ 360°: let y = 2x (0–720°): y = 60°, 300°, 420°, 660° → x = 30°, 150°, 210°, 330°.</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t5-l3', title:'Identities', html:'<h3>Identities</h3><p><strong>tan θ = sin θ/cos θ</strong>; <strong>sin<sup>2</sup>θ + cos<sup>2</sup>θ = 1</strong>. Use to simplify and solve: e.g. solve 2 sin<sup>2</sup>x + cos x = 2 → 2(1 − cos<sup>2</sup>x) + cos x − 2 = 0 → 2cos<sup>2</sup>x − cos x = 0 → cos x(2cos x − 1) = 0.</p><p>Proving identities: work one side into the other, often via sin<sup>2</sup> + cos<sup>2</sup> = 1 or tan = sin/cos.</p><p>Exam reasoning: for equations mixing sin and cos, convert everything to one trig function first.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'Sine rule is', options:['a<sup>2</sup> = b<sup>2</sup> + c<sup>2</sup> − 2bc cosA','a/sinA = b/sinB = c/sinC','sinA = a/c','a = b cosC'], answer:1, explain:'Ratios of side to sine of opposite angle are equal.'},
    {q:'Use the cosine rule when given', options:['ASA','SSS or SAS','AAA','RHS'], answer:1, explain:'Cosine rule needs three sides or two sides + included angle.'},
    {q:'Area of triangle with sides 5, 7 and included angle 60°', options:['35','(35√3)/4','17.5','35√3'], answer:1, explain:'½ × 5 × 7 × sin60° = 17.5 × √3/2 = 35√3/4.'},
    {q:'sin 60° equals', options:['1/2','√3/2','√2/2','1'], answer:1, explain:'Exact value from the 30–60–90 triangle.'},
    {q:'Solutions of sin x = 0.5 in 0° ≤ x ≤ 360°', options:['30° only','30°, 150°','150°, 330°','30°, 210°'], answer:1, explain:'sin θ = sin(180° − θ).'},
    {q:'tan θ in terms of sin and cos', options:['sinθ + cosθ','sinθ/cosθ','cosθ/sinθ','1 − sinθ'], answer:1, explain:'tan θ = sin θ/cos θ.'},
    {q:'sin<sup>2</sup>θ + cos<sup>2</sup>θ equals', options:['0','1','2','tan<sup>2</sup>θ'], answer:1, explain:'Fundamental Pythagorean identity.'},
    {q:'Period of y = tan x is', options:['360°','180°','90°','720°'], answer:1, explain:'tan repeats every π (180°).'},
    {q:'If cos x = 0.5, tan x (acute x) is', options:['1/2','√3','1/√3','2'], answer:1, explain:'x = 60°: tan 60° = √3.'},
    {q:'The ambiguous case arises with', options:['SSS','SSA using the sine rule','SAS','AAA'], answer:1, explain:'SSA: sin gives θ or 180° − θ — check validity of both.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-mat-t6', title:'Series (P1)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-mat-t6-l1', title:'Arithmetic progressions', html:'<h3>Arithmetic progressions</h3><p><strong>AP:</strong> constant difference d. <strong>nth term:</strong> a + (n−1)d. <strong>Sum:</strong> S<sub>n</sub> = n/2(2a + (n−1)d) = n/2(a + l), where l = last term.</p><p>Worked example: 5, 9, 13, …: a = 5, d = 4. 20th term = 5 + 19×4 = 81. Sum of first 20 = 20/2(10 + 76) = 860.</p><p>To find n given S<sub>n</sub>: substitute and solve the quadratic (take the positive root).</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t6-l2', title:'Geometric progressions', html:'<h3>Geometric progressions</h3><p><strong>GP:</strong> constant ratio r. <strong>nth term:</strong> ar<sup>n−1</sup>. <strong>Sum:</strong> S<sub>n</sub> = a(1 − r<sup>n</sup>)/(1 − r) = a(r<sup>n</sup> − 1)/(r − 1).</p><p><strong>Sum to infinity:</strong> S<sub>∞</sub> = a/(1 − r), valid only for |r| < 1 (convergent). Derivation: as n → ∞, r<sup>n</sup> → 0.</p><p>Worked example: 12, 6, 3, …: r = 1/2, S<sub>∞</sub> = 12/(1/2) = 24. Check: partial sums 12, 18, 21, 22.5 → 24 ✓.</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t6-l3', title:'Sigma notation and applications', html:'<h3>Sigma notation and applications</h3><p><strong>Σ notation:</strong> Σ<sub>r=1</sub><sup>n</sup> f(r) = f(1) + … + f(n). E.g. Σ<sub>r=1</sub><sup>n</sup> (3r + 2): arithmetic with a = 5, d = 3 → n/2(10 + 3(n−1)).</p><p>Applications: compound interest (GP), depreciation, bouncing ball total distance (S<sub>∞</sub> with initial drop added separately).</p><p>Worked example: £1000 at 5% compound: after n years 1000(1.05)<sup>n</sup> — a GP with r = 1.05.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'nth term of an AP', options:['ar<sup>n−1</sup>','a + (n−1)d','n/2(2a+(n−1)d)','a + nd'], answer:1, explain:'Start at a, add d exactly (n−1) times.'},
    {q:'Sum of first n terms of an AP', options:['n(a + l)','n/2(a + l)','n/2(2a − (n−1)d)','a + (n−1)d'], answer:1, explain:'n/2 × (first + last).'},
    {q:'10th term of 3, 7, 11, …', options:['39','43','31','40'], answer:0, explain:'a=3, d=4: 3 + 9×4 = 39.'},
    {q:'nth term of a GP', options:['a + (n−1)d','ar<sup>n−1</sup>','ar<sup>n</sup>','a/r<sup>n</sup>'], answer:1, explain:'Multiply by r exactly (n−1) times.'},
    {q:'S<sub>∞</sub> = a/(1−r) is valid when', options:['r > 1','|r| < 1','r = 1','r < 0 only'], answer:1, explain:'Convergent GP: r<sup>n</sup> → 0.'},
    {q:'S<sub>∞</sub> of 20, 10, 5, …', options:['30','40','20','10'], answer:1, explain:'a=20, r=1/2: 20/(1/2) = 40.'},
    {q:'Σ<sub>r=1</sub><sup>n</sup> r equals', options:['n<sup>2</sup>','n(n+1)/2','n(n−1)/2','2n+1'], answer:1, explain:'AP 1..n: n/2(1+n).'},
    {q:'A ball dropped from 10 m rebounds to half the height each time. Total distance ≈', options:['20 m','30 m','15 m','10 m'], answer:1, explain:'10 + 2×(5/(1−1/2)) = 10 + 20 = 30 m (up+down after first drop).'},
    {q:'Which is a GP?', options:['2, 5, 8, 11','3, 6, 12, 24','1, 4, 9, 16','5, 5, 5, 5 (only)'], answer:1, explain:'Constant ratio 2. (5,5,5,5 is both AP d=0 and GP r=1.)'},
    {q:'Sum of first 15 terms of 2, 6, 10, …', options:['450','465','435','900'], answer:0, explain:'a=2, d=4: S = 15/2 × (2×2 + 14×4) = 7.5 × 60 = 450.'}
   ]}
,
  {id:'al-mat-t7', title:'Differentiation (P1)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-mat-t7-l1', title:'First principles and basic rules', html:'<h3>First principles and basic rules</h3><p><strong>Derivative from first principles:</strong> f′(x) = lim<sub>h→0</sub> [f(x+h) − f(x)]/h. For f(x) = x<sup>2</sup>: [(x+h)<sup>2</sup> − x<sup>2</sup>]/h = 2x + h → 2x.</p><p><strong>Power rule:</strong> d/dx(x<sup>n</sup>) = nx<sup>n−1</sup>. Differentiate term by term; the derivative of a constant is 0. E.g. y = 3x<sup>4</sup> − 2x + 7 → dy/dx = 12x<sup>3</sup> − 2.</p><p>Notation: dy/dx, f′(x), y′. The derivative is the <strong>gradient function</strong>.</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t7-l2', title:'Tangents, normals and stationary points', html:'<h3>Tangents, normals and stationary points</h3><p><strong>Tangent:</strong> gradient = dy/dx at the point; use y − y<sub>1</sub> = m(x − x<sub>1</sub>). <strong>Normal:</strong> perpendicular, gradient = −1/m.</p><p><strong>Stationary points:</strong> dy/dx = 0. Classify: <strong>second derivative test:</strong> d<sup>2</sup>y/dx<sup>2</sup> > 0 → minimum; < 0 → maximum; = 0 → test further (possible point of inflexion). Or check the sign change of dy/dx.</p><p>Worked example: y = x<sup>3</sup> − 3x<sup>2</sup>: dy/dx = 3x<sup>2</sup> − 6x = 0 → x = 0, 2. d<sup>2</sup>y/dx<sup>2</sup> = 6x − 6: at x=0 → −6 (max, y=0); at x=2 → 6 (min, y=−4).</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t7-l3', title:'Increasing/decreasing and connected rates', html:'<h3>Increasing/decreasing and connected rates</h3><p><strong>Increasing</strong> where dy/dx > 0; <strong>decreasing</strong> where dy/dx < 0. <strong>Point of inflexion:</strong> concavity changes (d<sup>2</sup>y/dx<sup>2</sup> changes sign); a stationary point of inflexion has dy/dx = 0 too (e.g. y = x<sup>3</sup> at x = 0).</p><p><strong>Connected rates:</strong> use the chain rule in reverse: dA/dt = dA/dr × dr/dt. E.g. balloon V = 4/3πr<sup>3</sup>, dV/dt given → dr/dt = (dV/dt)/(4πr<sup>2</sup>).</p><p>Exam reasoning: for “show x = a is a minimum”, evaluate d<sup>2</sup>y/dx<sup>2</sup> at a and confirm positive.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'d/dx(5x<sup>3</sup>) equals', options:['15x<sup>2</sup>','5x<sup>2</sup>','15x<sup>3</sup>','(5/4)x<sup>4</sup>'], answer:0, explain:'Power rule: 3 × 5x<sup>2</sup> = 15x<sup>2</sup>.'},
    {q:'The derivative from first principles is', options:['[f(x+h)+f(x)]/h','lim<sub>h→0</sub> [f(x+h)−f(x)]/h','f(x)/h','Δy×Δx'], answer:1, explain:'Definition of the derivative as a limit.'},
    {q:'Gradient of the tangent to y = x<sup>2</sup> at x = 3 is', options:['3','6','9','2'], answer:1, explain:'dy/dx = 2x = 6.'},
    {q:'Stationary points of y = x<sup>3</sup> − 3x are at', options:['x = 0 only','x = ±1','x = ±√3','x = 3'], answer:1, explain:'dy/dx = 3x<sup>2</sup> − 3 = 0 → x = ±1.'},
    {q:'At a minimum point, d<sup>2</sup>y/dx<sup>2</sup> is', options:['negative','positive','zero','undefined'], answer:1, explain:'Positive second derivative = concave up = minimum.'},
    {q:'y = x<sup>3</sup> has at x = 0 a', options:['maximum','minimum','stationary point of inflexion','undefined point'], answer:2, explain:'dy/dx = 3x<sup>2</sup> = 0 but sign does not change: point of inflexion.'},
    {q:'Normal to y = x<sup>2</sup> at (2, 4) has gradient', options:['4','−4','−1/4','1/4'], answer:2, explain:'Tangent gradient 4 → normal −1/4.'},
    {q:'d/dx(1/x<sup>2</sup>) equals', options:['−2/x<sup>3</sup>','2/x<sup>3</sup>','−1/x<sup>3</sup>','−2x<sup>−1</sup>'], answer:0, explain:'x<sup>−2</sup> → −2x<sup>−3</sup> = −2/x<sup>3</sup>.'},
    {q:'A curve is decreasing where', options:['dy/dx > 0','dy/dx < 0','dy/dx = 0','d<sup>2</sup>y/dx<sup>2</sup> > 0'], answer:1, explain:'Negative gradient = falling.'},
    {q:'If dA/dt = 10 and dA/dr = 4πr<sup>2</sup>, dr/dt equals', options:['40πr<sup>2</sup>','10/(4πr<sup>2</sup>)','4πr<sup>2</sup>/10','10 × 4πr<sup>2</sup>'], answer:1, explain:'dr/dt = (dA/dt)/(dA/dr) by the chain rule.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-mat-t8', title:'Integration (P1)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-mat-t8-l1', title:'Indefinite integration', html:'<h3>Indefinite integration</h3><p><strong>Reverse of differentiation:</strong> ∫x<sup>n</sup> dx = x<sup>n+1</sup>/(n+1) + c (n ≠ −1). Integrate term by term; always add the <strong>constant of integration c</strong>.</p><p>E.g. ∫(6x<sup>2</sup> − 4x + 3) dx = 2x<sup>3</sup> − 2x<sup>2</sup> + 3x + c. Check by differentiating.</p><p>Find c using a given point: if dy/dx = 3x<sup>2</sup> and y = 5 at x = 2: y = x<sup>3</sup> + c → 8 + c = 5 → c = −3.</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t8-l2', title:'Definite integration and area', html:'<h3>Definite integration and area</h3><p><strong>Definite integral:</strong> ∫<sub>a</sub><sup>b</sup> f(x) dx = [F(x)]<sub>a</sub><sup>b</sup> = F(b) − F(a). No constant needed.</p><p><strong>Area under a curve</strong> (above x-axis): A = ∫<sub>a</sub><sup>b</sup> y dx. Areas below the axis are negative — take absolute values for total area, or integrate separately.</p><p>Worked example: area under y = x<sup>2</sup> from 0 to 2: [x<sup>3</sup>/3]<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> = 8/3.</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t8-l3', title:'Area between curves and kinematics', html:'<h3>Area between curves and kinematics</h3><p><strong>Area between curves:</strong> ∫<sub>a</sub><sup>b</sup> (top − bottom) dx. Find intersection points for limits.</p><p><strong>Kinematics:</strong> given acceleration a(t): v = ∫a dt, s = ∫v dt. Given v(t): displacement = ∫v dt; distance = ∫|v| dt.</p><p>Worked example: v = 3t<sup>2</sup> − 4t, t = 0 to 2: displacement = [t<sup>3</sup> − 2t<sup>2</sup>]<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> = 8 − 8 = 0 (returns to start).</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'∫x<sup>4</sup> dx equals', options:['4x<sup>3</sup> + c','x<sup>5</sup>/5 + c','x<sup>5</sup> + c','5x<sup>5</sup> + c'], answer:1, explain:'Power rule reversed: x<sup>5</sup>/5 + c.'},
    {q:'∫<sub>1</sub><sup>2</sup> 2x dx equals', options:['3','4','2','6'], answer:0, explain:'[x<sup>2</sup>]<sub>1</sub><sup>2</sup> = 4 − 1 = 3.'},
    {q:'If dy/dx = 4x and y = 1 when x = 1, y equals', options:['2x<sup>2</sup>','2x<sup>2</sup> − 1','4x<sup>2</sup> + 1','x<sup>2</sup> + 1'], answer:1, explain:'y = 2x<sup>2</sup> + c; 2 + c = 1 → c = −1.'},
    {q:'Area under y = 3x<sup>2</sup> from x = 0 to x = 1', options:['3','1','2','1/3'], answer:1, explain:'[x<sup>3</sup>]<sub>0</sub><sup>1</sup> = 1.'},
    {q:'∫(1/x<sup>2</sup>) dx equals', options:['−1/x + c','1/x + c','−2/x<sup>3</sup> + c','ln|x| + c'], answer:0, explain:'x<sup>−2</sup> → −x<sup>−1</sup> + c = −1/x + c.'},
    {q:'The definite integral gives', options:['a function','a number','always an area','always positive'], answer:1, explain:'Evaluating between limits yields a numerical value.'},
    {q:'Area between y = x and y = x<sup>2</sup> from 0 to 1', options:['1/3','1/6','1/2','5/6'], answer:1, explain:'∫(x − x<sup>2</sup>)dx = [x<sup>2</sup>/2 − x<sup>3</sup>/3]<sub>0</sub><sup>1</sup> = 1/6.'},
    {q:'If a = 6t, v(0) = 0, then v equals', options:['6t<sup>2</sup>','3t<sup>2</sup>','6','3t<sup>2</sup> + c'], answer:1, explain:'v = ∫6t dt = 3t<sup>2</sup> + c; v(0)=0 → c=0.'},
    {q:'∫cos x dx equals', options:['−sin x + c','sin x + c','tan x + c','−cos x + c'], answer:1, explain:'The derivative of sin x is cos x, so ∫cos x dx = sin x + c.'},
    {q:'A definite integral below the x-axis is', options:['positive','negative','zero','undefined'], answer:1, explain:'Signed area: below the axis counts negative.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-mat-t9', title:'Algebra (A Level)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-mat-t9-l1', title:'The modulus function', html:'<h3>The modulus function</h3><p><strong>|x|</strong> = x if x ≥ 0, −x if x < 0. Graph: V-shape with vertex at origin. <strong>|f(x)|:</strong> reflect parts below the x-axis up.</p><p>Solving <strong>|2x − 1| = 5</strong>: 2x − 1 = ±5 → x = 3 or x = −2. Solving <strong>|x − 2| < 3</strong>: −3 < x − 2 < 3 → −1 < x < 5.</p><p>For |ax + b| = |cx + d|: square both sides OR consider both sign cases. Sketch graphs to verify solutions.</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t9-l2', title:'Polynomials and the factor theorem', html:'<h3>Polynomials and the factor theorem</h3><p><strong>Factor theorem:</strong> (x − a) is a factor of p(x) iff p(a) = 0. <strong>Remainder theorem:</strong> p(x) ÷ (x − a) leaves remainder p(a).</p><p>Worked example: p(x) = x<sup>3</sup> − 2x<sup>2</sup> − 5x + 6. p(1) = 0 → (x − 1) factor. Divide: = (x − 1)(x<sup>2</sup> − x − 6) = (x − 1)(x − 3)(x + 2).</p><p>For cubics with unknown coefficients, use given factors to set up equations: e.g. (x + 2) factor and remainder 12 when divided by (x − 1) → p(−2) = 0, p(1) = 12.</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t9-l3', title:'Partial fractions', html:'<h3>Partial fractions</h3><p>Split into simpler fractions for integration: <strong>distinct linear factors:</strong> (3x+5)/((x+1)(x+2)) = A/(x+1) + B/(x+2). Multiply through, compare coefficients or substitute x = −1, −2.</p><p><strong>Repeated factor:</strong> (x+1)/(x−1)<sup>2</sup> = A/(x−1) + B/(x−1)<sup>2</sup>. <strong>Improper fraction:</strong> divide first (if numerator degree ≥ denominator).</p><p>Worked example: (3x+5)/((x+1)(x+2)): 3x+5 = A(x+2) + B(x+1); x=−1: 2 = A; x=−2: −1 = −B → B = 1. So = 2/(x+1) + 1/(x+2).</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'|−7| equals', options:['−7','7','±7','0'], answer:1, explain:'Modulus: distance from zero, always non-negative.'},
    {q:'Solutions of |2x − 1| = 5', options:['x = 3 only','x = 3 or x = −2','x = −3 or 2','x = 2.5'], answer:1, explain:'2x−1 = 5 → x=3; 2x−1 = −5 → x=−2.'},
    {q:'Solution of |x − 4| < 2', options:['2 < x < 6','x < 2 or x > 6','x > 6','x < 2'], answer:0, explain:'−2 < x−4 < 2 → 2 < x < 6.'},
    {q:'Factor theorem: (x − 2) is a factor of p(x) iff', options:['p(2) = 2','p(2) = 0','p(−2) = 0','p(0) = 2'], answer:1, explain:'p(a) = 0 ⟺ (x − a) is a factor.'},
    {q:'Remainder when x<sup>3</sup> + 2x<sup>2</sup> − x + 1 is divided by (x + 1)', options:['0','3','−1','5'], answer:1, explain:'p(−1) = −1 + 2 + 1 + 1 = 3.'},
    {q:'x<sup>3</sup> − 6x<sup>2</sup> + 11x − 6 factorises as', options:['(x−1)(x−2)(x−3)','(x+1)(x+2)(x+3)','(x−1)(x−2)(x+3)','(x−6)(x<sup>2</sup>+1)'], answer:0, explain:'p(1)=0: (x−1)(x<sup>2</sup>−5x+6) = (x−1)(x−2)(x−3).'},
    {q:'Partial fractions of 1/((x+1)(x+3))', options:['A/(x+1) + B/(x+3)','A/(x+1)<sup>2</sup>','A + B/(x+1)','cannot split'], answer:0, explain:'Distinct linear factors: one constant numerator each.'},
    {q:'For (x+1)/(x−1)<sup>2</sup>, the form is', options:['A/(x−1) + B/(x−1)<sup>2</sup>','A/(x−1) + B/(x+1)','A + B/(x−1)','A/(x−1)<sup>2</sup> only'], answer:0, explain:'Repeated linear factor needs both powers.'},
    {q:'Graph of y = |x − 2| has vertex at', options:['(0, 0)','(2, 0)','(−2, 0)','(0, 2)'], answer:1, explain:'V-shape with zero at x = 2.'},
    {q:'If p(x) has factor (x + 3), then', options:['p(3) = 0','p(−3) = 0','p(0) = 3','p(3) = 3'], answer:1, explain:'(x − a) factor with a = −3: p(−3) = 0.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-mat-t10', title:'Logarithmic and exponential functions (A Level)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-mat-t10-l1', title:'Laws of logarithms', html:'<h3>Laws of logarithms</h3><p><strong>log<sub>a</sub> x = y ⟺ a<sup>y</sup> = x.</strong> Laws: <strong>log(ab) = log a + log b</strong>; <strong>log(a/b) = log a − log b</strong>; <strong>log(a<sup>n</sup>) = n log a</strong>; log<sub>a</sub> a = 1; log 1 = 0.</p><p>Solving: log<sub>2</sub>(x + 1) + log<sub>2</sub>(x − 1) = 3 → log<sub>2</sub>((x+1)(x−1)) = 3 → x<sup>2</sup> − 1 = 8 → x = ±3; reject x = −3 (log of negative undefined) → x = 3.</p><p><strong>Change of base:</strong> log<sub>a</sub> b = log b/log a. Always check solutions are valid (arguments positive).</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t10-l2', title:'Exponential and log graphs', html:'<h3>Exponential and log graphs</h3><p><strong>y = a<sup>x</sup>:</strong> passes through (0, 1); growth if a > 1, decay if 0 < a < 1; asymptote y = 0. <strong>y = e<sup>x</sup>:</strong> the natural exponential; <strong>y = ln x:</strong> its inverse, domain x > 0, passes through (1, 0), asymptote x = 0.</p><p>e<sup>x</sup> is its own derivative: d/dx(e<sup>x</sup>) = e<sup>x</sup>; d/dx(ln x) = 1/x. Solve e<sup>2x</sup> = 5 → 2x = ln 5 → x = (ln 5)/2.</p><p>Modelling: N = N<sub>0</sub>e<sup>kt</sup> for growth/decay; k from data via logs.</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t10-l3', title:'Log-linear relationships', html:'<h3>Log-linear relationships</h3><p>If y = ax<sup>n</sup>, then <strong>log y = n log x + log a</strong>: plotting log y vs log x gives a straight line, gradient n, intercept log a. If y = ab<sup>x</sup>, then <strong>ln y = x ln b + ln a</strong>: ln y vs x is linear, gradient ln b.</p><p>Worked example: data fits y = ab<sup>x</sup>; ln y vs x line has gradient 0.5 and intercept 2 → ln b = 0.5 → b = e<sup>0.5</sup>; ln a = 2 → a = e<sup>2</sup>.</p><p>Exam reasoning: identify which log plot linearises the given form, then read gradient/intercept carefully (note the log base).</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'log<sub>2</sub> 32 equals', options:['4','5','6','16'], answer:1, explain:'2<sup>5</sup> = 32.'},
    {q:'log a + log b equals', options:['log(a+b)','log(ab)','log a × log b','log(a<sup>b</sup>)'], answer:1, explain:'First law of logarithms.'},
    {q:'2 log x − log y equals', options:['log(2x − y)','log(x<sup>2</sup>/y)','log(x<sup>2</sup> − y)','2 log(x/y)'], answer:1, explain:'2 log x = log x<sup>2</sup>; minus → divide.'},
    {q:'Solving log<sub>2</sub>(x+1) + log<sub>2</sub>(x−1) = 3 gives x =', options:['±3','3','−3','4'], answer:1, explain:'x<sup>2</sup>−1 = 8 → x = ±3; x = −3 invalid (log of negative).'},
    {q:'d/dx(e<sup>3x</sup>) equals', options:['3e<sup>3x</sup>','e<sup>3x</sup>','3e<sup>x</sup>','e<sup>3x</sup>/3'], answer:0, explain:'Chain rule: 3e<sup>3x</sup>.'},
    {q:'d/dx(ln 5x) equals', options:['1/(5x)','1/x','5/x','ln 5'], answer:1, explain:'ln 5x = ln 5 + ln x → derivative 1/x.'},
    {q:'y = ax<sup>n</sup> is linearised by plotting', options:['y vs x','log y vs log x','ln y vs x','y vs log x'], answer:1, explain:'log y = n log x + log a.'},
    {q:'The graph of y = e<sup>x</sup> passes through', options:['(1, 0)','(0, 1)','(0, 0)','(1, 1)'], answer:1, explain:'e<sup>0</sup> = 1.'},
    {q:'ln y vs x is linear with gradient 0.5 for y = ab<sup>x</sup>. b equals', options:['0.5','e<sup>0.5</sup>','2','ln 0.5'], answer:1, explain:'Gradient = ln b → b = e<sup>0.5</sup>.'},
    {q:'log<sub>10</sub> 1000 equals', options:['2','3','4','10'], answer:1, explain:'10<sup>3</sup> = 1000.'}
   ]}
,
  {id:'al-mat-t11', title:'Trigonometry (A Level)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-mat-t11-l1', title:'Compound angle formulae', html:'<h3>Compound angle formulae</h3><p><strong>sin(A±B) = sinA cosB ± cosA sinB</strong>; <strong>cos(A±B) = cosA cosB ∓ sinA sinB</strong>; <strong>tan(A±B) = (tanA ± tanB)/(1 ∓ tanA tanB)</strong>.</p><p>Uses: exact values (sin 75° = sin(45°+30°)); simplifying; solving equations. Worked: sin 75° = sin45cos30 + cos45sin30 = (√2/2)(√3/2) + (√2/2)(1/2) = (√6 + √2)/4.</p><p>Memorise by pattern: sine keeps the sign, cosine flips it, tangent is a fraction.</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t11-l2', title:'Double angle formulae', html:'<h3>Double angle formulae</h3><p><strong>sin 2A = 2 sinA cosA</strong>; <strong>cos 2A = cos<sup>2</sup>A − sin<sup>2</sup>A = 2cos<sup>2</sup>A − 1 = 1 − 2sin<sup>2</sup>A</strong>; <strong>tan 2A = 2tanA/(1 − tan<sup>2</sup>A)</strong>.</p><p>Key rearrangements: <strong>1 + cos 2A = 2cos<sup>2</sup>A</strong>; <strong>1 − cos 2A = 2sin<sup>2</sup>A</strong> — essential for integrating sin<sup>2</sup>/cos<sup>2</sup> and solving equations.</p><p>Worked: solve 2cos<sup>2</sup>x = 1 + sin x → use 2cos<sup>2</sup>x = 2(1 − sin<sup>2</sup>x): 2 − 2sin<sup>2</sup>x = 1 + sin x → 2sin<sup>2</sup>x + sin x − 1 = 0 → (2sin x − 1)(sin x + 1) = 0.</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t11-l3', title:'The form R sin(x + α)', html:'<h3>The form R sin(x + α)</h3><p><strong>a sin x + b cos x = R sin(x + α)</strong>, where <strong>R = √(a<sup>2</sup> + b<sup>2</sup>)</strong> and <strong>tan α = b/a</strong>. Similarly = R cos(x − α) with the same R.</p><p>Worked: 3 sin x + 4 cos x = R sin(x+α): R = 5, tan α = 4/3 → α ≈ 53.1°. Maximum value = 5, minimum = −5.</p><p>Uses: finding max/min of trig expressions; solving a sin x + b cos x = c (divide by R, then solve sin(x+α) = c/R).</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'sin(A + B) equals', options:['sinA + sinB','sinA cosB + cosA sinB','sinA cosB − cosA sinB','cosA cosB + sinA sinB'], answer:1, explain:'Compound angle formula.'},
    {q:'cos 2A in terms of cos A is', options:['1 − 2sin<sup>2</sup>A only','2cos<sup>2</sup>A − 1','2sinA cosA','cos<sup>2</sup>A + sin<sup>2</sup>A'], answer:1, explain:'cos 2A = 2cos<sup>2</sup>A − 1 (also = 1 − 2sin<sup>2</sup>A = cos<sup>2</sup>A − sin<sup>2</sup>A).'},
    {q:'sin 2A equals', options:['2sinA','2 sinA cosA','sin<sup>2</sup>A','cos<sup>2</sup>A − sin<sup>2</sup>A'], answer:1, explain:'Double angle formula.'},
    {q:'1 − cos 2A equals', options:['2cos<sup>2</sup>A','2sin<sup>2</sup>A','sin 2A','2tan<sup>2</sup>A'], answer:1, explain:'Rearrangement of cos 2A = 1 − 2sin<sup>2</sup>A.'},
    {q:'For 3 sin x + 4 cos x = R sin(x + α), R equals', options:['7','5','1','25'], answer:1, explain:'R = √(9+16) = 5.'},
    {q:'Maximum value of 3 sin x + 4 cos x is', options:['7','5','3','4'], answer:1, explain:'Max of R sin(x+α) is R = 5.'},
    {q:'tan(A + B) equals', options:['tanA + tanB','(tanA + tanB)/(1 − tanA tanB)','(tanA − tanB)/(1 + tanA tanB)','tanA tanB'], answer:1, explain:'Compound angle formula for tangent.'},
    {q:'sin 75° exactly is', options:['(√6 − √2)/4','(√6 + √2)/4','√3/2','(√2)/2'], answer:1, explain:'sin(45+30) = sin45cos30 + cos45sin30 = (√6+√2)/4.'},
    {q:'To integrate sin<sup>2</sup> x, first write it as', options:['1 + cos 2x','(1 − cos 2x)/2','2sin x cos x','cos<sup>2</sup> x'], answer:1, explain:'From cos 2x = 1 − 2sin<sup>2</sup>x.'},
    {q:'In R sin(x + α), tan α equals', options:['a/b','b/a','R/a','a/R'], answer:1, explain:'Expanding R sin(x+α) = R sin x cos α + R cos x sin α: a = R cos α, b = R sin α → tan α = b/a.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-mat-t12', title:'Differentiation (A Level)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-mat-t12-l1', title:'Chain, product and quotient rules', html:'<h3>Chain, product and quotient rules</h3><p><strong>Chain rule:</strong> d/dx f(g(x)) = f′(g(x)) × g′(x). E.g. d/dx(3x<sup>2</sup> + 1)<sup>5</sup> = 5(3x<sup>2</sup>+1)<sup>4</sup> × 6x = 30x(3x<sup>2</sup>+1)<sup>4</sup>.</p><p><strong>Product rule:</strong> (uv)′ = u′v + uv′. E.g. d/dx(x<sup>2</sup> sin x) = 2x sin x + x<sup>2</sup> cos x.</p><p><strong>Quotient rule:</strong> (u/v)′ = (u′v − uv′)/v<sup>2</sup>. E.g. d/dx(x/(x+1)) = [(1)(x+1) − x(1)]/(x+1)<sup>2</sup> = 1/(x+1)<sup>2</sup>.</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t12-l2', title:'Differentiating trig, exponential and log functions', html:'<h3>Differentiating trig, exponential and log functions</h3><p><strong>d/dx(sin x) = cos x</strong>; <strong>d/dx(cos x) = −sin x</strong>; <strong>d/dx(tan x) = sec<sup>2</sup> x</strong>. <strong>d/dx(e<sup>x</sup>) = e<sup>x</sup></strong>; <strong>d/dx(ln x) = 1/x</strong>.</p><p>Combine with chain rule: d/dx(sin 3x) = 3cos 3x; d/dx(e<sup>x<sup>2</sup></sup>) = 2xe<sup>x<sup>2</sup></sup>; d/dx(ln(x<sup>2</sup>+1)) = 2x/(x<sup>2</sup>+1).</p><p>Exam reasoning: identify the “outer” and “inner” functions first; never forget the × g′(x).</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t12-l3', title:'Parametric and implicit differentiation', html:'<h3>Parametric and implicit differentiation</h3><p><strong>Parametric:</strong> x = f(t), y = g(t): <strong>dy/dx = (dy/dt)/(dx/dt)</strong>. E.g. x = t<sup>2</sup>, y = t<sup>3</sup>: dy/dx = 3t<sup>2</sup>/2t = 3t/2.</p><p><strong>Implicit:</strong> differentiate every term, using dy/dx for y terms: d/dx(y<sup>2</sup>) = 2y dy/dx. E.g. x<sup>2</sup> + y<sup>2</sup> = 25: 2x + 2y dy/dx = 0 → dy/dx = −x/y.</p><p>For tangents to implicit curves, find dy/dx at the point, then use the line equation. Stationary points: dy/dx = 0 with y ≠ 0.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'d/dx[(2x+1)<sup>4</sup>] equals', options:['4(2x+1)<sup>3</sup>','8(2x+1)<sup>3</sup>','(2x+1)<sup>3</sup>','8(2x+1)<sup>4</sup>'], answer:1, explain:'Chain rule: 4(2x+1)<sup>3</sup> × 2.'},
    {q:'Product rule: (uv)′ equals', options:['u′v′','u′v + uv′','u′v − uv′','uv′ + u′'], answer:1, explain:'Differentiate first × second + first × differentiate second.'},
    {q:'d/dx(x<sup>2</sup>e<sup>x</sup>) equals', options:['2xe<sup>x</sup>','e<sup>x</sup>(x<sup>2</sup> + 2x)','2x + e<sup>x</sup>','x<sup>2</sup>e<sup>x</sup>'], answer:1, explain:'u′v + uv′ = 2xe<sup>x</sup> + x<sup>2</sup>e<sup>x</sup>.'},
    {q:'Quotient rule: (u/v)′ equals', options:['(u′v + uv′)/v<sup>2</sup>','(u′v − uv′)/v<sup>2</sup>','u′/v′','(uv′ − u′v)/v'], answer:1, explain:'(u′v − uv′)/v<sup>2</sup>.'},
    {q:'d/dx(tan x) equals', options:['−sec<sup>2</sup> x','sec<sup>2</sup> x','cot x','−cosec<sup>2</sup> x'], answer:1, explain:'Derivative of tan is sec<sup>2</sup>.'},
    {q:'d/dx[ln(x<sup>2</sup>)] equals', options:['1/x<sup>2</sup>','2/x','2x','1/(2x)'], answer:1, explain:'Chain rule: (1/x<sup>2</sup>) × 2x = 2/x. (Or ln x<sup>2</sup> = 2 ln x.)'},
    {q:'If x = t<sup>3</sup>, y = t<sup>2</sup>, dy/dx equals', options:['(3/2)t','2/(3t)','(2t)/(3t<sup>2</sup>)','6t'], answer:1, explain:'(dy/dt)/(dx/dt) = 2t/3t<sup>2</sup> = 2/(3t).'},
    {q:'For x<sup>2</sup> + y<sup>2</sup> = 25, dy/dx equals', options:['x/y','−x/y','−y/x','2x + 2y'], answer:1, explain:'2x + 2y(dy/dx) = 0.'},
    {q:'d/dx(e<sup>−2x</sup>) equals', options:['e<sup>−2x</sup>','−2e<sup>−2x</sup>','−e<sup>−2x</sup>','2e<sup>−2x</sup>'], answer:1, explain:'Chain rule: e<sup>−2x</sup> × (−2).'},
    {q:'d/dx(sin<sup>2</sup> x) equals', options:['2cos<sup>2</sup> x','sin 2x','2sin x','cos 2x'], answer:1, explain:'2 sin x cos x = sin 2x.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-mat-t13', title:'Integration (A Level)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-mat-t13-l1', title:'Integrating standard functions', html:'<h3>Integrating standard functions</h3><p><strong>∫e<sup>x</sup> dx = e<sup>x</sup> + c</strong>; <strong>∫(1/x) dx = ln|x| + c</strong>; <strong>∫sin x dx = −cos x + c</strong>; <strong>∫cos x dx = sin x + c</strong>; <strong>∫sec<sup>2</sup> x dx = tan x + c</strong>.</p><p>Reverse chain rule: ∫2x cos(x<sup>2</sup>) dx = sin(x<sup>2</sup>) + c; ∫f′(x)/f(x) dx = ln|f(x)| + c. E.g. ∫(2x+3)/(x<sup>2</sup>+3x+1) dx = ln|x<sup>2</sup>+3x+1| + c.</p><p>∫(ax+b)<sup>n</sup> dx = (ax+b)<sup>n+1</sup>/(a(n+1)) + c — divide by the inner derivative a.</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t13-l2', title:'Integration by parts and substitution', html:'<h3>Integration by parts and substitution</h3><p><strong>By parts:</strong> ∫u dv = uv − ∫v du. Choose u by ILATE (Inverse trig, Log, Algebraic, Trig, Exponential). E.g. ∫x e<sup>x</sup> dx: u = x, dv = e<sup>x</sup>dx → x e<sup>x</sup> − ∫e<sup>x</sup>dx = e<sup>x</sup>(x − 1) + c.</p><p><strong>By substitution:</strong> e.g. ∫x√(x+1) dx: let u = x+1, x = u−1, dx = du → ∫(u−1)√u du = ∫(u<sup>3/2</sup> − u<sup>1/2</sup>)du.</p><p>Definite by parts: apply limits to uv − ∫v du. For ∫ln x dx: u = ln x, dv = dx → x ln x − x + c.</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t13-l3', title:'Volumes of revolution and trig integrals', html:'<h3>Volumes of revolution and trig integrals</h3><p><strong>Volume about the x-axis:</strong> V = π∫<sub>a</sub><sup>b</sup> y<sup>2</sup> dx. About the y-axis: V = π∫ y<sup>2</sup>… use x as function of y: V = π∫<sub>c</sub><sup>d</sup> x<sup>2</sup> dy.</p><p>Worked: y = √x from 0 to 4 about x-axis: V = π∫x dx = π[x<sup>2</sup>/2]<sub>0</sub><sup>4</sup> = 8π.</p><p><strong>Trig powers:</strong> ∫sin<sup>2</sup>x dx = ∫(1−cos2x)/2 dx = x/2 − sin2x/4 + c. Partial fractions for rational functions (see Algebra topic).</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'∫e<sup>2x</sup> dx equals', options:['e<sup>2x</sup> + c','e<sup>2x</sup>/2 + c','2e<sup>2x</sup> + c','e<sup>x</sup> + c'], answer:1, explain:'Divide by the inner derivative 2.'},
    {q:'∫(3x<sup>2</sup>+1)/(x<sup>3</sup>+x) dx equals', options:['ln|x<sup>3</sup>+x| + c','(x<sup>3</sup>+x)<sup>2</sup>/2 + c','3ln|x| + c','arctan x + c'], answer:0, explain:'Numerator is the derivative of the denominator: ln|f(x)| form.'},
    {q:'∫x cos x dx equals', options:['x sin x + cos x + c','x sin x − cos x + c','sin x + c','−x sin x + c'], answer:0, explain:'By parts: u=x, dv=cos x dx → x sin x − ∫sin x dx = x sin x + cos x + c.'},
    {q:'∫ln x dx equals', options:['1/x + c','x ln x − x + c','x ln x + c','(ln x)<sup>2</sup>/2 + c'], answer:1, explain:'By parts with u = ln x, dv = dx.'},
    {q:'Volume about the x-axis: V equals', options:['∫y dx','π∫y<sup>2</sup> dx','2π∫y dx','π∫y dx'], answer:1, explain:'Discs of radius y: V = π∫y<sup>2</sup>dx.'},
    {q:'∫sin<sup>2</sup> x dx equals', options:['−cos<sup>2</sup>x + c','x/2 − sin2x/4 + c','cos2x/2 + c','sin<sup>3</sup>x/3 + c'], answer:1, explain:'Use sin<sup>2</sup>x = (1−cos2x)/2.'},
    {q:'∫sec<sup>2</sup> 3x dx equals', options:['tan 3x + c','(tan 3x)/3 + c','3tan 3x + c','sec<sup>2</sup>x + c'], answer:1, explain:'Reverse chain rule: divide by 3.'},
    {q:'∫<sub>0</sub><sup>π/2</sup> cos x dx equals', options:['0','1','−1','π/2'], answer:1, explain:'[sin x]<sub>0</sub><sup>π/2</sup> = 1 − 0 = 1.'},
    {q:'∫(2x+1)<sup>3</sup> dx equals', options:['(2x+1)<sup>4</sup>/4 + c','(2x+1)<sup>4</sup>/8 + c','8(2x+1)<sup>4</sup> + c','(2x+1)<sup>4</sup> + c'], answer:1, explain:'(2x+1)<sup>4</sup>/(4×2) + c.'},
    {q:'When integrating by parts, choose u preferring', options:['exponentials first','logarithms before algebraics (ILATE)','trig first','constants'], answer:1, explain:'ILATE: Inverse trig, Log, Algebraic, Trig, Exponential.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-mat-t14', title:'Numerical solution of equations (A Level)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-mat-t14-l1', title:'Location of roots', html:'<h3>Location of roots</h3><p><strong>Change of sign:</strong> if f is continuous and f(a), f(b) have opposite signs, a root lies in (a, b). Show f(a) × f(b) < 0.</p><p>Worked: f(x) = x<sup>3</sup> − 2x − 5: f(2) = −1, f(3) = 16 → root in (2, 3).</p><p>Refine by decimal search: evaluate at 2.1, 2.2… until sign change in a smaller interval, to the required accuracy (beware: need the NEXT digit to round correctly).</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t14-l2', title:'Fixed-point iteration', html:'<h3>Fixed-point iteration</h3><p>Rearrange f(x) = 0 into <strong>x = g(x)</strong>, then iterate <strong>x<sub>n+1</sub> = g(x<sub>n</sub>)</strong>. Converges if |g′(x)| < 1 near the root (staircase/cobweb diagram); diverges otherwise.</p><p>Worked: x<sup>3</sup> − 2x − 5 = 0 → x = ∛(2x+5). x<sub>0</sub> = 2: x<sub>1</sub> = ∛9 ≈ 2.080, x<sub>2</sub> = ∛(9.16) ≈ 2.090… → 2.0946 (4 dp).</p><p>Different rearrangements converge to different roots or diverge — e.g. x = (x<sup>3</sup>−5)/2 diverges for this root.</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t14-l3', title:'Newton–Raphson method', html:'<h3>Newton–Raphson method</h3><p><strong>x<sub>n+1</sub> = x<sub>n</sub> − f(x<sub>n</sub>)/f′(x<sub>n</sub>)</strong>. Geometrically: follow the tangent at x<sub>n</sub> to the x-axis. Fast (quadratic) convergence near a simple root.</p><p>Worked: f(x) = x<sup>3</sup> − 2x − 5, f′ = 3x<sup>2</sup> − 2, x<sub>0</sub> = 2: x<sub>1</sub> = 2 − (−1)/10 = 2.1; x<sub>2</sub> = 2.1 − 0.061/11.23 ≈ 2.0946.</p><p>Fails if f′(x<sub>n</sub>) = 0 (tangent horizontal), near stationary points, or with a poor starting value (may cycle or diverge).</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'A root lies in (a, b) if', options:['f(a) = f(b)','f(a) and f(b) have opposite signs (f continuous)','f(a) > f(b)','a < b'], answer:1, explain:'Sign change + continuity → root by the intermediate value theorem.'},
    {q:'f(1) = −2, f(2) = 3 for continuous f implies', options:['no root','a root in (1, 2)','a root at 1.5 exactly','two roots'], answer:1, explain:'Sign change: at least one root in the interval.'},
    {q:'Fixed-point iteration uses', options:['x<sub>n+1</sub> = f(x<sub>n</sub>)','x<sub>n+1</sub> = g(x<sub>n</sub>) where x = g(x)','x<sub>n+1</sub> = x<sub>n</sub>/2','tangents'], answer:1, explain:'Rearrange to x = g(x), then iterate.'},
    {q:'Iteration converges (typically) when', options:['|g′(x)| > 1','|g′(x)| < 1 near the root','g(x) = 0','x<sub>0</sub> = 0'], answer:1, explain:'Gradient of g less than 1 in magnitude: staircase/cobweb converges.'},
    {q:'Newton–Raphson formula', options:['x<sub>n+1</sub> = x<sub>n</sub> + f/f′','x<sub>n+1</sub> = x<sub>n</sub> − f(x<sub>n</sub>)/f′(x<sub>n</sub>)','x<sub>n+1</sub> = f(x<sub>n</sub>)','x<sub>n+1</sub> = x<sub>n</sub><sup>2</sup>'], answer:1, explain:'Follow the tangent to the axis.'},
    {q:'Newton–Raphson fails when', options:['f is linear','f′(x<sub>n</sub>) = 0','x<sub>0</sub> is close','f is a cubic'], answer:1, explain:'Horizontal tangent: division by zero / shoots far away.'},
    {q:'For f(x) = x<sup>2</sup> − 3, x<sub>0</sub> = 2, x<sub>1</sub> equals', options:['1.75','1.5','2.5','1.25'], answer:0, explain:'x<sub>1</sub> = 2 − (4−3)/4 = 2 − 0.25 = 1.75.'},
    {q:'Decimal search to 1 decimal place needs', options:['values at 1 dp','values at 2 dp to round','values at 3 dp','no working'], answer:1, explain:'Find sign change between consecutive 2-dp values, then round.'},
    {q:'A cobweb diagram that spirals outward shows', options:['convergence','divergence','a root','oscillation to the root'], answer:1, explain:'|g′| > 1: iterates move away from the root.'},
    {q:'Newton–Raphson converges', options:['linearly','quadratically near a simple root','never','only for quadratics'], answer:1, explain:'Correct digits roughly double each iteration near the root.'}
   ]}
,
  {id:'al-mat-t15', title:'Vectors (P3)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-mat-t15-l1', title:'Vector notation and operations', html:'<h3>Vector notation and operations</h3><p>Vectors: <strong>a</strong>, AB⃗, or column (x, y, z). <strong>Magnitude</strong> |a| = √(x<sup>2</sup>+y<sup>2</sup>+z<sup>2</sup>). <strong>Unit vector</strong> â = a/|a|. Position vector of P: OP⃗.</p><p>Add/subtract component-wise; scalar multiplication scales. <strong>i, j, k</strong> are unit vectors along axes: 3i − 2j + k = (3, −2, 1).</p><p>Worked: a = (1, 2, 3), b = (4, 0, −1): a + b = (5, 2, 2); |a| = √14.</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t15-l2', title:'The scalar (dot) product', html:'<h3>The scalar (dot) product</h3><p><strong>a·b = |a||b|cos θ</strong> = x<sub>1</sub>x<sub>2</sub> + y<sub>1</sub>y<sub>2</sub> + z<sub>1</sub>z<sub>2</sub>. Gives the <strong>angle between vectors</strong>: cos θ = (a·b)/(|a||b|).</p><p><strong>Perpendicular ⟺ a·b = 0</strong>. Parallel ⟺ one is a scalar multiple of the other.</p><p>Worked: a = (1, 2), b = (3, −1): a·b = 3 − 2 = 1; |a| = √5, |b| = √10; cos θ = 1/√50 → θ ≈ 81.9°.</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t15-l3', title:'Vector equations of lines', html:'<h3>Vector equations of lines</h3><p>Line through point a in direction d: <strong>r = a + λd</strong>. Cartesian: (x − a<sub>1</sub>)/d<sub>1</sub> = (y − a<sub>2</sub>)/d<sub>2</sub> (= (z − a<sub>3</sub>)/d<sub>3</sub> in 3D).</p><p><strong>Intersection:</strong> set r equal with different parameters (λ, μ), solve. <strong>Skew lines:</strong> not parallel, do not meet (only in 3D).</p><p><strong>Angle between lines:</strong> angle between direction vectors via dot product. Foot of perpendicular / shortest distance: minimise |r(λ) − point|.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'Magnitude of (3, 4, 0) is', options:['7','5','25','12'], answer:1, explain:'√(9+16+0) = 5.'},
    {q:'a·b for a = (2, −1), b = (3, 4) is', options:['10','2','−2','14'], answer:1, explain:'6 − 4 = 2.'},
    {q:'Two vectors are perpendicular iff', options:['a·b = 1','a·b = 0','|a| = |b|','a = b'], answer:1, explain:'cos 90° = 0.'},
    {q:'Unit vector in direction (6, 8) is', options:['(6, 8)/14','(3/5, 4/5)','(1, 1)','(6, 8)'], answer:1, explain:'Divide by magnitude 10: (0.6, 0.8).'},
    {q:'Vector equation of line through (1, 2) direction (3, −1)', options:['r = (3,−1) + λ(1,2)','r = (1,2) + λ(3,−1)','r = λ(1,2)','r = (1,2) + λ(1,2)'], answer:1, explain:'r = position + λ × direction.'},
    {q:'Lines r = a + λd and r = b + μe are parallel if', options:['a = b','d is a scalar multiple of e','λ = μ','a·b = 0'], answer:1, explain:'Direction vectors proportional.'},
    {q:'Angle between (1, 0) and (1, 1)', options:['90°','45°','60°','30°'], answer:1, explain:'cos θ = 1/(1×√2) → θ = 45°.'},
    {q:'i − 2j + 3k as a column is', options:['(1, 2, 3)','(1, −2, 3)','(−1, 2, 3)','(3, −2, 1)'], answer:1, explain:'Coefficients of i, j, k.'},
    {q:'Skew lines', options:['intersect','are parallel and never meet','are not parallel and do not intersect','are the same line'], answer:2, explain:'In 3D: non-parallel, non-intersecting.'},
    {q:'Midpoint of A(1,2,3) and B(5,6,7) has position vector', options:['(6, 8, 10)','(3, 4, 5)','(2, 2, 2)','(4, 4, 4)'], answer:1, explain:'(a+b)/2 = (3, 4, 5).'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-mat-t16', title:'Differential equations (P3)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-mat-t16-l1', title:'Separation of variables', html:'<h3>Separation of variables</h3><p>For <strong>dy/dx = f(x)g(y)</strong>: separate → <strong>∫(1/g(y)) dy = ∫f(x) dx</strong>, then solve for y if possible. Always include +c.</p><p>Worked: dy/dx = xy: ∫dy/y = ∫x dx → ln|y| = x<sup>2</sup>/2 + c → y = Ae<sup>x<sup>2</sup>/2</sup>.</p><p>Use initial conditions to find c (particular solution) vs general solution.</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t16-l2', title:'Forming and solving models', html:'<h3>Forming and solving models</h3><p>Translate words to DEs: “rate proportional to amount” → dx/dt = kx. <strong>Exponential growth/decay:</strong> dx/dt = kx → x = Ae<sup>kt</sup>.</p><p>Worked: Newton’s cooling dθ/dt = −k(θ − θ<sub>0</sub>): let u = θ − θ<sub>0</sub> → du/dt = −ku → u = Ae<sup>−kt</sup> → θ = θ<sub>0</sub> + Ae<sup>−kt</sup>.</p><p>Check models: dimensions, long-term behaviour (t → ∞), and whether the solution fits the context (e.g. population cannot be negative).</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t16-l3', title:'Sketching solution curves', html:'<h3>Sketching solution curves</h3><p><strong>Direction fields:</strong> at each (x, y), draw a short segment of gradient f(x, y) — solutions follow the flow. <strong>Equilibrium solutions:</strong> where dy/dx = 0.</p><p>For dy/dx = ky: exponential curves; for dy/dx = −ky(1 − y/L): logistic S-curve approaching L.</p><p>Exam reasoning: sketch several curves through given points, showing concavity consistent with the DE.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'Separating dy/dx = x/y gives', options:['y dy = x dx','dy/y = x dx','y dx = x dy','dy = xy dx'], answer:0, explain:'Multiply by y dx: y dy = x dx.'},
    {q:'Solution of dy/dx = 2y is', options:['y = 2x + c','y = Ae<sup>2x</sup>','y = e<sup>2x</sup> + c','y = 2e<sup>x</sup>'], answer:1, explain:'∫dy/y = ∫2dx → ln|y| = 2x + c.'},
    {q:'dy/dx = xy, y(0) = 2 gives', options:['y = 2e<sup>x<sup>2</sup>/2</sup>','y = e<sup>x<sup>2</sup></sup>','y = 2 + x<sup>2</sup>/2','y = 2e<sup>x</sup>'], answer:0, explain:'y = Ae<sup>x<sup>2</sup>/2</sup>; A = 2.'},
    {q:'“Rate proportional to x” translates to', options:['dx/dt = k + x','dx/dt = kx','dx/dt = k/x','x = kt'], answer:1, explain:'Proportional: dx/dt = kx.'},
    {q:'Newton’s cooling solution has form', options:['θ = Ae<sup>kt</sup>','θ = θ<sub>0</sub> + Ae<sup>−kt</sup>','θ = θ<sub>0</sub>e<sup>kt</sup>','θ = kt + c'], answer:1, explain:'Approaches room temperature θ<sub>0</sub> exponentially.'},
    {q:'An equilibrium solution of dy/dx = y(1 − y) is', options:['y = x','y = 0 and y = 1','y = 1 only','none'], answer:1, explain:'dy/dx = 0 → y = 0 or 1.'},
    {q:'∫dy/(1−y) equals', options:['ln|1−y| + c','−ln|1−y| + c','1/(1−y)<sup>2</sup> + c','−y + c'], answer:1, explain:'Substitute u = 1−y: −ln|u| + c.'},
    {q:'A direction field shows', options:['exact solutions','short gradient segments that solutions follow','only equilibria','isoclines only'], answer:1, explain:'Slope marks guide sketching of solution curves.'},
    {q:'For exponential decay, k in x = Ae<sup>kt</sup> is', options:['positive','negative','zero','imaginary'], answer:1, explain:'Decay: k < 0.'},
    {q:'Particular vs general solution', options:['same thing','particular uses initial conditions to find c','general has no c','particular is approximate'], answer:1, explain:'Initial/boundary conditions determine the constant.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-mat-t17', title:'Complex numbers (P3)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-mat-t17-l1', title:'The imaginary unit and basic operations', html:'<h3>The imaginary unit and basic operations</h3><p><strong>i<sup>2</sup> = −1</strong>. Complex number <strong>z = x + iy</strong>: real part Re(z) = x, imaginary part Im(z) = y. Add/subtract: combine real and imaginary parts. Multiply: expand, replace i<sup>2</sup> with −1.</p><p><strong>Conjugate</strong> z* = x − iy. <strong>zz* = x<sup>2</sup> + y<sup>2</sup> = |z|<sup>2</sup></strong> (real). Divide: multiply top and bottom by the conjugate.</p><p>Worked: (2 + 3i)/(1 − i) = (2+3i)(1+i)/((1−i)(1+i)) = (−1 + 5i)/2 = −0.5 + 2.5i.</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t17-l2', title:'Argand diagram, modulus and argument', html:'<h3>Argand diagram, modulus and argument</h3><p><strong>Argand diagram:</strong> x = real axis, y = imaginary axis; z = x + iy is the point (x, y). <strong>Modulus</strong> |z| = √(x<sup>2</sup>+y<sup>2</sup>) (distance from origin). <strong>Argument</strong> arg(z) = θ, angle from positive real axis (−π < θ ≤ π).</p><p><strong>|z − a| = r:</strong> circle centre a, radius r. <strong>arg(z − a) = α:</strong> half-line from a at angle α.</p><p>Worked: |z − (1+i)| = 2: circle centre (1,1), radius 2.</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t17-l3', title:'Solving equations with complex numbers', html:'<h3>Solving equations with complex numbers</h3><p>Quadratics with negative discriminant: x<sup>2</sup> + 4 = 0 → x = ±2i. Formula works: x<sup>2</sup> + 2x + 5 = 0 → x = −1 ± 2i.</p><p><strong>Complex roots of real polynomials occur in conjugate pairs.</strong> If 2 + i is a root of a real cubic, so is 2 − i; find the third root via sum of roots or polynomial division.</p><p>Worked: cubic with roots 2+i, 2−i, r: (x − (2+i))(x − (2−i)) = x<sup>2</sup> − 4x + 5; divide the cubic by this to find r.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'i<sup>2</sup> equals', options:['1','−1','i','0'], answer:1, explain:'Definition of the imaginary unit.'},
    {q:'(3 + 2i) + (1 − 5i) equals', options:['4 − 3i','4 + 7i','2 − 3i','4 − 7i'], answer:0, explain:'(3+1) + (2−5)i.'},
    {q:'(1 + i)<sup>2</sup> equals', options:['1 + 2i','2i','1 − 1','2 + 2i'], answer:1, explain:'1 + 2i + i<sup>2</sup> = 2i.'},
    {q:'Conjugate of 4 − 3i is', options:['−4 + 3i','4 + 3i','−4 − 3i','3 + 4i'], answer:1, explain:'Change the sign of the imaginary part.'},
    {q:'|3 + 4i| equals', options:['7','5','25','12'], answer:1, explain:'√(9+16) = 5.'},
    {q:'1/(1 + i) in a + ib form is', options:['1 − i','(1 − i)/2','(1 + i)/2','1 + i'], answer:1, explain:'Multiply by (1−i)/(1−i): (1−i)/2.'},
    {q:'|z − 2i| = 3 represents', options:['a line','a circle centre (0,2) radius 3','a half-line','a point'], answer:1, explain:'Points at distance 3 from 2i.'},
    {q:'Roots of x<sup>2</sup> + 2x + 5 = 0 are', options:['−1 ± 2','−1 ± 2i','1 ± 2i','−2 ± i'], answer:1, explain:'x = (−2 ± √(4−20))/2 = −1 ± 2i.'},
    {q:'If 1 + 2i is a root of a real cubic, another root is', options:['1 − 2','−1 + 2i','1 − 2i','−1 − 2i'], answer:2, explain:'Complex roots of real polynomials come in conjugate pairs.'},
    {q:'arg(−1 + i) (principal) is', options:['π/4','3π/4','−π/4','−3π/4'], answer:1, explain:'Second quadrant: π − π/4 = 3π/4.'}
   ]}
,
  {id:'al-mat-t18', title:'Forces and equilibrium (Mechanics)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-mat-t18-l1', title:'Forces as vectors', html:'<h3>Forces as vectors</h3><p>Force is a vector: magnitude + direction. <strong>Resultant:</strong> vector sum. <strong>Resolving:</strong> F at angle θ to x-axis → components (F cosθ, F sinθ).</p><p><strong>Equilibrium:</strong> resultant = 0 (particle at rest or uniform velocity). Solve by resolving in two perpendicular directions: ΣF<sub>x</sub> = 0, ΣF<sub>y</sub> = 0.</p><p>Worked: particle held by strings at 30° and 60° to horizontal, weight 10 N: T<sub>1</sub>cos30 + T<sub>2</sub>cos60… set up: horizontal: T<sub>1</sub>cos30 = T<sub>2</sub>cos60; vertical: T<sub>1</sub>sin30 + T<sub>2</sub>sin60 = 10. Solve.</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t18-l2', title:'Friction', html:'<h3>Friction</h3><p><strong>Friction</strong> opposes relative motion (or tendency). <strong>Limiting friction</strong> F<sub>max</sub> = μR, where R = normal reaction, μ = coefficient of friction. For equilibrium on the point of slipping, F = μR.</p><p><strong>Smooth</strong> surfaces: no friction (R perpendicular only). <strong>Rough:</strong> friction acts parallel to the surface.</p><p>Worked: block on 30° rough incline, μ = 0.4, weight 20 N: R = 20cos30 = 17.3 N; component down slope = 20sin30 = 10 N; F<sub>max</sub> = 0.4×17.3 = 6.9 N < 10 N → slides.</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t18-l3', title:'Moments', html:'<h3>Moments</h3><p><strong>Moment</strong> = force × perpendicular distance from the point. <strong>Principle of moments:</strong> for equilibrium, sum of clockwise = sum of anticlockwise moments about any point (and resultant force = 0).</p><p>Worked: uniform 4 m beam, weight 60 N, pivoted 1 m from one end; what mass at the other end balances? Let pivot P: 60 N acts at 2 m from left end = 1 m right of P (clockwise); mass m at right end, 3 m right of P… set moments about P: 60×1 = mg×3 → m = 60/(3×9.81) ≈ 2.04 kg.</p><p>Centre of mass of a uniform body is at its midpoint; of a lamina, use symmetry.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'Resolving 10 N at 60° to the x-axis gives x-component', options:['10 sin60','10 cos60 = 5 N','10 tan60','6 N'], answer:1, explain:'F<sub>x</sub> = F cosθ = 10 × 0.5 = 5 N.'},
    {q:'A particle is in equilibrium when', options:['it is at rest only','resultant force is zero','forces are equal','friction is zero'], answer:1, explain:'ΣF = 0: rest or uniform velocity.'},
    {q:'Limiting friction equals', options:['μ + R','μR','R/μ','μ − R'], answer:1, explain:'F<sub>max</sub> = μR.'},
    {q:'A smooth surface exerts', options:['friction only','normal reaction perpendicular only','no force','a tangential force'], answer:1, explain:'Smooth: no friction component.'},
    {q:'Moment of a force about a point is', options:['F/d','F × perpendicular distance','F + d','F<sup>2</sup>d'], answer:1, explain:'Moment = force × perpendicular distance.'},
    {q:'Principle of moments for equilibrium', options:['resultant force is maximum','clockwise moments = anticlockwise moments','moments are zero','only one force acts'], answer:1, explain:'Balanced turning effects about any point.'},
    {q:'Block weight 20 N on 30° smooth incline: normal reaction is', options:['20 N','20cos30 ≈ 17.3 N','20sin30 = 10 N','0'], answer:1, explain:'Perpendicular: R = mg cosθ.'},
    {q:'If F < μR, the block', options:['slides','does not slide (static)','accelerates','tips'], answer:1, explain:'Friction needed is below the maximum: equilibrium holds.'},
    {q:'Centre of mass of a uniform rod is at', options:['one end','its midpoint','one-third along','the heavier end'], answer:1, explain:'Symmetry: midpoint.'},
    {q:'Two equal opposite parallel forces form', options:['equilibrium','a couple (pure moment)','zero moment','a resultant'], answer:1, explain:'A couple: net force zero, net moment = F × separation.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-mat-t19', title:'Kinematics of motion in a straight line (Mechanics)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-mat-t19-l1', title:'SUVAT equations', html:'<h3>SUVAT equations</h3><p>For <strong>constant acceleration</strong>: v = u + at; s = ut + ½at<sup>2</sup>; v<sup>2</sup> = u<sup>2</sup> + 2as; s = ½(u+v)t; s = vt − ½at<sup>2</sup>.</p><p>Choose the equation missing the quantity you do NOT know/want. Sign convention: pick positive direction, keep signs consistent.</p><p>Worked: u = 5, a = 2, t = 4: v = 13; s = 5×4 + ½×2×16 = 36.</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t19-l2', title:'Motion under gravity', html:'<h3>Motion under gravity</h3><p>Free fall: a = g = 9.81 m s<sup>-2</sup> downwards. Up positive: a = −9.81.</p><p>Worked: ball thrown up at 14.7 m s<sup>-1</sup>: max height v<sup>2</sup> = u<sup>2</sup> − 2gs → s = 14.7<sup>2</sup>/(2×9.81) = 11.0 m; time up = 14.7/9.81 = 1.5 s.</p><p>Multi-stage: split motion (e.g. powered then freefall), matching v/s at the junction.</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t19-l3', title:'Velocity–time graphs', html:'<h3>Velocity–time graphs</h3><p><strong>Gradient = acceleration;</strong> <strong>area = displacement</strong>. Distance = total area (absolute).</p><p>Worked: v–t graph: 0–5 s constant 10 m/s, 5–9 s decelerates to 0: displacement = 5×10 + ½×4×10 = 70 m; deceleration = 10/4 = 2.5 m s<sup>-2</sup>.</p><p>Non-uniform acceleration: area still gives displacement; gradient at a point gives instantaneous acceleration.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'v = u + at: a car accelerates from 5 to 25 m/s in 10 s. a equals', options:['2 m s<sup>-2</sup>','2.5 m s<sup>-2</sup>','20 m s<sup>-2</sup>','0.5 m s<sup>-2</sup>'], answer:0, explain:'a = (25−5)/10 = 2.'},
    {q:'s = ut + ½at<sup>2</sup>: u=0, a=4, t=3 gives s =', options:['12 m','18 m','36 m','6 m'], answer:1, explain:'½×4×9 = 18.'},
    {q:'A ball dropped from rest falls 20 m (g=10). Time is', options:['1 s','2 s','4 s','√2 s'], answer:1, explain:'20 = 5t<sup>2</sup> → t = 2.'},
    {q:'Area under a v–t graph gives', options:['acceleration','displacement','speed','jerk'], answer:1, explain:'∫v dt = displacement.'},
    {q:'Gradient of a v–t graph gives', options:['displacement','acceleration','distance','speed'], answer:1, explain:'dv/dt = acceleration.'},
    {q:'v<sup>2</sup> = u<sup>2</sup> + 2as: u=10, a=−2, s=24 gives v =', options:['±2','2','−2','±4'], answer:0, explain:'v<sup>2</sup> = 100 − 96 = 4 → v = ±2.'},
    {q:'SUVAT applies only when', options:['velocity is zero','acceleration is constant','motion is vertical','time is known'], answer:1, explain:'Derived assuming constant a.'},
    {q:'A stone thrown up at 20 m/s (g=10) reaches max height', options:['10 m','20 m','40 m','2 m'], answer:1, explain:'s = 400/20 = 20 m.'},
    {q:'Distance differs from displacement when', options:['speed is constant','direction reverses','acceleration is zero','motion is vertical'], answer:1, explain:'Distance accumulates; displacement is net.'},
    {q:'s = ½(u+v)t is useful when', options:['a is unknown/unwanted','t is unknown','v is unknown','u = 0'], answer:0, explain:'No acceleration term: use when a is not involved.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-mat-t20', title:'Momentum (Mechanics)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-mat-t20-l1', title:'Momentum and impulse', html:'<h3>Momentum and impulse</h3><p><strong>Momentum p = mv</strong> (vector). <strong>Impulse = Ft = Δp</strong> (change in momentum). Newton’s second law: F = dp/dt = ma (constant mass).</p><p>Worked: 0.5 kg ball hits a wall at 8 m/s and rebounds at 6 m/s: Δp = 0.5×(−6 − 8) = −7 N s (magnitude 7 N s).</p><p>Units: kg m s<sup>-1</sup> = N s.</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t20-l2', title:'Conservation of momentum', html:'<h3>Conservation of momentum</h3><p><strong>Total momentum conserved</strong> if no external impulse: m<sub>1</sub>u<sub>1</sub> + m<sub>2</sub>u<sub>2</sub> = m<sub>1</sub>v<sub>1</sub> + m<sub>2</sub>v<sub>2</sub>.</p><p>Worked: 3 kg at 4 m/s hits stationary 2 kg; they stick: 12 = 5v → v = 2.4 m/s.</p><p><strong>Explosions:</strong> gun recoil: 0 = m<sub>gun</sub>v<sub>gun</sub> + m<sub>bullet</sup>v<sub>bullet</sub> → opposite directions.</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t20-l3', title:'Elastic collisions', html:'<h3>Elastic collisions</h3><p><strong>Perfectly elastic:</strong> momentum AND kinetic energy conserved. <strong>Relative speed of approach = relative speed of separation</strong> along the line of impact.</p><p>Worked: particle A (2 kg, 5 m/s) hits stationary B (3 kg) elastically: 10 = 2v<sub>A</sub> + 3v<sub>B</sub>; 5 = v<sub>B</sub> − v<sub>A</sub> → v<sub>A</sub> = −1, v<sub>B</sub> = 4 m/s.</p><p>Inelastic: momentum conserved, KE lost (heat/sound/deformation).</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'Momentum is', options:['mv<sup>2</sup>','mv','m/v','½mv<sup>2</sup>'], answer:1, explain:'p = mv, vector.'},
    {q:'Impulse equals', options:['F/v','Ft = Δp','mv<sup>2</sup>','F/t'], answer:1, explain:'Impulse = force × time = change in momentum.'},
    {q:'A 4 kg mass at 3 m/s has momentum', options:['7 kg m s<sup>-1</sup>','12 kg m s<sup>-1</sup>','1.33 kg m s<sup>-1</sup>','36 kg m s<sup>-1</sup>'], answer:1, explain:'4 × 3 = 12.'},
    {q:'Momentum is conserved when', options:['collisions are elastic','no external impulse acts','masses are equal','speeds are equal'], answer:1, explain:'Only condition: zero net external force/impulse.'},
    {q:'2 kg at 6 m/s collides with stationary 4 kg; they stick. Speed is', options:['3 m/s','2 m/s','4 m/s','6 m/s'], answer:1, explain:'12 = 6v → v = 2.'},
    {q:'In a perfectly elastic collision', options:['only momentum conserved','momentum and KE conserved','only KE conserved','neither conserved'], answer:1, explain:'Both conserved by definition.'},
    {q:'Newton’s second law in momentum form', options:['F = mv','F = dp/dt','p = Ft','F = m/a'], answer:1, explain:'Force = rate of change of momentum.'},
    {q:'A gun recoils because', options:['of air pressure','momentum must sum to zero (explosion)','of friction','of gravity'], answer:1, explain:'Initially zero momentum: bullet forward, gun backward.'},
    {q:'For elastic impact, relative speeds satisfy', options:['approach = 2 × separation','approach = separation','separation = 0','approach > separation'], answer:1, explain:'Newton’s experimental law: e = 1.'},
    {q:'A 0.2 kg ball changes velocity from 10 to −6 m/s. Impulse magnitude is', options:['0.8 N s','3.2 N s','2 N s','1.2 N s'], answer:1, explain:'Δp = 0.2×(−16) = −3.2: magnitude 3.2 N s.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-mat-t21', title:'Newton’s laws of motion (Mechanics)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-mat-t21-l1', title:'F = ma', html:'<h3>F = ma</h3><p><strong>Newton’s second law:</strong> resultant force = mass × acceleration. Resolve forces, then apply in each direction.</p><p>Worked: 5 kg pulled by 30 N horizontal, friction 10 N: resultant 20 N → a = 4 m s<sup>-2</sup>.</p><p>Weight = mg acts vertically down. Tension pulls away from the body along the string.</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t21-l2', title:'Connected particles', html:'<h3>Connected particles</h3><p><strong>Pulley:</strong> same tension both sides (light, smooth pulley), same speed/acceleration magnitude. Write F = ma for each mass, solve simultaneously.</p><p>Worked: 3 kg and 2 kg over a pulley: 3g − T = 3a; T − 2g = 2a → add: g = 5a → a = 1.962 m s<sup>-2</sup>; T = 2(g + a) = 23.5 N.</p><p><strong>Tow:</strong> car towing caravan: same acceleration; coupling force from separate equations.</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t21-l3', title:'Motion on inclined planes', html:'<h3>Motion on inclined planes</h3><p>Resolve <strong>parallel and perpendicular</strong> to the plane. Perpendicular: R = mg cosθ (no acceleration). Parallel: mg sinθ (± friction) = ma.</p><p>Worked: 10 kg slides down 30° smooth incline: a = g sin30 = 4.905 m s<sup>-2</sup>.</p><p>With friction: a = g(sinθ − μcosθ) down the plane. If μ > tanθ, it does not slide.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'A 4 kg mass under resultant 12 N accelerates at', options:['48 m s<sup>-2</sup>','3 m s<sup>-2</sup>','8 m s<sup>-2</sup>','0.33 m s<sup>-2</sup>'], answer:1, explain:'a = F/m = 3.'},
    {q:'Weight of 2 kg is', options:['2 N','19.6 N','9.81 kg','2/9.81 N'], answer:1, explain:'W = mg = 19.62 N.'},
    {q:'Over a smooth pulley, tension', options:['differs each side','is the same both sides','is zero','equals weight'], answer:1, explain:'Light smooth pulley: uniform string tension.'},
    {q:'3 kg and 2 kg over a pulley: acceleration is', options:['g','g/5 ≈ 1.96 m s<sup>-2</sup>','5g','g/2'], answer:1, explain:'(3−2)g = 5a → a = g/5.'},
    {q:'On a smooth incline θ, acceleration down is', options:['g cosθ','g sinθ','g tanθ','g'], answer:1, explain:'Component mg sinθ = ma.'},
    {q:'With friction μ on incline, a down the plane is', options:['g(sinθ + μcosθ)','g(sinθ − μcosθ)','g cosθ','μg'], answer:1, explain:'Net: mg sinθ − μmg cosθ = ma.'},
    {q:'A block does not slide if', options:['μ < tanθ','μ > tanθ','μ = 0','θ = 90°'], answer:1, explain:'Need mg sinθ ≤ μmg cosθ → tanθ ≤ μ.'},
    {q:'Tension in a tow rope is found by', options:['guessing','F = ma on one vehicle separately','adding masses','ignoring friction'], answer:1, explain:'Separate equation for the towed vehicle gives the coupling force.'},
    {q:'Newton’s third law pair for weight is', options:['normal reaction','the pull of the mass on the Earth','friction','tension'], answer:1, explain:'Weight = Earth pulling mass; pair = mass pulling Earth.'},
    {q:'A lift accelerates up at 2 m/s²; 70 kg person feels normal reaction', options:['686 N','826 N','546 N','70 N'], answer:1, explain:'R − mg = ma → R = 70(9.81+2) = 826.7 N.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-mat-t22', title:'Energy, work and power (Mechanics)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-mat-t22-l1', title:'Work and kinetic energy', html:'<h3>Work and kinetic energy</h3><p><strong>Work</strong> W = Fd cosθ. <strong>Work–energy principle:</strong> net work = ΔKE = ½mv<sup>2</sup> − ½mu<sup>2</sup>.</p><p>Worked: 1500 N braking force stops a 1000 kg car from 20 m/s: −1500d = 0 − ½×1000×400 → d = 133 m.</p><p>Work done against friction becomes heat; work by weight changes GPE.</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t22-l2', title:'Potential energy and conservation', html:'<h3>Potential energy and conservation</h3><p><strong>GPE = mgh</strong>; <strong>EPE = ½kx<sup>2</sup> = ½Fx</strong>. <strong>Conservation of mechanical energy:</strong> if only weight (and normal) do work, KE + PE = constant.</p><p>Worked: pendulum bob (2 kg) released from 0.5 m: v at bottom: ½×2v<sup>2</sup> = 2×9.81×0.5 → v = 3.13 m/s.</p><p>With non-conservative work: KE<sub>1</sub> + PE<sub>1</sub> + W<sub>nc</sub> = KE<sub>2</sub> + PE<sub>2</sub>.</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t22-l3', title:'Power', html:'<h3>Power</h3><p><strong>Power</strong> P = W/t = <strong>Fv</strong> (force in direction of motion). Units: watts.</p><p>Worked: car engine 60 kW at 30 m/s: driving force = 60000/30 = 2000 N.</p><p><strong>Efficiency</strong> = useful power out / total power in. Uphill at constant speed: driving force = component of weight + resistance.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'Work done by 50 N over 4 m (in line) is', options:['12.5 J','200 J','54 J','46 J'], answer:1, explain:'W = Fd = 200 J.'},
    {q:'KE of 2 kg at 6 m/s', options:['12 J','36 J','72 J','6 J'], answer:1, explain:'½×2×36 = 36 J.'},
    {q:'Work–energy principle: net work equals', options:['change in momentum','change in kinetic energy','power','force'], answer:1, explain:'W<sub>net</sub> = Δ(½mv<sup>2</sup>).'},
    {q:'GPE of 5 kg at 10 m (g=10)', options:['50 J','500 J','15 J','2 J'], answer:1, explain:'mgh = 500 J.'},
    {q:'EPE in a spring (k=200, x=0.1) is', options:['20 J','1 J','10 J','2 J'], answer:1, explain:'½×200×0.01 = 1 J.'},
    {q:'Power = Fv: 5000 N at 20 m/s gives', options:['250 W','100 kW','100 W','25 kW'], answer:1, explain:'5000×20 = 100 000 W.'},
    {q:'A 2 kg mass falls 5 m (g=10), no air resistance. Speed at bottom', options:['10 m/s','100 m/s','25 m/s','5 m/s'], answer:0, explain:'½v<sup>2</sup> = 50 → v = 10.'},
    {q:'Efficiency is', options:['input/output','useful output/input','input − output','power × time'], answer:1, explain:'Useful energy out ÷ total energy in.'},
    {q:'Work done by a force perpendicular to displacement is', options:['Fd','zero','−Fd','F/d'], answer:1, explain:'cos 90° = 0.'},
    {q:'Car climbs hill at constant speed: driving force equals', options:['zero','weight component + resistance','weight only','mass × acceleration'], answer:1, explain:'Constant speed: resultant zero; engine balances resistive forces.'}
   ]}
,
  {id:'al-mat-t23', title:'Representation of data (S1)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-mat-t23-l1', title:'Averages and spread', html:'<h3>Averages and spread</h3><p><strong>Mean</strong> x̄ = Σx/n (or Σfx/Σf). <strong>Median:</strong> middle value. <strong>Mode:</strong> most frequent. <strong>Variance</strong> σ<sup>2</sup> = Σ(x−x̄)<sup>2</sup>/n = Σx<sup>2</sup>/n − x̄<sup>2</sup>; <strong>standard deviation</strong> σ = √variance.</p><p>Worked: 2, 4, 4, 6: mean 4; Σx<sup>2</sup> = 72; variance = 72/4 − 16 = 2; σ = √2 ≈ 1.414.</p><p>Coding: y = (x − a)/b → x̄ = a + bȳ; σ<sub>x</sub> = |b|σ<sub>y</sub> (shifting does not change spread).</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t23-l2', title:'Diagrams: histograms, box plots, stem-and-leaf', html:'<h3>Diagrams: histograms, box plots, stem-and-leaf</h3><p><strong>Histogram:</strong> frequency density = frequency/class width on the y-axis (area = frequency). <strong>Box plot:</strong> min, Q<sub>1</sub>, median, Q<sub>3</sub>, max; IQR = Q<sub>3</sub> − Q<sub>1</sub>. <strong>Outlier:</strong> beyond Q<sub>1</sub> − 1.5×IQR or Q<sub>3</sub> + 1.5×IQR.</p><p><strong>Stem-and-leaf:</strong> retains data values; back-to-back compares two sets. <strong>Cumulative frequency</strong> curves: read medians/quartiles/percentiles.</p><p>Skew: mean > median → positive skew (tail right); symmetric → mean ≈ median.</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t23-l3', title:'Choosing summaries', html:'<h3>Choosing summaries</h3><p>Use <strong>median/IQR</strong> for skewed data or with outliers; <strong>mean/SD</strong> for symmetric data. Compare distributions with a measure of location AND spread.</p><p>Worked comparison: “Boys’ median higher but IQR larger → typically taller but more variable.”</p><p>Exam reasoning: always comment on both average and spread; quote values from the data.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'Mean of 3, 5, 7, 9, 11', options:['6','7','5','9'], answer:1, explain:'Sum 35/5 = 7.'},
    {q:'Median of 2, 9, 4, 7, 5', options:['4','5','7','5.4'], answer:1, explain:'Ordered 2,4,5,7,9: middle = 5.'},
    {q:'Variance formula', options:['Σ(x−x̄)/n','Σ(x−x̄)<sup>2</sup>/n','√(Σx<sup>2</sup>/n)','Σx/n'], answer:1, explain:'Mean of squared deviations.'},
    {q:'σ<sup>2</sup> = Σx<sup>2</sup>/n − x̄<sup>2</sup> for 2,4,4,6 gives', options:['1','2','4','16'], answer:1, explain:'72/4 − 16 = 2.'},
    {q:'On a histogram, the y-axis is', options:['frequency','frequency density','class width','cumulative frequency'], answer:1, explain:'Area represents frequency.'},
    {q:'IQR equals', options:['max − min','Q<sub>3</sub> − Q<sub>1</sub>','median − Q<sub>1</sub>','2 × SD'], answer:1, explain:'Interquartile range: middle 50% spread.'},
    {q:'An outlier is beyond', options:['Q<sub>3</sub> + IQR','Q<sub>1</sub> − 1.5×IQR or Q<sub>3</sub> + 1.5×IQR','the median','2 × mean'], answer:1, explain:'1.5×IQR rule.'},
    {q:'If y = (x − 10)/2, σ<sub>x</sub> in terms of σ<sub>y</sub> is', options:['σ<sub>y</sub>','2σ<sub>y</sub>','σ<sub>y</sub>/2','σ<sub>y</sub> + 10'], answer:1, explain:'σ<sub>x</sub> = |b|σ<sub>y</sub> = 2σ<sub>y</sub>.'},
    {q:'Mean > median suggests', options:['negative skew','positive skew','symmetry','no mode'], answer:1, explain:'Tail to the right pulls the mean up.'},
    {q:'For skewed data with outliers, best summaries are', options:['mean and SD','median and IQR','mode only','range only'], answer:1, explain:'Median/IQR resist outliers and skew.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-mat-t24', title:'Permutations and combinations (S1)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-mat-t24-l1', title:'The factorial and counting principles', html:'<h3>The factorial and counting principles</h3><p><strong>Multiplication principle:</strong> m ways then n ways → m×n total. <strong>n! = n(n−1)…1</strong>; 0! = 1.</p><p>Arranging n distinct objects in a row: n! ways. With restrictions: fix positions first, then arrange the rest.</p><p>Worked: 5 books in a row: 120 ways. Two specific books together: treat as a block: 2 × 4! = 48.</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t24-l2', title:'Permutations', html:'<h3>Permutations</h3><p><strong>Permutation</strong> (order matters): <strong><sup>n</sup>P<sub>r</sub> = n!/(n−r)!</strong> — choose and arrange r from n.</p><p>Worked: 3-digit numbers from {1,2,3,4,5} without repetition: <sup>5</sup>P<sub>3</sub> = 60. Even numbers: last digit 2 or 4: 2 × <sup>4</sup>P<sub>2</sub> = 24.</p><p>Arrangements with repeated items: n!/(repeats!) e.g. MISSISSIPPI: 11!/(4!4!2!).</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t24-l3', title:'Combinations', html:'<h3>Combinations</h3><p><strong>Combination</strong> (order irrelevant): <strong><sup>n</sup>C<sub>r</sub> = n!/(r!(n−r)!)</strong>.</p><p>Worked: choose 3 from 10: <sup>10</sup>C<sub>3</sub> = 120. Committee of 2 men from 5 and 3 women from 7: <sup>5</sup>C<sub>2</sub> × <sup>7</sup>C<sub>3</sub> = 10 × 35 = 350.</p><p>Exam reasoning: “arrange/seated/order” → permutations; “choose/team/committee” → combinations. <sup>n</sup>C<sub>r</sub> = <sup>n</sup>C<sub>n−r</sub>.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'5! equals', options:['25','120','60','20'], answer:1, explain:'5×4×3×2×1 = 120.'},
    {q:'<sup>6</sup>P<sub>2</sub> equals', options:['15','30','12','36'], answer:1, explain:'6×5 = 30.'},
    {q:'<sup>6</sup>C<sub>2</sub> equals', options:['30','15','12','36'], answer:1, explain:'6!/(2!4!) = 15.'},
    {q:'Ways to arrange 4 distinct books', options:['12','24','16','4'], answer:1, explain:'4! = 24.'},
    {q:'Choose 2 from 8 (order irrelevant)', options:['56','28','16','64'], answer:1, explain:'<sup>8</sup>C<sub>2</sub> = 28.'},
    {q:'<sup>n</sup>C<sub>r</sub> equals', options:['<sup>n</sup>P<sub>r</sub>','<sup>n</sup>C<sub>n−r</sub>','<sup>n</sup>P<sub>n−r</sub>','n!/r!'], answer:1, explain:'Choosing r is the same as leaving n−r.'},
    {q:'0! equals', options:['0','1','undefined','−1'], answer:1, explain:'By definition (empty arrangement).'},
    {q:'Letters of “AAB” arranged', options:['6','3','2','1'], answer:1, explain:'3!/2! = 3.'},
    {q:'Use permutations when', options:['selecting a team','order/arrangement matters','choosing lottery numbers','forming a committee'], answer:1, explain:'Permutations count ordered selections.'},
    {q:'PIN codes of 4 distinct digits from 0–9', options:['<sup>10</sup>C<sub>4</sub>','<sup>10</sup>P<sub>4</sub> = 5040','10<sup>4</sup>','4!'], answer:1, explain:'Order matters, no repetition: <sup>10</sup>P<sub>4</sub>.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-mat-t25', title:'Probability (S1)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-mat-t25-l1', title:'Basic rules', html:'<h3>Basic rules</h3><p><strong>P(A′) = 1 − P(A)</strong>. <strong>Addition:</strong> P(A∪B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A∩B); if mutually exclusive, P(A∪B) = P(A) + P(B). <strong>Multiplication:</strong> P(A∩B) = P(A) × P(B|A); if independent, P(A∩B) = P(A)P(B).</p><p><strong>Conditional:</strong> P(B|A) = P(A∩B)/P(A).</p><p>Worked: P(rain) = 0.3, P(wind|rain) = 0.6: P(rain and wind) = 0.18.</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t25-l2', title:'Venn diagrams and tree diagrams', html:'<h3>Venn diagrams and tree diagrams</h3><p><strong>Venn:</strong> organise overlapping categories; fill from the centre out. <strong>Tree diagrams:</strong> sequential events; multiply along branches, add across outcomes.</p><p>Worked: bag with 3 red, 2 blue; two drawn without replacement: P(both red) = (3/5)×(2/4) = 0.3; P(one of each) = 2×(3/5)×(2/4) = 0.6.</p><p>“Without replacement” → dependent; “with replacement” → independent.</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t25-l3', title:'Independence and exclusive events', html:'<h3>Independence and exclusive events</h3><p><strong>Independent:</strong> P(B|A) = P(B); check P(A∩B) = P(A)P(B). <strong>Mutually exclusive:</strong> P(A∩B) = 0 — cannot happen together.</p><p>Worked: die roll: A = even, B = >4: P(A∩B) = P(6) = 1/6; P(A)P(B) = (1/2)(1/3) = 1/6 → independent.</p><p>Common trap: “mutually exclusive” ≠ “independent” (except trivial cases).</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'P(A′) if P(A) = 0.7', options:['0.7','0.3','1.7','0.43'], answer:1, explain:'1 − 0.7 = 0.3.'},
    {q:'If A and B are mutually exclusive, P(A∪B) equals', options:['P(A)P(B)','P(A) + P(B)','P(A) − P(B)','0'], answer:1, explain:'No overlap: add.'},
    {q:'If independent, P(A∩B) equals', options:['P(A) + P(B)','P(A)P(B)','0','P(A)/P(B)'], answer:1, explain:'Multiply for independent events.'},
    {q:'P(B|A) equals', options:['P(A∩B)/P(A)','P(A)/P(B)','P(A∪B)','P(A)P(B)'], answer:0, explain:'Conditional probability formula.'},
    {q:'Two coins tossed: P(two heads)', options:['1/2','1/4','3/4','1/3'], answer:1, explain:'(1/2)×(1/2).'},
    {q:'Bag 3R 2B, two drawn without replacement: P(both red)', options:['9/25','3/10','6/25','1/2'], answer:1, explain:'(3/5)×(2/4) = 6/20 = 3/10.'},
    {q:'Events A, B with P(A∩B) = P(A)P(B) are', options:['mutually exclusive','independent','dependent','complementary'], answer:1, explain:'Product rule characterises independence.'},
    {q:'P(A∪B) = 0.8, P(A) = 0.5, P(B) = 0.4. P(A∩B) is', options:['0.9','0.1','0.2','0.0'], answer:1, explain:'0.8 = 0.5 + 0.4 − P(A∩B) → 0.1.'},
    {q:'“At least one” is found via', options:['addition','1 − P(none)','multiplication','subtraction'], answer:1, explain:'Complement is usually easier.'},
    {q:'Rolling a die twice: P(sum 12)', options:['1/12','1/36','1/6','2/36 simplified 1/18'], answer:1, explain:'Only (6,6): 1/36.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-mat-t26', title:'Discrete random variables (S1)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-mat-t26-l1', title:'Probability distributions', html:'<h3>Probability distributions</h3><p>A <strong>discrete random variable</strong> takes countable values. <strong>Probability distribution:</strong> table of x and P(X=x); <strong>ΣP = 1</strong>.</p><p>Worked: two coins, X = number of heads: P(0)=1/4, P(1)=1/2, P(2)=1/4.</p><p>Find missing probabilities using ΣP = 1: if P(1)=k, P(2)=2k, P(3)=3k: 6k=1 → k=1/6.</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t26-l2', title:'Expectation and variance', html:'<h3>Expectation and variance</h3><p><strong>E(X) = ΣxP(X=x)</strong> (mean). <strong>Var(X) = E(X<sup>2</sup>) − [E(X)]<sup>2</sup></strong> = Σx<sup>2</sup>P − μ<sup>2</sup>.</p><p>Worked: X: 0(0.25), 1(0.5), 2(0.25): E(X) = 1; E(X<sup>2</sup>) = 0 + 0.5 + 1 = 1.5; Var = 1.5 − 1 = 0.5.</p><p><strong>E(aX + b) = aE(X) + b</strong>; <strong>Var(aX + b) = a<sup>2</sup>Var(X)</strong> (adding b does not change spread).</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t26-l3', title:'The binomial distribution', html:'<h3>The binomial distribution</h3><p><strong>Binomial B(n, p):</strong> n fixed independent trials, two outcomes, constant p. <strong>P(X=r) = <sup>n</sup>C<sub>r</sub> p<sup>r</sup>(1−p)<sup>n−r</sup></strong>.</p><p><strong>Mean = np</strong>; <strong>variance = np(1−p)</strong>.</p><p>Worked: 10 coins, X = heads ~ B(10, 0.5): P(X=3) = <sup>10</sup>C<sub>3</sup>(0.5)<sup>10</sup> = 120/1024 ≈ 0.117. Use cumulative tables for P(X ≤ r).</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'For a probability distribution, ΣP(X=x) equals', options:['0','1','E(X)','n'], answer:1, explain:'Probabilities sum to 1.'},
    {q:'E(X) for X: 1(0.2), 2(0.5), 3(0.3)', options:['2.0','2.1','1.8','6.0'], answer:1, explain:'0.2 + 1.0 + 0.9 = 2.1.'},
    {q:'Var(X) = E(X<sup>2</sup>) − μ<sup>2</sup>. If E(X<sup>2</sup>)=5, μ=2: Var =', options:['1','9','3','21'], answer:0, explain:'5 − 4 = 1.'},
    {q:'Var(3X + 2) in terms of Var(X)', options:['3Var(X)','9Var(X)','3Var(X)+2','Var(X)+2'], answer:1, explain:'Var(aX+b) = a<sup>2</sup>Var(X).'},
    {q:'Binomial applies when', options:['trials dependent','fixed n, independent trials, constant p','p changes','infinite trials'], answer:1, explain:'B(n,p) conditions.'},
    {q:'X ~ B(8, 0.25). P(X=2) is', options:['<sup>8</sup>C<sub>2</sub>(0.25)<sup>2</sup>(0.75)<sup>6</sup>','(0.25)<sup>2</sup>','<sup>8</sup>C<sub>2</sub>(0.25)<sup>8</sup>','8×0.25×2'], answer:0, explain:'Binomial formula.'},
    {q:'Mean of B(20, 0.3)', options:['6','14','4.2','0.3'], answer:0, explain:'np = 6.'},
    {q:'Variance of B(20, 0.3)', options:['6','4.2','14','2.05'], answer:1, explain:'npq = 20×0.3×0.7 = 4.2.'},
    {q:'E(2X − 1) if E(X) = 4', options:['8','7','9','3'], answer:1, explain:'2×4 − 1 = 7.'},
    {q:'A discrete random variable', options:['takes any real value','takes countable distinct values','is always binomial','has no variance'], answer:1, explain:'Countable outcomes (e.g. counts).'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-mat-t27', title:'The normal distribution (S1)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-mat-t27-l1', title:'Properties of the normal curve', html:'<h3>Properties of the normal curve</h3><p><strong>X ~ N(μ, σ<sup>2</sup>)</strong>: symmetric bell curve, mean = median = mode = μ. <strong>Empirical rule:</strong> ~68% within μ±σ, ~95% within μ±2σ, ~99.7% within μ±3σ.</p><p>Total area = 1. The curve never touches the axis. σ controls spread (width).</p><p>Many natural variables (heights, measurement errors) are approximately normal (central limit theorem).</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t27-l2', title:'Standardisation', html:'<h3>Standardisation</h3><p><strong>Z = (X − μ)/σ ~ N(0, 1)</strong>. Convert to z-scores, then use tables (or symmetry): P(Z < a) = Φ(a).</p><p>Worked: X ~ N(100, 15<sup>2</sup>): P(X < 115) = P(Z < 1) = 0.8413. P(X > 115) = 1 − 0.8413 = 0.1587. P(85 < X < 115) = Φ(1) − Φ(−1) = 0.6826.</p><p>Use <strong>Φ(−a) = 1 − Φ(a)</strong>. For “find a given probability”, invert: find z then x = μ + zσ.</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t27-l3', title:'Inverse normal and approximations', html:'<h3>Inverse normal and approximations</h3><p><strong>Inverse:</strong> given P(X < a) = 0.95 with X ~ N(50, 10<sup>2</sup>): z = 1.6449 → a = 50 + 16.449 = 66.45.</p><p><strong>Binomial → normal:</strong> if np > 5 and nq > 5, B(n,p) ≈ N(np, npq) with <strong>continuity correction</strong> (±0.5): P(X ≤ 40) ≈ P(Y < 40.5).</p><p>Worked: B(100, 0.4) ≈ N(40, 24): P(X ≥ 45) ≈ P(Z > (44.5−40)/√24) = P(Z > 0.9186) ≈ 0.179.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'In N(μ, σ<sup>2</sup>), the percentage within μ ± 2σ is about', options:['68%','95%','99.7%','50%'], answer:1, explain:'Empirical rule.'},
    {q:'Standardising: Z equals', options:['(X + μ)/σ','(X − μ)/σ','Xσ/μ','(μ − X)σ'], answer:1, explain:'Z = (X−μ)/σ ~ N(0,1).'},
    {q:'Φ(−1.5) equals', options:['Φ(1.5)','1 − Φ(1.5)','−Φ(1.5)','0'], answer:1, explain:'Symmetry: Φ(−a) = 1 − Φ(a).'},
    {q:'X ~ N(20, 4<sup>2</sup>). P(X < 24) is', options:['Φ(0.5)','Φ(1) = 0.8413','Φ(2)','0.5'], answer:1, explain:'z = (24−20)/4 = 1.'},
    {q:'The normal curve is', options:['skewed right','symmetric about μ','bimodal','discrete'], answer:1, explain:'Symmetric bell: mean = median = mode.'},
    {q:'X ~ N(50, 10<sup>2</sup>), P(X < a) = 0.95 → a ≈', options:['60','66.4','70','55'], answer:1, explain:'z = 1.6449: a = 50 + 16.449.'},
    {q:'Continuity correction for P(X ≤ 40) uses', options:['40','40.5','39.5','41'], answer:1, explain:'Include the whole bar at 40: up to 40.5.'},
    {q:'Normal approximation to binomial needs', options:['n > 30 only','np > 5 and nq > 5','p = 0.5','np < 5'], answer:1, explain:'Both successes and failures expected > 5.'},
    {q:'σ in N(μ, σ<sup>2</sup>) controls', options:['centre','spread','skew','height only'], answer:1, explain:'Larger σ: wider, flatter curve.'},
    {q:'P(μ − σ < X < μ + σ) ≈', options:['0.95','0.68','0.997','0.5'], answer:1, explain:'One sigma: ~68%.'}
   ]}
,
  {id:'al-mat-t28', title:'The Poisson distribution (S2)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-mat-t28-l1', title:'Conditions and formula', html:'<h3>Conditions and formula</h3><p><strong>Poisson Po(λ):</strong> events occur singly, at a constant average rate, independently, in time/space. <strong>P(X = r) = e<sup>−λ</sup>λ<sup>r</sup>/r!</strong></p><p><strong>Mean = λ, variance = λ.</strong> Examples: calls per hour, flaws per metre, decays per second.</p><p>Worked: calls average 3 per hour, X ~ Po(3): P(X=2) = e<sup>−3</sup>×9/2 ≈ 0.224.</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t28-l2', title:'Scaling λ and sums', html:'<h3>Scaling λ and sums</h3><p><strong>Scale λ</strong> with the interval: 3/hour → 6 per 2 hours; 1.5 per 30 min. <strong>Sum of independent Poissons:</strong> Po(λ<sub>1</sub>) + Po(λ<sub>2</sub>) ~ Po(λ<sub>1</sub>+λ<sub>2</sub>).</p><p>Worked: flaws average 0.4/m; 5 m of cable: λ = 2; P(no flaws) = e<sup>−2</sup> ≈ 0.135.</p><p>Use cumulative tables for P(X ≤ r); P(X > r) = 1 − P(X ≤ r).</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t28-l3', title:'Poisson as a limit of binomial', html:'<h3>Poisson as a limit of binomial</h3><p>When <strong>n large, p small, np = λ moderate</strong>, B(n,p) ≈ Po(np). Rule of thumb: n > 50, p < 0.1.</p><p>Worked: 200 components, defect probability 0.01: X ≈ Po(2): P(X=0) = e<sup>−2</sup> ≈ 0.135.</p><p>Exam reasoning: check conditions (rare events, many trials) before approximating.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'Poisson formula P(X=r)', options:['<sup>n</sup>C<sub>r</sub>p<sup>r</sup>q<sup>n−r</sup>','e<sup>−λ</sup>λ<sup>r</sup>/r!','λ<sup>r</sup>/r!','e<sup>−r</sup>λ<sup>r</sup>/r!'], answer:1, explain:'Standard Poisson probability.'},
    {q:'Mean and variance of Po(λ)', options:['λ and λ<sup>2</sup>','both λ','λ and √λ','np and npq'], answer:1, explain:'Mean = variance = λ.'},
    {q:'X ~ Po(4). P(X=0) is', options:['0','e<sup>−4</sup> ≈ 0.0183','4e<sup>−4</sup>','1/4'], answer:1, explain:'e<sup>−4</sup>×4<sup>0</sup>/0! = e<sup>−4</sup>.'},
    {q:'Calls average 2 per hour. For 3 hours, λ is', options:['2','6','2/3','5'], answer:1, explain:'Scale: 2×3 = 6.'},
    {q:'Po(2) + Po(3) (independent) is', options:['Po(5)','Po(6)','Po(2.5)','Normal'], answer:0, explain:'Sums add: λ = 5.'},
    {q:'Poisson approximates binomial when', options:['n small, p large','n large, p small (np moderate)','p = 0.5','n = λ'], answer:1, explain:'Rare events in many trials.'},
    {q:'200 items, p(defect) = 0.02. Approximate with', options:['B(200,0.5)','Po(4)','N(4,4)','Po(200)'], answer:1, explain:'λ = np = 4.'},
    {q:'Poisson models', options:['fixed trials','events in time/space at constant rate','only successes','continuous data'], answer:1, explain:'Counts of random events per interval.'},
    {q:'X ~ Po(5). Using tables, P(X ≤ 2) is found by', options:['formula only','cumulative Poisson tables','normal tables','1 − P(X>2) only'], answer:1, explain:'Sum r=0..2 or cumulative tables.'},
    {q:'If events occur at 0.5/min, in 10 min λ equals', options:['0.5','5','10','0.05'], answer:1, explain:'0.5 × 10 = 5.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-mat-t29', title:'Linear combinations of random variables (S2)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-mat-t29-l1', title:'Expectation of combinations', html:'<h3>Expectation of combinations</h3><p><strong>E(aX + bY + c) = aE(X) + bE(Y) + c</strong> — always true, no independence needed.</p><p>Worked: E(X)=10, E(Y)=4: E(3X − 2Y + 5) = 30 − 8 + 5 = 27.</p><p>Applies to sample means: E(X̄) = μ.</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t29-l2', title:'Variance of combinations', html:'<h3>Variance of combinations</h3><p>For <strong>independent</strong> X, Y: <strong>Var(aX + bY + c) = a<sup>2</sup>Var(X) + b<sup>2</sup>Var(Y)</strong> — note PLUS even for subtraction (variance of a difference adds).</p><p>Worked: Var(X)=4, Var(Y)=9: Var(2X − Y) = 4×4 + 1×9 = 25.</p><p>Common trap: Var(X − Y) = Var(X) + Var(Y), NOT minus.</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t29-l3', title:'Normal combinations', html:'<h3>Normal combinations</h3><p>Linear combinations of <strong>independent normals are normal</strong>: if X ~ N(μ<sub>1</sub>, σ<sub>1</sub><sup>2</sup>), Y ~ N(μ<sub>2</sub>, σ<sub>2</sub><sup>2</sup>): <strong>aX + bY ~ N(aμ<sub>1</sub>+bμ<sub>2</sub>, a<sup>2</sup>σ<sub>1</sub><sup>2</sup>+b<sup>2</sup>σ<sub>2</sub><sup>2</sup>)</strong>.</p><p>Worked: X ~ N(10, 4), Y ~ N(6, 9): X + Y ~ N(16, 13); X − Y ~ N(4, 13).</p><p>Use for total weight, differences, averages of normal variables.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'E(2X + 3Y) given E(X)=5, E(Y)=7', options:['31','25','17','10'], answer:0, explain:'10 + 21 = 31.'},
    {q:'Var(X − Y) for independent X, Y equals', options:['Var(X) − Var(Y)','Var(X) + Var(Y)','|Var(X) − Var(Y)|','0'], answer:1, explain:'Variance adds even for differences.'},
    {q:'Var(4X) equals', options:['4Var(X)','16Var(X)','Var(X) + 4','4 + Var(X)'], answer:1, explain:'a<sup>2</sup> = 16.'},
    {q:'E(aX+bY+c) needs X, Y independent?', options:['yes','no — always true','only for normal','only if a=b'], answer:1, explain:'Expectation is linear regardless.'},
    {q:'X ~ N(5, 2), Y ~ N(3, 8) independent. X+Y ~', options:['N(8, 10)','N(8, 100)','N(2, 10)','N(8, 6)'], answer:0, explain:'Mean 8, variance 2+8 = 10.'},
    {q:'X − Y for the above is', options:['N(2, 10)','N(2, −6)','N(8, 10)','N(2, 6)'], answer:0, explain:'Mean 5−3=2, variance 2+8=10.'},
    {q:'2X where X ~ N(10, 9) is', options:['N(20, 18)','N(20, 36)','N(20, 9)','N(10, 36)'], answer:1, explain:'Mean 20, variance 4×9 = 36.'},
    {q:'Var(X + 5) equals', options:['Var(X) + 5','Var(X)','Var(X) + 25','5Var(X)'], answer:1, explain:'Adding a constant does not change spread.'},
    {q:'Sums of independent normals are', options:['binomial','normal','Poisson','uniform'], answer:1, explain:'Normal family closed under linear combinations.'},
    {q:'If Var(X)=3 and Var(Y)=2 (independent), Var(2X−3Y) equals', options:['12','20','30','18'], answer:2, explain:'4×3 + 9×2 = 12 + 18 = 30 (variance adds for differences).'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-mat-t30', title:'Continuous random variables (S2)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-mat-t30-l1', title:'Probability density functions', html:'<h3>Probability density functions</h3><p><strong>p.d.f. f(x):</strong> f(x) ≥ 0; <strong>∫f(x)dx = 1</strong> over the domain. <strong>P(a < X < b) = ∫<sub>a</sub><sup>b</sup> f(x) dx</strong> (area).</p><p>Worked: f(x) = kx<sup>2</sup>, 0 ≤ x ≤ 2: ∫<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup>kx<sup>2</sup>dx = 8k/3 = 1 → k = 3/8.</p><p>P(X = a) = 0 for continuous variables — only intervals have probability.</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t30-l2', title:'Expectation and variance', html:'<h3>Expectation and variance</h3><p><strong>E(X) = ∫x f(x) dx</strong>; <strong>Var(X) = E(X<sup>2</sup>) − μ<sup>2</sup></strong>, where E(X<sup>2</sup>) = ∫x<sup>2</sup>f(x)dx.</p><p>Worked: f(x) = 3x<sup>2</sup>/8, 0–2: E(X) = ∫3x<sup>3</sup>/8 dx = [3x<sup>4</sup>/32]<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> = 48/32 = 1.5.</p><p>Median m: ∫<sup>m</sup>f = 0.5. Mode: maximum of f.</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t30-l3', title:'Cumulative distribution functions', html:'<h3>Cumulative distribution functions</h3><p><strong>c.d.f. F(x) = P(X ≤ x) = ∫<sup>x</sup>f(t)dt</strong>. Properties: F → 0 (left), F → 1 (right), non-decreasing; <strong>f(x) = F′(x)</strong>.</p><p>Worked: F(x) = x<sup>2</sup>/4, 0 ≤ x ≤ 2: P(X > 1) = 1 − F(1) = 1 − 1/4 = 3/4; f(x) = x/2.</p><p>P(a < X < b) = F(b) − F(a).</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'A valid p.d.f. must satisfy', options:['f(x) ≤ 0','∫f(x)dx = 1 and f(x) ≥ 0','f(x) = 1','Σf = 1'], answer:1, explain:'Non-negative, total area 1.'},
    {q:'P(a < X < b) for continuous X is', options:['f(b) − f(a)','∫<sub>a</sub><sup>b</sup> f(x)dx','F(a) − F(b)','f(a) + f(b)'], answer:1, explain:'Area under the p.d.f.'},
    {q:'f(x) = kx, 0 ≤ x ≤ 4 is a p.d.f. k equals', options:['1/4','1/8','1/2','1/16'], answer:1, explain:'∫<sub>0</sub><sup>4</sup>kx dx = 8k = 1 → k = 1/8.'},
    {q:'P(X = 2) for a continuous variable is', options:['f(2)','0','1','0.5'], answer:1, explain:'Single points have zero probability.'},
    {q:'E(X) for continuous X is', options:['ΣxP(x)','∫x f(x)dx','∫f(x)dx','max of f'], answer:1, explain:'Integral analogue of ΣxP.'},
    {q:'The c.d.f. F(x) equals', options:['f′(x)','P(X ≤ x)','1 − f(x)','∫f'], answer:1, explain:'Cumulative probability up to x.'},
    {q:'f(x) in terms of F(x) is', options:['∫F','F′(x)','1 − F','F<sup>2</sup>'], answer:1, explain:'Differentiate the c.d.f.'},
    {q:'F(x) = x<sup>3</sup>/8, 0≤x≤2. P(X ≤ 1) is', options:['1/2','1/8','1/4','3/8'], answer:1, explain:'F(1) = 1/8.'},
    {q:'The median m satisfies', options:['f(m) = 0.5','F(m) = 0.5','E(X) = m','f′(m) = 0'], answer:1, explain:'Half the probability below m.'},
    {q:'P(X > b) equals', options:['F(b)','1 − F(b)','f(b)','F(b) − 1'], answer:1, explain:'Complement of P(X ≤ b).'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-mat-t31', title:'Sampling and estimation (S2)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-mat-t31-l1', title:'Populations, samples and bias', html:'<h3>Populations, samples and bias</h3><p><strong>Population:</strong> all items of interest; <strong>sample:</strong> the subset studied. <strong>Random sampling:</strong> every member equally likely — avoids <strong>bias</strong>.</p><p>Methods: <strong>simple random</strong> (lottery/random numbers), <strong>systematic</strong> (every kth), <strong>stratified</strong> (proportional from each subgroup), <strong>quota</strong> (non-random — can be biased), <strong>opportunity</strong> (convenient — biased).</p><p><strong>Sampling frame:</strong> the list used. Bias sources: non-response, leading questions, unrepresentative frame.</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t31-l2', title:'Unbiased estimators', html:'<h3>Unbiased estimators</h3><p><strong>Unbiased estimator:</strong> E(estimator) = true parameter. <strong>Sample mean x̄</strong> is unbiased for μ. <strong>Sample variance:</strong> unbiased estimator of σ<sup>2</sup> is <strong>s<sup>2</sup> = Σ(x−x̄)<sup>2</sup>/(n−1)</strong> (divide by n−1, not n).</p><p>Worked: data 4, 6, 8: x̄ = 6; Σ(x−x̄)<sup>2</sup> = 8; unbiased variance = 8/2 = 4.</p><p>Standard error of the mean: σ/√n.</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t31-l3', title:'Confidence intervals', html:'<h3>Confidence intervals</h3><p><strong>Central limit theorem:</strong> X̄ ≈ N(μ, σ<sup>2</sup>/n) for large n (n ≥ 30, or normal population). <strong>95% CI for μ:</strong> <strong>x̄ ± 1.96σ/√n</strong> (σ known); 99%: ±2.576σ/√n.</p><p>Worked: n = 100, x̄ = 50, σ = 10: 95% CI = 50 ± 1.96 = (48.04, 51.96).</p><p>Interpretation: 95% of such intervals contain μ (NOT “95% probability μ is inside”). Wider with higher confidence or smaller n.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'Random sampling is important to', options:['save time','avoid bias','get large samples','please examiners'], answer:1, explain:'Every member equally likely: representative.'},
    {q:'Stratified sampling', options:['picks conveniently','samples proportionally from each subgroup','picks every 10th','asks volunteers'], answer:1, explain:'Guarantees representation of each stratum.'},
    {q:'An unbiased estimator satisfies', options:['estimate = parameter always','E(estimator) = parameter','variance = 0','n is large'], answer:1, explain:'Correct on average.'},
    {q:'Unbiased estimate of population variance uses divisor', options:['n','n − 1','n + 1','√n'], answer:1, explain:'s<sup>2</sup> = Σ(x−x̄)<sup>2</sup>/(n−1).'},
    {q:'Data 4,6,8: unbiased variance estimate', options:['8/3','4','8','2'], answer:1, explain:'Σ(x−x̄)<sup>2</sup> = 8; ÷(3−1) = 4.'},
    {q:'95% CI for μ (σ known)', options:['x̄ ± σ','x̄ ± 1.96σ/√n','x̄ ± 2.576σ','x̄ ± σ/n'], answer:1, explain:'Standard normal 95% critical value 1.96.'},
    {q:'99% CI uses critical value', options:['1.96','2.576','1.645','3.00'], answer:1, explain:'Φ(z) = 0.995 → z = 2.576.'},
    {q:'Central limit theorem: X̄ is approximately normal when', options:['n is small','n large (or population normal)','data is skewed','σ unknown'], answer:1, explain:'n ≥ 30 typically suffices.'},
    {q:'Increasing n makes the CI', options:['wider','narrower','unchanged','invalid'], answer:1, explain:'σ/√n shrinks: more precision.'},
    {q:'A 95% CI means', options:['P(μ inside) = 0.95','95% of such intervals contain μ','μ is inside','95% of data inside'], answer:1, explain:'Frequentist interpretation: long-run coverage.'}
   ]},
  {id:'al-mat-t32', title:'Hypothesis tests (S2)', urdu:'', comingSoon:false,
   lessons:[
    {id:'al-mat-t32-l1', title:'The logic of hypothesis testing', html:'<h3>The logic of hypothesis testing</h3><p><strong>H<sub>0</sub> (null):</strong> the default (e.g. p = 0.5, μ = μ<sub>0</sub>). <strong>H<sub>1</sub> (alternative):</strong> what we suspect (≠, <, >). <strong>Significance level α</strong> (often 5%): reject H<sub>0</sub> if the test statistic is in the <strong>critical region</strong> (probability ≤ α under H<sub>0</sub>).</p><p>One-tailed (H<sub>1</sub>: > or <) vs two-tailed (H<sub>1</sub>: ≠). <strong>p-value:</strong> probability of results at least as extreme under H<sub>0</sub>; reject if p < α.</p><p><strong>Type I error:</strong> rejecting true H<sub>0</sub>; <strong>Type II:</strong> accepting false H<sub>0</sub>.</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t32-l2', title:'Binomial and Poisson tests', html:'<h3>Binomial and Poisson tests</h3><p><strong>Binomial test:</strong> X ~ B(n, p<sub>0</sub>) under H<sub>0</sub>; compute P(X ≥ observed) (for H<sub>1</sub>: p > p<sub>0</sub>) using tables/formula; reject if ≤ α.</p><p>Worked: coin tossed 20 times, 15 heads, H<sub>1</sub>: p > 0.5, α = 5%: P(X ≥ 15) under B(20, 0.5) = 0.0207 < 0.05 → reject H<sub>0</sub>: evidence of bias towards heads.</p><p><strong>Poisson test:</strong> similar with Po(λ<sub>0</sub>). <strong>Normal test for mean:</strong> z = (x̄ − μ<sub>0</sub>)/(σ/√n); compare with ±1.96 (two-tailed 5%).</p>'},
    {id:'al-mat-t32-l3', title:'Normal tests and conclusions', html:'<h3>Normal tests and conclusions</h3><p><strong>Test for μ (σ known):</strong> z = (x̄ − μ<sub>0</sub>)/(σ/√n) ~ N(0,1) under H<sub>0</sub>. Two-tailed 5%: reject if |z| > 1.96. One-tailed 5%: z > 1.645 (or z < −1.645).</p><p>Worked: H<sub>0</sub>: μ = 100, n = 64, x̄ = 103, σ = 12: z = 3/(12/8) = 2.0 > 1.96 → reject H<sub>0</sub> at 5% (two-tailed): significant evidence mean differs from 100.</p><p>Conclusions: “reject H<sub>0</sub>: sufficient evidence at the 5% level that…”; “do not reject: insufficient evidence…”. Never “prove” H<sub>0</sub>.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'H<sub>0</sub> is', options:['what we want to prove','the default assumption tested','always false','the alternative'], answer:1, explain:'Null hypothesis: status quo, tested against data.'},
    {q:'A 5% significance level means', options:['95% sure','reject H<sub>0</sub> if P(data|H<sub>0</sub>) ≤ 0.05','H<sub>0</sub> is 5% true','accept H<sub>1</sub> 5% of the time'], answer:1, explain:'Critical region has 5% probability under H<sub>0</sub>.'},
    {q:'A one-tailed test is used when H<sub>1</sub> is', options:['p ≠ p<sub>0</sub>','p > p<sub>0</sub> (directional)','always','never'], answer:1, explain:'Directional alternative: one tail.'},
    {q:'Type I error is', options:['accepting false H<sub>0</sub>','rejecting a true H<sub>0</sub>','a calculation error','using wrong α'], answer:1, explain:'False positive: probability = α.'},
    {q:'The p-value is', options:['P(H<sub>0</sub> true)','P(results at least as extreme | H<sub>0</sub>)','the significance level','1 − α'], answer:1, explain:'Smaller p: stronger evidence against H<sub>0</sub>.'},
    {q:'Coin 20 tosses, 15 heads, H<sub>1</sub>: p>0.5: test statistic probability is', options:['P(X=15)','P(X ≥ 15) under B(20,0.5)','P(X ≤ 15)','15/20'], answer:1, explain:'“At least as extreme” in the H<sub>1</sub> direction.'},
    {q:'z-test for mean: z equals', options:['(x̄ − μ<sub>0</sub>)σ/√n','(x̄ − μ<sub>0</sub>)/(σ/√n)','(μ<sub>0</sub> − x̄)/σ','x̄√n/σ'], answer:1, explain:'Standardised sample mean.'},
    {q:'Two-tailed 5% critical values are', options:['±1.645','±1.96','±2.576','±1.28'], answer:1, explain:'2.5% in each tail.'},
    {q:'One-tailed 5% (upper) critical value', options:['1.96','1.645','2.576','1.28'], answer:1, explain:'5% in one tail: z = 1.645.'},
    {q:'If p-value > α, you', options:['reject H<sub>0</sub>','do not reject H<sub>0</sub> (insufficient evidence)','accept H<sub>1</sub>','retest'], answer:1, explain:'Not significant: insufficient evidence against H<sub>0</sub>.'}
   ]}
]});
